const extraStudyPages = [{"chapter": "chapter-4", "page": 2, "english": ["The wrist pulse is floating and tight, yet the physician purges the patient. The text calls this a grave error. It reads the floating quality as a lack of blood and the tight quality as cold. When cold acts on the body, the intestines rumble. The physician fails to recognize this and gives the patient cold water instead, preventing sweat from coming out. The water meets the cold influence and the two reinforce each other, bringing on hiccups.", "The wrist pulse is faint and the cubit pulse is tight. The person is depleted and sweats profusely. The text reads this as yin continually present with no sign of yang.", "The wrist pulse is floating and large. The floating quality indicates wind in a state of deficiency, and the large quality indicates strong qi. As wind and qi interact, an itchy rash develops. The text calls the itching leaking wind and says that persistent cases develop into crusted sores.", "The wrist pulse is floating and large. Here the floating quality indicates deficiency and the large quality indicates excess. At the cubit the pattern is called closure, and at the inch it is called blockage. The text associates closure with inability to urinate and blockage with upward surging and vomiting.", "The wrist pulse is faint and rough. Faintness means that defensive qi fails to circulate, and roughness means that nutritive qi falls short. When the two can no longer support each other, the three burners lose their support, and the body becomes numb and loses sensation. Insufficient nutritive qi is associated with distressing pain and difficulty speaking. Deficient defensive qi is associated with aversion to cold and frequent yawning. When the three burners fail to operate in their proper regions, the text associates the upper burner with belching and sour regurgitation, the middle burner with inability to digest food or take more in, and the lower burner with involuntary urination.", "The wrist pulse is faint and rough. Faintness indicates waning defensive qi and roughness indicates insufficient nutritive qi. In this account, the complexion turns yellow when defensive qi wanes and takes on a bluish cast when nutritive qi is insufficient. Nutritive qi is the root and defensive qi is the foliage. When both grow faint, root and leaves wither, and the text describes chills with shivering, coughing, fishy-smelling spittle, and the bringing up of frothy saliva.", "The wrist pulse is faint and relaxed. Faintness indicates that defensive qi is dispersed, leaving the skin loosely guarded. A relaxed pulse indicates that stomach qi is full, so that food is digested and water is transformed. Once food enters the stomach, circulation along the pulse pathways begins. Once water enters the channels, blood is formed. The text then describes abundant nutritive qi, loosely guarded skin, and a severing of the channels of the three burners, a condition it calls flooding hemorrhage.", "The wrist pulse is weak and relaxed. Weakness means that yang qi is insufficient and a relaxed quality means that stomach qi is abundant. The passage describes belching, sour regurgitation, and a sudden inability to take food down, as qi crowds above the diaphragm.", "The wrist pulse is weak and slow. Weakness indicates faint defensive qi, while slowness indicates cold in the nutritive aspect. The passage associates the nutritive aspect with blood and says that cold in the blood brings on fever. It associates the defensive aspect with qi. When this qi is faint, the person feels hungry inside, yet a sense of empty fullness prevents eating.", "The wrist pulse is weak and rough, while the pulse at the cubit is floating and large. In the absence of exterior signs, the text classifies the illness as internal injury.", "The wrist pulse is weak and rough, and the cubit pulse is soft and weak. The passage associates this pattern with loss of semen in men and with red or white vaginal discharge in women.", "The wrist pulse is surging and rapid. If it feels wiry and tense under pressure, the text expects an itchy rash. If the pulse is floating and rapid but becomes even under pressure, it classifies the pattern as exterior toxin. If the pulse is rapid and large and feels wiry and straight under pressure, it classifies the pattern as interior toxin and describes raising it so it can emerge. In its account, mistakenly using an attacking treatment drives the influence inward, with a fatal outcome.", "The wrist pulse is surging and rapid. If it feels tense and slippery under pressure, the text expects a suppurating abscess. With fever, it says, the abscess erupts suddenly. Without fever, it says, the abscess develops after some time.", "The wrist pulse is floating and slippery. If it feels wiry and tense under pressure, the text expects an internal abscess. It interprets coughing with pain in the chest as a lung abscess, with pus and blood brought up, and gripping pain in the abdomen as an intestinal abscess, with pus and blood passed in the stool.", "The wrist pulse is large and rough, becoming wiry from time to time, with no signs of cold or heat. The text attributes this to a spreading sore. It describes the condition as difficult to treat if the pulse also becomes fine and rapid.", "The Fuyang pulse is tight and floating. The floating quality indicates qi and the tight quality indicates cold. Floating is linked to abdominal fullness, and tightness to twisting pain. When floating and tightness occur together, the bowels rumble and churn. The churning signals movement of qi, and the obstructed qi then descends.", "The Shaoyin pulse cannot be felt. The passage associates this with marked swelling and deficiency in the genital region.", "The Fuyang pulse is faint and tight. Tightness indicates cold and faintness indicates deficiency. The passage associates their combination with shortness of breath.", "If the Fuyang pulse is large and tight, the text expects diarrhea to begin at once and considers the condition difficult to treat.", "A floating Fuyang pulse indicates deficiency. The text says that floating and deficiency together bring on hiccups, describing stomach qi as depleted to the point of exhaustion. It blames the physician for treating deficiency as though it were excess and pressing on the blood where there is already emptiness. It associates a slippery pulse with retching, and a floating pulse with dryness inside the nose with a nosebleed.", "When the Fuyang pulse is slow and relaxed, the text considers stomach qi to be following its normal course."], "notes": [["寸口脈浮而緊", "wrist pulse is floating and tight", "寸口", "寸口在这些句子里泛指腕部取脉处。遇到单写「寸」或「尺」时，才进一步区分腕上的具体位置。", "In these lines, cunkou is the wrist site for taking the pulse. The shorter terms inch and cubit identify specific positions at the wrist."], ["下之", "purges the patient", "下之", "「下」在这里是古代疗法动词，指使大便排出。「反」表示旧文认为医者的做法与当时应采取的做法相背。", "Here xia is a classical treatment verb meaning to purge through the bowels. The character fan marks the action as contrary to what the passage expects."], ["泄風", "leaking wind", "泄风", "这里是作者给持续发痒这一表现用的名称。上一句从风与气相搏解释发痒，下一句说病久会结痂成疮。", "The text uses this name for itching. It first attributes the itch to an interaction of wind and qi, then describes crusted sores in persistent cases."], ["在尺為關，在寸為格", "At the cubit the pattern is called closure, and at the inch it is called blockage", "关与格", "此处「尺」「寸」是腕部脉位，「关」「格」是古书给两种表现起的名称。作者把关与排尿不出相连，把格与呕吐相连。", "Cubit and inch are wrist positions here. The passage calls the two patterns closure and blockage, associating the former with inability to urinate and the latter with vomiting."], ["榮衛不能相將", "the two can no longer support each other", "营卫不能相将", "「榮」通「營」，与「衛」连用时，指古书所说的营气与卫气。「相将」说两者相互扶助、配合。", "Rong is a variant spelling of ying, the nutritive aspect. It is paired with wei, the defensive aspect. Xiangjiang describes the two supporting one another."], ["三焦不歸其部", "the three burners fail to operate in their proper regions", "三焦不归其部", "三焦分上、中、下。本句的「归其部」指各焦在各自范围内行其常。后面三个分句分别写偏离常态的表现。", "The three burners are upper, middle, and lower. The phrase refers to each functioning in its own region. The three clauses that follow describe what the text expects when each falls out of order."], ["按之弦急者，當發癮疹", "wiry and tense under pressure, the text expects an itchy rash", "按之", "「按」是手指加压察脉。读这一段时，先记作者初察到的脉象，再看加压以后是弦急、变平，还是弦直。", "The examiner presses down with the fingers. Follow the initial quality first, then notice whether pressure reveals a wiry, tense quality, an even pulse, or a wiry, straight beat."], ["趺陽脈緊而浮", "Fuyang pulse is tight and floating", "趺阳脉", "趺阳是古书在足背取脉的位置。从这一段开始，文章把注意力由腕部移到足部。", "Fuyang is a classical pulse site on the top of the foot. The focus here shifts from the wrist to the foot."], ["少陰脈不出", "Shaoyin pulse cannot be felt", "少阴脉", "这几条与趺阳脉并论。此处少阴通常理解为足少阴脉，取脉位置在内踝一带。", "Several of these passages discuss Shaoyin alongside Fuyang. Here Shaoyin is generally read as the foot Shaoyin pulse, felt in the region of the inner ankle."], ["鼻中燥者", "dryness inside the nose", "鼻中燥与衄", "「衄」在本条指鼻出血。作者把鼻腔干燥和浮脉并列，随后写出他预期的表现。", "Here nü refers to a nosebleed. The text gives nasal dryness together with a floating pulse before stating the outcome it expects."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-4", "page": 3, "english": ["The Fuyang pulse is floating and rapid. The text takes floating as injury to the stomach and rapidity as disturbance of the spleen. It attributes these changes to the physician's purging and distinguishes them from the original illness. Nutritive and defensive qi sink inward. The rapid quality first weakens, leaving an unexpectedly floating pulse alone. The text expects hard stool and persistent belching. How does it reach this conclusion? The rapid pulse initially indicated that the spleen was disturbed. The weakening of that rapid quality shows, in this account, that spleen qi has failed to regulate its functions, hence the hard stool and persistent belching. Now the pulse floats while the rapid quality has dwindled, leaving the pathogenic influence behind by itself. The person feels hungry inside, but the pathogenic heat fails to digest food. Tidal fever and thirst develop. The text expects the formerly rapid pulse to slow and describes hunger as the illness eases. If rapidity recurs at irregular times, it warns of severe sores.", "When the Fuyang pulse is floating and rough and the Shaoyin pulse follows its normal course, the text locates the illness in the spleen and expects diarrhea. How does it know? A floating, large pulse would indicate excess qi and deficient blood. In this case the Fuyang pulse is floating and rough, which the text reads as insufficient spleen qi and deficient stomach qi. The Shaoyin pulse is wiry and only just perceptible at depth. This is considered a well-regulated pulse, hence the phrase follows its normal course. If the Shaoyin pulse is instead slippery and rapid, the passage expects pus in the stool.", "The Fuyang pulse is floating and hollow. Floating indicates deficient stomach qi, and hollowness indicates injury to nutritive qi. The person grows thin and the skin over the flesh looks rough and scaly. When floating and hollowness occur together, gathering qi wanes and its connections with the four limbs are severed, as the text describes it.", "The Fuyang pulse is floating and large. The floating quality indicates excess qi and the large quality indicates deficient blood. The text treats blood deficiency as a loss of yin. If isolated yang descends into the yin region, it expects red urine that is difficult to pass and deficiency in the bladder. Here, however, urine passes freely and the person sweats profusely. Stomach qi would be expected to weaken, yet it becomes more excessive. The fluids disperse in all directions, nutritive qi and blood are exhausted, and the person is dry, restless, and unable to sleep. The blood grows thin, the flesh wastes away, and a sudden discharge of fluid follows. The physician then uses harsh medicinal substances to attack the stomach, deepening the deficiency. The text predicts that the temporary yang will depart, followed by a muddy discharge from the bowels and death.", "A question asks why a pulse that gathers and suddenly sinks is called slippery. The teacher replies that sinking is pure yin and gathering is true yang. When yin and yang come into harmony, the pulse feels slippery, with the bar and cubit naturally even.", "The Fuyang pulse is slightly deep, and eating and drinking are normal. The Shaoyin pulse is slightly slippery. The passage calls this slipperiness the superficial form of tightness and reads it as yin excess. It expects sweating on the inner thighs and dampness in the genital region.", "The Fuyang pulse is floating and slippery. Floating indicates yang, and slipperiness indicates excess. When these qualities act together, the pulse races and defensive qi loses its normal regulation. When this racing, floating, slippery pulse is accompanied by fever and sweating, the text judges the condition untreatable.", "The Fuyang pulse is slippery and tight. Slipperiness indicates excess stomach qi, and tightness indicates strong spleen qi. The text compares setting excess against strength to taking hold of a blade by its edge. The pain comes back on the person holding it, who creates the wound through that very act.", "The Fuyang pulse is deep and faint. The text takes depth as a sign of excess and describes food being digested repeatedly. A tight pulse is said to make the illness difficult to treat.", "The Fuyang pulse is hidden and rough. The text associates a hidden pulse with vomiting and failure to digest food and drink, and a rough pulse with inability to take food in. It calls the combination closure and blockage.", "The teacher said that a faint and rough pulse in this patient results from the physician's treatment. The physician induced profuse sweating and repeatedly purged the patient heavily, causing loss of blood. The text expects aversion to cold followed by fever, with no respite. In intense summer heat the person wants to wear lined clothing, and in bitter winter cold the person wants to strip. The explanation given is that faint yang brings aversion to cold and weak yin brings fever. The sweating weakened yang qi, and the heavy purging weakened yin qi. In the fifth lunar month, yang qi is at the exterior and the stomach is deficient and cold. With yang qi weak inside, the person cannot withstand the cold and wants lined clothing. In the eleventh lunar month, yang qi is in the interior and there is distressing heat in the stomach. With yin qi weak inside, the person cannot withstand the heat and wants to strip. The passage also takes a slow, rough yin pulse as evidence of blood loss.", "The Shaoyin pulse is weak and rough. In this account, weakness corresponds to mild restlessness and roughness to reversal cold.", "The Fuyang pulse cannot be felt. The text describes spleen function as failing to move up or down, with the body cold and the skin hard.", "The Shaoyin pulse fails to reach the site. The passage describes faint kidney qi and depleted essence and blood. Surging qi rushes upward into the chest and diaphragm, gathering qi collects abnormally, and blood binds below the heart. Yang qi retreats downward. Heat goes to the inner thighs and interacts with yin, leaving the body numb. The text calls this corpse-like collapse and names Qimen and Juque as the points an ancient practitioner would needle.", "During pregnancy, the pulse is wiry, rapid, and fine, with pain in the lower abdomen and heat in the palms. The passage interprets this as heat binding in the womb and predicts difficult labor if the condition is left unaddressed until delivery."], "notes": [["大便鞕，氣噫不除", "hard stool and persistent belching", "便硬与嗳气", "「鞕」就是硬。「噫」在这里指气从口上出，可理解为嗳气。「不除」说它持续未消。", "The character 鞕 means hard. Here 噫 refers to qi rising and escaping through the mouth as a belch, and 不除 means the symptom persists."], ["潮熱", "Tidal fever", "潮热", "「潮」借潮汐来形容发热有时而来，可以留意作者同时提到口渴。", "The tide image describes fever that returns at particular times. The passage mentions thirst alongside it."], ["少陰脈如經", "the Shaoyin pulse follows its normal course", "少阴脉如经", "「如经」说脉象合乎常态。下文以「弦而沉才见」解释为什么作者称它为「调脉」。", "Follows its normal course means the author sees the pulse as regular. The next lines explain this through a wiry pulse only just perceptible at depth."], ["芤", "hollow", "芤脉", "读作 kōu。传统脉象用这个字形容手下中间空的感觉。本句把它与营气受伤联系起来。", "Pronounced kōu, it names a pulse traditionally described as feeling hollow in the middle. This passage connects that quality with injury to nutritive qi."], ["肌肉甲錯", "the skin over the flesh looks rough and scaly", "肌肉甲错", "「甲」让人想到鳞甲或甲片。这里用来形容肌肤粗糙、像鳞片参差的外观。", "The character 甲 evokes armor or scales. The image describes skin that looks rough and unevenly scaly."], ["津液四射", "The fluids disperse in all directions", "津液四射", "「津液」是古书对体液的概称。「四射」是形象说法，承接上文的大汗和小便通利。", "Jinye is a broad classical term for bodily fluids. Dispersing in all directions is a vivid image following the references to profuse sweating and free urination."], ["翕奄沉", "gathers and suddenly sinks", "翕奄沉", "「翕」可读为聚合，「奄」表示忽然，「沉」写脉势向深处落。整句用一个快速变化的动作描写滑脉。", "Xi is gathering, an is suddenness, and sinking describes the beat moving deeper. Together they depict a swift change in the pulse."], ["伏而澀", "hidden and rough", "伏与涩", "「伏」说脉很深，取脉时不容易摸到。「涩」说脉来不流利。两字各写一种触感。", "Hidden describes a pulse that is deep and hard to feel. Rough describes a beat that moves unevenly. Each word describes a different quality under the fingers."], ["五月之時", "the fifth lunar month", "五月与十一月", "这段沿用传统历法的月份。作者用五月与十一月来对照阳气在表、在里，以及夏季反欲穿厚衣、冬季反欲脱衣的现象。", "The text uses months of the traditional lunar calendar. It contrasts the fifth and eleventh months to explain why the person wants lined clothing in summer and wants to strip in winter."], ["屍厥", "corpse-like collapse", "尸厥", "「尸」描述患者倒下时外观看起来像尸体，「厥」在这里写突然失去反应。期门、巨阙是接下来出现的古代穴名。", "Corpse-like describes the appearance of the person after collapse, and jue here refers to a sudden loss of responsiveness. Qimen and Juque are the classical point names that follow."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-4", "page": 4, "english": ["The text says that a surging, rapid pulse after childbirth that feels wiry and tense under pressure indicates that the postpartum discharge has yet to pass. If the discharge has already passed and the pulse remains the same, it calls this a departure of the hun soul and considers the case difficult to treat.", "The text expects fever with a floating, rapid pulse. If the person instead shivers with an aversion to cold and has pain in one spot while eating and drinking as usual, it attributes the signs to an accumulation of pus.", "A question: What is the pulse like in someone who is frightened? The teacher replied: It feels like tracing a series of silk threads, and the face goes pale.", "A question: What is the pulse like in someone who has not been drinking? The teacher replied: The pulse is rough, and the lips and mouth are dry.", "A question: What is the pulse like in someone who feels ashamed? The teacher replied: The pulse floats, and the face alternates between pallor and flushing.", "The teacher said: At the wrist, faint pulses are associated with loss of yang, yielding pulses with loss of blood, weak pulses with fever, and tight pulses with cold. When cold gains the upper hand, the text describes cold extremities, a clouded or faint feeling, and loss of sensation. It attributes these signs to the stomach lacking qi from food and the spleen’s activity being impeded. The mouth tightens so the person cannot speak, and they shake and shiver.", "The teacher said: The pulse becomes restless with fever and quiet with aversion to cold. When the pulse changes in step with these signs, the text calls the illness an intermittent fever disorder.", "The teacher said: In cold damage with coughing and qi rising upward, the text takes a scattered pulse as a fatal sign because the body has deteriorated.", "The teacher said: When the pulse alternates between large and small, quiet and erratic, and the person is frightened at the sight of others, the text describes this as a malign disturbance arising in the gallbladder because qi is exhausted.", "The teacher said: If the pulses give no sign of illness, yet grave illness comes on suddenly and the person becomes unresponsive, the text attributes it to a malevolent spirit and mentions ritual invocation. In the account, the ability to speak is taken as a sign that the person can still be treated, and inability to speak is taken as a fatal sign.", "The teacher said: The text calls the following signs signs of life ending: a floating, surging pulse, sweat like oil, ceaseless panting, inability to swallow fluids, loss of bodily sensation, and alternation between calm and agitation. It then asks which organ system fails first. Sweat that wets the hair together with ceaseless panting is assigned to the lung. Yang remaining on its own, a smoke-darkened appearance, a fixed gaze, and shaking of the head are assigned to the heart. Bluish lips and repeated jerking of the limbs are assigned to the liver. A dark rim around the mouth and yellowish, oily sweat are assigned to the spleen. Incontinence, delirious speech, and a fixed gaze are assigned to the kidney. The text then distinguishes which aspect fails first. If yang qi fails before yin qi runs out, it says the body turns blue-green after death. If yin qi fails before yang qi runs out, it says the body turns red, with warmth under the arms and heat below the breastbone.", "The eight extraordinary vessels have their own courses outside the network of the twelve channels. The text groups their disorders under yin and yang and places their treatment within the twelve-channel system.", "In a disorder of the Governing Vessel, the back and spine are stiff with a dull ache. The pulse is slightly floating and tense, and feels rough under pressure. The text assigns treatment to Taiyang.", "In a disorder of the Conception Vessel, there is painful binding within, with shan and jia disorders. The pulse is deep and bound. The text assigns treatment to Taiyin.", "In a disorder of the Penetrating Vessel, qi rushes upward and there is tightness within. The pulse is floating, forceless, and rapid. The text assigns treatment to Taiyin.", "In a disorder of the Belt Vessel, there is abdominal pain and a cold ache around the waist. The pulse is deep and fine. The text assigns treatment to Shaoyin.", "In a disorder of the Yang Motility Vessel, the side of the body is affected and qi moves on the outside. The pulse is wiry and tense, and relaxes under pressure. The text assigns treatment to Shaoyang.", "In a disorder of the Yin Motility Vessel, the side of the body is affected and qi moves on the inside. The pulse is floating and relaxed, and feels slightly tense and wiry under pressure. The text assigns treatment to Jueyin.", "The Yang Linking Vessel meets the yang channels. Its disorders occur outside the vessels, with chills and fever and a floating, forceless pulse. The text assigns treatment to the qi aspect.", "The Yin Linking Vessel meets the yin channels. Its disorders occur within the vessels, with pain in the heart region, heat in the palms, and a wiry, rough pulse. The text assigns treatment to the blood aspect.", "The Yang Linking Vessel links the yang channels and the Yin Linking Vessel links the yin channels. The text distinguishes their roles through qi and blood and gives each a reach beyond any one channel.", "The text describes disorders of the eight extraordinary vessels as developing over time, either when a pathogenic influence passes from another channel or through injury from prolonged exertion."], "notes": [["魂脫", "departure of the hun soul", "魂脱", "「魂」指古人用来理解精神与生命活动的「魂」。本条用「魂脱」解释产后恶露已排而脉象仍不变的情况。", "Hun belongs to a classical account of mental and vital activity. Here the text invokes its departure when the postpartum discharge has passed and the pulse stays the same."], ["灑淅惡寒", "shivers with an aversion to cold", "洒淅恶寒", "「洒淅」形容发冷发抖的样子。本句先说按脉象本来预期发热，再写身体实际的感觉。", "The expression evokes shivering. The sentence first gives the expected sign from the pulse, then describes what the person experiences."], ["郁冒不仁", "a clouded or faint feeling, and loss of sensation", "郁冒与不仁", "「郁冒」在这里可读作昏蒙不清，「不仁」指身体感觉迟钝或麻木。两个词分别写神志感受与身体知觉。", "The first expression describes feeling clouded or faint. The second describes dulled or lost bodily sensation."], ["病瘧", "intermittent fever disorder", "瘧", "本段用寒热转换时脉也跟着改变来讲「瘧」。「瘧」在这里是古书的病类，英译保留寒热交作的线索，避免直接指定现代病名。", "The defining clue here is that the pulse changes as chills and fever alternate. The English preserves the classical category through its pattern of signs."], ["祝由", "ritual invocation", "祝由", "古代以祝祷等仪式处理疾病的做法。此段把突然发病放在当时的鬼神观念中解释。", "A historical practice involving spoken invocations or related rites. It appears here within the text’s account of illness in terms of spirits."], ["肺先絕", "assigned to the lung", "肺先绝", "本段依次用出汗、呼吸、面色和肢体表现，推断哪一藏先衰绝。「肺先绝」是这一套古代判断中的第一种归属。", "This passage groups signs of dying under the classical organ systems. The first grouping associates hair-wetting sweat and persistent panting with the lung."], ["奇經八脈", "eight extraordinary vessels", "奇经八脉", "古书所说的八条另有循行路线的脉。下面八段依次讲督、任、冲、带、阳跷、阴跷、阳维、阴维。", "These are eight pathways given distinct courses in classical channel theory. The next eight passages take them up in order."], ["疝瘕", "shan and jia disorders", "疝瘕", "「疝」和「瘕」是范围较广的古代病名，可涉及腹部疼痛、牵引或结聚。本句把它们与任脉病的内结痛相连。", "Shan and jia are broad historical categories that can involve abdominal pain, pulling sensations, or accumulations. The line associates them with painful binding inside."], ["氣分", "qi aspect", "气分与血分", "这一段把阳维所主归为气分。下一段相应把阴维所主归为血分。这里的「分」提示气与血是两种归类角度。", "The passage assigns the Yang Linking Vessel to the qi aspect. The next assigns the Yin Linking Vessel to the blood aspect. These are two ways the text classifies the patterns."], ["久久移傳", "passes from another channel", "久久移传", "「久久」指经过一段较长时间，「移传」指病邪由一经逐渐传到另一处。这解释了为什么作者把奇经病写成渐成。", "The phrase describes an influence moving from one channel to another over time. It explains why the passage treats these disorders as gradual in onset."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-4", "page": 5, "english": ["A question: How can internal injury to the eight vessels be distinguished? The teacher replied: With injury to the Governing Vessel, the person feels limp, is reluctant to straighten up, and cannot stand long without a dull feeling of distention. With injury to the Conception Vessel, there is abundant, cloudy white urine. With injury to the Penetrating Vessel, the person has bouts of persistent, audible coughing without bringing anything up, and pants on exertion. With injury to the Belt Vessel, cold is felt all around the waist. With injury to the Yang Motility Vessel, sensation is dulled on the left side, and with injury to the Yin Motility Vessel, on the right. With injury to the Yang Linking Vessel, the person has a strong aversion to cold and the skin stays damp. With injury to the Yin Linking Vessel, the person has a strong aversion to heat and the skin stays dry.", "A question: What are the pulses like in internal injury to the eight vessels? The teacher replied: With injury to the Governing Vessel, the cubit pulse is large and rough. With injury to the Conception Vessel, the bar pulse is large and rough. With injury to the Penetrating Vessel, the inch pulse is short and rough. With injury to the Belt Vessel, the pulse is deep, slow, and bound. With injury to the Yang Motility Vessel, it is at times large and wiry. With injury to the Yin Motility Vessel, it is sometimes fine, sometimes wiry. With injury to the Yang Linking Vessel, it is sometimes relaxed, sometimes wiry. With injury to the Yin Linking Vessel, it is sometimes tight, sometimes rough.", "A question: How are these treated? The teacher replied: The text pairs injury to the Governing Vessel with supplementing marrow, injury to the Conception Vessel with supplementing essence, injury to the Penetrating Vessel with supplementing qi, and injury to the Belt Vessel with supplementing the kidney system. For injury to the Yang Motility Vessel, it names supporting the gallbladder system, and for injury to the Yin Motility Vessel, supplementing the liver system. For the Yang Linking Vessel it names regulating defensive qi, and for the Yin Linking Vessel, nourishing nutritive qi.", "A question: How is a prescription chosen? The teacher replied: Assess the person’s condition for deficiency or excess and consider how severe the illness is. Select a method and weigh how to apply it. The teacher says this is how to avoid error."], "notes": [["八脈內傷", "internal injury to the eight vessels", "八脉内伤", "承接上一页末尾的奇经八脉病。本段先从身体表现辨别是哪一脉，下一段改从脉象辨别。", "This continues the discussion of the eight extraordinary vessels. The first paragraph distinguishes them by bodily signs, and the next by pulse findings."], ["勞則地喘", "pants on exertion", "劳则地喘", "底本写「地喘」，其中「地」的用法不清楚。这里根据「劳则」和「喘」读作劳作时气喘，原字照录。", "The character 地 is unclear in this reading. The translation follows the surrounding words for exertion and panting and leaves the Chinese transcription intact."], ["尺脈", "cubit pulse", "寸、关、尺", "腕部三处取脉位置叫寸、关、尺。英译分别是 inch、bar、cubit，沿用名称帮助区分位置。", "The three wrist positions are called inch, bar, and cubit in this translation. The names distinguish where the pulse is felt."], ["調衛", "regulating defensive qi", "调卫与养荣", "此处「卫」指卫气，「荣」通「营」，指营气。本段借这对传统概念区分阳维和阴维的两种治法。", "Wei refers to defensive qi, and rong is a written variant of ying, meaning nutritive qi. The text uses the pair to distinguish the two Linking Vessels."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-5", "page": 1, "english": ["A question: How do we distinguish host and guest among the six qi? The teacher replied: Jueyin gives rise to Shaoyin, Shaoyin to Shaoyang, Shaoyang to Taiyin, Taiyin to Yangming, Yangming to Taiyang, and Taiyang back to Jueyin. This cycle begins again and remains unchanged year after year. These are the host qi. Jueyin gives rise to Shaoyin, Shaoyin to Taiyin, Taiyin to Shaoyang, Shaoyang to Yangming, Yangming to Taiyang, and Taiyang back to Jueyin. This repeating sequence gives the guest qi.", "A question: Where do they begin and end? The teacher replied: The first qi begins at Major Cold, the second at the Spring Equinox, the third at Lesser Fullness, the fourth at Major Heat, the fifth at the Autumn Equinox, and the final qi at Minor Snow, ending again at Major Cold. Host and guest share these divisions. Their differences are given as thirty degrees each.", "A question: How do heaven-governing and earth-governing qi work? The teacher replied: These are guest qi. In Zi and Wu years, for example, Shaoyin governs heaven and Yangming governs earth. Taiyang is first, Jueyin second, the heaven-governing qi third, Taiyin fourth, Shaoyang fifth, and the earth-governing qi last. In Mao and You years, Yangming governs heaven and Shaoyin governs earth. Taiyin is first, Shaoyang second, Yangming third, Taiyang fourth, Jueyin fifth, and Shaoyin last. In Xu and Chen years, Taiyang governs heaven and Taiyin governs earth. In Chou and Wei years, Taiyin governs heaven and Taiyang governs earth. In Yin and Shen years, Shaoyang governs heaven and Jueyin governs earth. In Si and Hai years, Jueyin governs heaven and Shaoyang governs earth. The other positions can be worked out from these examples.", "A question: How do they give rise to illness? The teacher replied: Here too host and guest are distinguished. When Jueyin governs heaven, if the host prevails there is chest and flank pain and difficulty speaking. If the guest prevails there is ringing in the ears, shaking and dizziness, and in severe cases an upward-surging cough.", "When Shaoyin governs heaven, if the host prevails there is heat in the heart region, restlessness, flank pain, and distending fullness. If the guest prevails there are a runny nose and sneezing, a stiff neck, a dazed feeling and heat in the shoulders and back, headache, shortness of breath, fever, deafness, and dim vision. In severe cases there is swelling over the foot, bleeding, sores, loss of voice, panting, and coughing.", "When Taiyin governs heaven, if the host prevails there is chest and abdominal fullness and a dazed feeling after eating. If the guest prevails the head, face, and insteps swell and breathing becomes labored and panting.", "When Shaoyang governs heaven, if the host prevails there is chest fullness, upward-surging cough, and a need to lift the head to breathe. Severe cases bring bleeding and hot hands. If the guest prevails a red rash breaks out, with fiery red eruptions, sores and ulcers, retching, throat obstruction, headache, and a swollen throat. There may also be deafness and bleeding, with spasms and twitching within.", "When Yangming governs heaven and the host prevails, coolness returns and lingers within. There is cough, nosebleed, throat blockage, and heat around the heart and diaphragm. The text regards persistent cough with pale blood as fatal. Metal occupies Shaoyang's position and the guest fails to prevail over the host.", "When Taiyang governs heaven, if the host prevails there is noise in the throat. If the guest prevails there is discomfort in the chest and clear nasal discharge, with cough on exposure to cold.", "When Jueyin governs earth, if the host prevails the sinews and bones move or shake together and pain in the lower back and abdomen comes and goes. If the guest prevails the joints move poorly, with rigidity and spasm within and impaired movement outwardly.", "When Shaoyin governs earth, if the host prevails counterflow qi rises, with pain in the heart region and fever. Various forms of obstruction arise around the diaphragm and extend to the sides of the chest and flanks. Sweat breaks out and the four extremities grow cold. If the guest prevails there is lower back pain. The buttocks, thighs, knees, hips, calves, shins, and feet feel hot, heavy, and sore. The insteps swell, standing for long becomes difficult, and urination and defecation change.", "When Taiyin governs earth, if the host prevails cold qi surges upward and causes fullness. Food and drink fail to go down, and severe cases bring shan disorder. If the guest prevails the feet are weak and the lower body swells. Urination and defecation become irregular. Dampness lodges in the lower burner and gives rise to watery diarrhea, genital swelling, and disorders of the private parts.", "When Shaoyang governs earth, if the host prevails heat rises against its usual course and lodges in the heart, causing pain in the heart region and fever. Blockage in the middle brings vomiting. If the guest prevails there is lower back and abdominal pain with an unexpected aversion to cold. In severe cases there is whitish discharge in the stool and pale urine."], "notes": [["主氣", "host qi", "主气", "主气的六个名称按固定次序出现，每年从头再排一遍。把它看作本篇每年共用的时间表。", "The six names occur in a fixed order that starts over each year. Think of it as the annual schedule shared by all years in this chapter."], ["客氣", "guest qi", "客气", "客气与主气用同一组六名，但太阴与少阳的先后对调。具体哪一气排在年内哪一步，要看后面列出的年份。", "The guest cycle uses the same six names with Taiyin and Shaoyang in switched positions. The year determines where each qi falls in the six-step calendar."], ["初氣始于大寒", "The first qi begins at Major Cold", "六步的起点", "大寒、春分等是节气名，每个名称标出一段的开头。最后一段到下一个大寒，接回第一段。", "Major Cold and the Spring Equinox are names of solar terms. Each marks the start of a period. The last period ends at the next Major Cold."], ["司天在泉", "heaven-governing and earth-governing qi", "司天与在泉", "本段把司天放在第三步，把在泉放在最后一步。读子午年的例子，可先圈出这两个位置，再看其他气怎样排列。", "The third position is heaven-governing and the last is earth-governing. Mark these two positions in the Zi and Wu example before following the rest of the sequence."], ["子午之年", "Zi and Wu years", "地支纪年", "子和午是十二地支中的两个名称。文中把十二支配为六对，同一对共享一组司天与在泉。", "Zi and Wu are two of the twelve Earthly Branches used to name years. The text pairs the branches into six groups, each sharing its heaven- and earth-governing qi."], ["主勝", "if the host prevails", "主胜与客胜", "下文用两套条件来组织表现。主胜是固定次序的一方占上风，客胜是随年份安排的一方占上风。", "The signs are grouped under two conditions. In host predominance, the fixed side prevails. In guest predominance, the side assigned by the year prevails."], ["鼽嚏", "a runny nose and sneezing", "鼽嚏", "鼽指这里的清涕一类鼻部表现，嚏就是打喷嚏。两字合起来读，便容易在后面的长句中认出这一组。", "The first character refers here to nasal discharge and the second to sneezing. Read them as one pair of signs in the longer list."], ["跗腫", "swelling over the foot", "跗肿", "跗指足背，腫指肿胀。后文还会把足背肿与小腿肿并列。", "跗 refers to the top of the foot. Later, the chapter lists swelling there alongside swelling of the lower leg."], ["濕客下焦", "Dampness lodges in the lower burner", "湿客下焦", "此处的「客」作动词，指停留。「下焦」是三焦分区的下部，可和紧随的滑泄、阴肿一起理解。", "Here 客 is a verb meaning to lodge. The lower burner is the lower region of the three-burner scheme, which organizes the diarrhea and swelling described next."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-5", "page": 2, "english": ["When Yangming governs earth, if the host prevails there is heaviness in the lower back, abdominal pain, and cold in the lower abdomen, with loose, duck-like stools. Cold surges through the intestines and upward into the chest. Severe cases bring panting and fullness with inability to stand for long. If the guest prevails cool qi stirs below, the lower abdomen becomes hard and full, and loose stools are frequent.", "When Taiyang governs earth, Water occupies the Water position, so there is no predominance.", "A question: How do predominance and counter-response work? The teacher replied: Predominance is always followed by a counter-response. Where there is no predominance, there is no counter-response.", "When Jueyin prevails, there is ringing in the ears, dizziness, and a confused, nauseated feeling. The stomach and diaphragm feel cold and qi gathers at the sides of the chest and flanks, then changes into heat. The urine turns yellow-red. Pain in the upper abdomen beneath the heart reaches up to both flanks. There are rumbling bowels, diarrhea with undigested food, lower abdominal pain, and gushing stools with red and white discharge. Severe cases bring vomiting and blockage around the diaphragm.", "In its counter-response, the lower abdomen becomes hard and full, with urgent internal cramping and sudden pain. There is pain in the heart region with reversal, sweating, and vomiting. Food and drink fail to go down and come back up if taken. There is shaking of the sinews and bones, dizziness, and cold extremities. In severe cases the pattern reaches the spleen, with obstruction on eating and vomiting.", "When Shaoyin prevails, there is heat below the heart and frequent hunger. Qi stirs below the navel and moves through the three burners. Other signs include vomiting, restlessness, abdominal fullness and pain, and loose stools with red discharge.", "In its counter-response, intense heat develops within, with restlessness, a runny nose and sneezing, gripping pain in the lower abdomen, and a dry throat. Qi stirs on the left and rises on the right. Coughing hurts the skin. There may be sudden loss of voice, pain in the heart region, and clouded consciousness with failure to recognize others. Shivering aversion to cold, trembling, and delirious speech are followed by heat when the chill ends, thirst and a desire to drink, shortness of breath, weakness of the bones, and discomfort around the diaphragm and intestines. Outwardly there is puffiness, with hiccups and belching, prickly rash, sores and ulcers, abscesses and deep sores, acne-like eruptions, and hemorrhoids. In severe cases the pattern reaches the lung, with cough and profuse nasal discharge.", "When Taiyin prevails, fire qi is pent up within. Sores and ulcers form inside and spread outward. The disorder is in the sides of the chest and flanks. Severe cases bring pain in the heart region, obstructing heat, headache, throat obstruction, and a stiff nape. In other cases damp qi accumulates within and cold presses on the lower burner, with lower abdominal fullness, pain and stiffness in the lumbar spine, gushing diarrhea, dampness underfoot, a heavy head, swelling of the insteps, feet, and shins, and retained fluid developing within as swelling appears on top of the foot.", "In its counter-response, the body is heavy and the middle is full. Food and drink fail to transform. Yin qi surges upward, bringing chest discomfort. Retained fluid develops within, with noisy coughing and panting, heavy pain in the head and nape, and especially severe shaking and twitching. The person vomits and falls silent, spitting clear fluid. In severe cases the pattern reaches the kidney, with uncontrolled, persistent leakage of stool.", "When Shaoyang prevails, heat lodges in the stomach, with vexation and pain in the heart region, red eyes, vomiting sour fluid, frequent hunger, ear pain, and reddish urine. The person startles easily and speaks deliriously. Sudden scorching heat consumes fluids, the lower abdomen hurts, and the stool carries red and white discharge.", "In its counter-response, there is dryness, vexing heat, startle and twitching, coughing, nosebleeds, heat in the heart region, restlessness, frequent elimination, and aversion to wind. Counterflow qi rises. The face looks as if dust has settled on it, and the eyes flutter and twitch. Fire qi flares within, bringing sores and erosion in the mouth, retching, bleeding, and bloody stool. There are intermittent bouts of chills and fever, with aversion to cold and violent shivering that gives way to heat at its peak. The throat networks become scorched and dry. The person is thirsty and drinks water or other liquids. The complexion turns yellow-red, breathing becomes short, and lung wilting occurs. The pattern transforms into retained water and proceeds to swollen insteps. In severe cases it reaches the lung, with cough and discharge of blood.", "When Yangming prevails, coolness arises within. There is pain on the left side of the chest and flanks, and loose stools. Internally there is throat blockage, and externally a shan disorder with genital swelling. The chest feels uncomfortable, and the throat is affected with coughing.", "In its counter-response, illness arises at the sides of the chest and flanks. Qi gathers on the left and the person often sighs deeply. Severe cases bring pain in the heart region and obstructed fullness, abdominal distention and diarrhea, vomiting of bitter fluid, coughing, hiccups, and vexation. The disorder is around the diaphragm. In severe cases it reaches the liver, with fright and contracture of the sinews.", "When Taiyang prevails, there are hemorrhoids and intermittent chills and fever, with onset of cold. When reversal enters the stomach, pain arises within at the heart region. The genital area develops sores and the private parts are affected. The disorder draws into the inner thighs, with painful tightness of the sinews and flesh. Blood congeals in the vessels, the networks fill, and the blood changes. There may be bloody stool, obstructive swelling of the skin, and abdominal fullness that subsides at times. Heat then rises in the opposite direction, bringing pain in the head and nape, at the crown and the back of the head, with a sensation that the eyes will come out. When cold enters the lower burner, watery diarrhea develops."], "notes": [["鶩溏", "duck-like stools", "鹜溏", "鹜是鸭子，溏说粪便稀软。古文以鸭粪的形态来形容这一类稀便。", "鶩 means duck and 溏 describes loose stool. The text uses duck droppings as a visual comparison."], ["以水居水位", "Water occupies the Water position", "水居水位", "太陽在本篇配寒水。这里说气的五行属性与所在的位置同为水，于是这一例不列胜。", "Taiyang is associated with Cold Water in this chapter. Its phase and the phase of the position are both Water, so this case has no predominance."], ["有勝必有複", "Predominance is always followed by a counter-response", "胜与复", "胜讲一方先占上风，复讲继起的反应。接下来每组通常先写「某气之胜」，再写「其复也」。", "Predominance names the initial ascendancy of one qi. Counter-response names what follows. Each pair typically starts with one qi prevailing and continues with its counter-response."], ["飧泄", "diarrhea with undigested food", "飧泄", "飧本可指饭食。飧泄在这里指稀便中带有未消化的食物，可和本页其他便泄的形容区分。", "飧 can refer to a meal. In this phrase it denotes diarrhea containing undigested food, one of several kinds of loose stool described on this page."], ["郁冒不知人", "clouded consciousness with failure to recognize others", "郁冒", "「冒」可借蒙蔽来理解，后面的「不知人」说明这里意识昏蒙的程度。", "冒 suggests being clouded or obscured. The following words describe the extent of that clouding through failure to recognize other people."], ["飲發於中", "Retained fluid develops within", "饮发于中", "「饮」在这里是留聚水液的古典术语，「中」说体内。太阴的胜和复两段都用到这个说法。", "飲 here is a classical term for fluid accumulating in the body. The phrase appears in both the Taiyin predominance and counter-response paragraphs."], ["發而為瘧", "intermittent bouts of chills and fever", "疟", "下文用先恶寒发抖、寒极转热说明发作次序。此处按古文描述的寒热发作来读。", "The following words give the order of the episode through shivering cold that turns into heat. The translation follows that classical description."], ["善太息", "often sighs deeply", "太息", "太息就是深长叹息。「善」放在动作前面，表示容易或常常发生。前文的「善饑」「善驚」也是一样的句式。", "太息 means a deep sigh. Before an action, 善 can mean a tendency to do it often or readily. The earlier phrases for frequent hunger and startling easily use the same pattern."], ["血脈凝泣", "Blood congeals in the vessels", "凝泣", "这里的「泣」和后文的血络相连，读作行涩不畅。整句描写血在脉中凝滞。", "In this description of vessels and networks, 泣 suggests impeded movement. The phrase pictures blood congealing in the vessels."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-5", "page": 3, "english": ["In its counter-response, cold develops in the heart and stomach. There is discomfort in the chest and around the diaphragm, pain in the heart region with obstructed fullness, headache, a tendency to sadness, occasional dizziness and collapse, and diminished appetite. The lumbar spine becomes painful and bending or straightening is difficult. Pain in the lower abdomen pulls on the testes and along the lower back and spine, then surges upward toward the heart. Clear fluid is spat up, with hiccups and belching. In severe cases the pattern reaches the heart, with forgetfulness and a tendency to sadness. When cold returns and lingers within, the lower back and buttocks hurt, bending and straightening are difficult, and there is pain in the thighs, shins, feet, and knees.", "Learn carefully how these six qi are said to give rise to illness, so that their distinctions are kept in mind.", "The teacher said: If you know the six qi but are unfamiliar with the five movements, you have yet to grasp their full workings. Let me explain. Suppose Taiyang governs heaven in a Jia or Ji year. Jia and Ji correspond to the Earth movement and Taiyang to Cold Water. Earth restrains Water, so Taiyang cannot fully exercise its position. The same principle applies when Jueyin governs heaven in a Yi or Geng year of Metal, when Shaoyin or Shaoyang governs heaven in a Bing or Xin year of Water, when Taiyin governs heaven in a Ding or Ren year of Wood, or when Yangming governs heaven in a Wu or Gui year of Fire.", "A question: What are the methods of treatment? The teacher replied: Wind, cold, summer heat, dampness, dryness, and heat are each addressed in accordance with the qi involved. Where there is a deceptive appearance, reverse the approach. For a pronounced pattern, follow its course. For a slight one, counter it. Choose the method with care and keep these distinctions clear."], "notes": [["其複也", "In its counter-response", "接上页的太阳之胜", "本页开头的「其」指上一页结尾的太陽。「复」是太阳之胜以后出现的反应，前后两段要连读。", "The opening pronoun refers to Taiyang at the end of the previous page. Read the two paragraphs together to see the predominance and the response that follows."], ["少腹控睾引腰脊上沖心", "Pain in the lower abdomen pulls on the testes and along the lower back and spine, then surges upward toward the heart", "疼痛的走向", "少腹是下腹。控和引都有牵拉的意思，后面的上沖用来写向上的势头。", "少腹 is the lower abdomen. The next verbs describe a pulling sensation and then the upward direction in which the pain surges."], ["五運", "five movements", "五运", "此处把木、火、土、金、水按年份配为五运，再与同年的司天气相比较。", "The five movements assign Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal, and Water to years. The text compares a year's movement with the qi that governs heaven in that year."], ["甲己土運", "Jia and Ji correspond to the Earth movement", "天干与年运", "甲和己是两个天干，文中把它们归在同一土运。前页司天用的是子午等地支，所以同一年要看天干和地支两套名称。", "Jia and Ji are two Heavenly Stems assigned to the same Earth movement. The preceding page uses Earthly Branches such as Zi and Wu for heaven-governing qi. Both parts of a year name matter."], ["土能克水", "Earth restrains Water", "五行相克", "「克」表示制约。此例用土运制约太陽寒水，说明司天气虽在它的位置上，作用仍会受到年运影响。", "克 means to restrain. In this example the Earth movement restrains Taiyang Cold Water, so the annual movement affects how the heaven-governing qi can act."], ["有假者反之", "Where there is a deceptive appearance, reverse the approach", "假与反", "「假」指可以误导判断的表象。「反」提示改变所取的处理方向。句中只列原则，具体含义要联系其后的从、逆读。", "假 refers to an appearance that can mislead judgment. 反 signals a reversal of approach. Read this brief principle alongside the two that follow, matching a pattern's course and countering it."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-7", "page": 1, "title": "General Principles of Miscellaneous Diseases", "english": ["A question: What does it mean that the finest practitioner treats illness before it takes hold? The teacher replied: On seeing liver illness, a practitioner who expects it to pass to the spleen first strengthens the spleen. If the spleen is already vigorous through the seasons and does not receive the pathogenic influence, leave it alone. A middling practitioner who does not understand transmission treats the liver alone. In liver illness the text describes supplementing with sour flavor, assisting with scorched bitterness, and regulating with sweet medicinals. [The source marks the following account for deletion: Sour enters the liver, scorched bitterness the heart, and sweetness the spleen. The spleen can injure the kidneys; weak kidney qi impedes Water. Impeded Water lets Heart Fire flourish and harm the lung. Injury to the lung impedes Metal, allowing liver qi to flourish so the liver recovers.] The passage calls this the subtle principle of treating the liver by supporting the spleen. It limits the method to liver deficiency. The classic says: Do not make deficiency more deficient, or excess more excessive. Supplement insufficiency and reduce what is excessive. Apply the principle to the other organs by analogy.", "People receive the five constant qualities and grow through the influence of wind. Wind can sustain or harm living things, just as water can carry or capsize a boat. When the original vitality of the five zang organs moves freely, a person is at ease. Intrusive qi and harmful wind can be deadly. The text groups the many causes of illness in three ways: an influence received in the channels and transmitted to the organs; blockage involving the limbs, nine openings, and vessels from an influence striking the exterior; and injuries involving sexual activity, blades, insects, or animals. It advocates care before such influences enter deeply. Its examples include treating them while still in the channels, using guided movement and breathing, acupuncture, moxibustion, and rubbing with ointments when the limbs first feel heavy, avoiding injury and exhaustion, and moderating the foods and flavors one consumes. Còu are the spaces where the three burners meet and original vitality and blood and qi converge. Lǐ are the patterns of the skin and organs.", "A question: Please explain the coloration that appears on the face in illness. The teacher replied: The passage associates a blue-green nose tip with abdominal pain and regards severe cold in this pattern as fatal. A slightly dark nose tip is associated with water qi, yellow with cold above the chest, and white with blood loss. A slight redness out of season is called fatal, and markedly rounded eyes are taken as a sign of a spasm disorder. The passage also associates blue-green with pain, black with exhaustion, red with wind, yellow with difficult defecation, and bright coloration with retained fluid.", "The teacher said: A subdued voice with sudden cries suggests illness between bones and joints. A muffled voice that does not carry suggests illness in the region of the heart and diaphragm. A thin, drawn-out chirping voice suggests illness in the head.", "The teacher said: Breathing that lifts the shoulders is associated with hardness in the heart region. Breath that draws upward in the chest accompanies coughing. Open-mouthed breathing and shortness of breath are associated with lung wasting and foamy sputum.", "The teacher said: Slightly rapid inhalation is placed in the middle burner. In a full pattern the text describes purging as leading to recovery, while it regards a deficient presentation as untreatable. In the upper burner, inhalation is hurried, and in the lower burner it is drawn out. The passage calls these hard to treat and regards breathing accompanied by pronounced shaking as untreatable.", "The teacher said: A stirring wrist pulse moves according to the time when its corresponding influence is ascendant. For example, when the liver is ascendant the color is blue-green. Each season follows its color. If liver blue-green appears as white instead, the coloration is out of season. The text treats pulse and color that do not fit the season as signs of illness.", "A question: What does it mean that a phase comes early, fails to come when due, comes but the preceding phase stays, or arrives in excess? The teacher replied: After the winter solstice, at midnight on a jiǎzǐ day, lesser yang begins to rise and the weather should become mild. Mild weather before jiǎzǐ is an early arrival. If jiǎzǐ comes and the weather has yet to soften, the expected change has failed to arrive. If severe cold persists, the previous phase has failed to go. If the weather is as hot as the fifth or sixth month, warmth has arrived in excess."], "notes": [["治未病", "before it takes hold", "治未病", "本段用肝病可能传脾作例子。意思是已经看到一处病变时，预先照顾书中认为还可能受影响的另一处。", "The example begins with an existing liver illness. The practitioner considers the next organ that might be affected in the text’s model."], ["肝傳脾", "pass to the spleen", "肝传脾", "「传」写的是书中两类脏腑功能之间病势的转移，用来引出下一句先实脾。", "Transmission is a change in which organ system is affected in the text’s framework. It explains the attention given to the spleen next."], ["勿虛虛，勿實實", "make deficiency more deficient", "勿虛虛，勿實實", "两个「虚」的作用不同，一个指已经虚的状态，另一个指让它更虚。「实实」同样读法。", "In each phrase, the first repeated word functions as a verb and the second describes an existing state: avoid worsening either deficiency or excess."], ["腠理", "Còu are the spaces", "腠理", "原文自己拆开解释了两个字。「腠」着重间隙和会合，「理」着重组织纹理。", "The text glosses these two characters separately. Còu emphasizes spaces and convergence; lǐ emphasizes patterns or grain."], ["導引、吐納", "guided movement and breathing", "导引与吐纳", "导引是古代伸展屈伸等身体练习，吐纳指调息。它们在这里列为古人养慎的例子。", "Dǎoyǐn refers to guided bodily movement; tǔnà refers to regulated breathing. They are examples from the text’s historical practices."], ["留飲", "retained fluid", "留饮", "「饮」在这里是古代医书对停留体液的分类，「留」说明它没有顺利离开。", "Yǐn is the classical category for retained fluid. The preceding character emphasizes its persistence."], ["甲子", "jiǎzǐ day", "甲子", "甲子是六十干支循环的首个组合。此处用来标出古人预计节气转换的时间点。", "Jiǎzǐ heads the sixty-position stem-and-branch cycle. The passage uses it as a calendrical marker for an expected seasonal change."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-7", "page": 2, "title": "General Principles of Miscellaneous Diseases", "english": ["A question: The classic says that reversed yang travels alone. What does that mean? The teacher replied: There is yang without yin, hence the designation reversed yang.", "A question: A deep, large, slippery inch pulse is read as fullness in its depth and qi in its slipperiness. When fullness and qi combine, the text says that blood and qi entering a zang organ is fatal and entering a fu organ leads to recovery. What is this sudden reversal? The teacher replied: Blue-green lips and a cold body indicate entry into a zang organ, which the text regards as fatal. A comfortable body with spontaneous sweating indicates entry into a fu organ and recovery.", "A question: When the pulse collapses, why is entry into a zang organ fatal and entry into a fu organ followed by recovery? The teacher replied: The principle is applied broadly. Take a spreading sore: the text calls its spread from the mouth toward the limbs treatable, and the reverse direction untreatable. It regards illness remaining outside as treatable and illness entering deep inside as fatal.", "A question: What are the eighteen yang diseases? The teacher listed pain in the head and nape, lower back and spine, and drawing pain in the arms and legs. What are the eighteen yin diseases? The reply listed cough, upward surging qi, panting, hiccups, throat pain, intestinal sounds, distention, heart-region pain, and tightening. The text adds thirty-six zang and thirty-six fu diseases, yielding 108 in all. The five taxations, seven damages, six extremes, and thirty-six conditions of women lie outside this count. Clear influences reside above and turbid below. Large influences strike the exterior and small ones the interior. An influence from food entering by the mouth is retained food.", "A question: Why do some cases call urgently for supporting the interior and others the exterior? The teacher replied: After purging, unremitting diarrhea with undigested food and body aches calls for attention to the interior first. If bowel movements settle but body pain remains, attend to the exterior. If a sudden illness arises on top of a chronic disorder, the passage gives priority to the sudden illness.", "The teacher said: The five zang illnesses each have things that favor recovery and things they tend to dislike. If a patient unexpectedly craves a food they ordinarily dislike, the passage anticipates fever. It describes matching an attacking method to the presenting pattern, giving Zhūlíng Decoction for thirst as its example. Cold extremities with surging qi and drawn-up feet are read as the six fu losing qi externally. Incontinence of stool with lack of sensation in the limbs is read as the five zang losing qi internally. The text calls a loss of qi inside and out untreatable and fatal.", "The teacher said: Upper-burner heat is associated with coughing and lung wasting, middle-burner heat with abdominal hardness, and lower-burner heat with bloody urine or difficult, obstructed urination. Cold in the large intestine is associated with loose, duck-like stools and heat with foul intestinal discharge. The text pairs small-intestine cold with bearing-down and pus and blood in the stool, and heat with hemorrhoids.", "A question: What is exhaustion of the three burners? The teacher replied: The upper burner receives qi from the middle. If the middle is disharmonious and fails to digest grain, exhaustion of the upper is associated with frequent belching. The lower burner also receives qi from the middle. When the middle fails to move grain qi, exhaustion of the lower is associated with involuntary urination and defecation.", "A question: How do fixed accumulations, shifting gatherings, and grain qi differ? The teacher replied: A fixed accumulation belongs to a zang organ and stays in place. A gathering belongs to a fu organ and flares from time to time, its pain changing location. Grain qi is associated with pain below the ribs that eases under pressure and then returns. The pulse of a fixed accumulation is deep and fine close to the bone. The text assigns chest and throat sites to the inch and just beyond it, the navel area to the bar, the region below the heart just above the bar, and the lower abdomen just below it. At the cubit it places the accumulation at qìchōng. When the pulse emerges on the left, right, or both sides, it assigns the accumulation to the left, right, or center respectively."], "notes": [["厥陽", "reversed yang", "厥阳", "这一条用下一句「有阳无阴」给「厥阳独行」下了本段的定义。", "The reply explains the expression locally: yang is described as acting alone, with yin absent."], ["卒厥", "sudden reversal", "卒厥", "「卒」读 cù，意为突然。这里的「厥」要放回后面唇色、身体冷热的比较里理解。", "Here 卒 means sudden. The reply distinguishes the two presentations by the observed color and warmth."], ["浸淫瘡", "spreading sore", "浸淫疮", "「浸淫」形容逐渐扩展。作者用扩展方向来说明本段的外与里。", "The name conveys progressive spread. The text uses direction of spread to illustrate its exterior–interior distinction."], ["宿食", "retained food", "宿食", "「宿」有留下、隔时不去的意思。这里指书中所说留滞的食物。", "Sù suggests something that stays over. The compound describes food considered to have remained or lodged."], ["下利清穀", "diarrhea with undigested food", "下利清谷", "「下利」写腹泻，「清谷」指便中有未消化的谷食。两词一起提示书中所讨论的是哪一种下利。", "The phrase specifies diarrhea in which undigested food is visible. That detail is central to the comparison in this paragraph."], ["痼疾", "chronic disorder", "痼疾与卒病", "痼疾指久而难愈的病，卒病指突然发生的病。原段讨论两者同时出现时的先后。", "The passage contrasts an entrenched condition with a newly arising illness and discusses which takes priority."], ["三焦竭", "exhaustion of the three burners", "三焦竭", "这段主要说上焦和下焦都承受中焦之气。读懂这条上下与中焦的关系，才容易看出为什么两种表现会放在同一问下。", "Both upper and lower regions are said to depend on the middle burner. That relationship links the two seemingly different examples."], ["積、有聚", "fixed accumulations", "积与聚", "最简区别在这一问已说出：「积」位置固定，「聚」可时发时止，疼痛处也会移动。", "The key contrast is fixed versus shifting. The text distinguishes persistent location from intermittent pain that changes location."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-10", "page": 1, "title": "Heat Diseases", "english": ["Heat illness can arrive from outside or arise within. What comes from outside can move, while what arises within has a fixed site. It does not follow a channel sequence but lodges where it has an affinity. It resembles warmth illness, yet has a different root. Transmission through the channels transforms into heat, whereas a latent influence changes into warmth. The author censures physicians who conflate the two and says that the error can be fatal. These distinctions are set down for later readers.", "In heat illness, a red face, sores in the mouth, pain in the heart region, an urge to vomit, and a surging, rapid pulse are attributed to a heat influence affecting the heart. The text names Huánglián–Huángqín Heart-Draining Decoction for this pattern.", "Formula record: Huánglián–Huángqín Heart-Draining Decoction.", "The record lists huánglián, three liǎng, and huángqín, two liǎng.", "The historical directions describe reducing two shēng of water to one and dividing the warm decoction into two servings.", "In heat illness, bodily heat and pain in the left flank, with disordered speech when severe and a wiry, rapid pulse, are attributed to a heat influence encroaching on the liver. The text names Huánglián, Huángqín, Bànxià, and Pig Bile Decoction.", "Formula record: Huánglián, Huángqín, Bànxià, and Pig Bile Decoction.", "The record lists huánglián, two liǎng; huángqín, three liǎng; rinsed bànxià, one shēng; and the juice of one large pig gallbladder.", "The historical directions describe simmering the first three ingredients in six shēng of water until three remain, removing the dregs, mixing in the bile, and dividing the warm mixture into two servings.", "In heat illness, abdominal pain that does not tolerate pressure, inability to bend forward or back, difficult defecation, and a rapid, large pulse are attributed to a heat influence encroaching on the spleen. The text names Dàhuáng, Hòupò, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "Formula record: Dàhuáng, Hòupò, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists dàhuáng, four liǎng; hòupò, six liǎng; and gāncǎo, three liǎng.", "The historical directions describe reducing five shēng of water to two, with one shēng taken first and the second omitted if defecation becomes free.", "In heat illness, thirst, panting, cough, pain that draws into the chest, inability to take a deep breath, and a short, rapid pulse are attributed to a heat influence encroaching on the lung. The text names Huánglián, Shígāo, Bànxià, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "Formula record: Huánglián, Shígāo, Bànxià, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists huánglián, one liǎng; crushed shígāo, one jīn, wrapped in cotton; rinsed bànxià, half a shēng; and gāncǎo, three liǎng.", "The historical directions describe reducing six shēng of water to three, removing the dregs, and taking three warm servings of one shēng over a day.", "In heat illness, dry throat, low back pain, hot feet, and a deep, rapid pulse are attributed to a heat influence moving to the kidney. The text names Dìhuáng, Huángbǎi, Huánglián, and Bànxià Decoction.", "Formula record: Dìhuáng, Huángbǎi, Huánglián, and Bànxià Decoction.", "The record lists dìhuáng, half a jīn; huángbǎi, six liǎng; huánglián, three liǎng; and rinsed bànxià, one shēng.", "The historical directions describe reducing eight shēng of water to three, removing the dregs, and taking three warm servings of one shēng over a day."], "notes": [["有外至，有內生", "arrive from outside or arise within", "外至与内生", "这篇一开始按来源分两类，接着讲它们出现后是否移动。", "The chapter first distinguishes two origins and then distinguishes whether the influence can change location."], ["舍於所合", "lodges where it has an affinity", "舍于所合", "「舍」有停留、寄居之意。「所合」指在书中与这一类影响相应的位置。", "Shè suggests lodging. The phrase places the influence where the text considers it to have an affinity."], ["與溫相似", "resembles warmth illness", "热病与温病", "本篇强调两类病在表现上有相似处，篇首用传经化热和伏气变温来说明它所理解的来源差异。", "The author sees a resemblance in presentation. The difference here concerns channel transmission in one account and a latent influence in the other."], ["洪而數", "surging, rapid pulse", "洪而数", "洪和数说两件不同的事。洪形容脉势涌盛，数形容跳得偏快。", "Surging describes force or shape, while rapid describes pace. The two adjectives can apply together."], ["三兩", "three liǎng", "两、斤、升", "两和斤是古代重量单位，升是容量单位。这里照录古籍用语，和今天同名单位的大小不必相同。", "Liǎng and jīn are historical weights, and shēng is a historical volume. The transliterations retain the units of the text."], ["去滓", "removing the dregs", "去滓", "「滓」是煎后留下的固体残渣。这个短语在本篇的方文里反复出现。", "The dregs are the solids left after simmering. The same formula phrase appears repeatedly in this chapter."], ["短而數", "short, rapid pulse", "短而数", "「短」描述脉形所及范围，「数」描述快慢。从这两个不同的观察维度读会比较清楚。", "Short describes the extent of the pulse as felt, while rapid describes its pace."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "page": 5, "english": ["If the yin and yang aspects of the pulse are both abundant and the person is exposed to cold again, the text says the illness becomes warm intermittent fever. If the yang pulse is floating and slippery, the yin pulse yielding and weak, and the person is then affected by wind, it becomes wind warmth. If the yang pulse is surging and rapid, the yin pulse forceful and large, and the person then encounters warmth and heat, it becomes warm toxin, which the text calls the most severe form. If the yang pulse is yielding and weak, the yin pulse wiry and tight, and the person then encounters warm qi, it becomes epidemic warmth. These are the pulse changes and treatment categories the text gives for warm illness arising after exposure to winter cold.", "The text warns that putting off care and bearing illness in silence in the hope of recovery can allow it to become entrenched. It says this is especially true of children and women. When the seasonal influences are out of balance, it urges people to speak up early so the source can be traced while the influence is still in the interstices of the skin. It describes recovery as usual when care comes in time. If the person keeps silent for several days, it says the influence can enter the zang organ systems and become hard to control.", "The text urges prompt preparation of a decoction once illness is recognized, at any hour of the day. It says delay lets illness pass on and change until it is hard to resolve. It tells the practitioner to withhold treatment if a patient takes medicine at will and disregards the prescribed method.", "The text says cold damage often begins with wind and cold striking the exterior. Once they pass into the interior, they no longer readily disperse. It says that at the exterior they disperse with suitable warm covering. If stronger treatment is contemplated, the text calls for resolving the exterior before purging.", "If the exterior remains unresolved and the interior signs persist, but there is no pronounced fullness and there are still chills and fever, the text rules out purging. If the exterior has resolved and the interior shows marked fullness and excess, with a hard abdomen and dry stool within, it allows purging. Even at four or five days, it claims that repeated purging in this pattern can be harmless.", "The text warns that purging in the wrong pattern leaves the interior deficient and lets heat enter. It describes diarrhea accompanied by heat, restlessness, and many other changes. It portrays milder cases as debilitating and severe cases as fatal.", "In the text’s terms, when yang is abundant and yin deficient, inducing sweating is said to be fatal and purging to bring recovery. When yang is deficient and yin abundant, it predicts the reverse. How, then, could Spirit Elixir be used to induce sweating in error, or kansui be used rashly for purging? Approaches to deficiency and abundance lie a thousand miles apart, and consequences follow as closely as a shadow or an echo. The text pictures the yang-abundant patient dying as soon as Cinnamon Twig medicine is swallowed, and the yin-abundant patient dying as soon as a Qi-Ordering formula reaches the stomach. It says life and death can turn in moments. The signs of intertwined yin, yang, deficiency, and excess are subtle, and harm from confusing sweating, vomiting, and purging may follow quickly. An unskilled practitioner who misses the source can treat wrongly, cause a death, and call it fate. The passage closes with a lament for the dead and an appeal to anyone who cares for others.", "When illness from simultaneous involvement of two channels presents at once, the text says treatment still has an order. Releasing the exterior and purging the interior are distinct methods. It rejects the argument that combining Spirit Elixir with kansui in one drink can do both at the same time.", "The wise, the text says, weigh their actions carefully, while the foolish act rashly and quickly. A turn from safety to danger is no matter for deception. People chase the prestige of fashionable practice and overlook its failures. Those who see clearly safeguard what matters and begin by observing what is close at hand in their own bodies. Why look far away?", "For warm decoctions used to induce sweating, the text describes shortening the usual three-dose schedule when severe illness persists. It calls for watching serious cases over a complete day and night, and repeating the original decoction if the signs persist after one preparation. In some cases it describes relief only after sweating follows three preparations, and treats a continuing absence of sweat as a fatal sign."], "notes": [["溫瘧", "warm intermittent fever", "温疟", "本段用冬伤于寒后再感寒与两部脉盛解释温疟。这里按古书的病类来读。", "The text links this classical intermittent fever pattern to renewed cold exposure and abundance in both pulse aspects."], ["溫毒", "warm toxin", "温毒", "「毒」在这里是古书对严重温病的归类用字。原文紧接着说它是本段所列病形中最重的一种。", "Toxin here is a historical category word. The text immediately calls this the most severe form in its list."], ["腠理", "interstices of the skin", "腠理", "古书用来说明皮肤肌表之间细小的间隙。本段借它表达病邪尚浅。", "Classical texts use this term for fine spaces at the body surface. Here it indicates a shallow stage of illness."], ["先解表", "resolving the exterior before purging", "先解表", "「表」是作者区分病位时用的外层。「先」提示本段所讲的是先后次序。", "The exterior is the outer level in the text’s account. The sentence makes the sequence of treatment its main point."], ["燥屎", "dry stool", "燥屎", "本句把干燥的大便和腹硬、胀满并列，作为作者描述里实的线索。", "Dry stool appears with abdominal hardness and marked fullness as a clue to interior excess in the text."], ["應若影響", "as closely as a shadow or an echo", "应若影响", "「影」是影子，「响」是回声。古句借二者紧随形体和声音，比喻后果来得很快。", "The Chinese words evoke a shadow following a body and an echo following a sound. The image conveys how quickly consequences can follow."], ["兩感", "simultaneous involvement of two channels", "两感", "本句强调两方面同时发病，仍须分清先后。下面以发表、攻里说明两种方法有别。", "The text describes two channels affected together, while still distinguishing the order and purposes of exterior and interior methods."], ["晬時", "a complete day and night", "晬时", "古代计时说法，在这里指一昼夜。可与前面的「一日一夜」互相印证。", "An old time expression meaning one full day and night here. The preceding phrase spells out the interval."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "page": 6, "english": ["In illness attributed to seasonal influences, the text cautions against giving water on the fifth or sixth day if the person is thirsty yet can drink very little. It says there is too little heat in the abdomen to handle the water, so more can worsen the illness. On the seventh or eighth day, it says even great thirst should be judged with the other signs, and gives half the amount the person could drink as an example. If drinking brings abdominal fullness, difficulty urinating, panting, or hiccups, it advises against giving more. It takes sudden profuse sweating as a sign of spontaneous recovery.", "The text treats an unexpected ability to drink water during illness as a sign of approaching recovery. It warns against turning that observation into a rule and forcing water on anyone with slight thirst. It says harm from doing so is beyond counting.", "In illness with reversal, the text marks three changes as signs of recovery: a stirring, rapid pulse slowing after a decoction, a floating, large pulse becoming smaller, and restlessness giving way to calm.", "The text names fifty-nine points in connection with needling for warm illness. Of the 365 points it counts on the body, it says applying moxa to thirty can cause harm, and needling seventy-nine can bring disaster by reaching the marrow.", "The text calls one beat of the patient’s pulse in one breath of a healthy person the fourth degree of depletion. It gives three days as a fatal prognosis.", "The text calls one beat of the patient’s pulse in two breaths of a healthy person the fifth degree of depletion. It gives one day as a fatal prognosis.", "The text calls one beat of the patient’s pulse in three breaths of a healthy person the sixth degree of depletion. It says death comes within one time period.", "The fourth degree is assigned to cessation of qi in the channels, the fifth to cessation of qi in the fu organ systems, and the sixth to cessation of qi in the zang organ systems. The text defines these by where true qi has stopped circulating. It links cessation in the channels with inability to raise the limbs, in the fu with unresponsiveness, and in the zang with cold throughout the body.", "The text links an abundant pulse and a cold body with cold damage, and a forceless pulse and a hot body with summerheat injury. It regards persistent illness after profuse sweating and purging with abundance in both pulse aspects as fatal. It gives the same prognosis when both pulse aspects are deficient and fever persists.", "The text regards a pulse that alternates between rapid and widely spaced beats as fatal. When it feels like a twisting rope and does not change under pressure, it predicts death that day. It gives a favorable prognosis for delirious speech with slight body heat, a floating, large pulse, and warm hands and feet. For reversal cold with a deep, fine pulse, it predicts death within a day. The text identifies the preceding passages as signs and patterns of cold damage and heat illness.", "The pulse is yielding and weak, with weakness unexpectedly at the bar, yielding at the far end, faintness above, and roughness below. The text links faintness with insufficient yang qi and roughness with lack of blood. With yang qi faint, wind strike brings sweating and unexpected agitation. With lack of blood, there is reversal and cold. Sweating in yang reversal brings restlessness and inability to sleep. The text rules out purging when yang is faint because it says this brings hard focal fullness below the breastbone.", "When stirring qi is on the right, the text rules out inducing sweat. It says doing so causes nosebleeds, thirst, distressing agitation, and vomiting as soon as water is drunk.", "When stirring qi is on the left, the text rules out inducing sweat. It says doing so causes dizziness, persistent sweating, and then twitching of the sinews and flesh.", "When stirring qi is above, the text rules out inducing sweat. It says doing so sends qi rushing upward until it stops below the breastbone.", "When stirring qi is below, the text rules out inducing sweat. It describes an absence of sweat to bring forth, severe agitation, painful joints, dizziness, aversion to cold, vomiting of food, and qi unable to go forward.", "When the throat is blocked, the text rules out inducing sweat. It says doing so causes vomiting of blood, breath almost failing, and cold hands and feet. The person wants to curl up but cannot get warm.", "The text rules out inducing sweat when pulse qualities include rapidity, stirring, faintness, and weakness. It says doing so brings difficulty passing stool, dryness in the abdomen and stomach, and agitation. It remarks that appearances can be similar while their roots differ. It then describes a yielding and weak pulse, with weakness unexpectedly at the bar, yielding at the far end, wiriness above, and faintness below. It links wiriness to yang movement and faintness to yin cold. With excess above and deficiency below, the person seeks warmth.", "The text reads a faint, wiry pulse as deficiency and rules out inducing sweat. It says doing so brings chills and shaking that the person cannot recover from on their own. When coughing is present, sweating is said to make the cough worse. The person repeatedly spits froth, the throat dries, urination becomes difficult, and hunger comes with agitation. After a complete day and night, a bout like intermittent fever appears, with chills alone, weakness, and shaking. The person curls up in distressing fullness, the abdomen grows hard again, and the text considers survival unlikely."], "notes": [["時氣病", "illness attributed to seasonal influences", "时气病", "「时气」指古书所说的一时或一季的气候影响。本段用病程与饮水反应分辨不同情形。", "The phrase refers to influences of the time or season in classical thought. This passage describes different responses to drinking as the illness progresses."], ["大汗出", "profuse sweating", "大汗出", "本页有的句子把汗出当作自然转愈的迹象，有的写治疗后仍有病。理解「汗出」时要一起看前后迹象。", "The page describes sweating during spontaneous improvement as well as in an illness that persists after treatment. The surrounding signs determine how to read it."], ["五十九穴", "fifty-nine points", "五十九穴", "「穴」是针灸所用的部位。本句记数量，下面另谈古书所说针刺或施灸可能伤人的穴位。", "These are points used in acupuncture and moxibustion. The following clause gives a separate historical warning about harm at certain points."], ["四損", "fourth degree of depletion", "四损至六损", "这里以平人呼吸为计时参照，比较病人脉来一次所需的时间。四损、一至二息的五损和一至三息的六损逐条加重。", "The passage counts the patient’s pulse against a healthy person’s breathing. One beat takes one, two, or three breaths in the three successive grades."], ["真氣", "true qi", "真气", "本段借真气运行不及经、腑、藏来解释三层衰绝，并逐层列出相应的身体表现。", "The text explains three levels of failure by assigning the cessation of true qi to the channels, fu, or zang."], ["動氣在右", "stirring qi is on the right", "动气", "这里按右、左、上、下列出动气所在，作为四条发汗禁例。「动气」提示身内有可察觉的动象。", "The text locates a perceptible stirring in the body on the right, left, above, or below, and makes each location a reason against inducing sweat."], ["筋惕肉瞤", "twitching of the sinews and flesh", "筋惕肉瞤", "「惕」与「瞤」在此写颤动、抽动的样子。本段把它放在出汗一直不停之后。", "Both characters evoke involuntary movement. The passage places the twitching after sweating that continues."], ["其形相象，根本異源", "appearances can be similar while their roots differ", "形似而源异", "作者提醒外在表现相似时仍要追究来由。接下来把同一脉的几个特征分别解释。", "The author cautions that similar signs may have different sources, then interprets the pulse qualities separately."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "page": 7, "english": ["For reversal cold with a tight pulse, the text rules out inducing sweat. It says doing so disturbs the voice, makes the throat hoarse, weakens the tongue, and prevents the voice from coming freely.", "When inducing sweat goes against the pattern, the text portrays mild illness as hard to resolve and severe illness as fatal.", "For induced sweating, the text describes the intended result as a light dampness all over the body. It warns against sweat streaming like water. If the illness persists, it describes inducing sweat again, but rules this out after profuse sweating because it associates it with loss of yang.", "When a sweating decoction takes effect on the illness, the text says to stop at that point, even if some of the preparation remains.", "When an emetic decoction takes effect on the illness, the text likewise says to stop. It rules out inducing vomiting in cases of cold hands and feet in reversal, and in people with deficiency.", "The text describes signs of excess high in the chest: oppressive pain, inability to eat, a desire for pressure on the chest, and saliva to spit out. It adds more than ten episodes of diarrhea, a pulse unexpectedly rough, and slight slipperiness at the inch opening. It presents induced vomiting as a means of stopping the diarrhea in this situation. It also assigns this method to retained food in the upper stomach region.", "When stirring qi is on the right, the text rules out purging. It says purging depletes fluids within, with a dry throat and nose, dizziness, and palpitations.", "When stirring qi is on the left, the text rules out purging. It says purging causes abdominal tightness, inability to get food and drink down, and worse stirring. Even with bodily heat, the person wants to curl up when lying down.", "When stirring qi is above, the text rules out purging. It says purging brings distressing heat in the palms, cold at the body surface, and hot sweat that escapes on its own. The person wants to pour water over the body.", "When stirring qi is below, the text rules out purging. It says purging brings abdominal distention and fullness, dizziness on rising suddenly, diarrhea with undigested food after eating, and focal fullness below the breastbone.", "When cold closes the throat, the text rules out purging. It says purging makes the upper body feel light and the lower body heavy, prevents liquids from going down, makes the person want to curl up, and causes bodily tightness and pain with dozens of bouts of diarrhea a day.", "In exterior excess, the text rules out purging. It says purging brings slight fever. If the pulse disappears and reversal occurs, it mentions warmth applied at the navel.", "In cases of deficiency, the text rules out purging. It says purging causes great thirst, then gives a better prognosis for those who seek water and a worse one for those who recoil from it.", "The pulse is yielding and weak, with weakness unexpectedly at the bar, yielding at the far end, wiriness above, and faintness below. The text links wiriness to yang movement and faintness to yin cold. With excess above and deficiency below, the person seeks warmth.", "The text reads a faint, wiry pulse as deficiency and rules out purging. It associates this pulse with coughing and spitting saliva. Purging is said to stop the cough but bring relentless diarrhea. As it persists, the chest feels gnawed from within, porridge passes straight through, urination is difficult, both sides of the ribs draw tight, breathing is labored, and the neck and back pull on each other. The arms lose sensation. Despite extreme cold the person sweats, the body is cold as ice, the eyes lose their brightness, and speech is unceasing. The person takes in a great deal of food. The text calls this failure of the center. Though the person wants to speak, the tongue cannot move forward.", "The pulse is yielding and weak, with weakness unexpectedly at the bar, yielding at the far end, floating above, and rapidity below. The text connects floating with yang deficiency and rapidity with lack of blood. It also connects floating with deficiency and rapidity with the arising of heat. Floating deficiency is associated with spontaneous sweating, aversion to cold, and shaking chills. With faintness and weakness at the bar, there is tightness below the chest, panting and sweating, and difficulty breathing. Rapidity signals pain at the ribs during breathing. The shaking chills give the appearance of intermittent fever. The text then describes a practitioner purging in error, followed by a rapid pulse, fever, frantic running, visions of spirits, focal fullness below the breastbone, dribbling urine, a very hard lower abdomen, and blood in the urine.", "The pulse is yielding and tight. The text associates yielding with faint defensive qi and tightness with cold in the nutritive aspect. With faint yang and wind affecting the defensive aspect, there is fever and aversion to cold. With tightness in the nutritive aspect and cold stomach qi, there is slight vomiting with agitation. Mistaking this for strong heat, a practitioner releases the muscle layer and induces sweat. The text links the ensuing loss of yang with agitation and hard focal fullness below the breastbone. Exterior and interior are both drained, and standing suddenly causes dizziness. Heat lodged at the skin leaves the person distressed and unable to sleep. Missing the cold stomach qi and tight cold at the Gate of Origin, the practitioner pours drawn water over the body. The superficial heat disappears immediately, and shaking chills set in. Heavy quilts bring sweat and a clouded feeling in the head. The body twitches and shudders again, and urination is somewhat difficult. The text traces the cold to the water and describes nonstop diarrhea with undigested food. The vomiting gives way to discharge from the bowels and the person finds no rest. The hands and feet turn a little cold, the body chills, and agitation remains within. By the time rescue is attempted, the text asks how recovery could still be brought about."], "notes": [["厥逆", "reversal cold", "厥逆", "本页用「厥逆」开头，后文又有「四逆」。这里从手足发冷等迹象理解古书所描述的逆冷。", "Reversal cold is the classical pattern term. The later reference to the four limbs helps connect it to cold hands and feet."], ["漐漐微似汗", "a light dampness all over the body", "漐漐", "「漐漐」写微润近于汗出的样子。下面紧接着用流水般的汗量作参照。", "The expression describes slight moisture on the skin. The next line gives sweat streaming like water as the other degree."], ["中病便止", "takes effect on the illness", "中病便止", "「中」读作命中之中，表示用法已经达到作者所说的预期效果。汗汤、吐汤两条都接「不必尽剂」。", "The verb 中 means to hit the intended target. For both sweating and emetic decoctions, the text says to stop once the desired effect appears."], ["上脘", "upper stomach region", "上脘", "古书用来指胃脘上部的名称。本句说食物留停在这里，并据此安排治法。", "A classical name for the upper stomach region. Its location matters to the way this passage classifies retained food."], ["津液內竭", "depletes fluids within", "津液内竭", "「津液」在这里统称滋润身体的体液。后面的咽燥、鼻干具体说明了作者怎样理解耗竭。", "The term covers fluids that moisten the body in classical theory. The dry throat and nose that follow make the idea more concrete."], ["下利清穀", "diarrhea with undigested food", "下利清谷", "「下利」指腹泻。「清谷」在这一短语中说吃下的食物随大便排出，仍可认出没有消化。", "The term describes diarrhea in which the food passed remains recognizably undigested."], ["除中", "failure of the center", "除中", "作者用这个古病名概括这一段的危险转变。能多进食与身冷、下利、不安等同时出现，需连起来理解。", "This is the historical name the text gives to the deterioration described here. The appetite appears together with bodily cold, persistent diarrhea, and unrest."], ["衛氣微", "faint defensive qi", "卫气与营中", "本段把濡脉和卫气微相连，把紧脉和营中寒相连。「荣」是本底本用来写「营」的字。", "The paragraph connects the yielding pulse with faint defensive qi and the tight pulse with cold in the nutritive aspect. 榮 is this edition’s written form for 營."], ["客熱在皮膚", "Heat lodged at the skin", "客热", "「客」带有暂时寄居的意思。后文以体表热退后反而剧烈发冷，展开这段关于客热的叙述。", "Guest heat is presented as heat temporarily lodged at the skin. In the episode, severe chills follow its disappearance."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "page": 8, "english": ["The pulse is floating and tight. The text takes floating as wind and tightness as cold. Wind affects the defensive aspect and cold affects the nutritive aspect. When both are affected, the joints ache. The text assigns this pattern to induced sweating and rules out purging.", "When the pulse is floating and large, there may unexpectedly be hardness and heat below the breastbone. For what the text assigns to the zang organ systems, it describes aggressive treatment and rules out inducing sweat. For what it assigns to the fu organ systems, it cautions against allowing frequent urination, which it links to hard stool. It says profuse sweating makes matters worse and a slow pulse means it is still too early for aggressive treatment.", "In cold damage with tightness in both yin and yang pulse aspects, aversion to cold and fever mark a pulse approaching reversal. Here the text defines reversal by a beat that starts large, gradually becomes small, then gradually grows large again. In this condition it describes aversion to cold, and in severe cases gentle, continuous sweating and a sore throat. When heat predominates, the eyes are red, their vessels prominent, and the gaze loses its clarity. The text says inducing sweat again injures the throat. If purging is then used, it says both eyes close. Predominant cold is associated with passing undigested food and predominant heat with passing pus and blood. It says fumigation brings yellow discoloration of the body and warming applications dry the throat. Smooth urination is given as a sign that the person can still recover, and difficult urination as a sign of danger.", "In cold damage with fever, the person exhales forcefully through the mouth, has a headache and yellow eyes, and has nosebleeding that cannot be stopped. The text describes both yin and yang as deficient. It associates craving water with vomiting and aversion to water with reversal. It says purging causes throat sores, and where the hands and feet are warm it predicts rectal heaviness and passage of pus and blood. For headache with yellow eyes, it says purging makes the eyes close. In people who crave water, it says purging brings reversal of the pulse, a thin whining voice, and throat blockage, while inducing sweat brings shaking chills. In people averse to water, it says purging brings internal cold, loss of appetite, and undigested food in the stool. Inducing sweat is said to injure the mouth and bring a white tongue coating, restlessness, an unexpectedly rapid pulse, no bowel movement, and at six or seven days blood in the stool and difficulty urinating.", "When a purging decoction has produced evacuation, the text says to stop even if some remains. This closes its account of the essentials of the three methods of sweating, vomiting, and purging. It asks whether one may begin to grasp the art by seeing clearly how to apply the principles beyond the cases given here."], "notes": [["榮衛俱病", "both are affected", "营卫俱病", "前句分别说风伤卫、寒伤营，「俱」把两处并在一起，下面接关节疼痛。", "The preceding sentences assign wind to the defensive aspect and cold to the nutritive aspect. This phrase brings the two together before mentioning joint pain."], ["脈欲厥", "a pulse approaching reversal", "脉欲厥", "此处「厥」直接说脉的形状变化。下一句描写脉先大、渐小、再渐大。", "Here reversal refers to the pulse itself. The next sentence gives the sequence from large to small and back to large."], ["下重", "rectal heaviness", "下重", "古书用「下重」写排便时下坠、沉重的感觉。本句把它与便中有脓血相连。", "The term describes a dragging or heavy sensation with bowel movements. The text pairs it here with passing pus and blood."], ["汗吐下三法", "three methods of sweating, vomiting, and purging", "汗、吐、下", "本篇把发汗、催吐、攻下并称三法。末段重申已经见到预期反应时可以停下，并邀请读者领会各条例背后的判断。", "The chapter groups inducing sweat, inducing vomiting, and purging as three methods. The closing asks readers to understand the reasoning behind the cases."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-8", "page": 1, "title": "Warm Diseases", "english": ["(In Taiyang disease, fever with thirst and no aversion to cold is called warm disease. If sweating has been induced and the body burns with heat afterward, this is called wind-warmth. In wind-warmth, both yin and yang pulse aspects are floating. There is spontaneous sweating, bodily heaviness, frequent sleep, snoring, and difficulty speaking. If purging has been used, urination is difficult, the gaze is fixed straight ahead, and urine is passed involuntarily. If fire has been applied, a slight yellow color appears. Severe cases resemble a convulsive seizure, with intermittent spasms and twitching. If the patient is exposed to fire fumes, the text says one mistreatment may still leave some days and a second hastens death.) Quoted from Volume Six, 'Pulse Signs and Treatment of Taiyang Disease, Part One.'", "There are three warm diseases: spring warmth, autumn warmth, and winter warmth. All emerge from latent qi. In summer the text classifies the illness as summerheat disease.", "When cold causes injury in winter, its qi lies latent in Shaoyin. It emerges in spring as a warm disease called spring warmth.", "When dampness causes injury in summer, its qi lies latent in Taiyin. When dryness becomes prevalent in autumn, the warm disease emerges and is called autumn warmth.", "Qi arrives when it is out of season. If there is already severe cold at the beginning of winter, dryness gathers inward and its qi lies latent in Jueyin. After the Winter Solstice, the weather should be cold, but it becomes warm and a warm disease emerges. This is called winter warmth.", "Warm disease in spring and autumn follows the usual pattern. Warm disease in winter is a variation. When winter should be cold but becomes very warm, the unseasonable qi accumulates. Illness does not appear at the time and emerges only in spring. This is called great warmth. The text attributes it to a failure to store essence in winter and a loss of the proper order of qi. In spring, yang qi comes outward. If the two qi contend and cause illness, it is severe. The author goes on to link mistaken treatment through ignorance of the source of the illness with the spread of corpse qi and the development of an epidemic.", "In spring warmth, the qi is in the upper region. There is headache, a dry throat, fever, and dizziness. Severe cases bring delirious speech. The pulse is wiry and urgent. The text indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction with Huanglian and Moutan.", "Formula for Minor Bupleurum Decoction with Huanglian and Moutan.", "Chaihu, half a jin. Huangqin, three liang. Renshen, three liang. Gualou root, four liang. Huanglian, three liang. Moutan bark, four liang. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Fresh ginger, three liang. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these nine ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In autumn warmth, the qi is in the middle region. There is fever, thirst, burning abdominal pain, and diarrhea with pus and blood in the stool. The pulse is large, short, and rough. The text indicates Dihuang, Zhimu, Huanglian, and Ejiao Decoction. Without pus and blood in the stool it indicates White Tiger Decoction.", "Formula for Dihuang, Zhimu, Huanglian, and Ejiao Decoction.", "Dihuang, eight liang. Zhimu, four liang. Huanglian, three liang. Ejiao, one liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng. It says to boil until the rice is cooked, remove the dregs when the decoction is ready, and take one sheng warm three times daily.", "In winter warmth, the qi is in the lower region. There is fever, abdominal pain extending into the lower abdomen, and a dry, painful throat at midnight. The pulse is deep and replete and at times large and rapid. The text indicates Shigao, Huanglian, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction. If there is no bowel movement for six or seven days, it indicates Dahuang, Huangqin, Dihuang, and Moutan Decoction.", "Formula for Shigao, Huanglian, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction.", "Shigao, half a jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton. Huanglian, three liang. Huangqin, four liang. Gancao, two liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for Dahuang, Huangqin, Dihuang, and Moutan Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang. Huangqin, three liang. Dihuang, four liang. Moutan bark, three liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, reduced by boiling to two sheng, with the dregs removed. It records two warm portions, with the later portion omitted once the bowels move freely.", "Warm disease with headache, a red face, fever, tightness and cramping in the hands and feet, and a floating, wiry, and rapid pulse is called wind-warmth. The text indicates Huanglian, Huangqin, Zhizi, Moutan, and Shaoyao Decoction.", "Formula for Huanglian, Huangqin, Zhizi, Moutan, and Shaoyao Decoction.", "Huanglian, three liang. Huangqin, three liang. Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Moutan, three liang. Shaoyao, three liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily."], "notes": [["發熱而渴，不惡寒", "fever with thirst and no aversion to cold", "开篇的三个线索", "原文先列发热、口渴，再交代没有怕冷。这三点合起来引出温病。", "The opening gives fever and thirst, then adds the absence of aversion to cold. The three clues together introduce warm disease."], ["自汗出", "spontaneous sweating", "自汗", "「自」指汗自行出现。前面的「發汗」是用方法使人出汗，两处说的是汗出的不同起因。", "The sweat appears on its own. The earlier phrase describes inducing sweat, so the two phrases specify different ways sweating begins."], ["伏氣", "latent qi", "伏气", "伏有潜藏的意思。下文的冬寒和夏湿都先有受伤与潜伏，到后来才发病。", "伏 means to lie hidden. The examples of winter cold and summer dampness both describe an earlier injury, a latent period, and a later onset."], ["大溫", "great warmth", "大温", "冬季反常温暖、蓄气、春天发病，是这一段给「大温」安排的三个环节。", "The passage describes three stages for great warmth: unseasonable winter warmth, accumulation of qi, and illness emerging in spring."], ["譫語", "delirious speech", "谵语", "谵语指神志受扰时出现的错乱言语。此段用「甚则」引出，表示文中把它放在较重的情形里。", "The phrase describes disordered speech when awareness is disturbed. Here it follows the phrase introducing severe cases."], ["脈弦而急", "wiry and urgent", "弦与急", "弦借弓弦提示脉的绷直感。急在本句提示急迫。本章另用「数」明确说脉跳得快。", "Wiry evokes the taut feel of a bowstring. Urgent conveys a pressing quality here. Elsewhere the chapter uses 數 for a rapid rate."], ["短澀", "short, and rough", "短与涩", "短指脉势的范围短，涩形容脉来不流利。原句还写了「大」，于是同一脉上可并列三种形容。", "Short describes a limited span and rough describes a less smooth movement. The text also calls this pulse large, giving three qualities together."], ["煮米熟", "boil until the rice is cooked", "正文提到米", "本段煎法写到煮米，但上一段所列四味未写米。译文保留这两处原文的差异，方便按底本核对。", "The directions mention cooking rice. Rice is absent from the preceding list of four ingredients. Both details are preserved so the reader can compare the source lines."], ["大便利，止後服", "with the later portion omitted once the bowels move freely", "便利与后服", "这里的「便利」连着「大便」读，是排便通畅。「后服」接前面的分作两服，指后面那份。", "The phrase refers to the bowels moving freely. The later portion is the second of the two portions just mentioned."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-8", "page": 2, "title": "Warm Diseases", "english": ["In warm disease, a person with longstanding dampness develops fever, parched lips, diarrhea, and burning abdominal pain. The pulse is large and rapid. This is called damp-warmth. The text indicates Zhuling Decoction with Huanglian and Moutan.", "Formula for Zhuling Decoction with Huanglian and Moutan.", "Zhuling, one liang. Fuling, one liang. Ejiao, one liang. Zexie, one liang. Huashi, one liang. Huanglian, one liang. Moutan, one liang.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Six ingredients are boiled first to yield two sheng. After the dregs are removed, the Ejiao is added and melted. The text records two warm portions.", "In warm disease with a red tongue, dry throat, vexing heat in the heart region, and an urgent, rapid pulse extending up toward the inch opening, the text says a warm pathogen encroaches on the heart. It indicates Huanglian, Huangqin, Ejiao, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Huanglian, Huangqin, Ejiao, and Gancao Decoction.", "Huanglian, one liang. Huangqin, one liang. Ejiao, one liang. Gancao, one liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. Three ingredients are boiled first to yield four sheng. After the dregs are removed, the Ejiao is added and melted. The text records three warm portions.", "In warm disease with thirst, cough, an unceasing nosebleed, and a floating, rapid, and large pulse, the text says a warm pathogen takes advantage of the lung. It indicates Huangqin, Shigao, Xingzi, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Huangqin, Shigao, Xingzi, and Gancao Decoction.", "Huangqin, three liang. Shigao, half a jin, crushed. Fourteen apricot kernels with the skins and tips removed. Roasted Gancao, one liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In warm disease with fever, water qi below the waist, burning pain in the lower abdomen, frequent passing of reddish urine, and an urgent, rapid pulse extending down toward the cubit position, the text says a warm pathogen has shifted to the kidney. It indicates Dihuang, Huangbai, Qinpi, Fuling, and Zexie Decoction.", "Formula for Dihuang, Huangbai, Qinpi, Fuling, and Zexie Decoction.", "Dihuang, six liang. Huangbai, three liang. Qinpi, two liang. Fuling, three liang. Zexie, one liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In great warmth with fever, lightheadedness, dizziness, parched teeth and lips, delirious speech, loss of awareness, a complexion now bluish and now red, and an urgent, large, and rapid pulse, the text indicates Dahuang and Xiangpu Decoction. When the throat is obstructed and swallowing difficult, it calls for bleeding at Shaoshang by needling. It treats a pulse that alternates between spaced-out and rapid beats, together with sunken eyes, as a sign of death.", "Formula for Dahuang and Xiangpu Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang. Xiangpu, one liang. Huanglian, three liang. Dihuang, half a jin. Moutan bark, six liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, reduced by boiling to six sheng, with the dregs removed. It records two sheng taken warm three times daily.", "If purging in warm disease produces loose stools, the text expects spontaneous recovery. If diarrhea persists after purging, abdominal fullness is expected and the text indicates Fuling, Baizhu, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling, Baizhu, and Gancao Decoction.", "Fuling, four liang. Baizhu, three liang. Roasted Gancao, one liang.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In wind-warmth, the person already has heat and falls ill after further injury by wind. The pulse is floating, wiry, and rapid. Without headache, the text indicates Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Guizhi removed and Huangqin and Moutan added. If warm disease arises from latent qi and sweating is induced in error, there is intense heat and distress, with parched lips, red eyes, and sometimes nosebleed, vomiting, or deafness. For a large and rapid pulse the text indicates White Tiger Decoction. For marked excess it indicates one of the Qi-Order Decoctions. If the disorder enters the interior after more than ten days, it indicates Huanglian and Ejiao Decoction. How is entry into the interior recognized? By a deep and rapid pulse and vexation that prevents sleep.", "Formula for Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Guizhi removed and Huangqin and Moutan added.", "Shaoyao, three liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split. Huangqin, three liang. Moutan bark, three liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction, given earlier.", "Formula for Major Qi-Order Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang, washed with wine. Prepared Houpo, half a jin. Five roasted Zhishi fruits. Mangxiao, three ge."], "notes": [["素有濕", "longstanding dampness", "素有", "「素」在这里指平素。作者先交代原先已有湿，再列发热、唇焦、下利等新出现的表现。", "素 means ordinarily or from before. The passage gives the previous presence of dampness before listing the signs of illness."], ["納膠烊消", "the Ejiao is added and melted", "烊消", "膠承接药名阿胶。烊消就是受热而融化。本句把先煮的药和后来放入的胶分开叙述。", "膠 refers back to Ejiao. 烊消 means melting with heat. The sentence describes the initial boiling and the later addition in separate steps."], ["上寸口", "extending up toward the inch opening", "寸口", "这里的「上」写脉势向寸口。后面另有「下尺中」，用相对的方向写脉势在腕部不同位置。", "上 describes the pulse extending toward the inch opening. A later passage describes the opposite direction toward the cubit position at the wrist."], ["衄不止", "an unceasing nosebleed", "衄", "衄在这里指鼻出血。「不止」给出持续的特点。该句把它与口渴、咳嗽和脉象合在一起看。", "衄 here means bleeding from the nose. 不止 means it continues. The sentence groups it with thirst, coughing, and the pulse qualities."], ["下尺中", "extending down toward the cubit position", "尺中", "尺是腕部寸、关、尺之一，位置靠近前臂。这里与上文的「上寸口」相对。", "The cubit is one of the three wrist positions and lies toward the forearm. This phrase pairs with the earlier description of movement up toward the inch opening."], ["乍疏乍數", "alternates between spaced-out and rapid beats", "乍……乍……", "两个「乍」连用表示一会儿这样、一会儿那样。这一处交替的是脉搏疏和数。", "The repeated 乍 means now one way, now another. Here the pulse alternates between spaced-out and rapid beats."], ["下之利不止", "diarrhea persists after purging", "下与利", "「下之」是使其下的治法，在句中先发生。「利不止」说之后泄泻持续，两个「下」「利」各有作用。", "下之 denotes purging, which occurs first. 利不止 describes persistent diarrhea afterward. The two expressions have different roles in the sentence."], ["承氣輩", "one of the Qi-Order Decoctions", "承气辈", "「辈」在这里表示同一类方名。大承气、小承气和调胃承气在本页末和下一页接着列出。", "輩 points to a family of formulas. The Major, Minor, and Stomach-Regulating Qi-Order Decoctions follow at the end of this page and on the next."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-8", "page": 3, "title": "Warm Diseases", "english": ["For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. Two ingredients are boiled first to yield five sheng and the dregs are removed. Dahuang is added and boiled further to yield two sheng. The dregs are removed again, Mangxiao is added, and the liquid is returned to low heat for one or two brief boils. It is divided into two warm portions. Once the bowels move, the rest is omitted.", "Formula for Minor Qi-Order Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang, washed with wine. Prepared Houpo, two liang. Three large roasted Zhishi fruits.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water, reduced by boiling to one sheng and two ge, with the dregs removed. It records two warm portions. If the first brings a bowel movement, the remaining portion is omitted. Otherwise the rest is taken.", "Formula for Stomach-Regulating Qi-Order Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang, washed with wine. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Mangxiao, half a jin.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water. Two ingredients are boiled down to one sheng and the dregs are removed. Mangxiao is added and the mixture is brought back to a boil over low heat. It is taken warm in small amounts.", "Formula for Huanglian and Ejiao Decoction.", "Huanglian, four liang. Shaoyao, two liang. Huangqin, two liang. Ejiao, three liang. Three egg yolks.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water. Three ingredients are boiled first to yield two sheng. The dregs are removed and Ejiao is added and melted. After the liquid has cooled slightly, the egg yolks are added and stirred until blended. The text records seven ge taken warm three times daily.", "When warm disease is treated improperly and persists, it shifts through the three burners. In the upper burner, the tongue moves with difficulty and consciousness is clouded. The text indicates Gardenia Decoction. In the middle burner, abdominal pain accompanies diarrhea and persists after it, with dryness of the lips and mouth. The text indicates White Tiger Decoction with Dihuang. In the lower burner, there is heat from the waist downward, with blackened teeth and a dry throat. The text indicates Baihe, Dihuang, Moutan Bark, Banxia, and Fuling Decoction.", "Formula for Gardenia Decoction.", "Sixteen gardenia fruits, split. Huangqin, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Gancao, two liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. The gardenia is boiled first to yield two and a half sheng. After the dregs are removed, the other three ingredients are added and boiled to yield one sheng, divided into two warm portions.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction with Dihuang.", "Zhimu, six liang. Shigao, one jin, crushed. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Non-glutinous rice, six ge. Dihuang, six liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, with boiling continued until the rice is cooked. The dregs are removed when the decoction is ready. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for Baihe, Dihuang, Moutan Bark, Banxia, and Fuling Decoction.", "Seven Baihe bulbs, split. Dihuang juice, one sheng. Moutan bark, six liang. Banxia, one sheng, washed. Fuling, four liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text first has the Baihe washed and soaked overnight. White foam is expected to appear. The soaking water is discarded, and the Baihe is boiled separately in two sheng of fresh water to yield one sheng, with the dregs removed. Three other ingredients are boiled separately in four sheng of spring water to yield two sheng. The Dihuang juice and Baihe liquid are added and the mixture is brought back to a boil. The text records one sheng taken warm three times daily."], "notes": [["右四味", "For these four ingredients", "接上页的药味", "「右」是古书指向上一列内容的说法。本页第一句接的是上一页末尾大承气汤所列四味。", "右 points back to an earlier list. The opening line continues the four ingredients of Major Qi-Order Decoction at the end of the previous page."], ["得下，餘勿服", "Once the bowels move, the rest is omitted", "得下", "「得下」指服后已经排便。这句话接在分成两份之后，说明出现这一结果时剩下的一份如何处理。", "得下 refers to a bowel movement after taking the decoction. The phrase comes after the division into two portions and gives the condition for omitting the second."], ["更衣", "a bowel movement", "更衣", "「更衣」字面是换衣，在这个上下文中委婉指排便。联系前一段的「得下」，就能读懂这条的停服条件。", "The literal meaning is changing clothes. Here it is a euphemism for a bowel movement, parallel to the wording in the preceding paragraph."], ["少少溫服", "taken warm in small amounts", "少少", "叠用的两个「少」表示一点一点。前面的方写成分两服，本方用这四字描写少量温服。", "Repeating 少 conveys a little at a time. The earlier formulas specify two portions, while this formula describes small amounts taken warm."], ["一升二合", "one sheng and two ge", "升与合", "升和合是原文的容量单位。同一处同时写两个单位，英译保留原单位，便于对读古文。", "Sheng and ge are units of volume in the source. The translation keeps both units as written so they can be matched against the Chinese."], ["移于三焦", "shifts through the three burners", "三焦", "这一段按上焦、中焦、下焦组织症候。初读可逐一找出每个「其在」所引出的部位和表现。", "The paragraph organizes signs by the upper, middle, and lower burners. Match each location with the signs immediately following it."], ["舌蹇", "the tongue moves with difficulty", "舌蹇", "蹇在这里表示不利。和紧接的神昏并列，是本段放在上焦的一组表现。", "蹇 conveys impaired movement. The text pairs difficulty moving the tongue with clouded consciousness in the upper-burner description."], ["與百合汁", "and Baihe liquid are added", "分煮再合", "这一句收束前面两段操作。百合另煮，三味另以泉水煮，后段再把百合汁和地黄汁与煮出的药液合在一起。", "This line brings the two streams of preparation together. The Baihe is boiled separately and the other three ingredients are boiled in spring water before the Baihe liquid and Dihuang juice are added."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-9", "page": 1, "title": "Summerheat Damage", "english": ["(Cold damage gives rise to illness because cold is the qi with the strongest killing force. Illness that begins immediately after being struck is called cold damage. If illness does not begin immediately, the cold toxin lies hidden in the skin and flesh. By spring it becomes warm disease, and by summer it becomes summerheat disease. In summerheat disease the heat is more intense than in warm disease.) Quoted from 'General Principles of Cold Damage, Fourth.'", "In summerheat damage, the lung is affected first. The lung is the storehouse of qi. Summerheat injures original qi, and the pulse at the inch opening is weak. There is thirst, sweating, clouded consciousness, and shortness of breath. The text indicates Bamboo Leaf and Shigao Decoction.", "Formula for Bamboo Leaf and Shigao Decoction.", "Two handfuls of bamboo leaves. Non-glutinous rice, half a sheng. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Shigao, one jin. Renshen, three liang. Maimendong, one sheng. Roasted Gancao, two liang.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. Six ingredients are boiled first to yield six sheng, then the dregs are removed. The non-glutinous rice is added and boiled until cooked. The text records one sheng of the finished decoction taken warm three times daily.", "Summerheat damage with fever, sweating, thirst, and a floating and large pulse is called being struck by summerheat. The text indicates White Tiger Decoction with Renshen, Huanglian, and Ejiao.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction with Huanglian and Ejiao.", "Zhimu, six liang. Shigao, one jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Non-glutinous rice, six ge. Renshen, three liang. Huanglian, three liang. Ejiao, two liang.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. Six ingredients are boiled first until the rice is cooked and the decoction is ready. The dregs are removed and the Ejiao is added and melted. The text records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "When sweating has occurred in summerheat damage and there is fever, restlessness, hoarseness, and an unexpectedly floating and rapid pulse, the text attributes this to injury to the fluids of the lung. It indicates Baihe and Dihuang Decoction with Muli.", "Formula for Baihe and Dihuang Decoction with Muli.", "Seven Baihe bulbs. Dihuang juice, one sheng. Muli, two liang.", "For these three ingredients, the text first has the Baihe washed and soaked overnight. White foam is expected to appear, then the soaking water is discarded. Two ingredients are boiled separately in two sheng of spring water to yield one sheng and the dregs are removed. Dihuang juice is added and the liquid is boiled down to one sheng and five ge. It is divided into two warm portions.", "In summerheat damage with water qi below the heart, there is sweating, coughing, and thirst with a desire to drink. Water is vomited as soon as it is swallowed. The pulse is weak and slippery. The text indicates Gualou and Fuling Decoction.", "Formula for Gualou and Fuling Decoction.", "One large Gualou fruit, pounded together with its rind and seeds. Fuling, three liang. Banxia, three liang, washed. Huanglian, two liang. Roasted Gancao, one liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, reduced by boiling to two sheng. It records one sheng taken warm twice daily.", "In summerheat damage with fever and no sweating, the text attributes this to water moving within the skin. It expects a floating and slippery pulse. It describes pouring hot water over the person to bring on sweating, then giving Zhuru and Banxia Decoction.", "Formula for Zhuru and Banxia Decoction.", "Zhuru, two liang. Gualou root, two liang. Fuling, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng and divided into three warm portions.", "Being struck by heat in Taiyang is called yē, summerheat strike. (The Shuowen glosses yē as summerheat damage.) The person sweats, has an aversion to cold, feels hot, and is thirsty. The text indicates White Tiger Decoction with Renshen.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction with Renshen.", "Zhimu, six liang. Shigao, one liang, crushed and wrapped in cotton. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Non-glutinous rice, six ge. Renshen, three liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, with boiling continued until the rice is cooked. The dregs are removed when the decoction is ready. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "When summerheat strikes Taiyang and there is bodily heat, aching and heaviness, and a faint, weak pulse, the text attributes this to injury by cold water in summer, with water moving within the skin. It indicates Zhuling Decoction with Renshen or the single-ingredient Guadi Decoction.", "Formula for Zhuling Decoction with Renshen.", "Zhuling, one liang. Fuling, one liang. Huashi, one liang. Zexie, one liang. Ejiao, one liang. Renshen, three liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Five ingredients are boiled first to yield two sheng and the Ejiao is added and melted. It records seven ge taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for single-ingredient Guadi Decoction.", "Twenty melon stalks.", "The text has the ingredient chopped and boiled in one sheng of water to yield five ge. The dregs are removed and the entire amount is taken at once."], "notes": [["中而即病", "Illness that begins immediately after being struck", "中而即病", "中在这里读 zhòng，表示受到外来之气。即病说立刻发病，下面转写未立即发病的情形。", "Here 中 is read zhòng and means to be struck by an external influence. 即 gives the timing as immediate. The next sentence describes delayed onset."], ["熱極重于溫", "the heat is more intense than in warm disease", "暑病与温病", "前文先按春、夏分出温病和暑病，本句再用「重于」表示热的程度比较。", "The passage first associates warm disease with spring and summerheat disease with summer. It then compares their intensity of heat."], ["寸口脈弱", "the pulse at the inch opening is weak", "寸口与弱脉", "寸口是腕部取脉的地方。弱说所触到的脉势弱，下面的「微弱」另外加了「微」。", "The inch opening is a place at the wrist where the pulse is felt. 弱 describes weakness. A later line adds faintness in the phrase 微弱."], ["中暍", "being struck by summerheat", "暍", "暍读 yē。本页后面的《说文》引文直接用「伤暑」解释这个字。", "暍 is pronounced yē. The later quotation from the Shuowen glosses it as summerheat damage."], ["白虎加黃連阿膠湯方", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction with Huanglian and Ejiao", "方题与上句", "本方标题只写黄连和阿胶。上句的方名和下面药味表均有人参。英译依各处底本保留。", "The heading lists Huanglian and Ejiao. The name in the preceding sentence and the ingredient list both include Renshen. The translation preserves the wording of each line."], ["肺液傷", "injury to the fluids of the lung", "肺液伤", "这里「液」用来解释汗出以后出现的声音嘶哑等表现。下页把程度更重的一种情况称作「肺液枯」。", "The text uses injury to fluids to explain hoarseness and other signs that follow sweating. The next page describes a more severe case as depletion of the fluids of the lung."], ["水入則吐", "Water is vomited as soon as it is swallowed", "渴与水入即吐", "前面写想喝水，这句写水进了又吐。「则」把水入与呕吐连成前后相接的一对。", "The preceding words describe wanting a drink. 則 links swallowing water with the vomiting that follows."], ["水行皮中", "water moving within the skin", "水行皮中", "这是本篇解释发热无汗的说法。后文在太阳中暍条再次用这个词组，并加上「夏月伤冷水」。", "The text uses this phrase to explain fever without sweating. Later it repeats the phrase in the discussion of Taiyang summerheat strike and adds a reference to cold water in summer."], ["石膏一兩碎", "Shigao, one liang, crushed", "两与斤", "这一方的石膏在底本作一两。本页其他方中也见石膏一斤，英译按每一处的原单位对应。", "The source gives one liang of Shigao here. Other formulas on the page list one jin. The translation retains each source unit."], ["頓服", "the entire amount is taken at once", "顿服", "顿表示一次服完。前文的日再服、日三服都把服次分开，本句则将煮成的药液作一次记载。", "頓 describes taking the full amount in one sitting. Earlier passages record two or three portions over a day."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-9", "page": 2, "title": "Summerheat Damage", "english": ["In general, the text expects sweating in summerheat illness. Without sweating it expects fever, and in this formulation the person with fever does not sweat. It warns against inducing sweating, saying that it brings fever, restlessness, and loss of voice. This is called depletion of the fluids of the lung. The text regards labored high breathing with surging qi as beyond treatment.", "In summerheat damage with restless sleep at night, agitation, delirious speech, a red tongue, and a rapid pulse, the text says summerheat encroaches on the heart. It indicates Huanglian, Banxia, Shigao, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Huanglian, Banxia, Shigao, and Gancao Decoction.", "Huanglian, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Shigao, one jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton. Roasted Gancao, two liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "When summerheat strikes Taiyang, there is fever, aversion to cold, bodily heaviness and pain, and a wiry, fine, hollow, and slow pulse. After urination the person shivers, the body hair stands on end, and the hands and feet become cold with reversal. Even slight exertion brings heat to the body. The mouth stays open and the front teeth dry out. The text says induced sweating worsens aversion to cold, warm needling worsens fever, and repeated purging worsens painful, dribbling urination. It indicates White Tiger Decoction with Guizhi, Renshen, and Shaoyao.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction with Guizhi, Renshen, and Shaoyao.", "Zhimu, six liang. Shigao, one jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Non-glutinous rice, six ge. Guizhi, one liang. Renshen, three liang. Shaoyao, two liang.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, with boiling continued until the rice is cooked and the decoction is ready. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In summerheat damage with a weak pulse, thirst, profuse sweating, and dizziness, the text indicates Renshen and Shigao Decoction.", "Formula for Renshen and Shigao Decoction.", "Renshen, three liang. Shigao, one jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton. One handful of bamboo leaves. Huanglian, one liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily. The text closes by describing summerheat damage without headache and associating a painful head with wind and a heavy head with dampness."], "notes": [["不可發汗", "warns against inducing sweating", "汗出与发汗", "开头说的是汗是否自行出现。这一句换成「发汗」，说的是用方法使人出汗。", "The opening discusses whether sweating happens on its own. 發汗 here means deliberately inducing it."], ["肺液枯", "depletion of the fluids of the lung", "枯", "枯有干涸的意思。可以和上页「肺液伤」并读，体会原文所用的程度不同。", "枯 suggests drying up. Compare the previous page's phrase for injury to the fluids of the lung to see the change in intensity."], ["暑邪干心", "summerheat encroaches on the heart", "干心", "「干」表示侵及或扰及。此处把夜寐不安、烦躁、谵语、舌赤和脉数归入同一解释。", "干 means to encroach on or disturb. Here the text groups restless sleep, agitation, delirious speech, red tongue, and a rapid pulse under that description."], ["弦細芤遲", "wiry, fine, hollow, and slow", "四种脉感", "弦写绷直感，细写宽窄，芤写按时的中空感，迟写跳得慢。四字分别提示脉的不同方面。", "Wiry describes tautness, fine describes narrowness, hollow describes the impression under pressure, and slow describes the rate."], ["灑灑然毛聳", "the person shivers, the body hair stands on end", "洒洒然", "「然」收在状态词之后。后面的「毛耸」把这种寒栗写成可见的汗毛竖起。", "然 follows an expression describing a state. The next words make the shivering visible through the body hair standing on end."], ["前板齒燥", "the front teeth dry out", "前板齿", "指前面的门牙。本句把张口和前齿干燥连着写。", "The phrase points to the front teeth. The line describes their dryness alongside an open mouth."], ["數下之", "repeated purging", "数下之", "这里的「数」读 shuò，意思是多次；本页「脉数」的数读 shuò 时表示脉搏来得快，两个词组要按搭配理解。", "Here 數 means repeatedly and modifies purging. In 脈數 it describes a fast pulse, so the phrase around it determines the meaning."], ["頭痛者風也，頭重者濕也", "associating a painful head with wind and a heavy head with dampness", "痛与重", "章末把头部的两种感觉分开。痛归于风，重归于湿，是这一句用于辨别的两个要点。", "The chapter ends by distinguishing two sensations in the head. It associates pain with wind and heaviness with dampness."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-13", "page": 1, "title": "Wind Damage", "english": ["Wind is called the foremost of the many illnesses. Striking the face, it descends along Yang Brightness and in severe cases enters the spleen. Striking the nape, it descends along Greater Yang and can enter the kidney. Striking the side, it descends along Lesser Yang and can enter the liver. The changes vary, and the author urges careful attention to the distinctions.", "In wind illness, headache, profuse sweating, aversion to wind, pain below the armpit, inability to turn on one's side, and a floating, wiry, rapid pulse are attributed to wind affecting the liver. The text names Minor Cháihú Decoction. If the influence passes into a fu organ, with discomfort, upward retching, abdominal distention, and frequent deep sighing, it names Cháihú, Zhǐshí, Sháoyào, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "Formula record: Minor Cháihú Decoction.", "The record lists cháihú, half a jīn; huángqín, three liǎng; rénshēn, three liǎng; rinsed bànxià, half a shēng; processed gāncǎo, three liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical directions reduce one dǒu and two shēng of water to six shēng, discard the dregs, reduce the liquid again to three shēng, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "Formula record: Cháihú, Zhǐshí, Sháoyào, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists cháihú, eight liǎng; sháoyào, three liǎng; four toasted zhǐshí; and processed gāncǎo, three liǎng.", "The historical directions reduce one dǒu of water to six shēng, discard the dregs, reduce the liquid again to three shēng, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "In wind illness, chest pain, flank fullness, pain in the front of the chest, back, and between the shoulder blades, dry throat, frequent belching, throat swelling or obstruction, and a floating, surging, rapid pulse are attributed to wind encroaching on the heart. The text names Huánglián, Huángqín, Màidōng, Jiégěng, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "Formula record: Huánglián, Huángqín, Màidōng, Jiégěng, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists huánglián, one and a half liǎng; huángqín, three liǎng; màiméndōng, two liǎng; jiégěng, three liǎng; and processed gāncǎo, two liǎng.", "The historical directions reduce six shēng of water to three, discard the dregs, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "In wind illness, listless limbs, bodily heaviness, a feeling of being unable to bear even clothing, pain below the ribs drawing toward the shoulders and back, and a floating, wiry, rough pulse are attributed to wind encroaching on the spleen. The text names Guìzhī Decoction with Cinnamon Removed and Fúlíng and Báizhú Added. If the influence passes into a fu organ and there is abdominal fullness and distention with poor appetite, it names Zhǐshí, Hòupò, Báizhú, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "Formula record: Guìzhī Decoction with Cinnamon Removed and Fúlíng and Báizhú Added.", "The record lists sháoyào, three liǎng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; fúlíng, three liǎng; báizhú, three liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical directions reduce eight shēng of water to three, discard the dregs, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "Formula record: Zhǐshí, Hòupò, Báizhú, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists four toasted zhǐshí; processed, peeled hòupò, two liǎng; báizhú, three liǎng; and processed gāncǎo, one liǎng.", "The historical directions reduce six shēng of water to three, discard the dregs, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "In wind illness, cough, noisy panting, in severe cases spitting blood, dry throat, shoulder and back pain, and a floating, wiry, rapid pulse are attributed to wind encroaching on the lung. The text names Jiégěng, Gāncǎo, Zhǐshí, and Sháoyào Decoction. If the influence passes into the large intestine with dry, bound stool or bleeding below, it names the same decoction with Dìhuáng and Mǔdān added.", "Formula record: Jiégěng, Gāncǎo, Zhǐshí, and Sháoyào Decoction.", "The record lists jiégěng, three liǎng; gāncǎo, two liǎng; four zhǐshí; and sháoyào, three liǎng.", "The historical directions reduce six shēng of water to three, discard the dregs, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "Formula record: Jiégěng, Gāncǎo, Zhǐshí, and Sháoyào Decoction with Dìhuáng and Mǔdān Added.", "The record lists jiégěng, three liǎng; gāncǎo, two liǎng; four zhǐshí; sháoyào, three liǎng; dìhuáng, three liǎng; and mǔdānpí, two liǎng.", "The historical directions reduce six shēng of water to three, discard the dregs, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "In wind illness, swelling of the face and eyes, spinal pain with inability to stand upright, impaired function in the private regions, severe cases described as bone wasting, and a deep, wiry pulse are attributed to wind encroaching on the kidney. The text names Cháihú–Guìzhī Decoction.", "Formula record: Cháihú–Guìzhī Decoction."], "notes": [["風為百病之長", "foremost of the many illnesses", "风为百病之长", "「長」在这里表示居首。本段用这个说法引出风从面、项、侧三个位置侵入的古代解释。", "Foremost introduces the chapter’s organizing idea. It then compares wind striking the face, nape, or side."], ["流於腑", "passes into a fu organ", "流于腑", "句子前半讲肝，后半用「若」另列转入腑的情形。读的时候可以把两组表现分开。", "The if-clause introduces a second pattern after the first one associated with the liver. The shift is signaled by 若."], ["善太息", "frequent deep sighing", "善太息", "医书里的「善」有容易、常常的意思。「太息」就是深长叹气。", "Here 善 indicates a tendency. The phrase refers to frequent deep sighing."], ["一鬥二升", "one dǒu and two shēng", "斗与升", "方文使用古代容量单位，斗和升在这里是相对大小的单位。读本保留原单位。", "Dǒu and shēng are historical measures of volume. The translation retains their names from the source."], ["善噫", "frequent belching", "噫", "这里的「噫」是嗳气，「善」表示容易出现。它与上一处的「太息」是不同表现。", "Here 噫 means belching, distinct from the deep sighing described earlier."], ["不能勝衣", "unable to bear even clothing", "不能胜衣", "「胜」读 shèng，意为承受。原句以连衣服都像承受不住来形容沉重乏力的感觉。", "Shèng means to bear or endure here. The phrase vividly describes how heavy and weary the body feels."], ["浮弦而數", "floating, wiry, rapid pulse", "浮、弦、数", "同一句可用多个词形容一个脉。浮说深浅，弦形容绷紧如弦的感觉，数说速度。", "Floating describes depth, wiry the taut feel, and rapid the pace. All three can describe the same pulse."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-13", "page": 2, "title": "Wind Damage", "english": ["The record lists guìzhī, one and a half liǎng; sháoyào, one and a half liǎng; processed gāncǎo, one liǎng; cháihú, four liǎng; rinsed bànxià, two and a half gě; rénshēn, huángqín, and fresh ginger, one and a half liǎng each; and six split jujubes.", "The historical directions reduce seven shēng of water to three, discard the dregs, and describe three warm servings of one shēng in a day."], "notes": [["二合半", "two and a half gě", "合", "这是古代容量单位，读 gě。此处用于登记半夏的量，译文保留原单位。", "Gě is a historical measure of volume. The translation keeps the source unit for the quantity of bànxià."], ["去滓", "discard the dregs", "去滓", "「滓」是煎煮后留下的渣滓。读方文时，从「煮取」到「去滓」是相邻的两步。", "Dregs are what remains after simmering. Reducing the liquid and removing the solids are consecutive actions in the record."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-11", "title": "Dampness Diseases", "page": 1, "english": ["The text describes dampness as moving through four regions: within and without, above and below. As it combines with other pathogenic influences, the illness takes different forms. The reader is urged to distinguish and address them by the principles that follow.", "For dampness above after exposure to mist and dew, the text describes headache, a stiff neck, pain at both sides of the forehead, and a floating, rough pulse. It assigns Astragalus, Cinnamon Twig, Poria, and Asarum Decoction.", "Astragalus, Cinnamon Twig, Poria, and Asarum Decoction", "Astragalus 3 liǎng, cinnamon twig 2 liǎng, poria 3 liǎng, and asarum 1 liǎng.", "For these four ingredients, the recorded preparation starts with 5 shēng of water and reduces it to 3 shēng. The liquid is strained. The text lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "For dampness below after exposure to cold water, the text describes heaviness from the waist down, swollen feet, and a deep, rough pulse. It assigns Cinnamon Twig, Poria, White Atractylodes, and Asarum Decoction.", "Cinnamon Twig, Poria, White Atractylodes, and Asarum Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, poria 4 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, and asarum 2 liǎng.", "For these four ingredients, the recorded preparation reduces 6 shēng of water to 2 shēng and strains the liquid. The text lists 1 shēng warm twice a day.", "When dampness is at the exterior and interacts with wind, the text says it flows into the channels and networks. The joints ache, the person lies down and has no desire to eat, and the pulse is floating and relaxed but rough under pressure. The text assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction to bring on light sweating so that wind and dampness leave together. For aversion to cold, body aches, numbness in the limbs, and a floating, fine, tight pulse, it identifies cold qi and assigns Equal Parts Cinnamon Twig and Ephedra Decoction.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng (outer layer removed), peony root 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text calls for coarsely crushing the five ingredients, simmering in 7 shēng of water over low heat until 3 shēng remain, straining, and taking 1 shēng at a comfortable temperature.", "After a short interval, the text describes sipping a little over 1 shēng of hot, thin rice gruel to support the decoction, then keeping warmly covered for about one time period. It seeks a faint sheen of sweat over the whole body. It says sweat streaming like water will leave the illness unresolved.", "If sweating follows the first serving and the illness resolves, the text says to stop even if some of the preparation remains. With no sweat it repeats the procedure, then shortens the intervals if needed so that the three servings fall within roughly half a day.", "For severe illness the text describes dosing over a day and a night, observing the whole cycle. If the signs remain after a complete batch, it describes preparing another.", "If sweating still does not occur, the text mentions using up to two or three batches. It lists raw and cold foods, sticky and slippery foods, meat and flour-based foods, the five pungent foods, wine and fermented dairy, and foul-smelling foods among the prohibitions.", "Ephedra Decoction", "Ephedra 3 liǎng (joints removed), cinnamon twig 3 liǎng (outer layer removed), roasted licorice 1 liǎng, and 70 apricot kernels (skins and tips removed).", "The text starts with 9 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces the liquid to 2½ shēng before straining. It lists 8 hé warm and covering to obtain slight sweat. It omits gruel and otherwise follows the aftercare for Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Equal Parts Cinnamon Twig and Ephedra Decoction", "The text combines 3 hé of Cinnamon Twig Decoction with 3 hé of Ephedra Decoction for 6 hé taken at once. It follows the aftercare for Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "When dampness is within and interacts with the spleen system, the text says it produces fullness in the middle. When stomach cold accompanies it, it turns into diarrhea. The text assigns White Atractylodes, Poria, and Magnolia Bark Decoction for the fullness and Regulate the Middle Decoction for diarrhea.", "White Atractylodes, Poria, and Magnolia Bark Decoction", "White atractylodes 3 liǎng, poria 4 liǎng, and roasted magnolia bark 2 liǎng (outer layer removed).", "The text reduces 5 shēng of water with the three ingredients to 1 shēng and 5 hé, strains the liquid, and divides it into two warm servings.", "Regulate the Middle Decoction", "Ginseng 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, and roasted licorice 3 liǎng.", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains the liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "If dampness encroaches upward on the lung system, the text calls the result lung cold and assigns Minor Blue Dragon Decoction. If it shifts downward to the kidney system, the text associates it with dribbling urination and assigns Five-Ingredient Poria Powder. If it spreads into the flesh, the text associates it with yellow discoloration and swelling and assigns Ephedra and Poria Decoction.", "Minor Blue Dragon Decoction", "Ephedra 3 liǎng (joints removed), peony root 3 liǎng, asarum 3 liǎng, cinnamon twig 3 liǎng (outer layer removed), dried ginger 3 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), roasted licorice 3 liǎng, and schisandra fruit ½ shēng."], "notes": [["濕氣為病", "dampness as moving through four regions", "湿气为病", "本篇开头用内、外、上、下四处概括湿气可以显现的位置。", "The opening organizes classical dampness patterns by four locations: interior, exterior, upper, and lower."], ["脈浮而澀", "a floating, rough pulse", "浮而涩", "浮说脉容易在浅处摸到，涩说脉来不流利。这是两个不同角度的描述。", "Floating describes depth. Rough describes how smoothly the beat feels under the fingers."], ["右四味", "For these four ingredients", "右四味", "方后说的「右」指上文所列药味。后接水量、煎取量和服法。", "The formula instruction refers back to the four ingredients just listed, then gives the liquid amounts and serving method."], ["㕮咀", "coarsely crushing", "㕮咀", "方书中的加工用语，这里指先把药材粗碎后再煎。", "In formula instructions this refers to breaking the ingredients into coarse pieces before decocting."], ["漐漐", "a faint sheen of sweat", "漐漐", "形容皮肤微润。下一小句说汗如流水，是作者在本段所区分的另一种程度。", "The expression conveys slight moisture on the skin. The following clause distinguishes it from sweat streaming like water."], ["八合", "8 hé", "合与升", "合、升在方中都是古量名，十合为一升。英译保留拼音以便对读。", "Both are historical volume units. Ten hé make one shēng. The translation keeps their names for comparison with the Chinese."], ["將息", "aftercare", "将息", "在方后是服药后调护、休养的意思。这里承用桂枝汤法所写的护理方式。", "This means care and rest after the preparation is taken. The directions refer the reader back to the Cinnamon Twig Decoction procedure."], ["中滿", "fullness in the middle", "中满", "「中」指中部脾胃区域。「满」写胀满感，下一小句另讲泄泻。", "Middle refers to the central region associated with the spleen and stomach. Fullness describes a sensation of distention."], ["淋瀝", "dribbling urination", "淋沥", "描述小便点滴而出。这里是作者把湿向下移与排尿变化相连的用词。", "The phrase describes urine passing in drops. The text associates it with dampness shifting downward."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-11", "title": "Dampness Diseases", "page": 2, "english": ["For the eight ingredients, the text starts with 1 dǒu of water, boils the ephedra first until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng before straining. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "Five-Ingredient Poria Powder", "Polyporus 18 zhū (outer layer removed), alisma 1 liǎng and 6 zhū, poria 18 zhū, cinnamon twig ½ liǎng (outer layer removed), and white atractylodes 18 zhū.", "The text pounds the five ingredients into powder and describes taking a square-inch spoonful mixed in plain rice drink three times a day, with plenty of warm water. It takes sweating as a sign of recovery.", "Ephedra and Poria Decoction", "Ephedra 2 liǎng (joints removed), poria 3 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, fangji 1 liǎng, and adzuki beans 1 shēng.", "The text starts with 7 shēng of water, briefly boils the ephedra first, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng before straining. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "When dampness spreads into the channels and networks and interacts with heat, the text says it produces abscesses with pus. When the spleen and stomach systems are already cold and dampness has lingered, the text describes retained water and fluid. If dampness interacts with dryness, it describes phlegm and retained fluid, which it classifies with retained-fluid disorders.", "In Taiyang illness with distressing joint pain and a deep, fine pulse, the text calls the pattern damp impediment. It describes difficulty urinating despite easy bowel movements and singles out promoting urination as the approach.", "For illness characterized by dampness, the text describes pain throughout the body, fever, and a smoky yellow cast to the skin.", "In illness characterized by dampness, sweating may be confined to the head. The back is stiff, and the person wants to wrap up and face a fire. The text says early purging brings hiccups or retching, chest fullness, and difficulty urinating. It reads a slippery coating on the tongue as a sign of heat in the cinnabar field and cold in the chest. The person wants water but cannot drink, and the mouth is dry and uncomfortable.", "The text predicts death if purging in this pattern is followed by sweating on the forehead, slight panting, and easy urination. It also predicts death if diarrhea continues without stopping.", "A question: Wind and dampness interacting cause pain throughout the body, and sweating is expected to resolve it. When the sky is overcast and rain goes on without stopping, a physician induces sweat. Why does the illness persist? The teacher replied that when sweat comes out profusely, wind leaves but dampness stays. With barely a hint of perspiration, the text says wind and dampness leave together.", "In illness characterized by dampness, the body may ache throughout, with fever, a yellow face, panting, headache, nasal blockage, and distress. The pulse is large, yet the person can eat and drink and the abdomen is undisturbed. The text places cold and dampness in the head and attributes the nasal blockage to them. It describes recovery after applying medicine inside the nose.", "Formula for Nasal Blockage", "Cattail ash, asarum, honeylocust pod, and ephedra.", "The text powders equal parts of the four ingredients, mixes them, and describes applying a small amount inside the nose. Sneezing is taken as a sign of relief.", "For distressing body aches in illness characterized by dampness, the text assigns Ephedra Decoction with Atractylodes to induce sweating. It expressly cautions against forcing sweat with fire.", "Ephedra Decoction with Atractylodes", "Ephedra 3 liǎng (joints removed), cinnamon twig 2 liǎng (outer layer removed), roasted licorice 1 liǎng, white atractylodes 4 liǎng, and 70 apricot kernels (skins and tips removed).", "The text starts with 9 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 2½ shēng before straining. It describes 8 hé warm with covering to obtain light sweating, repeating if needed and stopping when sweat appears.", "The text calls pain throughout the body with fever that worsens toward late afternoon wind-dampness. It attributes it to exposure to wind while sweating or to prolonged exposure to cold in an effort to cool down. It assigns Ephedra, Apricot Kernel, Job’s Tears, and Licorice Decoction.", "Ephedra, Apricot Kernel, Job’s Tears, and Licorice Decoction", "Ephedra 1 liǎng, 20 apricot kernels (skins and tips removed), Job’s tears 1 liǎng, and roasted licorice 1 liǎng.", "The text starts with 6 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng before straining. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "For wind-dampness with a floating pulse, a heavy body, sweating, and aversion to wind, the text assigns Fangji and Astragalus Decoction.", "Fangji and Astragalus Decoction", "Fangji 2 liǎng, roasted licorice 1 liǎng, white atractylodes 1 liǎng, astragalus 2 liǎng, fresh ginger 1 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes."], "notes": [["一鬥", "1 dǒu", "斗", "方书中的容量单位，一斗为十升。这里保存古方所列单位。", "A historical unit of volume. One dǒu equals ten shēng."], ["方寸匙", "square-inch spoonful", "方寸匙", "散剂所用的古量名，以方寸大小的匙取药。这里的「寸」按古量理解。", "A historical powder measure named for a spoon about one cùn square."], ["飲家", "retained-fluid disorders", "饮家", "「饮」在这里指古书归类的停留体液。「家」常用来称有某类病候的人。", "Here 飲 is a classical category of retained fluid. 家 commonly designates people characterized by a given pattern."], ["濕痹", "damp impediment", "湿痹", "「痹」是古病类名称。本句借关节痛、脉沉细以及二便的反常说明本条怎样识别湿痹。", "The line describes this classical impediment pattern through joint pain, a deep fine pulse, and differences between urination and bowel movements."], ["丹田", "cinnabar field", "丹田", "古书用来指身体某一区域的名称。本句把丹田有热和胸中有寒并列，便于看出部位有别。", "A classical name for a region of the body. Here the text places heat there and cold in the chest."], ["微微似欲汗出", "barely a hint of perspiration", "微汗", "本段把刚要出汗的程度和大汗相区分，用来解释作者认为风湿怎样同去。", "The degree of sweating is the focus of the teacher’s answer. Slight moisture is distinguished from profuse sweating."], ["火攻", "forcing sweat with fire", "火攻", "这里指用火热强行使人发汗。原文把它与前面所写的发汗方法分别看待。", "This refers to forceful heating to induce sweating. The text cautions against it in this pattern."], ["日晡所劇", "worsens toward late afternoon", "日晡所", "「日晡」指下午较晚的时段。「剧」说明症状在这时加重。", "Rìbū is the later part of the afternoon. The phrase says the signs become more severe at that time."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-11", "title": "Dampness Diseases", "page": 3, "english": ["For the six ingredients, the text starts with 1 dǒu of water, reduces it to 5 shēng and strains it, then simmers again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "For panting, the text adds 5 fēn of ephedra. For disharmony in the stomach, 3 fēn of peony root. For qi rushing upward, 3 fēn of cinnamon twig. For longstanding cold below, 3 fēn of asarum.", "After the preparation is taken, the text describes a crawling sensation under the skin and icy cold below the waist. It then describes sitting on a quilt with another wrapped around the body, and takes a light sweat from the warmth as a sign of improvement.", "On the eighth or ninth day of cold damage, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig and Aconite Decoction when wind and dampness interact, the person cannot turn onto their side unaided, there is neither vomiting nor thirst, and the pulse is floating, forceless, and rough. If stool is hard and urine passes freely, it assigns White Atractylodes and Aconite Decoction.", "Cinnamon Twig and Aconite Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 4 liǎng (outer layer removed), 2 processed aconite roots (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), roasted licorice 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng, strains the liquid, and divides it into three warm servings.", "White Atractylodes and Aconite Decoction", "White atractylodes 1 liǎng, 1 processed aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), roasted licorice 2 liǎng, fresh ginger 1½ liǎng, and 6 split jujubes.", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 1 shēng, strains the liquid, and divides it into three warm servings. It describes bodily numbness after the first serving, another serving roughly half a day later, and a clouded feeling after all three. It interprets this feeling as the atractylodes and aconite moving through the skin and pursuing water qi before it has cleared.", "When wind and dampness interact, the text describes distressing joint pain with pulling sensations. The joints cannot bend or straighten, and approaching them makes the pain more severe. It adds sweating, shortness of breath, difficult urination, aversion to wind, reluctance to remove clothing, and sometimes slight swelling. It assigns Licorice and Aconite Decoction.", "Licorice and Aconite Decoction", "Roasted licorice 2 liǎng, 2 processed aconite roots (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), white atractylodes 2 liǎng, and cinnamon twig 4 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains the liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day and takes a slight sweat after the first serving as a sign of resolution. If the person can eat and there is sweating but renewed agitation, it lists 5 hé. It gives 6 or 7 hé where 1 shēng is considered too much."], "notes": [["再煎", "simmers again", "再煎", "此方先煎到五升，去渣后继续煎液，最后取三升。", "The first reduction leaves five shēng. The strained liquid is then reduced further to three."], ["陳寒", "longstanding cold below", "陈寒", "「陈」说停留已久。本句把下部久有的寒作为加味所对应的一种情形。", "陳 conveys something longstanding. The text lists longstanding cold in the lower region among the patterns that change the formula."], ["不能自轉側", "cannot turn onto their side unaided", "不能自转侧", "「自」指出需要借助才能翻身。这是本段说明活动受限的一项表现。", "The person cannot turn onto their side without help. This is one of the signs of restricted movement in the passage."], ["炮", "fire-processed", "炮", "这是附子下注明的古代加工用语，与去皮、破片同列。", "This historical preparation term appears among the aconite processing instructions, alongside peeling and breaking into pieces."], ["如冒狀", "a clouded feeling", "如冒状", "「冒」在这里形容像被遮蔽的昏蒙感。古文接着按自身的药物运行理论解释这种感觉。", "The image is of being covered or clouded. The author explains the sensation through the text’s theory of how the ingredients move."], ["掣痛", "pulling sensations", "掣痛", "「掣」有牵拉之意。下一小句用关节不得屈伸说明疼痛伴随的活动困难。", "掣 conveys a pulling sensation. The next clause adds difficulty bending or straightening the joints."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-12", "title": "Dryness Damage", "page": 1, "english": ["In dryness damage, the text says the lung system is affected first. If the influence moves outward, it reaches the large intestine. As it passes to the five zang organ systems, it takes different forms. The reader is urged to distinguish and address these forms by attending closely to pulse and channel theory.", "In dryness illness with thirst, dry throat, panting, coughing, and chest fullness and pain, severe cases may bring blood in the sputum. When the pulse is floating, short, and tense, the text says pathogenic dryness has encroached on the lung system and assigns Bamboo Leaf, Gypsum, Apricot Kernel, and Licorice Decoction.", "Bamboo Leaf, Gypsum, Apricot Kernel, and Licorice Decoction", "Bamboo leaves 1 handful, gypsum ½ jīn, 30 apricot kernels (skins and tips removed), and licorice 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 5 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains the liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "If the influence shifts to the large intestine, the text describes difficult bowel movements, thirst with a desire for a hot drink, and a tense, large pulse at the lower position. It assigns Hemp Seed and White Honey Decoction.", "Hemp Seed and White Honey Decoction", "Hemp seed 1 shēng and white honey 6 hé.", "The text first boils the hemp seed in 4 shēng of water, reduces it to 1 shēng and 5 hé, strains the liquid, then adds the honey. It brings the mixture to a gentle boil, blends it, lets it cool slightly, and records taking it at once.", "In dryness illness with mouth sores, upward counterflow of qi, chest pain, and a large, rough pulse, the text says pathogenic dryness has encroached on the heart system and assigns Gardenia, Forsythia, Licorice, and Trichosanthes Decoction.", "Gardenia, Forsythia, Licorice, and Trichosanthes Decoction", "14 split gardenia fruits, forsythia fruit 2 liǎng, licorice 2 liǎng, and trichosanthes root 4 liǎng.", "The text reduces 7 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains the liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In dryness illness with red eyes, distress, dry throat, pain beneath the ribs, and a wiry, rapid pulse, the text says pathogenic dryness has encroached on the liver system. It assigns Scutellaria, Moutan Bark, Trichosanthes, Pinellia, and Immature Bitter Orange Decoction.", "Scutellaria, Moutan Bark, Trichosanthes, Pinellia, and Immature Bitter Orange Decoction", "Scutellaria 3 liǎng, moutan bark 2 liǎng, 1 large trichosanthes fruit (crushed), pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and 2 immature bitter oranges.", "The text reduces 5 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng and strains the liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In dryness illness with a yellow complexion, abdominal pain that cannot bear pressure, difficult bowel movements, and a rapid, slippery pulse, the text says pathogenic dryness has encroached on the spleen system and assigns White Tiger Decoction.", "White Tiger Decoction", "Anemarrhena rhizome 6 liǎng, gypsum 1 jīn (crushed and wrapped in cotton), roasted licorice 2 liǎng, and non-glutinous rice 6 hé.", "The text boils the four ingredients in 1 dǒu of water until the rice is cooked, then strains the finished liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In dryness illness with a dry, sore throat, tight pain in the lower abdomen, reddish urine, and a deep, tense pulse, the text says pathogenic dryness has shifted to the kidney system and assigns Rehmannia, Phellodendron Bark, Poria, and Trichosanthes Decoction.", "Rehmannia, Phellodendron Bark, Poria, and Trichosanthes Decoction", "Rehmannia root 6 liǎng, phellodendron bark 3 liǎng, poria 3 liǎng, and trichosanthes root 4 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains the liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day."], "notes": [["傷燥", "dryness damage", "伤燥", "「燥」在这里是古书所说能致病的一类影响。开头以肺、大肠及五藏分写它的不同去向。", "Dryness is a class of pathogenic influence in the text. The opening follows its movement through the lung, large intestine, and zang systems."], ["五臟", "five zang organ systems", "五藏", "传统所说肝、心、脾、肺、肾五个系统。本篇按受燥所影响的系统排列不同表现。", "The five systems are liver, heart, spleen, lung, and kidney. The chapter groups signs according to the system said to be affected."], ["唾血", "blood in the sputum", "唾血", "「唾」指吐出唾液或痰。此处和前面的咳喘连读，是作者列出的加重表现。", "Read this in the context of the cough and panting that precede it. The text presents blood in what is spat up as a sign of greater severity."], ["半斤", "½ jīn", "斤", "斤是古方里的重量单位。英译用 jīn 保存原单位，便于与同篇的两、升对照。", "Jīn is a historical unit of weight. Its name is retained alongside the other original measures."], ["頓服", "taking it at once", "顿服", "「顿」指一次服完这次所列的一份。同篇其他条文常把药液分多次服。", "This means taking the stated preparation in one serving. Many of the other entries spread their servings across the day."], ["氣上逆", "upward counterflow of qi", "气上逆", "「逆」指偏离作者所理解的正常方向。句中「上」明确指出逆行是向上的。", "Counterflow is movement against the usual direction in the text’s account. The word 上 identifies the movement as upward."], ["栝蔞實", "trichosanthes fruit", "栝蒌实与栝蒌根", "同篇有方用栝蒌实，也有方用栝蒌根。「实」在这里指果实，「根」指根，读配方时要区分。", "Some formulas use the fruit and others the root. The ingredient lines specify which part of the plant the text records."], ["粳米", "non-glutinous rice", "粳米", "白虎汤本条列粳米为一味，并把米熟作为煎煮完成的标志。", "The formula includes non-glutinous rice and marks the end of cooking by when the rice is done."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-1", "page": 1, "title": "Preface Attributed to Zhang Zhongjing", "english": ["The treatise begins: Whenever I read how Yueren examined the patient at Guo and how he looked at the complexion of the Marquis of Qi, I have never failed to marvel at his aptitude. Yet men of standing today seldom turn their attention to medicine or investigate its arts: to treat sovereign and parents above them, relieve the plight of the poor below them, and preserve their own bodies and nurture life. They pursue honor and power, follow in the footsteps of the mighty, and labor solely for reputation and profit. They polish the branches and neglect the root, making the outside splendid while the inside withers. If the skin is gone, what will the hair cling to? Then a harmful wind strikes, an uncommon illness takes hold, calamity arrives, and only then do they tremble. They lower their ambitions, yield their dignity, turn to ritual specialists, give themselves over to fate, and accept defeat with folded hands. Carrying the precious vessel of a hundred years of life, they entrust it to an ordinary practitioner and let him do as he will. Alas! Once the body has perished and the spirit faded, they become something other, hidden beneath the deep springs, with only weeping left behind. How painful! People wander in bewilderment and do not awaken. If they value life so little, what is all that honor worth? Unable to care for or know others in public, or to care for or know themselves in private, they meet calamity in a daze, inert as wandering souls. Such are those who chase influence and splendor, fail to secure the root, and forget the body in pursuit of possessions. Their danger is like that of standing over an icy ravine.", "My clan was large, once numbering over two hundred. Since the beginning of the Jiàn'ān era, fewer than ten harvests have passed, yet two-thirds have died. Cold damage accounted for seven in ten of those deaths. Grieving for these losses and for premature deaths I could not avert, I diligently sought ancient teachings and collected formulas widely. I drew on the Basic Questions, the Nine Scrolls, the Eighty-One Difficulties, the Great Treatise on Yin and Yang, and the Táilú Record of Medicinals, together with the methods of assessing the pulse and distinguishing patterns. In this way I assembled the Treatise on Cold Damage and Miscellaneous Diseases in sixteen scrolls. Though it cannot resolve every illness, I hope it helps readers discern its source from its signs. Those who investigate what I collected will already be well on their way.", "Heaven arrays the Five Phases and sets the myriad kinds in motion. People receive the five constant qualities and have the five zang organs. Channels and networks, organ and transport sites, yin and yang, all communicate. Their intricacies are obscure and their transformations inexhaustible. How could anyone without great aptitude and subtle judgment fathom them? In high antiquity there were Shennong, the Yellow Emperor, Qibo, Bogao, Leigong, Shaoyu, Shaoshi, and Zhongwen; in later ages, Changsang and Bianque; under the Han, Gongcheng Yangqing and Canggong. After those, I have heard of none to match them. Physicians now seldom seek the intent of the classics or extend what they know. Each carries on a family skill and follows old routines. In questioning the sick, they prize quick talk. After a brief encounter they dispense decoctions, feeling the inch without reaching the cubit and grasping the hand without attending to the foot. They do not compare rényíng, fūyáng, and the three positions. Their count of pulses through the breaths does not reach fifty. They pronounce upon a short span without knowing how to decide a diagnosis. Of the nine sites they have scarcely a notion; míngtáng and quètíng go unexamined. It is a view through a tube. To distinguish life from death is truly difficult. Confucius said that knowing from birth ranks highest and learning comes next; broad hearing and knowledge give another way to knowing. I have long esteemed these arts and would put these words into practice.", "Preface by Zhang Ji of Nanyang, Han Administrator of Changsha."], "notes": [["越人入虢", "Yueren examined the patient at Guo", "越人", "越人通常指秦越人，也就是扁鹊。开头借古代名医的故事引出作者对当时医术的感慨。", "Yueren refers to Qin Yueren, better known as Bianque. This story introduces the preface's contrast between past and present."], ["建安紀元", "Jiàn'ān era", "建安", "建安是东汉末年的年号。原文用它作为回顾族人去世经过的时间起点。", "Jiàn'ān is a late Eastern Han era name. The preface counts the clan's losses from its beginning."], ["十稔", "ten harvests", "稔", "稔指庄稼成熟，这里借收成次数记年。", "A harvest marks a year in this expression."], ["十居其七", "seven in ten of those deaths", "十居其七", "这句话接在「死亡者，三分有二」之后，七成所指的是已经去世的人。", "The denominator is those who died, following the claim that two-thirds of the clan had died."], ["按寸不及尺", "feeling the inch without reaching the cubit", "寸与尺", "这两个字指手腕上的切脉部位。作者批评只顾寸而忽略尺。", "Inch and cubit refer to named positions at the wrist. The criticism is of examining one while overlooking the other."], ["窺管", "a view through a tube", "窥管", "像隔着管子看，只看到很窄的一部分。作者用它比喻观察不全面。", "Looking through a tube is a metaphor for examining too little of the available evidence."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-2", "page": 1, "title": "Preface by Zuo Shengde of Guilin", "english": ["I heard my teacher Zhang Shaozu say: 'Our family tradition tells of thirteen drafts of the work on cold damage. Each draft was widely copied. What survives with us is the twelfth. The others must have been kept within branches of the family or lost to fire and calamity. According to tradition, Shuhe received the seventh draft. Compared with ours, it has omissions and a different arrangement. Some say Shuhe altered it; others suspect additions and deletions by Song editors. Each party holds to its story. But in Jin times the text still circulated in manuscript. Hand-copying was not replaced by printing until the late Tang and early Song. The text in current circulation, then, may be neither a Han manuscript nor even Shuhe's own draft.' At first I doubted this. Then I read the General Principles of Cold Damage. It includes the methods of when sweating, vomiting, and purging were or were not considered appropriate, yet does not repeat clauses already set out under the six divisions. Its arrangement is orderly. I came to think that the separate printed chapters on those procedures and on what follows them were added by later readers who organized material for study without attending to the plan of the book. If Confucius' students asked so often about humaneness and government, why would they not collect each topic with its own kind rather than repeat themselves?", "My teacher's personal name was Xuezheng. He described himself as a forty-sixth-generation descendant of Zhongjing. He said the family had moved more than once since the Jin. His great-great-grandfather Fuchu returned from Lingnan to the ancestral region, settled at Guāngzhōu (光州), and the family gathered there. My teacher inherited the family's learning but was not known publicly as a physician. He rarely showed the book to anyone. That I obtained it as his student seemed almost providential. I had long loved these arts and learned acupuncture and moxibustion from Deng Xianzhang of Yongchuan. Later I accompanied my late father when he took office in Lingnan. My father and my teacher were fellow officials and often visited. Seeing me with the Song version of the Treatise on Cold Damage, my teacher smiled and asked whether I liked it. I was barely twenty. I said I was still trying to grasp its essentials. He said that since I liked learning and knew needling and moxibustion, I could read it. His family had transmitted a sixteen-scroll manuscript of the Treatise on Cold Damage and Miscellaneous Diseases, never shown to outsiders. Fearing the thread would break if he failed to pass it to a suitable person, he told me the history of the book and of his family. My father urged me to bow at once, and there I became his pupil.", "Now Luo Zhechu, a well-known man of our district, has studied acupuncture and moxibustion with me for years and shares my interests. I have passed on all I received. As I did not fail my teacher, I trust Luo will not fail me. I write this preface to record the book's beginnings. May Luo keep it in mind and persevere in his work.", "Preface written by Zuo Shengde of Guilin in the third month of the jiǎwǔ year, the twentieth year of Guangxu."], "notes": [["一十三稿", "thirteen drafts", "一十三稿", "左盛德在这里转述师门传说。它可作为了解该序如何叙述传本的材料。", "The preface reports a lineage tradition about thirteen drafts. It tells us how the preface presents the book's transmission."], ["叔和", "Shuhe", "叔和", "这里指王叔和。序文借他所见稿本与家传稿本的比较，解释篇次为何不同。", "Shuhe refers to Wang Shuhe. The comparison with a family manuscript is the preface's explanation for a different order of the text."], ["傳抄", "widely copied", "传抄与刊刻", "传抄指手抄递传；刊刻指刻版印刷。序文把两种流传方式拿来说明文本可能发生变化。", "Manuscript copying and print publication are the two modes of transmission contrasted in the preface."], ["光州", "Guāngzhōu (光州)", "光州", "字面是「光」州，和今天常见的「广」州用字不同。", "Guāngzhōu is written 光州 here. Its first character differs from 廣州, the modern city of Guangzhou."], ["弱冠", "barely twenty", "弱冠", "古代男子二十岁行冠礼，「弱冠」因此用来形容二十岁上下。", "The coming-of-age cap ceremony traditionally marked age twenty for men; the idiom refers to that age."], ["光緒二十年", "twentieth year of Guangxu", "光绪二十年", "这是清末年号纪年，对应公元 1894 年。日期在左序末尾。", "The twentieth year of Guangxu corresponds to 1894 and dates Zuo's preface."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "page": 1, "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "english": ["A method for judging illness by the four seasons, eight seasonal divisions, twenty-four solar terms, and seventy-two five-day periods.", "Month 1. The opening term is Beginning of Spring, with the Dipper pointing to Gen. The central term is Rain Water, with the Dipper pointing to Yin.\nMonth 2. The opening term is Awakening of Insects, with the Dipper pointing to Jia. The central term is Spring Equinox, with the Dipper pointing to Mao.\nMonth 3. The opening term is Clear and Bright, with the Dipper pointing to Yi. The central term is Grain Rain, with the Dipper pointing to Chen.\nMonth 4. The opening term is Beginning of Summer, with the Dipper pointing to Xun. The central term is Lesser Fullness, with the Dipper pointing to Si.\nMonth 5. The opening term is Grain in Ear, with the Dipper pointing to Bing. The central term is Summer Solstice, with the Dipper pointing to Wu.\nMonth 6. The opening term is Lesser Heat, with the Dipper pointing to Ding. The central term is Greater Heat, with the Dipper pointing to Wei.\nMonth 7. The opening term is Beginning of Autumn, with the Dipper pointing to Kun. The central term is End of Heat, with the Dipper pointing to Shen.\nMonth 8. The opening term is White Dew, with the Dipper pointing to Geng. The central term is Autumn Equinox, with the Dipper pointing to You.\nMonth 9. The opening term is Cold Dew, with the Dipper pointing to Xin. The central term is Frost Descent, with the Dipper pointing to Xu.\nMonth 10. The opening term is Beginning of Winter, with the Dipper pointing to Qian. The central term is Lesser Snow, with the Dipper pointing to Hai.\nMonth 11. The opening term is Greater Snow, with the Dipper pointing to Ren. The central term is Winter Solstice, with the Dipper pointing to Zi.\nMonth 12. The opening term is Lesser Cold, with the Dipper pointing to Gui. The central term is Greater Cold, with the Dipper pointing to Chou.", "The twenty-four solar terms consist of twelve opening terms and twelve central terms. In both groups, every five days makes one period, giving seventy-two in all. The text considers it essential to understand this system thoroughly in order to judge whether a patient will live or die.", "The Great Treatise on Yin and Yang says that spring is mild and warm, summer is hot, autumn is clear and cool, and winter is bitterly cold. This is the normal sequence of seasonal qi. In severe winter cold, living things withdraw into shelter. The prudent person takes careful precautions and escapes harm from cold. Exposure to it may bring on what the text calls cold damage. In this account, exposure to the qi of any of the four seasons can give rise to illness.", "The text singles out cold because it considers it the strongest and harshest of these influences. Illness that begins immediately after exposure is called cold damage. Otherwise, the text holds, harmful cold lies concealed in the skin and flesh and later becomes warm disease in spring or summerheat disease in summer. The heat of summerheat disease is more severe. The text explains the warm and hot illnesses common among people who labor under harsh conditions in spring and summer by their winter exposure, distinguishing these cases from illness due to an unseasonable prevailing influence.", "An unseasonable prevailing influence occurs when spring should be warm but is severely cold, summer should be hot but is markedly cool, autumn should be cool but is intensely hot, or winter should be cold but is unusually warm. The climate of one season arrives at the wrong time. When young and old fall ill in similar ways in the same year, the text attributes it to this prevailing influence.", "To assess illness attributed to normal seasonal qi or to unseasonable epidemic qi, the text consults the Dipper calendar. After Frost Descent in the ninth month, weather should gradually grow colder, becoming severely cold toward winter. It should ease after Rain Water in the first month. The name Rain Water refers to ice and snow melting into water. After Awakening of Insects in the second month, the weather should warm gradually, grow intensely hot toward summer, and cool in autumn.", "From Frost Descent until before Spring Equinox, a person who exposes themselves to frost and dew, is struck by cold, and falls ill immediately is said to have cold damage. The cold is still mild in the ninth and tenth months, and the text expects milder illness. The cold is severe in the eleventh and twelfth months, and it expects more serious illness. When cold is easing in the first and second months, the illness is again described as mild. The passage attributes immediate illness to cold exposure when a person has not adjusted to winter conditions.", "Unseasonable warmth in winter is called winter warmth. The text distinguishes its harmful influence from cold damage. Episodes can occur in succession or overlap and vary in severity. The treatments also differ, as the patterns in a later chapter show.", "After Beginning of Spring, even without sudden severe cold, ice, or snow, some people fall ill with high fever. The text explains this as spring yang qi bringing out cold that had lain latent since winter and turning it into warm disease."], "notes": [["四時八節", "four seasons, eight seasonal divisions", "四时八节", "四时是春夏秋冬。八节通常指四立、两分和两至，是季节更替的八个节点。", "The eight divisions usually mean the four seasonal beginnings, two equinoxes, and two solstices."], ["立春正月節鬥指艮", "Beginning of Spring, with the Dipper pointing to Gen", "斗指艮", "「斗」指北斗，「艮」在这里标方向。这一段把各节气与斗柄所指相配。", "The Dipper means the Big Dipper, and Gen is a directional label. The paragraph pairs each solar term with a direction assigned to the Dipper's handle."], ["節有十二，中氣有十二", "twelve opening terms and twelve central terms", "节与中气", "二十四节气交替分为两组，一月一节、一中气。这里的月序是传统历法的月序。", "The terms alternate in two groups, with an opening term and a central term assigned to each month of the traditional calendar."], ["四時正氣之序", "normal sequence of seasonal qi", "四时正气", "这里指各季节本来应有的气候。春温、夏热、秋凉、冬寒就是本段给出的顺序。", "Here normal qi means the conditions expected in each season, from warmth in spring to cold in winter."], ["寒毒藏於肌膚", "harmful cold lies concealed in the skin and flesh", "寒毒藏于肌肤", "「毒」强调寒气在古书理论中致病的力量。「藏」解释为何冬天受寒，直到春夏才出现症状。", "Du emphasizes the harmful force attributed to cold. Concealment explains why the text links winter exposure with symptoms appearing in spring or summer."], ["凡時行者", "An unseasonable prevailing influence", "时行", "本段指不合季节而又普遍出现的气候。后面说这种情况出现时，老少的病多相似。", "The term describes a widespread weather pattern that occurs out of season. The text links it with similar illness among young and old."], ["名曰冬溫", "is called winter warmth", "冬温", "「非节之暖」说暖意出现在本来该冷的时候。下文进一步区分冬温发病的先后与轻重。", "Warmth occurs when the season ought to be cold. The text then says the resulting illnesses can vary in timing and severity."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "page": 2, "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "english": ["From after Spring Equinox until before Autumn Equinox, a sudden cold spell is classed as an unseasonable cold epidemic influence. If sudden cold occurs in the third or fourth month, yang qi is still weak. Cold checks it, but the resulting fever is relatively mild. In the fifth and sixth months yang qi is abundant, and if cold checks it then, the fever is severe. In the seventh and eighth months yang qi has waned, and the resulting fever is again slight. The illness resembles warm disease, though its treatment differs in the text. Each solar term spans fifteen days. Each of four seasons has six such terms, giving twenty-four in all.", "Seasonal conditions may fail to arrive on time, arrive too early, or arrive with excessive force. The text considers all three sources of illness. As Heaven and Earth move and rest and yin and yang exert their forces, each condition has its own proper time. Spring warmth develops into summer heat, and autumn harshness into winter severity. After Winter Solstice, one yang line rises and one yin line descends. After Summer Solstice, yang qi descends by one measure and yin qi rises by one. At the winter and summer solstices yin and yang meet, and at the spring and autumn equinoxes they separate. As yin and yang change places, people may undergo changes and fall ill. The prudent person nurtures yang in spring and summer and yin in autumn and winter, following firmness and yielding in Heaven and Earth. The careless person who exposes themselves may fall suddenly ill. The reader is urged to ascertain which channel holds the fierce pathogenic influence and which illness it produces. In the text's scheme, spring wind injury leads to summer diarrhea with undigested food, summerheat injury to autumn bouts of fever and chills, autumn damp injury to winter coughing, and winter cold injury to spring warm disease. The passage urges the reader to understand these relationships clearly.", "In treating cold damage, the classical approach selects a formula according to how superficial or deep the illness has become from day to day. People may delay treatment, receive unsuitable treatment, or consult a physician only after many days, when they are exhausted. Treatment likewise misses the illness if the physician fails to follow its course. The text calls for adjusting the prescription to the immediate circumstances and says this makes treatment effective. Regions differ in temperature and elevation. Materials differ in hardness and softness, and people differ in what they eat and where they live. An editorial note marks the character for eating as a variant in the Guilin text and questions it. The Yellow Emperor therefore asked about the four regions, and Qibo explained four methods of treatment to instruct later readers and clarify what they had yet to grasp. The practitioner is urged to weigh both the course of illness and local conditions.", "When cold damage passes along the channels, the text describes the resulting illness as febrile and says even intense fever can be survived. Cases with simultaneous cold affliction in paired channels, it says, often prove fatal.", "A floating pulse at both cubit and inch indicates illness in Taiyang, expected on the first or second day. Its channel runs upward to Fengfu, accounting for headache, pain at the nape, and stiffness in the lower back and spine.", "A long pulse at both cubit and inch indicates illness in Yangming, expected on the second or third day. Its channel runs beside the nose and connects with the eyes. The signs described are bodily heat, sweating, painful eyes, a dry nose, and inability to lie down.", "A wiry pulse at both cubit and inch indicates illness in Shaoyang, expected on the third or fourth day. Its channel follows the ribs and connects with the ears, accounting for pain in the chest and flanks and loss of hearing. For illness in any of these three channels that has yet to enter a fu organ, the text describes release through sweating.", "A deep, soft pulse at both cubit and inch indicates illness in Taiyin, expected on the fourth or fifth day. The channel spreads through the stomach and connects to the throat. The passage describes abdominal fullness and throat discomfort.", "A deep, fine pulse at both cubit and inch indicates illness in Shaoyin, expected on the fifth or sixth day. The channel passes through the kidney, connects with the lung, and attaches at the root of the tongue. The signs described are a dry mouth and tongue and thirst.", "A wiry, faint pulse at both cubit and inch indicates illness in Jueyin, expected on the sixth or seventh day. The channel follows the genitals and connects with the liver, accounting for distressing fullness and contraction of the scrotum. For illness in any of these three channels that has entered a fu organ, the text describes treatment through purging.", "When cold damage has passed to Taiyang, with a floating, tense, and rapid pulse, fever, no sweating, and agitation, the text assigns Mahuang Decoction.", "Formula for Mahuang Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang, outer layer removed. Gancao (licorice), 1 liang, roasted. Xingren (apricot kernels), 70, skins and tips removed."], "notes": [["時行寒疫", "unseasonable cold epidemic influence", "时行寒疫", "本句用春分后到秋分前的突发寒冷举例。「疫」表示古书把它与多人类似发病相联系。", "Sudden cold between the equinoxes is the example. Epidemic reflects the classical association with similar illness in many people."], ["一陽爻升，一陰爻降", "one yang line rises and one yin line descends", "阳爻与阴爻", "「爻」读 yáo，是传统卦象的一画。这里以升降写冬至后阴阳消长的趋势。", "A yao is a line in a traditional diagram. Rising and falling express seasonal changes in the balance of yin and yang after Winter Solstice."], ["飧泄", "diarrhea with undigested food", "飧泄", "「泄」是泄泻。「飧泄」用来形容大便中夹有未消化食物。", "The phrase describes diarrhea containing undigested food."], ["臨時消息制方", "adjusting the prescription to the immediate circumstances", "消息制方", "「消息」此处指斟酌增减。「制方」是拟定方药。", "Here xiaoxi means weighing circumstances and making adjustments. The last two characters refer to formulating a prescription."], ["傳經則為病熱", "passes along the channels", "传经", "这是古书借六经来描述病势传变的说法。后面依次给出太阳至厥阴各经的脉象和表现。", "Transmission through the six channels is the text's framework for progression. The next six passages list the pulse and signs from Taiyang through Jueyin."], ["兩感於寒而病者", "simultaneous cold affliction in paired channels", "两感于寒", "下一页之后会具体配对太阳与少阴、阳明与太阴、少阳与厥阴。", "The later account pairs Taiyang with Shaoyin, Yangming with Taiyin, and Shaoyang with Jueyin."], ["尺寸俱沉細", "deep, fine pulse at both cubit and inch", "尺寸俱沉细", "尺和寸是两个腕部脉位。「俱」说两处都一样。「沉」说深浅，「细」说脉体较窄。", "Cubit and inch are wrist positions, and ju means both. Deep describes where the beat is felt, and fine describes its narrow quality."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "page": 3, "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "english": ["For these four ingredients, the recipe calls for 9 sheng of water. The ephedra is boiled first until the volume drops by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed off. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 2 and a half sheng. The dregs are strained out, and the recipe records a warm dose of 8 he. The patient is covered to bring on a barely perceptible sweat. Rice gruel is unnecessary. The rest of the aftercare follows the method for Guizhi Decoction, given in a later volume.", "When the illness passes to Yangming, with a large, rapid pulse, fever, sweating, thirst, and a dry tongue, the text assigns White Tiger Decoction. If the illness fails to improve, it then assigns Chengqi Decoction.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction.", "Zhimu (anemarrhena rhizome), 6 liang. Shigao (gypsum), 1 jin. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Jingmi (nonglutinous rice), 6 he.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water and boils them until the rice is cooked. It strains out the dregs and records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "Formula for Major Chengqi Decoction.", "Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang, rinsed in wine. Houpo (magnolia bark), half a jin, roasted and stripped of its outer layer. Zhishi (immature bitter orange), 5 fruits. Mangxiao (mirabilite), 3 he.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water. The first two ingredients are boiled down to 5 sheng and the dregs strained out. The rhubarb is then added and the liquid boiled down to 2 sheng. After another straining the mirabilite is added and the liquid brought to a boil once or twice over low heat. The recipe records two warm doses, with the rest set aside once the bowels move.", "Formula for Minor Chengqi Decoction.", "Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang, rinsed in wine. Houpo (magnolia bark), 2 liang, roasted and stripped of its outer layer. Zhishi (immature bitter orange), 3 large fruits, roasted.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe takes 4 sheng of water, boils down to 1 sheng and 2 he, and strains out the dregs. It divides the liquid into two warm doses. The first is expected to produce a bowel movement. If that does not happen the remainder is taken. If it happens the remainder is set aside.", "Formula for Stomach-Regulating Chengqi Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Mangxiao (mirabilite), half a jin. Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang, rinsed in wine.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe boils two in 3 sheng of water until 1 sheng remains and strains out the dregs. The mirabilite is then added and the liquid brought to a boil once or twice over low heat. The recipe records taking the entire dose warm at once to regulate stomach qi.", "When the illness passes to Shaoyang, with a wiry, tense pulse, a bitter taste, dry throat, dizziness, visual vertigo, and alternating chills and fever with fever predominating, the text assigns Minor Chaihu Decoction. If the illness fails to improve, it assigns Major Chaihu Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Chaihu Decoction.", "Chaihu (bupleurum), half a jin. Huangqin (skullcap root), 3 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 3 liang, roasted. Dazao (jujubes), 12. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed.", "The recipe refers to seven ingredients. It uses 1 dou and 2 sheng of water, boils down to 6 sheng, strains out the dregs, and boils the liquid again to 3 sheng. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "Formula for Major Chaihu Decoction.", "Chaihu (bupleurum), half a jin. Huangqin (skullcap root), 3 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 5 liang, sliced. Zhishi (immature bitter orange), 4 fruits, roasted. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split. Dahuang (rhubarb), 2 liang.", "For these eight ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou and 2 sheng of water, boils down to 6 sheng, strains out the dregs, and boils the liquid again to 3 sheng. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "When the illness passes to Taiyin, with a soft, large pulse, fever, diarrhea, thirst, and cramping abdominal pain, the text assigns Fuling, Baizhu, Houpo, Shigao, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling, Baizhu, Houpo, Shigao, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction.", "Fuling (poria), 4 liang. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 3 liang. Houpo (magnolia bark), 4 liang. Shigao (gypsum), half a jin. Huangqin (skullcap root), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted.", "For these six ingredients, the recipe boils 1 dou of water down to 5 sheng. It records a dose of slightly more than 1 sheng and 5 he, three times daily.", "When the illness passes to Shaoyin, with a deep, fine, rapid pulse, hands and feet that alternate between reversal cold and heat, a painful throat, and difficulty urinating, the text assigns Fuzi, Xixin, Huanglian, and Huangqin Decoction.", "Formula for Fuzi, Xixin, Huanglian, and Huangqin Decoction.", "Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one large piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Xixin (asarum), 2 liang. Huanglian (coptis rhizome), 4 liang. Huangqin (skullcap root), 2 liang.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe boils 6 sheng of water down to 3 sheng and records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "When the illness passes to Jueyin, with a deep, wiry, tense pulse, fever with occasional shuddering, restlessness, and vomiting, the text assigns Guizhi and Danggui Decoction. If roundworms are vomited, it assigns Wumei Pill.", "Formula for Guizhi and Danggui Decoction.", "Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang. Danggui (Chinese angelica root), 3 liang. Banxia (pinellia), 1 sheng, washed. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Huangbai (phellodendron bark), 2 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted.", "For these six ingredients, the recipe uses 7 sheng of water, boils down to 4 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into three warm doses.", "Formula for Wumei Pill."], "notes": [["覆取微似汗", "covered to bring on a barely perceptible sweat", "微似汗", "这是古方服后护理的一环。「覆」指覆盖身体，「微似汗」形容汗意很轻。", "This is part of the recorded aftercare. The covering is described as bringing on just perceptible sweating."], ["不差與承氣湯", "If the illness fails to improve, it then assigns Chengqi Decoction", "不差与承气汤", "「差」在这里是病好转。「与」指给予。原文把承气汤列在白虎汤之后。", "Here cha means improvement and yu means to give. The text names Chengqi Decoction after White Tiger Decoction."], ["得下，餘勿服", "the rest set aside once the bowels move", "得下", "这是古方服法中的条件句。「得下」说大便已经排出，余药便不再服。", "This is a condition in the historical recipe. De xia means that the bowels have moved, at which point the remaining liquid is set aside."], ["初服當更衣", "The first is expected to produce a bowel movement", "更衣", "「更衣」字面是换衣，此处是大便的委婉说法。后两句根据有没有排便来区分。", "Gengyi literally means changing clothes. Here it is a euphemism for moving the bowels, which determines how the next two clauses are read."], ["往來寒熱，熱多寒少", "alternating chills and fever with fever predominating", "往来寒热", "「往来」写寒热交替。「热多寒少」补充说这组表现中，热更占主导。", "Wanglai means alternating chills and fever. The next phrase specifies that fever predominates."], ["右七味", "refers to seven ingredients", "右七味", "「右」指上面列的药。这一底本上面只列六味，而煎法写七味，两处文字有出入。", "The character 右 points to the ingredient list above. This edition lists six there while the preparation line counts seven."], ["手足時厥時熱", "hands and feet that alternate between reversal cold and heat", "时厥时热", "两个「时」写交替。「厥」在这里与热相对，指手脚发冷。", "The repeated word shi marks alternation. Jue is set against heat here and refers to cold hands and feet."], ["吐蛔者", "If roundworms are vomited", "吐蛔", "「蛔」指蛔虫。「吐蛔者」另列一种情形，并引出下一段的乌梅丸。", "Hui means roundworms. This clause introduces a separate case and leads into the heading for Wumei Pill."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-6", "page": 4, "title": "General Principles of Cold Damage", "english": ["Wumei (smoked ume plums), 300. Xixin (asarum), 6 liang. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 10 liang. Huanglian (coptis rhizome), 16 liang. Danggui (Chinese angelica root), 4 liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), 6 liang, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Shujiao (Sichuan pepper), 4 liang, heated until it sweats. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 6 liang, outer layer removed. Renshen (ginseng), 6 liang. Huangbai (phellodendron bark), 6 liang.", "The recipe gives ten ingredients to be pounded separately, sifted, and combined. It records soaking the plums in vinegar overnight, removing the pits, and steaming the plums beneath 5 dou of rice. Once the rice is cooked, the plums are pounded into a paste and blended with the other ingredients. The mixture is pounded in a mortar with honey for two thousand strokes and shaped into pills the size of Chinese parasol tree seeds. The source records swallowing ten pills with a drink before meals three times daily, increasing gradually to twenty. It lists raw, cold, slippery, and strong-smelling foods among the dietary restrictions.", "The preceding passages give the patterns and treatments the text considers standard as illness passes along the channels. Entry into a fu or zang organ counts as a changed pattern of channel transmission, with the treatments set out below.", "In simultaneous cold affliction, Taiyang receives cold on the first day and falls ill together with Shaoyin. The passage describes headache, dry mouth, distressing fullness, and thirst. The pulse is sometimes floating, sometimes deep, sometimes rapid, and sometimes fine. The text assigns Major Green Dragon Decoction with Fuzi.", "Formula for Major Green Dragon Decoction with Fuzi.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 6 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang, outer layer removed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Xingren (apricot kernels), 40, skins and tips removed. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 10, split. Shigao (gypsum), a piece the size of a hen's egg. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these eight ingredients, the recipe uses 9 sheng of water. It first boils the ephedra until the volume drops by 2 sheng and skims off the foam. The other ingredients are added, boiled down to 3 sheng, and strained. The recipe records a warm dose of 1 sheng to bring on barely perceptible sweating and describes applying warming powder if sweating is profuse. It directs that further doses stop if one dose brings on sweat. The text warns that repeating the dose can cause excessive sweating and loss of yang, followed by deficiency, aversion to wind, agitation, and inability to sleep.", "On the second day Yangming receives cold and falls ill together with Taiyin. The text describes abdominal fullness, bodily heat, lack of appetite, and delirious speech. The pulse is sometimes high and sometimes low, sometimes strong and sometimes weak. The passage assigns Dahuang, Shigao, Fuling, Baizhu, Zhishi, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Dahuang, Shigao, Fuling, Baizhu, Zhishi, and Gancao Decoction.", "Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang. Shigao (gypsum), 1 jin. Fuling (poria), 3 liang. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 4 liang. Zhishi (immature bitter orange), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 3 liang, roasted.", "For these six ingredients, the recipe uses 8 sheng of water, boils down to 5 sheng, and divides the liquid into three warm doses.", "On the third day Shaoyang receives cold and falls ill together with Jueyin. The text describes loss of hearing, contraction of the scrotum, reversal cold, and inability to take in even liquids. The pulse shifts abruptly between wiry and tense, fine and scattered. The passage assigns Danggui and Fuzi Decoction.", "Formula for Danggui and Fuzi Decoction.", "Danggui (Chinese angelica root), 4 liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one large piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Renshen (ginseng), 3 liang. Huanglian (coptis rhizome), 3 liang. Huangbai (phellodendron bark), 3 liang.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe boils 6 sheng of water down to 3 sheng and records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "The text calls these changed patterns of channel transmission and judges many untreatable. It predicts death on day six for a patient who no longer recognizes other people. If all three yin and all three yang channels, the five zang, and the six fu are affected, nutritive and defensive qi cease to circulate. The organs are cut off from one another, and death follows. The text asks twice whether this is what the saying means when it says there is no escaping death if cold afflicts paired channels.", "If no additional atypical influence intervenes, the text describes the Taiyang illness waning on day seven, with the headache easing a little. On day eight Yangming wanes and the bodily heat eases a little. On day nine Shaoyang wanes and some hearing returns. On day ten Taiyin wanes, abdominal fullness diminishes toward normal, and appetite returns. On day eleven Shaoyin wanes, thirst stops, the tongue remains dry, and sneezing follows. On day twelve Jueyin wanes, the scrotum relaxes, the lower abdomen settles a little, the strong pathogenic influence departs, and the patient's mind clears.", "If the illness still has not abated on or after day thirteen and the cubit and inch pulses sink away, the text judges the situation grave. If a fresh atypical influence gives rise to a different illness, the text refers the reader to the methods for deranged patterns."], "notes": [["苦酒漬烏梅一宿", "soaking the plums in vinegar overnight", "苦酒与一宿", "这个古方里的「苦酒」指醋。「漬」指浸泡，「一宿」是一整夜。", "In this historical recipe kujiu means vinegar. Zi means to soak and one night is the interval recorded."], ["丸如梧子大", "pills the size of Chinese parasol tree seeds", "如梧子大", "古方常借常见物件写丸剂的大小。「梧子」是梧桐树的种子。", "Historical recipes often express pill size by comparison with a familiar object. Here it is the seed of the Chinese parasol tree."], ["若兩感於寒者", "In simultaneous cold affliction", "两感于寒", "本段先把太阳和少阴配在第一日。接着按同样的句式写阳明与太阴、少阳与厥阴。", "The first day pairs Taiyang with Shaoyin. The next accounts use the same structure to pair Yangming with Taiyin, then Shaoyang with Jueyin."], ["若複服汗多亡陽", "repeating the dose can cause excessive sweating and loss of yang", "汗多亡阳", "「亡阳」是古书对阳气严重耗失的称呼。本段接在重复服药和汗出太多之后写它。", "Loss of yang is the text's term for severe depletion of yang qi. The phrase follows repeated dosing and excessive sweating in the account."], ["譫語", "delirious speech", "谵语", "「谵」读 zhān。这里指神志不清时的言语混乱，与腹满、身热、不想吃饭并列。", "Pronounced zhān, the word describes confused speech during a disturbance of awareness. The passage lists it with abdominal fullness, bodily heat, and lack of appetite."], ["乍弦乍急，乍細乍散", "shifts abruptly between wiry and tense, fine and scattered", "乍弦乍急，乍细乍散", "「乍」说忽然出现某种状态。四次重复强调脉象变得很快。", "The character zha signals a sudden change. Its four repetitions emphasize the rapid shifts in the pulse."], ["至七日太陽病衰", "the Taiyang illness waning on day seven", "病衰", "「衰」指病势减弱。这一段用六经分别写六天内的变化，包括头痛减轻和听力略有恢复。", "Waning means the illness is losing force. Six days are paired with six channels and their changing signs, such as easing headache and some return of hearing."], ["壞病證法", "the methods for deranged patterns", "坏病证法", "这里先说再感异气可以引出别的病，接着指向辨治新证的方法。「坏」与原来病势的改变有关。", "The text says further exposure can give rise to a different illness and refers to methods for reading the new pattern. Huai marks a change in the original course."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-14", "page": 1, "title": "Cold Diseases", "english": ["When cold gives rise to illness, the kidney is affected first. If cold lodges among the five zang organs, there is pain that pulls along the vessels. If it lodges in the chambers of the eight hollows, stagnant blood remains there. If it accumulates for a long time without clearing, it develops into a fixed disorder. How could one fail to take care?", "In cold disease with bone pain, yin obstruction, abdominal distention, lower back pain, difficulty moving the bowels, pain drawing into the shoulders, back, and neck, and a deep and slow pulse, the text says cold encroaches on the kidney. It indicates Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Gegen. When the disorder becomes fixed in the kidney, both hollows behind the knees hurt. The text indicates Gancao, Ganjiang, Fuling, and Baizhu Decoction.", "Formula for Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Gegen.", "Guizhi, three liang, with the bark removed. Shaoyao, three liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split. Gegen, four liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water. Gegen is boiled first and the surface foam removed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled to yield three sheng, and the dregs are removed. The text records one sheng taken warm three times daily. Sipping rice gruel is dispensed with, and the rest and dietary restrictions follow those for Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Formula for Gancao, Ganjiang, Fuling, and Baizhu Decoction.", "Roasted Gancao, two liang. Baizhu, two liang. Ganjiang, four liang. Fuling, four liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In cold disease with pain on both sides of the ribs, cold within tends to pull at the joints as it moves. When it surges upward there is headache and deafness, with a wiry, deep, and slow pulse. The text says cold takes advantage of the liver and indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction. When the disorder becomes fixed in the liver, there is sharp, gripping pain in both armpits and the person cannot turn onto either side. The text indicates Chaihu, Huangqin, Shaoyao, Banxia, and Gancao Decoction.", "Minor Bupleurum Decoction, as given earlier.", "Formula for Chaihu, Huangqin, Shaoyao, Banxia, and Gancao Decoction.", "Chaihu, four liang. Huangqin, three liang. Shaoyao, two liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Banxia, two liang, washed.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed and the liquid divided into three warm portions.", "In cold disease with distending fullness in the chest and flanks and pain across the chest, back, and shoulder blades, severe cases bring a tendency to sadness, spells of dizziness, and collapse with failure to recognize others. The text says cold takes advantage of the heart and indicates Tongmai Sini Decoction. When the disorder becomes fixed in the heart, there is pain on the outer side of the elbow and the arm cannot be extended. The text indicates Gancao Xiexin Decoction.", "Formula for Tongmai Sini Decoction.", "Roasted Gancao, two liang. One large Fuzi, used raw and broken apart. Ganjiang, three liang. Renshen, two liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water, reduced by boiling to one sheng and two ge, with the dregs removed and the liquid divided into two warm portions.", "Formula for Gancao Xiexin Decoction.", "Roasted Gancao, four liang. Huangqin, three liang. Ganjiang, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Renshen, three liang. Huanglian, one liang. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. The ingredients are boiled to yield six sheng, and the dregs are removed. The strained liquid is boiled again to yield three sheng. The text records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In cold disease with abdominal fullness, rumbling bowels, undigested food, and diarrhea with undigested food, severe cases bring weakness of the feet and loss of control of their movement. The pulse is slow and rough. The text says cold takes advantage of the spleen and indicates Regulate the Middle Decoction. When the disorder becomes fixed in the spleen, the hip joints are stiff and painful, with inability to bend or straighten. The text indicates Zhishi, Baizhu, Fuling, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Regulate the Middle Decoction.", "Renshen, three liang. Ganjiang, three liang. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Baizhu, three liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed and the liquid divided into three warm portions.", "Formula for Zhishi, Baizhu, Fuling, and Gancao Decoction.", "Four Zhishi fruits. Baizhu, three liang. Fuling, three liang. Roasted Gancao, one liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed and the liquid divided into three warm portions.", "In cold disease with panting, coughing, shortness of breath, inability to catch the next breath, spitting frothy saliva, deafness, and a dry throat, the text says cold takes advantage of the lung. For a deep and slow pulse it indicates Gancao and Ganjiang Decoction. When the disorder becomes fixed, there is pain on the inner side of the elbow and difficulty turning onto one's side. The text indicates Zhishi, Jupi, Jiegeng, Banxia, Fresh Ginger, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Gancao and Ganjiang Decoction.", "Roasted Gancao, four liang. Roasted Ganjiang, two liang.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water, reduced by boiling to one sheng and five ge, with the dregs removed and the liquid divided into two warm portions."], "notes": [["客於", "lodges among", "客作动词", "本句说寒停留在五脏之间。另一个「客」紧接着换了所在的部位，转到八虚。", "Here 客 is a verb meaning to lodge. Its second appearance introduces a different location, the eight hollows."], ["八虛之室", "chambers of the eight hollows", "八虚", "八虚是古书对身体若干成对凹陷部位的称呼。本段用「室」描写寒和瘀滞可停留之处。", "The eight hollows are a classical grouping of paired recesses of the body. The passage pictures them as chambers where cold and stagnation can lodge."], ["變而成著", "develops into a fixed disorder", "著", "著表示留着、停住。下文先叙寒侵及各脏的表现，再用「其著」过渡到留着后的表现。", "著 conveys lodging or becoming fixed. In the descriptions that follow, 其著 introduces what happens when the disorder settles in place."], ["兩膕痛", "both hollows behind the knees hurt", "腘", "膕读 guó，指膝盖后面弯曲的地方。两膕就是两边膝后。", "膕 is pronounced guó and names the hollow behind the knee. 兩 refers to the hollows on both sides."], ["不須啜粥", "Sipping rice gruel is dispensed with", "啜粥", "啜是小口饮食。原句在交代较常见的桂枝汤服后安排中，本条省去了喝粥这一项。", "啜 means to sip. The text describes the preparations used with Cinnamon Twig Decoction and says the gruel is omitted here."], ["弦而沉遲", "wiry, deep, and slow", "三个脉象合读", "弦写脉的绷直感，沉写深浅，迟写速度。同一句可以同时从这三方面形容脉。", "Wiry describes tautness, deep describes the level where the pulse is felt, and slow describes the rate. All three can describe the same pulse."], ["僕而不知人", "collapse with failure to recognize others", "仆与不知人", "僕是突然倒下。「不知人」接着说倒下时神志昏蒙，不能辨认人。", "僕 means to fall or collapse. The next words describe awareness so clouded that the person fails to recognize others."], ["飧泄", "diarrhea with undigested food", "飧泄", "飧可指饭食，和「泄」合起来指泄泻中带有未消化食物。前面的「食不化」也提供了线索。", "The first character can mean a meal. Together the characters describe diarrhea containing undigested food, a clue also given by the preceding words."], ["髀樞", "hip joints", "髀枢", "髀是大腿一带，枢借转轴说枢纽。后文的「不能屈伸」说明此处关节活动受限。", "髀 refers to the thigh region and 樞 pictures a pivot. Inability to bend or straighten describes the restricted movement here."], ["不能報息", "inability to catch the next breath", "报息", "报有接续之义。本词跟在「喘、咳、少气」之后，形容下一口气接不上。", "報 has the sense of continuing here. After panting, cough, and shortness of breath, this phrase describes difficulty catching the next breath."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-14", "page": 2, "title": "Cold Diseases", "english": ["Formula for Zhishi, Jupi, Jiegeng, Banxia, Fresh Ginger, and Gancao Decoction.", "Four Zhishi fruits. Jupi, two liang. Jiegeng, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Roasted Gancao, two liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, reduced by boiling to three sheng, with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily."], "notes": [["枳實橘皮桔梗半夏生薑甘草湯方", "Formula for Zhishi, Jupi, Jiegeng, Banxia, Fresh Ginger, and Gancao Decoction", "接前页的肺著条", "本页只有方名、药味和煎法。它承接前页写寒留着于肺、肘内痛的一句。", "This page gives the formula, ingredients, and preparation referred to in the preceding page's description of cold fixed in the lung with pain inside the elbow."], ["半夏半升", "Banxia, half a sheng", "两个半字", "第一个半是药名半夏的一部分，第二个半跟升连读，表示半升。", "The first 半 belongs to the ingredient name Banxia. The second belongs to the measure, half a sheng."], ["煮取三升", "reduced by boiling to three sheng", "煮取", "取后的数是煮成后所得的量。前面的「以水八升」才是开始时记下的用水量。", "The number after 取 is the volume obtained after boiling. The earlier eight sheng is the starting amount of water."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-19", "page": 1, "title": "Lesser Yang Diseases", "english": ["Shaoyang disease is characterized by a bitter taste in the mouth, a dry throat, and dizziness.", "In Shaoyang wind strike with loss of hearing in both ears, red eyes, fullness in the chest, and vexation, the text warns against inducing vomiting or purging. It says these bring palpitations and fright.", "In cold damage with a wiry and fine pulse, headache, and fever, the text classifies the disorder as Shaoyang. It warns against inducing sweating, which it says brings delirious speech and restlessness. The text attributes this to disharmony of the stomach and expects recovery once harmony is restored.", "If an original Taiyang disease fails to resolve and passes into Shaoyang, there is hard fullness below the ribs, dry retching with inability to eat, alternating chills and fever, and a deep and wiry pulse. The text warns against inducing vomiting or purging and indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Chaihu, eight liang. Renshen, three liang. Huangqin, three liang. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water. The ingredients are boiled to yield six sheng and the dregs are removed. The strained liquid is boiled again to yield three sheng. The text records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In Shaoyang disease, qi surges upward against its usual course and there is pain below the ribs. Severe cases bring upward-surging retching. The text attributes this to failure of gallbladder qi to descend and indicates Chaihu, Shaoyao, Zhishi, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Chaihu, Shaoyao, Zhishi, and Gancao Decoction.", "Chaihu, eight liang. Shaoyao, three liang. Four roasted Zhishi fruits. Roasted Gancao, three liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. The ingredients are boiled to yield six sheng and the dregs are removed. The strained liquid is boiled again to yield three sheng. The text records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "If inducing vomiting, purging, inducing sweating, or warm needling is followed by delirious speech and the signs indicating Bupleurum Decoction cease, the text calls this a spoiled disorder. It says to identify the misapplied treatment and address the resulting pattern accordingly. It considers Bupleurum Decoction unsuitable at that point.", "In combined disease of the three yang, the pulse is floating and large and rises toward the bar position. The person wants only to sleep and sweats when the eyes close. The text attributes this to blockage in the upper burner and indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction, given earlier.", "If on the fourth or fifth day of cold damage there is no marked fever but the person is restless and vexed, the text attributes it to departure from yang and entry into yin.", "On the third day of cold damage, the three yang have run their course and the three yin are expected to receive the pathogenic influence. If the person can still eat without vomiting, the text regards the three yin as having escaped the pathogenic influence.", "On the third day of cold damage, a small Shaoyang pulse indicates that the illness is about to cease.", "The text gives the period from Yin to Chen as the time when Shaoyang disease tends toward resolution."], "notes": [["口苦，咽乾，目眩", "a bitter taste in the mouth, a dry throat, and dizziness", "少阳开篇三候", "首句用口、咽、目三处来排出一组表现。目眩在这里有眼前发眩的意思。", "The opening groups three signs by mouth, throat, and eyes. 目眩 describes dizziness or a swimming sensation in the vision."], ["少陽中風", "Shaoyang wind strike", "中风", "这里的「中」读 zhòng，意思是受到。少阳中风是这篇用来组织后面一组表现的古典说法。", "中 is pronounced zhòng and means to be struck. Shaoyang wind strike is the classical label for the signs that follow."], ["吐、下則悸而驚", "these bring palpitations and fright", "吐法与下法", "这里「吐、下」指催吐和用下法。「则」引出文中所说的后果，悸指心跳不安，惊指容易受惊。", "The two words name induced vomiting and purging. 則 introduces the stated consequences, palpitations and fright."], ["脅下鞕滿", "hard fullness below the ribs", "胁下硬满", "胁下是肋下两侧。鞕是硬的写法，「满」写填满、胀满的感觉。", "This is the area below the ribs at the sides. 鞕 is a written form of 硬, hard, and 滿 describes fullness."], ["往來寒熱", "alternating chills and fever", "往来寒热", "往来形容寒与热彼此交替。与后面同列的沉弦脉一起，是这一段写病转入少阳时的线索。", "往來 describes a back-and-forth pattern of chills and fever. The paragraph lists this alongside the deep and wiry pulse after the passage into Shaoyang."], ["膽氣不降", "failure of gallbladder qi to descend", "上逆与不降", "开头先写气上逆，后面用「不降」解释为什么会向上。痛与呕是这一句把两者联起来的表现。", "The line first describes qi surging upward and then explains it through a failure to descend. Pain and retching are the signs the text links to that movement."], ["壞病", "a spoiled disorder", "坏病", "这里说先前的处置使原有证候变了。下句要求辨认误在何处，然后根据变化来处理。", "The passage describes a pattern altered by preceding interventions. It then calls for identifying the error and responding to the resulting change."], ["關上", "the bar position", "关上", "关是腕部寸、关、尺三处的中间。这一句在形容脉的浮和大之后，又写它到达的位置。", "The bar is the middle of the three wrist positions. After describing the pulse as floating and large, the sentence adds the place it reaches."], ["反能食而不嘔", "can still eat without vomiting", "反", "前面说三阴理当受邪，所以「反」提示后来所见与预期不同。还能吃而且不呕，是这句用来作判断的线索。", "The preceding words say the three yin are expected to receive the pathogenic influence. 反 marks the unexpected finding that the person can still eat without vomiting."], ["從寅至辰上", "the period from Yin to Chen", "寅至辰", "寅、卯、辰是相邻的地支时段。此句用寅起、辰止标出篇末所说的趋解时间。", "Yin, Mao, and Chen are consecutive time divisions named by the Earthly Branches. The closing line gives Yin and Chen as the limits of the period."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-15", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part I", "page": 1, "english": ["Greater Yang illness is defined here by a floating pulse, stiffness and pain in the head and neck, and aversion to cold.", "Greater Yang illness with fever, sweating, aversion to wind, and a relaxed pulse is called wind strike.", "In Greater Yang illness there may or may not already be fever. The text calls it cold damage when there is aversion to cold, body pain, retching, and tightness in both yin and yang aspects of the pulse.", "On the first day of cold damage, Greater Yang is said to receive it. A quiet pulse suggests that the illness is not passing onward. A strong urge to vomit, or agitation with a rapid and tense pulse, suggests that it is.", "On the second or third day of cold damage, the absence of signs of Yang Brightness or Lesser Yang indicates, in the text’s terms, that the illness has not passed onward.", "Greater Yang illness with fever and thirst and no aversion to cold is called warm illness. If the body burns with heat after sweating has been induced, the text calls the pattern wind warmth.", "The text describes wind warmth with floating pulses in both yin and yang, spontaneous sweating, bodily heaviness, frequent sleepiness, snoring, and difficulty speaking. It associates purging with difficult urination, a fixed gaze, and loss of bladder control. It associates treatment by fire with slight yellow discoloration and, in severe cases, convulsion-like episodes with intermittent jerking. In the case of fumigation with fire, the text says a first error prolongs the illness and a second hastens death.", "The text calls illness with fever and aversion to cold an onset in yang, and illness with aversion to cold without fever an onset in yin. It predicts recovery in seven or six days respectively, referring to seven as the number of yang and six as the number of yin.", "When headache in Greater Yang illness resolves of itself after seven days or more, the text says the illness has completed its course in the channel. When it seems about to begin another circuit, the text mentions needling Foot Yang Brightness to prevent transmission and allow recovery.", "The text gives the interval from the sì through the wèi double-hours as the time when Greater Yang illness tends to resolve.", "When the exterior signs have cleared in someone with wind strike but some symptoms linger, the text predicts recovery in twelve days.", "When the body feels very hot but the person wants to cover up, the text places heat at the skin and cold in the marrow. When the body feels very cold but the person wants no covers, it places cold at the skin and heat in the marrow.", "In Greater Yang wind strike, the yang pulse floats and the yin pulse is weak. The text associates floating yang with fever arising on its own and weak yin with spontaneous sweating. It assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction for shivery aversion to cold, shuddering aversion to wind, gentle sustained fever, audible nasal breathing, and dry retching.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng (outer layer removed), peony root 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text coarsely crushes the five ingredients and simmers them in 7 shēng of water over low heat until 3 shēng remain. It strains the liquid and lists 1 shēng at a comfortable temperature.", "After a short interval, the text describes sipping a little over 1 shēng of hot, thin rice gruel to support the decoction, then keeping warmly covered for about one time period. It seeks a faint sheen of sweat over the whole body. Sweat streaming like water is said to leave the illness unresolved.", "If sweating follows the first serving and the illness improves, the text says to stop even if some remains. If there is no sweat it repeats the procedure, then shortens the intervals if needed so that three servings fall within about half a day. In severe illness it describes dosing over a day and night and observing the whole cycle. It describes preparing another batch if signs persist, and using up to two or three batches if sweating still does not occur. It lists raw or cold foods, sticky or slippery foods, meat and flour-based foods, the five pungent foods, wine and fermented dairy, and foul-smelling foods among the prohibitions.", "For Greater Yang illness with headache, fever, sweating, and aversion to wind, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction. The formula appears above.", "For Greater Yang illness with marked stiffness of the neck and upper back, together with sweating and aversion to wind, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Kudzu Root.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Kudzu Root", "Kudzu root 4 liǎng, peony root 2 liǎng, cinnamon twig 2 liǎng (outer layer removed), roasted licorice 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu of water, boils the kudzu root first until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces the liquid to 3 shēng before straining. It lists 1 shēng warm and covering for slight perspiration. It omits gruel and follows the other aftercare and restrictions of Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "After purging in Greater Yang illness, the text allows Cinnamon Twig Decoction by the preceding method if qi rushes upward. It rules out the formula if qi does not surge upward."], "notes": [["太陽之為病", "Greater Yang illness", "太阳病", "太阳是全书六类病证之一。本篇开头以浮脉、头项强痛、恶寒标出这类证候。", "Greater Yang is one of the text’s six pattern categories. The opening line introduces it through a floating pulse, head and neck pain with stiffness, and aversion to cold."], ["中風", "wind strike", "中风", "本篇用发热、汗出、恶风和脉缓界定这里的「中风」。这个词在此属于古书的太阳病分类。", "Wind strike is the classical Greater Yang pattern defined here by fever, sweating, aversion to wind, and a relaxed pulse."], ["不傳", "not passing onward", "传", "这里的「传」说证候由太阳转见其他经类。下一条借阳明、少阳证是否出现来说明。", "Transmission means the illness presents in another channel category. The next line looks for signs of Yang Brightness or Lesser Yang."], ["失溲", "loss of bladder control", "失溲", "「溲」指小便，「失」指出现失控。它与前面所说小便不利是两种排尿变化。", "This phrase refers to losing control of urination. The earlier phrase describes difficulty passing urine."], ["從巳至未上", "from the sì through the wèi double-hours", "巳至未", "巳、午、未是地支计时名称。这里行文用它们来标出古书所谓病欲解的时段。", "Sì, wǔ, and wèi are traditional two-hour periods. The line gives this span as the expected window for resolution."], ["乾嘔", "dry retching", "干呕", "「干」说明有呕的动作却没有吐出东西。此处是桂枝汤证语句中的一项表现。", "Dry retching is the motion or urge of vomiting without bringing anything up."], ["漐漐", "a faint sheen of sweat", "漐漐", "指皮肤微微湿润。后面紧接「如水流漓」，描写汗出很多的情形。", "This means slight moisture on the skin. The following clause describes a much heavier degree of sweating."], ["項背強幾幾", "marked stiffness of the neck and upper back", "项背强几几", "「项背」指后颈和背部。这句用「几几」形容拘紧，和后面的汗出、恶风一起读。", "The phrase describes marked stiffness in the nape and upper back. Sweating and aversion to wind complete the pattern in this line."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-15", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part I", "page": 2, "english": ["If Greater Yang illness persists on the third day despite induced sweating, vomiting, purging, or warmed needling, the text calls it a disrupted pattern and rules out Cinnamon Twig Decoction. It calls for examining the pulse and signs to identify which treatment went astray, then responding to the resulting pattern.", "The text describes Cinnamon Twig Decoction as a means of releasing the muscle layer. If the pulse is floating and tight and there is fever without sweating, it rules out the formula and urges the reader to remember the distinction.", "The text also rules out Cinnamon Twig Decoction for a habitual drinker who becomes ill. It expects vomiting and attributes it to habitual drinkers’ aversion to sweet preparations.", "When an established tendency to pant flares, the text favors adding magnolia bark and apricot kernel to Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "The text predicts vomiting of pus and blood later if a person vomits after taking Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "If sweating has been induced in Greater Yang illness and sweat continues to leak without stopping, the text describes aversion to wind, difficult urination, and slight stiffness in the limbs that makes bending and straightening difficult. It assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Aconite.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Aconite", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng (outer layer removed), peony root 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, fresh ginger 3 liǎng, 12 split jujubes, and 1 aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces).", "The text reduces 7 shēng of water with the six ingredients to 3 shēng, strains the liquid, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day, with the aftercare of Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "After purging in Greater Yang illness, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Peony Root for a skipping pulse with fullness in the chest.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Peony Root. The text omits peony root from the original formula. It reduces 7 shēng of water with the four remaining ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day, with the aftercare of Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "After purging in Greater Yang illness, if the person has aversion to cold, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Peony Root, with Aconite.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Peony Root, with Aconite", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, 12 split jujubes, and 1 aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces).", "The text reduces 7 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day, with the aftercare of Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "On the eighth or ninth day, Greater Yang illness may resemble intermittent fever, with bouts of fever and aversion to cold, more heat than cold, no vomiting, and elimination tending to remain normal. When there are two or three bouts a day and the pulse is faint and relaxed, the text takes it as a sign of approaching recovery.", "A faint pulse with aversion to cold is classed as deficiency of both yin and yang. The text rules out further sweating, vomiting, or purging. If the face instead looks flushed, the text says resolution is unlikely yet, and expects itching because a light sweat cannot emerge. It assigns Equal Parts Cinnamon Twig and Ephedra Decoction.", "Equal Parts Cinnamon Twig and Ephedra Decoction", "The text combines 3 hé of Cinnamon Twig Decoction and 3 hé of Ephedra Decoction for 6 hé taken at once. Aftercare follows the Cinnamon Twig Decoction procedure.", "If the first serving of Cinnamon Twig Decoction in Greater Yang illness instead brings agitation without relief, the text describes first needling Fengfu and Fengchi and then giving the decoction again.", "If profuse sweating follows Cinnamon Twig Decoction in Greater Yang illness and the pulse is surging and large, the text assigns White Tiger Decoction. If the illness resembles intermittent fever and recurs twice a day, it assigns Two Parts Cinnamon Twig to One Part Ephedra Decoction.", "White Tiger Decoction", "Anemarrhena rhizome 6 liǎng, gypsum 1 jīn (crushed and wrapped in cotton), roasted licorice 2 liǎng, and non-glutinous rice 6 hé.", "The text boils the four ingredients in 1 dǒu of water until the rice is cooked, then strains the finished liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "Two Parts Cinnamon Twig to One Part Ephedra Decoction. The text combines 2 shēng of Cinnamon Twig Decoction and 1 shēng of Ephedra Decoction to make 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng three times a day with the aftercare of Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "If profuse sweating after Cinnamon Twig Decoction brings marked agitation and thirst along with a surging, large pulse, the text assigns White Tiger Decoction with Ginseng.", "White Tiger Decoction with Ginseng. The text adds ginseng 3 liǎng to the White Tiger Decoction formula.", "When Greater Yang illness shows fever and aversion to cold, more heat than cold, and a faint, weak pulse, the text calls this absence of yang and rules out inducing sweat. With a floating, large pulse it assigns Two Parts Cinnamon Twig to One Part Yuebi Decoction.", "Two Parts Cinnamon Twig to One Part Yuebi Decoction"], "notes": [["壞病", "a disrupted pattern", "坏病", "指原有病证经处理后变化，已需要重新辨认的情形。原文接着说要观其脉证、判断哪一步有误。", "The pattern has changed after treatment and must be reassessed. The text turns to pulse and signs to see where treatment went astray."], ["解肌", "releasing the muscle layer", "解肌", "古书描述作用在肌表的一种治法。本句用汗出与否及浮紧脉说明桂枝汤的使用界线。", "This is a classical method directed at the muscle layer or surface. The line uses sweating and pulse quality to delimit the pattern."], ["酒客", "habitual drinker", "酒客", "这里称平素饮酒的人。下文用对甘味的反应说明作者为何另作考虑。", "This means someone accustomed to drinking alcohol. The explanation in the line turns on how the person responds to a sweet preparation."], ["遂漏不止", "sweat continues to leak without stopping", "漏不止", "「漏」用漏水比喻汗一直向外出。之后列恶风、尿难、肢体拘急等同见的表现。", "The image is of a continuing leak of sweat. The passage then lists the other signs that accompany it."], ["脈促", "a skipping pulse", "促脉", "同书平脉篇把「促」描述为脉来急快、中间偶停。这里把它和胸满并提。", "Earlier in the book, a skipping pulse is described as rapid with occasional pauses. This line pairs it with chest fullness."], ["如瘧狀", "resemble intermittent fever", "如疟状", "「如」表示相似。下文数出一天两三次，提示这种相似在于症状分次发作。", "The wording marks resemblance. The two or three bouts in a day help show what kind of resemblance the text has in mind."], ["風府、風池", "Fengfu and Fengchi", "风府、风池", "这是两个穴名，此处表示作者安排针刺的一步。句中「先」和「却」写出先后。", "These are names of two acupuncture points. The wording places needling before the formula is given again."], ["桂枝二麻黃一", "Two Parts Cinnamon Twig to One Part Ephedra", "桂枝二麻黄一", "方名中的二与一说两种已煎好汤液合用的份数。后面方条明确写二升与一升。", "The numbers name the ratio of the two prepared decoctions. The formula gives two shēng of one and one shēng of the other."], ["無陽", "absence of yang", "无阳", "这里是作者对脉微弱等一组表现的判断。紧接着以脉浮大另立一种情形，阅读时留意这个分支。", "This is the author’s judgment for the cluster that includes a faint, weak pulse. The following clause gives a separate branch for a floating, large pulse."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-15", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part I", "page": 3, "english": ["Cinnamon twig 18 zhū (outer layer removed), peony root, ephedra, and licorice 18 zhū each (licorice roasted), 4 split jujubes, sliced fresh ginger 1 liǎng and 2 zhū, and gypsum 24 zhū (crushed and wrapped in cotton).", "The text starts with 6 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng. It strains the liquid and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "If Greater Yang illness has been treated with Cinnamon Twig Decoction or by purging and there is still stiffness and pain of the head and neck, gentle sustained fever, no sweat, fullness and slight pain below the breastbone, and difficulty urinating, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Cinnamon Twig, with Poria and White Atractylodes.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Cinnamon Twig, with Poria and White Atractylodes", "Peony root 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, 12 split jujubes, poria 3 liǎng, and white atractylodes 3 liǎng.", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the six ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In cold damage with a floating pulse, spontaneous sweating, frequent urination, agitation, slight aversion to cold, and cramping of the legs, the text calls it an error to give Cinnamon Twig Decoction to attack the exterior. If reversal cold, dry throat, agitation, and retching then occur, it assigns Licorice and Dried Ginger Decoction to restore yang. If the reversal resolves and the feet warm, it assigns Peony Root and Licorice Decoction so the legs can straighten. For disharmony of stomach qi with delirious speech, it gives a small amount of Regulate the Stomach and Order the Qi Decoction. If sweating has been induced again and fire needling added, it assigns Four Reversals Decoction.", "Licorice and Dried Ginger Decoction", "Roasted licorice 4 liǎng and fire-processed dried ginger 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the two ingredients to 1 shēng and 5 hé, strains, and divides the liquid into two warm servings.", "Peony Root and Licorice Decoction", "Peony root 4 liǎng and roasted licorice 4 liǎng.", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the two ingredients to 1 shēng and 5 hé, strains, and divides the liquid into two warm servings.", "Regulate the Stomach and Order the Qi Decoction", "Roasted licorice 1 liǎng, mirabilite ½ jīn, and rhubarb 4 liǎng (washed in wine).", "The text starts with 3 shēng of water, boils the other two ingredients until 1 shēng remains, strains, and adds the mirabilite. It brings the liquid briefly back to a boil and records taking it all at once.", "Four Reversals Decoction", "Ginseng 2 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, dried ginger 1½ liǎng, and 1 aconite root (used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces).", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 1 shēng and 2 hé, strains, and divides the liquid into two warm servings. For a robust person it records 1 large aconite root and 3 liǎng of dried ginger.", "A question: When Greater Yang illness seems to show the full pattern, why does treatment by the Cinnamon Twig method sometimes make it worse, with reversal cold, dry throat, restlessness, retching, and delirious speech? The teacher called it the Yangdan pattern and opposed attacking it. A floating wrist pulse signifies wind and also deficiency. In the text’s reasoning, wind produces heat and deficiency produces cramping. An erroneous attack on the exterior brings sweat and loss of yang, heavy sweating dries the fluids, and drying the fluids cramps the sinews. The text links binding inside Yang Brightness to agitation and delirious speech. It assigns Licorice and Dried Ginger to restore yang, Peony Root and Licorice to salvage fluids, and Regulate the Stomach and Order the Qi to stop delirious speech. A disrupted pattern of this kind, it says, must be addressed according to the pulse and signs.", "For the Yangdan pattern with fever that does not rise in daily waves, sweating, dry throat, drowsiness with restlessness, and unexpected calm at midnight, the text assigns Rehmannia, Pinellia, Oyster Shell, and Sour Jujube Seed Decoction. For thirst, agitation, reddish urine, and delirious speech, it assigns Bamboo Leaf, Gypsum, Scutellaria, Alisma, Pinellia, and Licorice Decoction.", "Rehmannia, Pinellia, Oyster Shell, and Sour Jujube Seed Decoction", "Rehmannia root 6 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), oyster shell 2 liǎng, and sour jujube seed 3 liǎng.", "The text reduces 4 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 2 shēng and divides the liquid into two warm servings.", "Bamboo Leaf, Gypsum, Scutellaria, Alisma, Pinellia, and Licorice Decoction", "Bamboo leaves 2 handfuls, gypsum ½ jīn (wrapped in cotton), scutellaria 3 liǎng, alisma 2 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and licorice 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 5 shēng of water with the six ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day."], "notes": [["十八銖", "18 zhū", "铢", "铢是古代衡重单位。同一方中有的药用铢，有的写两和铢合数。", "Zhū is a historical unit of weight. This formula lists some amounts in zhū and one in a combination of liǎng and zhū."], ["桂枝去桂", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Cinnamon Twig", "桂枝去桂", "方名先指原来的桂枝汤，再说明本方去桂枝、加茯苓和白术。下一段药味表可以对照。", "The name starts with the parent formula, then specifies omitting cinnamon twig and adding poria and white atractylodes. The ingredient list shows the change."], ["腳攣急", "cramping of the legs", "脚挛急", "「挛急」描述肌筋收紧。下文说「其脚即伸」，与这里的拘挛相呼应。", "The phrase describes tightening or cramping of the legs. The later reference to the legs straightening answers this sign."], ["譫語", "delirious speech", "谵语", "说话错乱。问答段继续以阳明内结解释作者为何把它和烦躁放在一起。", "This means disordered speech in delirium. The later question and answer link it with agitation and internal binding."], ["燒針", "fire needling", "烧针", "指以加热的针施治。这里与反复发汗并列，为作者考虑方治变化的一步。", "This is needling with heat. The passage names it alongside repeated induced sweating in the sequence of interventions."], ["陽旦證", "the Yangdan pattern", "阳旦证", "这是本篇的证名。问答段先解释原证为何不可攻，下一段再用两组所见表现分别展开。", "Yangdan is the pattern name used here. The exchange first explains the treatment error, then the following paragraph describes two presentations."], ["液枯", "dries the fluids", "液枯", "「枯」借干涸写津液耗损。这里用发汗多、液枯、筋挛组成一连串的解释。", "The word evokes drying up. The text presents heavy sweating, depletion of fluids, and cramping as a chain of effects."], ["發熱不潮", "fever that does not rise in daily waves", "发热不潮", "「潮」取潮水有时而至的意象。这句描述有发热，但没有按时起伏的潮热。", "The tide image suggests fever rising at predictable times. This phrase describes fever without that recurring rhythm."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-26", "page": 1, "title": "Periodic Fever Disorders", "english": ["The teacher said: In periodic fever disorders, the text associates a wiry, rapid pulse with more heat than cold, and a wiry, slow pulse with more cold than heat. The historical text pairs a wiry, slightly tight pulse with purging; a wiry, slow pulse with warming; a wiry, tight pulse with sweating, needling, or moxibustion; and a floating, large pulse with emesis. It treats a wiry, rapid pulse as wind setting the illness in motion and looks to Lesser Yang for its explanation.", "A question: If an attack occurs monthly, the text expects recovery by the fifteenth day, or by the end of the month in severe cases. What if there is no recovery? The teacher replied: It has bound into a mass. There must be a persistent root of the disorder. The text urges attention to it and names Biējiǎ Decoction Pills.", "Formula record: Biējiǎ Decoction Pills.", "The record lists biējiǎ, cháihú, huángqín, dàhuáng, mǔdān, zhèchóng, and ējiāo in equal portions.", "The historical directions describe pounding and sifting the seven ingredients and forming pills with prepared honey, each the size of a Chinese parasol-tree seed. The text records seven pills per serving, three servings a day, swallowed with clear wine or a plain drink by those who cannot drink wine.", "The teacher said: When yin qi is isolated and cut off and yang qi rises alone, the text describes heat, shortness of breath, vexation, hot hands and feet, and an urge to vomit. It calls this dǎn nüè and names White Tiger Decoction with Guìzhī and Rénshēn Added.", "Formula record: White Tiger Decoction with Guìzhī and Rénshēn Added.", "The record lists zhīmǔ, six liǎng; shígāo, one jīn; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; non-glutinous rice, two gě; guìzhī, three liǎng; and rénshēn, three liǎng.", "The historical directions describe cooking the six ingredients in one dǒu of water until the rice is done, removing the dregs, and taking three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "When the pulse seems even and there is heat without bodily cold, with painful joints and occasional retching, the text calls the pattern warm nüè and names White Tiger Decoction with Guìzhī Added. The recipe is given as the preceding formula with rénshēn omitted.", "When cold predominates or there is cold alone without heat, the text calls the pattern mǔ nüè. It names Shǔqī Powder and also Cháihú, Guìzhī, and Dried Ginger Decoction.", "Formula record: Shǔqī Powder.", "The record lists washed shǔqī, yúnmǔ heated for two days and nights, and lónggǔ in equal portions.", "The historical directions describe pounding the three ingredients into powder and taking half a qián-spoon mixed with fermented grain water before an attack.", "Formula record: Cháihú, Guìzhī, and Dried Ginger Decoction.", "The record lists cháihú, half a jīn; guìzhī, three liǎng; dried ginger, two liǎng; guālóu root, four liǎng; huángqín, three liǎng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; and heated mǔlì, two liǎng.", "The historical directions describe reducing one dǒu of water to six shēng, removing the dregs, reducing the liquid again to three shēng, and taking three warm servings of one shēng in a day. The text describes mild vexation after the first serving and sweating and improvement after the next."], "notes": [["瘧病", "periodic fever disorders", "瘧病", "「瘧」是古书按发作方式界定的一类病，关心寒热如何交替、何时再发。", "Nüè is a historical category organized around recurrent attacks and patterns of chills and heat. Its scope need not coincide with a modern disease name."], ["弦數", "wiry, rapid pulse", "弦数与弦迟", "两组都带有「弦」，区分它们的第二个字。数是偏快，迟是偏慢。", "Both patterns are wiry. The second term distinguishes pace: rapid in one, slow in the other."], ["瘧母", "persistent root of the disorder", "瘧母", "「母」有根源之意。上句先说久不愈，下面才用结成症瘕来解释它。", "Mother is used in the sense of a persistent root. The reply introduces a mass as part of the text's explanation for the recurring attacks."], ["梧桐子大", "Chinese parasol-tree seed", "梧桐子大", "古方常借常见种子形容丸剂大小。这里记的是梧桐籽，不是在换算现代规格。", "A familiar seed serves as a size comparison for the pills in the historical record."], ["疸瘧", "dǎn nüè", "疸瘧", "这条先叙所见，最后命名。理解本书的用词，应连着看这里的热、烦和欲呕。", "The term labels the pattern described in this line. The text gives heat, vexation, and nausea before it names it."], ["但熱", "heat without bodily cold", "但热", "「但」在这里是只有的意思。与下一条「但寒不热」放在一起看，正好是相反的两组。", "Dàn means only. This heat-without-cold pattern contrasts with the cold-without-heat pattern in the following passage."], ["未發前", "before an attack", "未发前", "这里的「发」指下一次发作。它承接全篇按发作节律观察的方式。", "Fā refers here to the onset of the next episode, continuing the chapter's focus on the timing of attacks."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 1, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["In a Taiyang illness, pronounced stiffness of the nape and upper back, no sweating, and aversion to wind are the signs for which the text assigns Gegen Decoction.", "Formula for Gegen Decoction.", "Gegen (kudzu root), 4 liang. Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang, outer layer removed. Shaoyao (peony root), 2 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water. Kudzu and ephedra are boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng. The dregs are strained out, and the recipe records a warm dose of 1 sheng and covering the patient until sweat is barely perceptible. No rice gruel is needed. The remaining aftercare and restrictions follow the Guizhi method, which is also the model for the other decoctions.", "When Taiyang and Yangming are ill together, the text expects spontaneous diarrhea and assigns Gegen Decoction, given above. When there is vomiting alone, it assigns Gegen Decoction with Banxia.", "Formula for Gegen Decoction with Banxia.", "Gegen (kudzu root), 4 liang. Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang, outer layer removed. Shaoyao (peony root), 2 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed.", "For these eight ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water. Kudzu and ephedra are boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng, and the dregs are removed. The recipe records a warm dose of 1 sheng and covering the patient until sweat is barely perceptible. The rest follows the Guizhi method.", "A Taiyang patient has a Guizhi pattern, yet the physician purges them. The resulting diarrhea continues. The text takes a skipping pulse as a sign that the heat has yet to resolve. For labored breathing with sweating, it assigns Gegen, Huanglian, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Gegen, Huanglian, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction.", "Gegen (kudzu root), half a jin. Huanglian (coptis rhizome), 3 liang. Huangqin (skullcap root), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 8 sheng of water. Kudzu is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 2 sheng, strained, and divided into two warm doses.", "For Taiyang illness with headache, fever, body aches, low back pain, aching joints, aversion to wind, absent sweating, and labored breathing, the text assigns Mahuang Decoction.", "Formula for Mahuang Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang, outer layer removed. Gancao (licorice), 1 liang, roasted. Xingren (apricot kernels), 70, skins and tips removed.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 9 sheng of water. Ephedra is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 2 and a half sheng, and the dregs are strained out. It records a warm dose of 8 he and covering the patient until sweat is barely perceptible. No rice gruel is needed. The rest of the aftercare follows the Guizhi method.", "When Taiyang and Yangming are ill together with labored breathing and chest fullness, the text forbids purging and assigns Mahuang Decoction, given above.", "In Taiyang illness after ten days, a floating, fine pulse and a strong desire to lie down are taken as signs that the exterior has resolved. For chest fullness and flank pain, the text assigns Minor Chaihu Decoction. When the pulse is simply floating, it assigns Mahuang Decoction, given above.", "Formula for Minor Chaihu Decoction.", "Chaihu (bupleurum), half a jin. Huangqin (skullcap root), 3 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 3 liang, roasted. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed.", "For these seven ingredients, the recipe starts with 1 dou and 2 sheng of water and boils down to 6 sheng. The dregs are removed and the liquid is boiled again to 3 sheng. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "For Taiyang cold damage with a floating, tight pulse, fever, aversion to cold, body aches, absent sweating, and agitation, the text assigns Major Green Dragon Decoction. With a faint, weak pulse, sweating, and aversion to wind, it forbids the formula. It calls its use in that case a treatment error and predicts reversal cold, tendon twitching, and muscle quivering.", "Formula for Major Green Dragon Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 6 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang, outer layer removed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Xingren (apricot kernels), 40, skins and tips removed. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split. Shigao (gypsum), a piece the size of a hen's egg, crushed."], "notes": [["項背強几几", "pronounced stiffness of the nape and upper back", "项背强几几", "「强」读作僵硬。「几几」加重这个形象，历代有用伸颈之状来说明的。", "Qiang means stiff. The repeated characters sharpen the picture. Traditional commentators describe a stiffly extended neck."], ["複取微似汗，不須啜粥，余如桂枝法將息及禁忌", "covering the patient until sweat is barely perceptible. No rice gruel is needed. The remaining aftercare and restrictions", "微似汗与啜粥", "「微似汗」说汗意很轻。「啜粥」是喝粥，原文说明这个古方服后省去这一环。", "Sweating is just perceptible. The next clause says that sipping rice gruel is omitted in this historical recipe."], ["太陽與陽明合病者", "When Taiyang and Yangming are ill together, the text expects spontaneous diarrhea", "合病", "这里两经同时受病。下文把有自利和只有呕吐分成两种情况。", "Both channels are affected together. The ensuing clauses distinguish spontaneous diarrhea from vomiting alone."], ["脈促者，熱未解也", "a skipping pulse as a sign that the heat has yet to resolve", "促脉与热未解", "促是前面脉法里讲过的脉名，本段用它解释误下之后病势还有什么未解。", "Skipping is a pulse name introduced earlier. Here the text uses it to explain what remains unresolved following purging."], ["骨節疼痛", "aching joints", "骨节疼痛", "「骨节」指关节处。这句与头痛、全身痛、腰痛并列，把疼痛部位写得更细。", "The phrase refers to the joints. It adds another location to the headache, body aches, and low back pain."], ["脈浮細而嗜臥", "a floating, fine pulse and a strong desire to lie down", "嗜卧", "「嗜」在这里是特别想要。「卧」是躺下，这一条同时把脉象和起居表现放在一起看。", "Shi here marks a strong inclination. Wo means to lie down. The text reads this inclination together with the pulse quality."], ["筋惕肉瞤", "tendon twitching, and muscle quivering", "筋惕肉瞤", "「瞤」读 shùn，写不由自主的跳动。两小句并列，描述误用古方之后的抽动表现。", "Shun describes involuntary quivering. The paired phrases describe twitching after a formula is used in the wrong pattern."], ["石膏如雞子大", "a piece the size of a hen's egg", "鸡子大", "「鸡子」指鸡蛋。这种写法以常见东西的大小说明原方所取的石膏块。", "Jizi means a hen's egg. The recipe uses a familiar object to describe the size of the gypsum piece."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 2, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["For these seven ingredients, the recipe uses 9 sheng of water. Ephedra is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng, then strained. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng to bring on barely perceptible sweating, with warming powder applied for profuse sweating. Once the first dose brings on sweat, later doses are withheld. The text warns that repeated dosing can lead to excessive sweating and loss of yang, followed by deficiency, aversion to wind, agitation, and inability to sleep.", "In Taiyang wind strike with a floating, relaxed pulse, the body feels heavy without pain, with occasional spells of lightness. If there are no signs of Shaoyin illness, the text assigns Major Green Dragon Decoction, given above, to bring on sweating.", "In cold damage, the exterior has yet to resolve and there is retained fluid below the heart. The signs include dry retching, fever, and cough. Thirst, diarrhea, choking, difficulty urinating with lower abdominal fullness, or labored breathing may also occur. The text assigns Minor Green Dragon Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Green Dragon Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang, joints removed. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Xixin (asarum), 3 liang. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 3 liang, roasted. Wuweizi (schisandra fruit), half a sheng. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed.", "For these eight ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water. Ephedra is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng, and the dregs are strained out. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "For thirst, the recipe omits pinellia and adds 3 liang of Trichosanthes root. For mild diarrhea or choking, it omits ephedra and adds one piece of fuzi. For difficult urination with lower abdominal fullness, it omits ephedra and adds 4 liang of poria. For labored breathing, it adds half a sheng of apricot kernels, skins and tips removed.", "In cold damage with retained fluid below the heart, there is coughing, slightly labored breathing, and fever without thirst. If thirst appears after taking the decoction, the text takes it as a sign that the cold is departing and the illness is about to resolve. The formula given is Minor Green Dragon Decoction, above.", "When the exterior signs of Taiyang illness are still present and the pulse is floating and weak, the text calls for release through sweating and assigns Guizhi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "When Taiyang illness is followed by slightly labored breathing after purging, the text attributes it to the unresolved exterior and assigns Guizhi Decoction with Houpo and Xingren.", "Formula for Guizhi Decoction with Houpo and Xingren.", "Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split. Houpo (magnolia bark), 2 liang. Xingren (apricot kernels), 50, skins and tips removed.", "For these seven ingredients, the recipe uses 7 sheng of water, boils gently down to 3 sheng, and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng and covering the patient until sweat is barely perceptible.", "When the exterior signs of Taiyang illness are still present, the text calls purging a treatment error. To release the exterior, it assigns Guizhi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "In Taiyang illness, the physician first induces sweating without resolving the illness and then purges. If the pulse is floating, recovery has yet to occur. A floating pulse places the illness at the exterior, and the text attributes the lack of recovery to purging at that stage. Because the floating pulse shows that the exterior remains involved, the text expects recovery once the exterior is released and assigns Guizhi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "In Taiyang illness with a floating, tight pulse, no sweating, fever, and body aches unresolved after eight or nine days, the exterior signs persist. The text calls for inducing sweat. After the medicine the signs ease slightly, and the person becomes restless and closes their eyes. In severe cases the text expects a nosebleed and says that the illness resolves afterward. It explains this by the strength of yang qi and assigns Mahuang Decoction, given above.", "In Taiyang illness with a floating, tight pulse, fever, and absent sweating, the text expects recovery after a spontaneous nosebleed.", "In an overlapping illness of the two yang channels, sweating is induced when Taiyang illness first occurs, but the initial sweating is incomplete. The illness then shifts to Yangming, with continuing light spontaneous sweating and no aversion to cold."], "notes": [["一服汗出，停後服", "Once the first dose brings on sweat, later doses are withheld", "一服汗出", "一服指第一次服药。这句话写已经见汗时，古方如何处置剩下的剂次。", "Yi fu is the first dose. This line gives the historical recipe's condition for withholding later doses."], ["身不疼，但重，乍有輕時", "the body feels heavy without pain, with occasional spells of lightness", "身重与乍轻", "「但」指只有。「乍」说明轻快的感觉时而出现，与大部分时间的身重相对。", "Dan means only. Zha signals brief changes from the prevailing sensation of heaviness."], ["心下有水氣，乾嘔", "retained fluid below the heart. The signs include dry retching", "心下水气与干呕", "「心下」是古书常用的上腹部位词。「水气」是古书的体液概念。「干呕」说有呕的动作。", "Below the heart is a classical location in the upper abdomen. Water qi describes retained fluid in this account. Dry retching is the action of vomiting without bringing anything up."], ["若微利，若噎者", "For mild diarrhea or choking", "微利与噎", "此处「利」说泄泻。「噎」说咽间梗阻的感觉。两个「若」分别列出两种可能。", "Li refers to diarrhea and ye to choking or obstruction in the throat. Each ruò introduces a possible presentation."], ["服湯已渴者", "If thirst appears after taking the decoction", "服汤已渴", "这里的「已」表示服汤已经完成。作者把喝汤之后才出现的渴，和前一句原本不渴连起来看。", "Yi marks the dose as already taken. The passage reads thirst after the decoction in light of its earlier absence."], ["欲解外者", "To release the exterior", "解外", "「外」指病势仍在表的层次。「解」写这一层病势解除，也就是下文所说仍要处理的地方。", "The exterior names the level where the text places the illness. To release it is to resolve the illness at that level."], ["自衄者愈", "recovery after a spontaneous nosebleed", "自衄", "「衄」在此指鼻出血。「自」表示自行发生，上句也写到鼻出血与病势缓解的先后。", "Here nü means a nosebleed, and zi means it occurs spontaneously. The preceding paragraph also links nosebleeding with subsequent relief."], ["二陽並病", "an overlapping illness of the two yang channels", "二阳并病", "本段先有太阳病，随后转属阳明。这里的「并病」着重写两经先后相连的经过。", "Taiyang illness comes first and subsequently shifts to Yangming. The term emphasizes the sequence by which the two channel patterns overlap."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 3, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["If the Taiyang signs persist, the text calls purging a treatment error and instead describes inducing a little sweat. If the face gradually turns bright red, it takes yang qi to be pent up at the exterior and describes releasing it by warming with vapors. If induced sweating remains incomplete, there is too little to count as a full release, and the pent-up yang qi cannot get out. When sweat should occur but does not, the person grows restless and cannot locate the pain. It is now in the abdomen, now in the limbs. Pressing fails to locate it. The text expects recovery after inducing sweat again. If the person is short of breath and can only sit upright, the text attributes that to incomplete sweating. How does it tell? The pulse is rough. A floating, tight pulse generally signals release through sweating. With bodily heaviness and palpitations, however, the text forbids inducing sweat and waits for spontaneous sweating. A faint pulse at the cubit indicates internal deficiency. Once the interior is replenished and the fluids come into balance, it expects sweat and recovery to follow spontaneously.", "With a floating, tight pulse, body aches are expected, and the text normally describes release through sweating. If the cubit pulse is slow, it forbids inducing sweat, on the grounds that nutritive qi is insufficient and the blood is weak. A floating pulse places illness at the exterior, for which the text assigns Mahuang Decoction, given above, to induce sweating.", "For a floating, tight pulse, the text describes inducing sweating with Mahuang Decoction, given above.", "If the patient sweats spontaneously all the time, the text says nutritive qi is in order but defensive qi is out of harmony. Nutritive qi circulates within the vessels and defensive qi outside them. The two are failing to work together. The text predicts recovery after inducing sweating again and assigns Guizhi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "When the organs have no other illness but the patient periodically develops fever and spontaneous sweating and fails to recover, the text takes defensive qi to be out of harmony. It describes inducing sweat before the next episode and assigns Guizhi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "For cold damage with a floating, tight pulse, when a nosebleed results after sweating was not induced, the text assigns Mahuang Decoction, given above.", "In cold damage with no bowel movement for six or seven days, headache, and fever, the text gives Chengqi Decoction. If the urine is clear, however, it places the illness at the exterior and describes inducing sweat with Guizhi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "When cold damage resolves after sweating is induced but restlessness returns about half a day later with a floating, tight pulse, the text describes inducing sweat again with Guizhi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "After induced sweating, vomiting, purging, loss of blood, or loss of body fluids, the text expects spontaneous recovery if yin and yang come into harmony on their own.", "Following repeated strong purging, the text attributes difficulty urinating to loss of fluids. It calls for leaving this alone and expects urination to resume on its own in time.", "Following strong purging and then induced sweating, the text expects shivering chills and a faint, fine pulse. It attributes the signs to deficiency affecting both the interior and exterior.", "After purging followed by induced sweating, the person is agitated by day and unable to sleep but quiet at night. There is no vomiting, thirst, exterior sign, or high fever, and the pulse is deep and faint. The text assigns Ganjiang and Fuzi Decoction.", "Formula for Ganjiang and Fuzi Decoction.", "Ganjiang (dried ginger), 1 liang, fire-processed. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng, strains out the dregs, and records the entire amount as a single dose.", "When body aches continue after induced sweating and the pulse is deep and slow, the text assigns Guizhi Decoction without Shaoyao, with Renshen and Shengjiang added.", "Guizhi Decoction without Shaoyao, with Renshen and Shengjiang added.", "Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang, outer layer removed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split. Renshen (ginseng), 3 liang. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 4 liang, sliced.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou and 2 sheng of water, boils down to 3 sheng, and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "After induced sweating or purging, the text warns against giving Guizhi Decoction again. With sweating and labored breathing but no high fever, it assigns Mahuang, Xingren, Gancao, and Shigao Decoction.", "Formula for Mahuang, Xingren, Gancao, and Shigao Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 4 liang, joints removed. Xingren (apricot kernels), 50, skins and tips removed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Shigao (gypsum), half a jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 7 sheng of water. Ephedra is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng, and the foam is skimmed. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 2 sheng, and the dregs are strained out. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng twice daily."], "notes": [["汗出不徹", "incomplete sweating", "汗出不彻", "「彻」在这里指汗法达到文中所说的充分程度。这一段用脉涩来判断先前汗出不足。", "Che means that sweating has brought about a full release in this account. The passage reads a rough pulse as a sign that the previous sweating was incomplete."], ["陽氣怫鬱在表", "yang qi to be pent up at the exterior", "怫郁在表", "「怫郁」写气郁住、出不去。「在表」说作者把这种郁滞放在表层来理解。", "The phrase describes qi being pent up. At the exterior names the level where the author locates it."], ["尺中脈微", "A faint pulse at the cubit", "尺中脉微", "「尺中」指腕部的尺位。此处把尺脉很微与后一句的「里虚」相连。", "Chizhong refers to the cubit position at the wrist. The faint pulse there is connected to internal deficiency in the next clause."], ["榮行脈中，衛行脈外", "Nutritive qi circulates within the vessels and defensive qi outside them", "营行与卫行", "「榮」通「營」。这两个分句用脉中、脉外区分营气与卫气的运行范围。", "Rong is a variant spelling for the nutritive aspect. The two clauses place nutritive qi within the vessels and defensive qi outside in the text's model."], ["陰陽自和", "yin and yang come into harmony on their own", "阴阳自和", "「自」表示自行恢复协调。前文列举若干已经发生的治疗或耗损，这里说之后观察阴阳能否自和。", "Zi means that harmony returns on its own. In this sentence the question is what happens after the treatments or losses already listed."], ["內外俱虛", "deficiency affecting both the interior and exterior", "内外俱虚", "「俱」说两边都虚。作者将先大下、后发汗与振寒、脉微细串在一起解释。", "Ju means both. The author reads shivering and a faint, fine pulse in light of the purging and sweating that came before."], ["去滓，頓服。", "strains out the dregs, and records the entire amount as a single dose", "去滓与顿服", "「滓」是煎后的药渣。「顿服」表示古方将煎成的量一次服用。", "The dregs are the solids left after boiling. Dunfu means the recipe records the liquid as one dose."], ["綿裹", "wrapped in cotton", "绵裹", "「裹」是包住。此处是石膏条后括号里的古方处理写法。", "Guo means to wrap. The phrase belongs to the historical preparation instructions after the gypsum entry."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 4, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["After excessive sweating, the person crosses their hands over the heart region, feels palpitations below the heart, and wants pressure applied. The text assigns Guizhi and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Guizhi and Gancao Decoction.", "Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 4 liang, outer layer removed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng, strains out the dregs, and records the entire amount as a single dose.", "When throbbing below the navel occurs after induced sweating, the text takes it as the beginning of a running piglet episode and assigns Fuling, Guizhi, Gancao, and Dazao Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling, Guizhi, Gancao, and Dazao Decoction.", "Fuling (poria), half a jin. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 4 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Dazao (jujubes), 15, split.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of aerated water. The poria is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng. The remaining ingredients are added, boiled down to 3 sheng, and strained. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "The historical method for making aerated water starts with 2 dou of water in a large basin. The water is lifted and poured with a ladle until five or six thousand pearl-like bubbles chase one another across the surface.", "In a running piglet episode, the sensation rushes from the lower abdomen up to the throat. During the attack the person feels as if they will die, and then it subsides. The text attributes such episodes to shock and fear.", "For running piglet with qi rushing up into the chest, abdominal pain, and alternating chills and fever, the text assigns Running Piglet Decoction.", "Formula for Running Piglet Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Xiongqiong (ligusticum rhizome), 2 liang. Danggui (Chinese angelica root), 2 liang. Huangqin (skullcap root), 2 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 2 liang. Banxia (pinellia), 4 liang, washed. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 4 liang. Gegen (kudzu root), 5 liang. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang.", "For these nine ingredients, the recipe boils 2 dou of water down to 5 sheng. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times by day and twice at night.", "For abdominal distension and fullness after induced sweating, the text assigns Houpo, Shengjiang, Banxia, Gancao, and Renshen Decoction.", "Formula for Houpo, Shengjiang, Banxia, Gancao, and Renshen Decoction.", "Houpo (magnolia bark), half a jin, roasted and stripped of its outer layer. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), half a jin, sliced. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Renshen (ginseng), 1 liang.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water, boils down to 3 sheng, and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "In cold damage following vomiting or purging, there is upward pressure and fullness below the heart, qi rushing into the chest, dizziness on standing, and a deep, tight pulse. The text says that inducing sweat in this state disturbs the channels and makes the body tremble and sway. It assigns Fuling, Guizhi, Baizhu, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling, Guizhi, Baizhu, and Gancao Decoction.", "Fuling (poria), 4 liang. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 2 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 6 sheng of water, boils down to 3 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into three warm doses.", "If induced sweating fails to resolve the illness and aversion to cold appears instead, the text attributes it to deficiency and assigns Shaoyao, Gancao, and Fuzi Decoction.", "Formula for Shaoyao, Gancao, and Fuzi Decoction.", "Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 3 liang, roasted. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe uses 5 sheng of water, boils down to 1 sheng and 5 he, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into three warm doses.", "If the illness persists after induced sweating or purging and the person is agitated, the text assigns Fuling Sini Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling Sini Decoction.", "Fuling (poria), 6 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 1 and a half liang.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe uses 5 sheng of water, boils down to 3 sheng, and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 7 he three times daily.", "After inducing sweat, the text attributes aversion to cold to deficiency. When there is only heat without aversion to cold, it attributes the pattern to excess and calls for harmonizing stomach qi with Stomach-Regulating Chengqi Decoction, given in the preceding volume.", "In Taiyang illness, profuse sweating after sweat is induced leaves the stomach dry. The person is agitated, cannot sleep, and wants water. The text describes giving water in small amounts and expects recovery as stomach qi comes into harmony. For a floating pulse, difficulty urinating, mild fever, and persistent thirst, it assigns Wuling Powder.", "Formula for Wuling Powder.", "Zhuling (polyporus), 18 zhu, outer layer removed. Zexie (alisma rhizome), 1 liang and 6 zhu. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 18 zhu. Fuling (poria), 18 zhu. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), half a liang.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe pounds them to a powder. It records taking one square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice liquid, three times daily, and drinking ample warm water. The text expects recovery after sweating and refers to the usual aftercare."], "notes": [["叉手自冒心", "crosses their hands over the heart region", "叉手自冒心", "「冒」是覆盖的意思。「叉手」写病人自己用双手护住心口一带。", "Mao means to cover. The phrase describes the person holding crossed hands over the heart region."], ["臍下悸者，欲作奔豚也", "throbbing below the navel", "脐下悸", "「悸」写跳动感。本句特别指出感觉在肚脐以下，接着说这是奔豚将作。", "Ji refers to a throbbing sensation. The passage places it below the navel and links it to the onset of a running piglet episode."], ["作甘瀾水法", "The historical method for making aerated water", "甘澜水", "本段用勺子反复扬水，直到水面出现许多细泡。「甘澜水」是这种历史做法所得的水。", "The recipe describes lifting and pouring water with a ladle until bubbles cover the surface. Ganlan water is the name given to water made in this way."], ["從少腹上沖咽喉", "the sensation rushes from the lower abdomen up to the throat", "奔豚上冲", "本句从下腹写到咽喉，借「冲」形容感觉往上涌。后面补充这阵发作自己可以停。", "The text traces the sensation from the lower abdomen to the throat. Chong conveys its upward rush. The next clause says the episode subsides."], ["身為振振搖", "makes the body tremble and sway", "振振摇", "重叠的「振振」突出身体一阵阵发抖。「摇」继续写身体摇晃。", "The repeated character zhen emphasizes trembling. Yao adds the motion of swaying."], ["不惡寒，但熱者，實也", "only heat without aversion to cold, it attributes the pattern to excess", "但热与实", "「但」表示只有。本段用发汗后怕冷或只有热来分辨作者所说的虚与实。", "Dan means only. The passage uses aversion to cold or heat alone after sweating to distinguish deficiency from excess in its framework."], ["少少與之", "giving water in small amounts", "少少与之", "两个「少」叠用写每次给一点。「之」在这里承接上文所说的水。", "The repeated word shao conveys a little at a time. The object is the water mentioned just before."], ["方寸匙", "one square-inch spoonful", "方寸匙", "这是底本写散剂用量的古量法。英译保留寸的字面形象，方便读者看见原方怎样记量。", "This is a historical measure for a powdered preparation. The literal rendering preserves the original image used to record the amount."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 5, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["In Taiyang disease with a floating and wiry pulse and vexing thirst after sweating has been induced, the text indicates Five Ling Powder, given earlier.", "In cold damage with sweating, thirst, and difficult urination, the text indicates Five Ling Powder. In the absence of thirst it indicates Fuling and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling and Gancao Decoction.", "Fuling, two liang. Guizhi, two liang. Roasted Gancao, one liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained, then divided into three warm portions.", "In wind strike with fever that persists for six or seven days, there is vexation along with exterior and interior signs. If the person is thirsty and wants to drink but vomits water as soon as it is swallowed, this is called water reversal. The text indicates Five Ling Powder, given earlier.", "Before the pulse is taken, the patient crosses both hands to cover the heart region. The teacher tests the patient by asking them to cough. If they do not cough, the text concludes they cannot hear in either ear. It attributes this to deficiency from repeated induction of sweating.", "After sweating has been induced, drinking a great deal of water brings panting. Pouring water over the person likewise brings panting. Inability to take water or medicine by mouth after induced sweating signals an adverse turn. The text says that inducing sweating again then brings unceasing vomiting and diarrhea. After induced sweating, vomiting, or purging, there may be deficiency vexation and inability to sleep. In severe cases the person tosses and turns in distress. The text indicates Gardenia and Ganjiang Decoction. For shortness of breath it indicates Gardenia, Gancao, and Douchi Decoction. For vomiting it indicates Gardenia, Fresh Ginger, and Douchi Decoction.", "Formula for Gardenia and Ganjiang Decoction.", "Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Fresh ginger, two liang, sliced.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives three and a half sheng of water, boiled down to one and a half sheng and strained, then divided into two warm portions. If vomiting follows the first portion, the later portion is omitted.", "Formula for Gardenia, Gancao, and Douchi Decoction.", "Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Xiangchi, four ge, wrapped in cotton.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Gardenia and Gancao are boiled first to yield two and a half sheng. Douchi is added and the liquid boiled down to one and a half sheng, strained, and divided into two portions. It records the first portion taken warm, with the second omitted if vomiting occurs.", "Formula for Gardenia, Fresh Ginger, and Douchi Decoction.", "Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Fresh ginger, five liang. Xiangchi, four ge, wrapped in cotton.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Gardenia and fresh ginger are boiled first to yield two and a half sheng. Douchi is added and the liquid boiled down to one and a half sheng, strained, and divided into two portions. It records the first portion taken warm, with the second omitted if vomiting occurs.", "If inducing sweating or purging is followed by vexing heat and constriction in the chest, the text indicates Gardenia and Douchi Decoction.", "Formula for Gardenia and Douchi Decoction.", "Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Xiangchi, four ge, wrapped in cotton.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Gardenia is boiled first to yield two and a half sheng. Douchi is added and the liquid boiled down to one and a half sheng, strained, and divided into two portions. It records the first portion taken warm, with the second omitted if vomiting occurs.", "If after strong purging on the fifth or sixth day of cold damage the bodily heat persists and there is binding pain in the heart region, the text regards the illness as still unresolved and indicates Gardenia and Douchi Decoction. In cold damage with vexation, abdominal fullness, and discomfort both lying down and getting up after purging, it indicates Gardenia, Houpo, and Zhishi Decoction.", "Formula for Gardenia, Houpo, and Zhishi Decoction.", "Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Houpo, four liang, roasted with the outer layer removed. Four Zhishi fruits, soaked in water and roasted until yellow.", "For the three ingredients above, the text gives three and a half sheng of water, boiled down to one and a half sheng and strained, then divided into two portions. It records the first portion taken warm, with the second omitted if vomiting occurs.", "If the physician uses pills to purge strongly in cold damage and bodily heat persists with mild vexation, the text indicates Gardenia and Ganjiang Decoction, given earlier.", "In general, the text advises against giving Gardenia Decoction when the patient's stools were already somewhat loose.", "If sweating is induced in Taiyang disease and sweating occurs without resolving it, there may still be fever, palpitations below the heart, dizziness, involuntary twitching of the body, and shaking as if about to fall to the ground. The text indicates Zhenwu Decoction.", "Formula for Zhenwu Decoction.", "Fuling, three liang. Shaoyao, three liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Baizhu, two liang. One Fuzi, processed by heating, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng with the dregs removed. It records seven ge taken warm three times daily.", "The text warns against inducing sweating when the throat is dry."], "notes": [["浮弦", "floating and wiry", "浮与弦", "浮讲脉在浅处就能摸到，弦讲脉来有绷直感，两种形容可以并在一个脉象中。", "Floating describes the depth where the pulse is felt. Wiry describes its taut quality. Both can describe the same pulse."], ["表裡證", "exterior and interior signs", "表里", "这里说表、里两方面的表现同时存在。下文接着写想喝水和水入即吐。", "The passage describes signs in both the exterior and the interior. It goes on to describe thirst and vomiting after drinking."], ["水逆", "water reversal", "水逆", "上句已给出这个词的情境。人想饮水，但水一进就被吐出来，所以说逆。", "The preceding clause gives the setting for this term. Water comes back up when the person tries to drink."], ["重發汗", "repeated induction of sweating", "重发汗", "重读 chóng，表示再一次。发汗是采取方法让人出汗。", "重 is pronounced chóng and means again. 發汗 means to bring about sweating."], ["虛煩", "deficiency vexation", "虚烦", "本句把不能入睡的烦放在汗、吐、下以后来写，并用虚概括其背景。", "The text places this sleepless vexation after sweating, vomiting, or purging and describes its background as deficiency."], ["梔子乾薑湯方", "Formula for Gardenia and Ganjiang Decoction", "方名与药味", "方名有乾薑，下行药味却写生薑。两处按底本分别译出。", "The heading names Ganjiang, dried ginger. The next line lists Shengjiang, fresh ginger. Each line is translated as it appears."], ["得吐者，止後服", "If vomiting follows the first portion, the later portion is omitted", "得吐与后服", "上一小句把药液分两服。此句记第一次服后若吐，后面那份便停。", "The preceding clause divides the liquid into two portions. This sentence makes vomiting after the first the condition for omitting the second."], ["身瞤動", "involuntary twitching of the body", "瞤动", "瞤在这里指不由自主的跳动。后面的「振振」另写全身发抖。", "瞤 refers here to involuntary twitching. The following words separately describe shaking."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 6, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["The text warns against inducing sweating in people prone to painful, dribbling urination, saying this invariably brings blood in the urine.", "Even if a person with sores has body pain, the text warns against inducing sweating, saying it brings spasms and rigidity.", "The text warns against inducing sweating in people prone to nosebleeds. It says sweating brings a sunken forehead, an urgent and tight pulse, a fixed stare with inability to blink, and inability to sleep.", "The text warns against inducing sweating in those who have lost blood, saying it brings chills and trembling.", "If sweating is induced again in someone prone to sweating, the text predicts a dazed, confused state and genital pain after urination. It indicates Yuyuliang Pills.", "Formula for Yuyuliang Pills.", "Yuyuliang, four liang. Renshen, three liang. Two Fuzi, processed by heating, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Wuweizi, three ge. Fuling, three liang. Ganjiang, three liang.", "The text records forming the six ingredients into honey pills the size of wutong seeds, with twenty pills per portion.", "If a patient has cold and sweating is induced again, the stomach becomes cold and vomiting is expected.", "In cold damage, the text calls purging before sweating has been induced an adverse sequence. When sweating is induced first, it regards the order as appropriate. In a case initially calling for purging, inducing sweating instead goes against the proper order. Purging first in that case accords with it.", "If a physician purges a person with cold damage and persistent diarrhea with undigested food and body pain follows, the text gives urgent priority to rescuing the interior. If the body pain remains after stools normalize on their own, it gives urgent priority to rescuing the exterior. It indicates Sini Decoction for the interior and Cinnamon Twig Decoction for the exterior, as given in the previous volume.", "If purging first fails to resolve Taiyang disease and sweating is then induced, both exterior and interior become deficient and the person becomes clouded. If sweat emerges spontaneously in this clouded state, the text expects recovery because the exterior has harmonized. If the interior has yet to harmonize, it describes purging again afterward.", "In unresolved Taiyang disease, if both yin and yang pulse aspects are faint, shivering precedes sweating and resolution. If only the yang pulse is faint, sweating precedes resolution. If the yin pulse is replete, the text describes resolution by purging and indicates Stomach-Regulating Qi-Order Decoction, given in the previous volume.", "In Taiyang disease with fever and sweating, the text attributes the sweat to weak nutritive qi and strong defensive qi. It indicates Cinnamon Twig Decoction to address pathogenic wind, as given in the previous volume.", "On the fifth or sixth day of cold damage or wind strike, there may be alternating chills and fever, oppressive fullness of the chest and flanks, quiet withdrawal with no desire to eat or drink, vexation, and a tendency to vomit. Other possible signs are vexation in the chest without vomiting, thirst, abdominal pain, obstructed hardness below the ribs, palpitations below the heart with difficult urination, slight bodily heat without thirst, or cough. The text indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Chaihu, half a jin. Huangqin, three liang. Renshen, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Thirteen jujubes, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, boiled down to six sheng. The dregs are removed and the strained liquid is boiled again to yield three sheng. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "The text lists modifications. For chest vexation without vomiting it omits Banxia and Renshen and adds one Gualou fruit. For thirst it omits Banxia, raises Renshen to four and a half liang in total, and adds four liang of Gualou root. For abdominal pain it omits Huangqin and adds three liang of Shaoyao. For obstructed hardness below the ribs it omits jujubes and adds four liang of Muli. For palpitations below the heart with difficult urination it omits Huangqin and adds four liang of Fuling. For slight exterior heat without thirst it omits Renshen and adds three liang of Guizhi, and describes keeping the person warmly covered until mild sweating brings relief. For cough it omits Renshen, jujubes, and fresh ginger, adding half a sheng of Wuweizi and two liang of Ganjiang.", "When blood is weak and qi depleted, the interstices open, allowing pathogenic qi to enter. It contends with correct qi and binds below the ribs. As the two contend, chills and fever alternate, with periods of onset and remission, and the person becomes quiet and unwilling to eat or drink. The zang and fu organs are connected, so the pain necessarily extends downward. The text explains vomiting through a pathogenic influence high up and pain below. It indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction. Thirst after taking the decoction is assigned to Yangming and addressed accordingly, as noted earlier."], "notes": [["淋家", "people prone to painful, dribbling urination", "淋家", "这几条的「家」指常有某种情况的人。淋说排尿淋沥、涩痛的一类表现。", "家 refers here to a person prone to a condition. 淋 names a pattern of dribbling and painful urination."], ["衄家", "people prone to nosebleeds", "衄家", "衄在这里是鼻出血。后面的亡血家和汗家也用同一构词法。", "衄 here means nosebleed. The next two lines use the same construction for those who have lost blood or are prone to sweating."], ["恍惚心亂", "a dazed, confused state", "恍惚", "这句用恍惚和心乱来形容神志不安定的状态，接着另外写小便后的疼痛。", "The phrase describes clouded, unsettled awareness. The sentence then separately describes pain after urination."], ["救裡", "rescuing the interior", "先里后表", "这一条在泄泻和身痛并见时先说救里。等排便自行恢复而身痛仍在，句子转到救表。", "When diarrhea and body pain occur together, the line gives priority to the interior. It turns to the exterior once stools normalize and the pain remains."], ["脈陰陽俱微", "both yin and yang pulse aspects are faint", "俱微", "俱是两边都如此。接下来的两句分别写只有阳脉微、阴脉实，读时可把三种条件排在一起。", "俱 means both. The next clauses separately consider faintness of the yang aspect alone and repletion of the yin aspect."], ["榮弱衛強", "weak nutritive qi and strong defensive qi", "营卫", "榮指营，衛指卫。本句用营弱、卫强的组合来说明汗出。", "榮 names nutritive qi and 衛 names defensive qi. The text explains sweating through weakness of one and strength of the other."], ["嘿嘿不欲飲食", "quiet withdrawal with no desire to eat or drink", "嘿嘿", "嘿嘿形容默然、少言，接着写没有饮食的愿望。它和前后症候合在一起描写病人的状态。", "嘿嘿 conveys becoming quiet or withdrawn. The next words add a lack of desire to eat or drink."], ["腠理開", "the interstices open", "腠理", "腠理在这里是身体表层的间隙。古文用开来解释后面邪气得以进入。", "The interstices here are spaces at the body's surface. The passage pictures them opening and allowing pathogenic qi to enter."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 7, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["On the sixth or seventh day of Taiyang disease, there is a slow, floating, weak pulse, aversion to wind and cold, and warmth of the hands and feet. If the physician purges two or three times and the person cannot eat, with fullness and pain below the ribs, yellowing of the face, eyes, and body, a stiff nape, and difficulty urinating, the text indicates Bupleurum Decoction. It predicts heaviness and urgency at the anus afterward. If the person was thirsty from the outset and vomited after drinking water, it considers Bupleurum Decoction unsuitable. Eating grain then brings hiccups.", "On the fourth or fifth day of cold damage, with bodily heat, aversion to wind, stiff nape, fullness below the ribs, warm hands and feet, and thirst, the text indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction, given earlier.", "In cold damage with a rough yang pulse and a wiry yin pulse, the text expects acute, cramping abdominal pain. It gives priority to Minor Center-Fortifying Decoction and indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction if the first does not resolve the disorder, as given earlier.", "Formula for Minor Center-Fortifying Decoction.", "Guizhi, three liang. Shaoyao, six liang. Gancao, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split. Malt syrup, one sheng.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water. Five ingredients are boiled first to yield three sheng. The dregs are removed and the malt syrup added, then dissolved over low heat. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily. The text excludes people prone to vomiting on account of the sweetness.", "If the Bupleurum pattern occurs in cold damage or wind strike, the text says a single sign can establish the pattern. The full set need not be present.", "If a condition indicating Bupleurum Decoction is mistakenly purged and the Bupleurum signs persist, the text describes giving the decoction again. A sensation of mounting heat and trembling is followed by renewed fever, sweating, and relief.", "In cold damage of two or three days with palpitations and vexation, the text indicates Minor Center-Fortifying Decoction, given earlier.", "If Taiyang disease goes beyond its course for more than ten days and is nevertheless purged two or three times, the text gives priority to Minor Bupleurum Decoction if the Bupleurum signs remain four or five days later. Persistent vomiting, tightness below the heart, and subdued vexation indicate that the illness has yet to resolve. The text then describes resolution after purging with Major Bupleurum Decoction.", "Formula for Major Bupleurum Decoction.", "Chaihu, half a jin. Huangqin, three liang. Shaoyao, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, five liang, sliced. Four roasted Zhishi fruits. Twelve jujubes, split. Dahuang, two liang.", "For these eight ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, boiled down to six sheng. The dregs are removed and the liquid boiled again. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "When cold damage persists for thirteen days, with fullness of the chest and flanks, vomiting, late-afternoon tidal fever, then mild diarrhea, the text says the original pattern was a Bupleurum pattern. It expects purging to yield no diarrhea in this case and takes the unexpected diarrhea as evidence that the physician used purgative pills inappropriately. Tidal fever indicates excess. The text gives Minor Bupleurum Decoction first to resolve the exterior, then Bupleurum Decoction with Mangxiao.", "Formula for Bupleurum Decoction with Mangxiao.", "Chaihu, two liang and sixteen zhu. Huangqin, one liang. Renshen, one liang. Roasted Gancao, one liang. Fresh ginger, one liang, sliced. Mangxiao, two liang. Four jujubes. Banxia, twenty zhu, washed.", "For these eight ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng. The dregs are removed and Mangxiao is added. The liquid is brought back to a gentle boil and divided into two warm portions. If the disorder persists, another batch is recorded.", "With delirious speech on the thirteenth day of cold damage, beyond its expected course, the text attributes it to heat and describes purging with a decoction. When urine passes freely, it expects hard stool and takes unexpected diarrhea as evidence of inappropriate purging with pills. With spontaneous diarrhea it expects a faint, failing pulse. If the pulse is harmonious instead, it reads this as interior excess and indicates Stomach-Regulating Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "When Taiyang disease remains unresolved and heat binds in the bladder, the person behaves as if manic. The text says bleeding may occur on its own and bring relief. If the exterior remains unresolved, it gives priority to resolving the exterior before attacking the accumulation. Once the exterior has resolved, if tight binding in the lower abdomen is the only remaining sign, the text indicates Taoren Qi-Order Decoction to attack it.", "Formula for Taoren Qi-Order Decoction.", "Fifty peach kernels, with the skins and tips removed. Dahuang, four liang. Guizhi, two liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Mangxiao, two liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water. Four ingredients are boiled first to yield two sheng. The dregs are removed, Mangxiao is added, and the liquid is brought back to a gentle boil and taken off the heat. The text records five ge taken warm before food three times daily, with mild loosening of the stool expected."], "notes": [["下重", "heaviness and urgency at the anus", "下重", "下重指下坠、想排便的沉重感觉。这里把它放在服柴胡汤后的叙述里。", "The phrase describes downward heaviness and an urge to pass stool. Here it appears among the changes after the decoction."], ["膠飴", "Malt syrup", "胶饴", "这味在煎法中简称「饴」，先煮其他药，再把饴放进药液中使它化开。", "The preparation shortens the name to 飴. It describes boiling the other ingredients first and then dissolving the syrup."], ["但見一證便是", "a single sign can establish the pattern", "一证与悉具", "但在这里有「只」的意思，下一句说一组表现无需全部具备。", "但 means only here. The next clause says that the full set of signs does not have to be present."], ["蒸蒸而振", "A sensation of mounting heat and trembling", "蒸蒸而振", "蒸蒸写热势渐起，振写发抖。接下来又按发热、汗出、解的顺序写变化。", "蒸蒸 describes heat building and 振 describes trembling. The sentence goes on to give the sequence of fever, sweating, and relief."], ["日晡所發潮熱", "late-afternoon tidal fever", "日晡与潮热", "日晡是下午偏晚的时候。潮热像潮水按时而来，此句特地给出它出现的时段。", "日晡 is late afternoon. Tidal fever evokes heat that rises at a regular time, specified here as late afternoon."], ["銖", "zhu", "铢", "铢是古代衡量重量的单位。本方一处两、铢并用，另一处只写铢，英译保留原单位。", "Zhu is a historical unit of weight. The formula uses both liang and zhu for one ingredient and zhu alone for another."], ["如狂", "behaves as if manic", "如狂", "如是好像。作者以「如狂」形容行为，再同热结、出血放在一起来看。", "如 means as if. The text describes behavior in those terms alongside heat binding and bleeding."], ["少腹急結", "tight binding in the lower abdomen", "少腹", "少腹指下腹。此处先交代外已解，再说只留下下腹的急结。", "少腹 means the lower abdomen. This line first says the exterior has resolved and then gives tight binding below as the remaining sign."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 8, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["If cold damage is purged on the eighth or ninth day and there is chest fullness, vexation, fright, difficulty urinating, delirious speech, and heaviness of the entire body with inability to turn onto one's side, the text indicates Bupleurum Decoction with Longgu and Muli.", "Formula for Bupleurum Decoction with Longgu and Muli.", "Chaihu, four liang. Longgu, Huangqin, fresh ginger, Renshen, peeled Guizhi, and Fuling, one and a half liang each. Banxia, two and a half ge, washed. Dahuang, two liang. Muli, one and a half liang. Six jujubes, split. Qiandan, red lead, one and a half liang.", "For these twelve ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled to yield four sheng. Dahuang, cut into pieces shaped like game pieces, is added and the liquid boiled briefly once or twice. The dregs are removed. The text records one sheng taken warm three times by day and once at night.", "In cold damage with abdominal fullness, delirious speech, a floating and tight pulse at the inch opening, and a wiry pulse at the bar, the text says the liver encroaches on the spleen. It calls this longitudinal encroachment and describes needling Qimen.", "In cold damage with fever, a shivery aversion to cold, and strong thirst, the text expects abdominal fullness, spontaneous sweating, and difficult urination. If the pulse at the inch opening is floating and rough and at the bar is wiry and urgent, the text says the liver encroaches on the lung. It calls this transverse encroachment and describes needling Qimen.", "On the second day of Taiyang disease, if the person is restless and the back is heated instead, profuse sweating follows. Fire heat enters the stomach and its fluids are exhausted. The person becomes agitated and develops delirious speech. Shivering and spontaneous diarrhea after more than ten days are read as signs of impending resolution. If there is no sweating below the waist, the person wants to urinate but cannot, vomits instead, and is on the verge of losing control of urination. The soles are averse to wind, the stool is hard, and urine, though expected to be frequent, is neither frequent nor abundant. After a bowel movement, head pain comes on sharply. The soles become hot. The text attributes this to grain qi flowing downward.", "When sweating is forced with fire in Taiyang wind strike, pathogenic wind is subjected to fire heat. Blood and qi spill over and lose their normal measure. The two yang influences scorch together and the body turns yellow. When yang prevails a nosebleed threatens, and when yin is deficient urination becomes difficult. Exhaustion of both yin and yang leaves the body parched. Sweat appears only on the head and stops at the neck. The abdomen is full, with slight panting, dry mouth and sores in the throat, and sometimes no bowel movement. Prolonged cases bring delirious speech. Severe cases progress to hiccups, restless hands and feet, and picking at the clothes and bed. The text regards free urination as a sign that the person can be treated and indicates Renshen, Dihuang, Longgu, Muli, and Fuling Decoction.", "Formula for Renshen, Dihuang, Longgu, Muli, and Fuling Decoction.", "Renshen, three liang. Dihuang, half a jin. Longgu, three liang. Muli, four liang. Fuling, four liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, boiled down to three sheng and divided into three warm portions.", "If the pulse is floating in cold damage and the physician forces sweating with fire, the text predicts loss of yang, fright, and manic behavior. When the person cannot rest lying down or getting up, it indicates Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Shaoyao, with Muli and Longgu to Counter Mistreatment.", "Formula for Cinnamon Twig Decoction without Shaoyao, with Muli and Longgu to Counter Mistreatment.", "Guizhi, three liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split. Muli, five liang, heated. Longgu, four liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "The appearance resembles cold damage, but the pulse is weak instead of wiry and tight. The text expects thirst with weakness and delirious speech if fire is applied. If there is weakness with fever and a floating pulse, it expects resolution with sweating.", "If Taiyang disease is treated with fire fumes and no sweat emerges, the person becomes restless. If it fails to resolve when the expected course is reached, the text predicts passing blood in the stool and calls it a fire pathogen.", "A floating pulse with intense heat indicates excess. If fire moxibustion is applied anyway, the text calls this treating excess as deficiency. Fire stirs the disorder, leading to a dry throat and spitting blood.", "The text cautions against moxibustion when the pulse is faint and rapid. Fire can act as a pathogenic influence and bring vexation and counterflow. It pursues deficiency and drives excess, and blood scatters within the vessels. Though the fire qi is slight, it attacks strongly within, scorching bone and injuring sinews, and the blood is slow to recover.", "For a floating pulse the text associates sweating with relief. With fire moxibustion instead, the pathogenic influence has no way out and grows stronger through the fire. Heaviness and obstruction below the waist are called fire mistreatment. If the illness is about to resolve on its own, vexation occurs first, then sweating brings relief. The text bases this expectation on the floating pulse."], "notes": [["鉛丹", "Qiandan, red lead", "铅丹", "这里按底本记下方中所列矿物名。铅丹含铅，有毒，阅读古方时要把药名的记载和现代安全性分开理解。", "This is the mineral named in the source. Red lead contains toxic lead. Its presence is part of the historical record."], ["關上弦", "a wiry pulse at the bar", "关上弦", "关是寸、关、尺三个腕部位置中的中间一处。「弦」形容此处摸到的脉有弓弦般的绷直感。", "The bar is the middle of the three wrist positions. Wiry describes a taut, bowstring-like quality felt there."], ["名曰縱", "longitudinal encroachment", "纵与横", "这一条把肝乘脾称纵。下一条把肝乘肺称横，术语随着所涉及的脏而变。", "The text calls liver encroaching on spleen longitudinal. In the next line it calls liver encroaching on lung transverse."], ["火劫發汗", "sweating is forced with fire", "火劫", "劫写强行施用，随后一段描述出汗以后怎样出现一系列变化。", "劫 conveys a forced intervention. The passage then follows the series of changes the text attributes to it."], ["劑頸而還", "stops at the neck", "汗齐颈", "上句已经把出汗范围限在头。此句进一步把边界写在颈部。", "The preceding words locate sweating at the head. This phrase places its lower boundary at the neck."], ["撚衣摸床", "picking at the clothes and bed", "捻衣摸床", "字面是手捻衣服、摸索床铺。古文把这些动作接在谵语和四肢躁动之后。", "The visible actions involve picking at clothing and feeling about on the bed. The text lists them after delirious speech and restless limbs."], ["清血", "passing blood in the stool", "清血", "此处的清依上下文作排便解。下句另写唾血，血出现的途径不同。", "Here 清 is read as passing stool. The following line describes spitting blood, a different route."], ["火逆", "fire mistreatment", "火逆", "逆在这里表示施治与病情相违。原文把腰以下沉重和痹阻列为它的表现。", "逆 here suggests treatment that runs counter to the condition. The text links it with heaviness and obstruction below the waist."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-16", "page": 9, "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part II", "english": ["If heated needling is used to bring on sweating and the needle site is exposed to cold, a raised red lump is taken as a sign that running piglet will develop. If qi surges from the lower abdomen toward the heart, the text records one moxa cone on each lump and Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Extra Guizhi.", "Formula for Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Extra Guizhi.", "Guizhi, five liang. Shaoyao, three liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng with the dregs removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "If fire mistreatment is followed by purging and heated needling causes restlessness, the text indicates Guizhi, Gancao, Longgu, and Muli Decoction.", "Formula for Guizhi, Gancao, Longgu, and Muli Decoction.", "Guizhi, one liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Longgu, two liang. Muli, two liang, heated.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily, with three liang of Renshen added in severe cases.", "In Taiyang cold damage, the text says warm needling invariably brings fright.", "In Taiyang disease, aversion to cold and fever are expected. If there is spontaneous sweating instead, with neither aversion to cold nor fever and a fine, rapid pulse at the bar, the text blames excessive use of induced vomiting. If vomiting was induced on day one or two, the person feels hungry but cannot eat. If induced on day three or four, the person dislikes gruel, wants cold food, and vomits in the evening what was eaten in the morning. This is called a minor adverse effect of treatment. If there is no aversion to cold and the person does not want clothes against the skin, this is called interior vexation. The text attributes all of these to the physician inducing vomiting.", "A rapid pulse generally indicates heat and is expected to go with the ability to digest grain. If the person wants food and then vomits instead, the text explains the rapid pulse through weakened yang qi and deficiency of qi around the diaphragm after induced sweating. The rapid pulse indicates guest heat. The person cannot digest grain and vomits because the stomach is deficient and cold.", "If Taiyang disease persists beyond its course for more than ten days, there may be persistent queasiness, chest pain, unexpectedly loose stool, slight abdominal fullness, and subdued vexation. If there was already severe spontaneous vomiting and diarrhea before this point, the text indicates Stomach-Regulating Qi-Order Decoction. Otherwise it excludes it. If there is only a desire to retch, chest pain, and slightly loose stool, the text excludes the Bupleurum pattern, reading the vomiting as evidence of severe prior vomiting and diarrhea. The formula is given in the previous volume.", "On the sixth or seventh day of Taiyang disease, if exterior signs remain and the pulse is faint and deep, there is unexpectedly no chest binding, but the person becomes manic, the text locates heat in the lower burner. It expects the lower abdomen to be hard and full. If urine passes freely, it takes passage of blood below as the condition for recovery. It explains this as Taiyang following its channel, with stagnant heat in the interior, and indicates Didang Decoction.", "Formula for Didang Decoction.", "Thirty Shuizhi, heated. Thirty Mengchong, with wings and legs removed, heated. Twenty peach kernels, with the skins and tips removed. Dahuang, three liang, washed with wine.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm and a further portion if there is no purgative effect.", "In Taiyang disease with a yellow body, a deep and bound pulse, and hardness of the lower abdomen, difficult urination leads the text to exclude a blood pattern. If urine passes freely and the person behaves as if manic, the text takes the blood pattern as established and indicates Didang Decoction, given earlier.", "In cold damage with heat and lower abdominal fullness, difficult urination is expected. If urine passes freely instead, the text reads this as a blood pattern and calls for purging. It specifies Didang Pills for this purpose.", "Formula for Didang Pills.", "Twenty Shuizhi, heated. Twenty Mengchong, with wings and legs removed, heated. Twenty-five peach kernels, with the skins and tips removed. Dahuang, three liang, washed with wine.", "The text records pounding the four ingredients and dividing them into four pills. One pill is boiled in one sheng of water to yield seven ge, which is taken. Within a full day, blood is expected to pass. If nothing passes, the text records a further portion.", "In Taiyang disease with free urination, the text says drinking a great deal of water invariably brings palpitations below the heart. If urine is scant, there is distressing tightness within."], "notes": [["奔豚", "running piglet", "奔豚", "这名字用小猪奔跑比喻气势骤起。下一句写从少腹往心部上冲，交代奔走的方向。", "The name likens a sudden rush to a running piglet. The next line traces its course upward from the lower abdomen toward the heart."], ["各一壯", "one moxa cone on each lump", "壮", "壮用来数灸的施用次数或灸炷。这里的「各」把一壮分别配到每个针处的核。", "壯 counts moxa applications or cones. 各 distributes one to each lump at a needle site."], ["桂枝加桂湯", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Extra Guizhi", "桂枝加桂", "方名里的「加桂」写在桂枝汤名后。下面列出桂枝五两，可以顺着方名找出所加的是哪一味。", "The title adds Extra Guizhi to the Cinnamon Twig formula. The ingredients below identify Guizhi as the component being increased."], ["客熱", "guest heat", "客热", "前面先说脉数通常主热。下面用「客热」解释为何这一例虽脉快，仍写胃虚冷和食入而吐。", "The passage first associates a rapid pulse with heat. Guest heat explains how the rapid pulse can accompany a deficient, cold stomach and vomiting."], ["少腹當鞕滿", "the lower abdomen to be hard and full", "鞕满", "鞕就是硬。这里在原文判断热在下焦之后，用硬和满写下腹触感与状态。", "鞕 is a form of 硬, hard. After locating heat in the lower burner, the text describes the lower abdomen as hard and full."], ["脈沉結", "a deep and bound pulse", "沉与结", "沉指脉要按到较深才能感到。结提示脉行有停顿，这两字分别写深浅和节律。", "Deep describes where the pulse is felt under pressure. Bound describes an interrupted rhythm. The two terms describe different aspects."], ["血證諦也", "the blood pattern as established", "谛", "諦在这里有确实的意思。这句把小便自利和如狂合在一起作为判断血证的线索。", "諦 conveys certainty here. The text treats free urination together with manic-like behavior as evidence for its blood pattern."], ["晬時", "Within a full day", "晬时", "晬时指经过昼夜一周。本句用它来交代文本预期的排出时间。", "晬時 denotes a full day and night. Here it gives the time by which the text expects passage of blood."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-20", "page": 1, "title": "Greater Yin Diseases", "english": ["Greater Yin disease is characterized here by abdominal fullness and vomiting, inability to keep food down, worsening spontaneous diarrhea, and intermittent abdominal pain. The text warns that purging in this presentation brings binding and hardness below the chest.", "When wind strikes Greater Yin, the limbs are painfully restless. If the yang pulse is faint and the yin pulse is rough and long, the text takes this as approaching recovery.", "For Greater Yin disease with a floating pulse, the text allows a sweating approach and names Guìzhī Decoction.", "Formula record: Guìzhī Decoction.", "The record lists guìzhī, three liǎng; sháoyào, three liǎng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method reduces seven shēng of water to three and removes the dregs. It records a warm serving of one shēng and, shortly afterwards, one shēng of hot porridge to support it. It describes warm covering to bring on sweating and another serving if sweat does not appear.", "Diarrhea without thirst is placed under Greater Yin, on the reasoning that there is cold in a zang organ. The text describes warming and names formulas of the Rectify the Middle and Reverse Frigid Extremities types.", "In cold damage, a floating, relaxed pulse with warm hands and feet is linked to Greater Yin. Greater Yin is expected here to produce yellowing of the body, but free urination is said to prevent it. By day seven or eight, even if there is sudden vexation and diarrhea more than ten times in a day, the text expects it to stop on its own as the spleen system is full and decayed waste is being expelled.", "If the illness was originally Greater Yang but a physician purged it, the ensuing abdominal fullness and intermittent pain are placed under Greater Yin. The text names Guìzhī Decoction with Sháoyào Added, and for pronounced pain of excess it names Guìzhī Decoction with Dàhuáng Added.", "Formula record: Guìzhī Decoction with Sháoyào Added.", "The record lists guìzhī, three liǎng; sháoyào, six liǎng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method reduces seven shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "Formula record: Guìzhī Decoction with Dàhuáng Added.", "The record lists guìzhī, three liǎng; dàhuáng, two liǎng; sháoyào, six liǎng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method reduces seven shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "In Greater Yin disease with a weak pulse and continuing loose bowel movements, the text says to reduce dàhuáng or sháoyào if employed. Its reason is that weak stomach qi is easily disturbed.", "In Greater Yin disease with unexpectedly hard stools, distention, and abdominal fullness, the text attributes the pattern to spleen qi failing to turn and names Báizhú, Zhǐshí, Dried Ginger, and White Honey Decoction. For shortness of breath without distention or fullness it names the Five-Ingredient Huángqí Decoction with Dried Ginger and Bànxià Added.", "Formula record: Báizhú, Zhǐshí, Dried Ginger, and White Honey Decoction.", "The record lists báizhú, three liǎng; zhǐshí, one and a half liǎng; dried ginger, one liǎng; and white honey, two liǎng.", "The historical method simmers the first three ingredients in six shēng of water, removes the dregs, and obtains three shēng. The white honey is dissolved in the liquid. The text records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "Formula record: Five-Ingredient Huángqí Decoction with Dried Ginger and Bànxià Added.", "The record lists huángqí, guìzhī, and sháoyào, three liǎng each; sliced fresh ginger, six liǎng; twelve split jujubes; dried ginger, three liǎng; and rinsed bànxià, half a shēng.", "The historical method reduces one dǒu of water to five shēng, removes the dregs, reduces it again to three shēng, and divides it into three warm servings.", "In Greater Yin disease, thirst with an urge to drink followed by vomiting immediately after drinking is described as water above the diaphragm. The text names Bànxià and Fúlíng Decoction.", "Formula record: Bànxià and Fúlíng Decoction.", "The record lists rinsed bànxià, one shēng; fúlíng, four liǎng; zéxiè, two liǎng; and dried ginger, one liǎng.", "The historical method reduces four shēng of water to three and removes the dregs, dividing it into two warm servings. The text expects recovery when urination flows freely.", "In Greater Yin disease, diarrhea, thirst, and a vacuous, slightly rapid pulse are described as injury to the body fluids. The text names Rénshēn, Báizhú, Sháoyào, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "Formula record: Rénshēn, Báizhú, Sháoyào, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists rénshēn, báizhú, and sháoyào, three liǎng each, and processed gāncǎo, two liǎng.", "The historical method reduces five shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "If Greater Yin disease presents without diarrhea or retching, but with a large and distended abdomen, the text calls this fullness of spleen qi and names Four-Ingredient Hòupò Decoction.", "Formula record: Four-Ingredient Hòupò Decoction.", "The record lists processed hòupò, two liǎng; three toasted zhǐshí; rinsed bànxià, half a shēng; and jú pí, one liǎng."], "notes": [["太陰之為病", "Greater Yin disease is characterized", "太阴病", "六经分类中的太阴在这一篇有自己的起始条文。初读先记它列出的腹满、吐、食不下、下利与腹痛。", "The opening defines how this chapter presents Greater Yin disease. Begin with the cluster of fullness, vomiting, inability to keep food down, loose stools, and pain."], ["自利益甚", "worsening spontaneous diarrhea", "自利益甚", "「自利」指自发下利，「益甚」指更加严重。这里的「利」说的是排便表现。", "Zìlì is spontaneous diarrhea; yì shèn means getting more severe."], ["若下之", "purging in this presentation", "下之", "「下」在这类古文里可作动词，指使用使大便下行的古代治法。这里的「若」引出作者所说的后果。", "Xià is a verb for purging in historical medical vocabulary. The conditional clause then states what the text expects to follow."], ["系在太陰", "linked to Greater Yin", "系在太阴", "「系在」是归属的说法。作者把前面几项脉与手足的表现合看，然后归入太阴。", "The phrase assigns the preceding cluster of pulse and extremity signs to the Greater Yin category."], ["脾家實", "spleen system is full", "脾家", "这里的「脾家」是在说本书脾所统摄的一类功能。「实」要结合下一句腐秽当去来理解。", "Spleen system is a classical functional category. Read the fullness with the next phrase about expelling decayed waste."], ["大實痛", "pronounced pain of excess", "实痛", "这句把间歇的腹满时痛和另一种较重的实痛并列，说明两种命名各自指向前述的不同情况。", "This follows the first description of fullness with intermittent pain and gives a second, more pronounced pattern."], ["水在膈上", "water above the diaphragm", "水在膈上", "前面写想喝水，喝后即吐，接着才提出「水在膈上」作为古书的解释。", "The text first describes thirst and immediate vomiting after drinking, then proposes an upper location for water to explain it."], ["津液傷", "injury to the body fluids", "津液", "津液是古书对身体液体的一组总称。此条用下利、口渴和脉的表现来作联读。", "The classical term covers bodily fluids broadly. Here it connects the signs of diarrhea, thirst, and pulse quality."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-20", "page": 2, "title": "Greater Yin Diseases", "english": ["The historical method reduces five shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "If Greater Yin disease presents without vomiting or fullness, but with uncontrollable passage of stool, the text attributes it to deficiency and names Rectify the Middle Decoction with Huángqí Added.", "Formula record: Rectify the Middle Decoction with Huángqí Added.", "The record lists rénshēn, báizhú, dried ginger, processed gāncǎo, and huángqí, three liǎng each.", "The historical method reduces eight shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "In Greater Yin disease with nausea without vomiting, diarrhea sometimes severe and sometimes less frequent, and a floating, rough pulse, the text names Guìzhī Decoction with Sháoyào Removed and Fúlíng and Báizhú Added.", "Formula record: Guìzhī Decoction with Sháoyào Removed and Fúlíng and Báizhú Added.", "The record lists guìzhī, three liǎng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; fúlíng, three liǎng; báizhú, three liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method reduces eight shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "In Greater Yin disease with retching, cold abdominal pain, thunderous rumbling with diarrhea, and a deep, tight pulse, the text names Minor Cháihú Decoction with Fúlíng and Báizhú Added.", "Formula record: Minor Cháihú Decoction with Fúlíng and Báizhú Added.", "The record lists cháihú, half a jīn; huángqín and rénshēn, three liǎng each; rinsed bànxià, half a shēng; processed gāncǎo and sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng each; twelve split jujubes; and fúlíng and báizhú, three liǎng each.", "The historical method reduces one dǒu and two shēng of water to six shēng, removes the dregs, reduces it again to three shēng, and records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "For Greater Yin disease with retained food and a slippery, full pulse, the text describes purging with an Order the Qi formula. If the stool is loose, it names Hòupò, Zhǐshí, Báizhú, and Gāncǎo Decoction instead.", "Formula record: Hòupò, Zhǐshí, Báizhú, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists hòupò and zhǐshí, three liǎng each, and báizhú and gāncǎo, two liǎng each.", "The historical method reduces six shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and records three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "The expected time of resolution for Greater Yin disease is given as the period from hài through chǒu."], "notes": [["遺矢無度", "uncontrollable passage of stool", "遗矢无度", "「矢」在这一句指粪便。「遗」是不由自主排出。", "In this expression 矢 denotes stool, and 遺 indicates involuntary passage."], ["欲吐不吐", "nausea without vomiting", "欲吐不吐", "把「欲」读成想要或将要，整句指有欲吐的感觉，却没有实际吐出来。", "Yù describes the urge to vomit. The second half says that vomiting does not occur."], ["時甚時疏", "sometimes severe and sometimes less frequent", "时甚时疏", "两个「时」都表示有时，写的是下利时重时缓的变化。", "The repeated 時 alternates two observations: sometimes severe, sometimes less frequent."], ["雷鳴下利", "thunderous rumbling with diarrhea", "雷鸣下利", "「雷鸣」形容腹中响声，并和下利一起出现。", "Thunder is an image for loud abdominal rumbling, here accompanying diarrhea."], ["宿食", "retained food", "宿食", "这里是食物留滞的古代分类。下一句另写大便溏的情形，读时要留意转折。", "Retained food is a classical category. The following conditional distinguishes the loose-stool presentation."], ["從亥至丑", "from hài through chǒu", "亥至丑", "亥、子、丑是连续的地支时段。作者用这个夜间范围表示预期病势转解的时段。", "Hài, zǐ, and chǒu are consecutive branch hours spanning the night. Here they mark the interval in which improvement is expected."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-17", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part III", "page": 1, "english": ["A question: What do organ binding and chest binding look like? The teacher replied that organ binding has a floating inch pulse and a small, fine, deep, tight bar pulse. Chest binding hurts under pressure and has a floating inch pulse and a deep bar pulse. Asked what organ binding means, the teacher says each of the five zang systems has its own form, with distinctions of cold and heat. The text traces these to the blood aspect, even where the binding appears to involve qi.", "For binding in the liver system, the text describes pain on both sides beneath the ribs and vomiting. With a deep, wiry, bound pulse, it assigns Evodia Decoction. Fever without vomiting is classified as excess, with an expected deep, wiry, tense pulse. The text assigns Cinnamon Twig, Danggui, Moutan Bark, Peach Kernel, and Immature Bitter Orange Decoction.", "Evodia Decoction", "Evodia fruit 1 shēng, ginseng 3 liǎng, fresh ginger 6 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text reduces 7 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 2 shēng and strains the liquid. It lists 7 hé warm three times a day.", "Cinnamon Twig, Danggui, Moutan Bark, Peach Kernel, and Immature Bitter Orange Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng (outer layer removed), danggui 2 liǎng, moutan bark 3 liǎng, 20 peach kernels (skins and tips removed), and immature bitter orange 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "For binding in the heart system, there is pain in the heart region or an oppressive, unsettled feeling below the breastbone, with a large, rough pulse. The text assigns Forsythia, Donkey-Hide Gelatin, Pinellia, and Adzuki Bean Decoction. Burning pain in the heart region with agitation and a large, wiry, tense pulse is classified as excess. The text assigns Coptis, Donkey-Hide Gelatin, Pinellia, Peach Kernel, and Poria Decoction.", "Forsythia, Donkey-Hide Gelatin, Pinellia, and Adzuki Bean Decoction", "Forsythia fruit 2 liǎng, donkey-hide gelatin 1½ liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and adzuki beans 3 liǎng.", "Of the four ingredients, the text first boils the three other than the gelatin in 4 shēng of water until 2 shēng remain. It strains the liquid and dissolves the gelatin in it. It lists 7 hé warm three times a day.", "Coptis, Donkey-Hide Gelatin, Pinellia, Peach Kernel, and Poria Decoction", "Coptis 3 liǎng, donkey-hide gelatin 2 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), 20 peach kernels (skins and tips removed), and poria 3 liǎng.", "The text boils the four ingredients other than gelatin in 5 shēng of water until 2 shēng remain. It strains the liquid and dissolves the gelatin in it. It lists 1 shēng warm twice a day.", "For binding in the lung system, the text describes a blocked feeling in the chest, panting, coughing, a tendency to sadness, and a short, rough pulse. It assigns Lily Bulb, Fritillaria, Poria, and Platycodon Decoction. Coughing up blood with chest pain is classed as excess. It assigns Tingli Seed, Trichosanthes, Platycodon, and Moutan Bark Decoction.", "Lily Bulb, Fritillaria, Poria, and Platycodon Decoction", "7 lily bulbs (washed to remove foam), fritillaria 3 liǎng, poria 3 liǎng, and platycodon root 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 7 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "Tingli Seed, Trichosanthes, Platycodon, and Moutan Bark Decoction", "Tingli seed 3 liǎng (dry-roasted), 1 large trichosanthes fruit (crushed), platycodon root 3 liǎng, and moutan bark 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "For binding in the spleen system, the abdomen feels painfully full. Under pressure it feels like an overturned cup. In severe cases the abdomen is enlarged and hard, with a deep, tight pulse. The text assigns White Atractylodes, Immature Bitter Orange, Peach Kernel, and Dried Ginger Decoction. If abdominal distention and pain cannot bear pressure, stool is initially loose and then hard, and the person passes gas, the text classifies this as excess and assigns Rhubarb, Magnolia Bark, Immature Bitter Orange, Pinellia, and Licorice Decoction. The Chinese transcription marks its reading of the gas expression as uncertain.", "White Atractylodes, Immature Bitter Orange, Peach Kernel, and Dried Ginger Decoction", "White atractylodes 2 liǎng, immature bitter orange 2 liǎng, 27 peach kernels (skins and tips removed), and dried ginger 1 liǎng.", "The text reduces 5 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 2 shēng, strains, and divides the liquid into two warm servings.", "Rhubarb, Magnolia Bark, Immature Bitter Orange, Pinellia, and Licorice Decoction", "Rhubarb 3 liǎng, magnolia bark 3 liǎng, immature bitter orange 3 liǎng, pinellia 1 shēng (washed), and roasted licorice 1 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day."], "notes": [["髒結", "organ binding", "藏结", "「结」在此描述病证凝聚不解。本段把藏结与结胸分开，并用寸、关两部脉来辨别。", "Binding describes a pattern thought to lodge or gather. The text distinguishes organ binding from chest binding by the inch and bar pulse positions."], ["關脈", "bar pulse", "关脉", "关是腕部寸、关、尺三处的中间位置。本段比较两种结证在关部的表现。", "The bar is the middle of the three wrist pulse positions. Here it helps distinguish the two binding patterns."], ["血分", "blood aspect", "血分", "这里是作者解释藏结的传统分类用语，并进一步说即使见气结也从血来理解。", "This is a classical classification. The text puts organ binding under the blood aspect even when it presents as qi binding."], ["沉弦而結", "deep, wiry, bound pulse", "沉、弦、结", "沉说深浅，弦说触感似弓弦，结在此是脉的名称。几个字分别提供不同脉象线索。", "Deep marks depth, wiry describes a bowstring-like feel, and bound names an interrupted pulse quality."], ["膠烊消", "dissolves the gelatin", "胶烊消", "「烊消」就是融化。此处先煮其他药，滤清液体后再加入阿胶。", "The gelatin is added after the other ingredients have been decocted and the liquid strained."], ["善悲", "a tendency to sadness", "善悲", "古文「善」可表示容易或常常出现某种情况。这里描述容易悲伤。", "In this construction, 善 indicates a tendency. Here the person is often or readily sad."], ["如覆杯", "like an overturned cup", "如覆杯", "以倒扣的杯子想象腹部按起来的轮廓，是古文的触诊比喻。", "The image of an overturned cup describes the contour felt when the abdomen is pressed."], ["轉失（疑為矢）氣", "the person passes gas", "转失气", "底本括号提示「失」疑作「矢」，英译据此读作排气，并保留原文的不确定性。", "The transcription suggests reading 矢 in place of 失. The translation follows that proposal in rendering the phrase as passing gas."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-17", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part III", "page": 2, "english": ["For binding in the kidney system, the lower abdomen is hard and aches dully, with what feels like a lump under pressure. Urine alternates between clear and cloudy, and the pulse is deep, fine, and bound. The text assigns Poria, Cinnamon Twig, Licorice, and Jujube Decoction. Tense lower abdominal pain with frequent, reddish urination is classed as excess, for which it assigns Cinnamon Twig, Poria, Immature Bitter Orange, Peony Root, and Licorice Decoction.", "Poria, Cinnamon Twig, Licorice, and Jujube Decoction", "Poria ½ jīn, cinnamon twig 4 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, and 15 split jujubes.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu of ganlan water, boils the poria first until 2 shēng have boiled away, adds the other ingredients, and reduces the liquid to 3 shēng before straining. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "Cinnamon Twig, Poria, Immature Bitter Orange, Peony Root, and Licorice Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng (outer layer removed), poria 2 liǎng, immature bitter orange 2 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, and roasted licorice 1 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In organ binding without yang signs or alternating chills and fever, the person is unexpectedly quiet. With a slippery tongue coating, the text rules out aggressive treatment. When eating and drinking remain as usual but there is occasional diarrhea and a white, slippery coating, it regards the pattern as difficult to treat.", "A question: What is chest binding? The teacher replied that when an illness begins in yang and is erroneously purged, heat enters the interior and chest binding results. When an illness begins in yin and is purged too early, focal fullness results. The text attributes chest binding to mistaken purging.", "When chest binding causes head and neck stiffness resembling soft spasm, the text expects purging to restore harmony and assigns Major Chest-Draining Pill.", "Major Chest-Draining Pill", "Rhubarb ½ jīn, tingli seed ½ shēng (dry-roasted), mirabilite ½ shēng, and apricot kernels ½ shēng (skins and tips removed, then dry-roasted).", "The text pounds and sifts two of the ingredients, adds the apricot kernels and mirabilite, and grinds the mixture to an oily paste. The recorded preparation takes a pellet about the size of a small ball, adds a separately pounded square-inch spoonful of kansui powder, 2 hé of white honey, and 2 shēng of water, and boils down to 1 shēng. It describes straining and taking the liquid warm at once. Evacuation is expected overnight. The text describes repeating if there is none and stopping once evacuation occurs, with the usual formula restrictions.", "For chest binding with a floating, large pulse, the text rules out purging and describes it as fatal in this pattern.", "The text also gives a fatal prognosis when all signs of chest binding are present together with agitation and restlessness.", "In Greater Yang illness, the text reads a floating, stirring, rapid pulse as wind, pain, and heat respectively. Headache, fever, slight night sweating, and unexpected aversion to cold indicate an unresolved exterior. If a physician purges anyway, the stirring, rapid pulse slows. Pain and resistance develop around the diaphragm while the stomach is empty and deficient. The invading influence stirs the diaphragm, with shortness of breath, agitation, and vexation in the chest. Yang qi falls inward, hardening develops below the breastbone, and the text calls this chest binding and assigns Major Chest-Draining Decoction. If chest binding does not form but sweat appears only on the head and stops at the neck, with difficult urination, the text predicts yellow discoloration and assigns Five-Ingredient Poria Powder.", "Major Chest-Draining Decoction", "Rhubarb 6 liǎng, mirabilite 1 shēng, and kansui 1 qián spoonful.", "The text starts with 6 shēng of water, boils the rhubarb until 2 shēng remain, strains, adds the mirabilite, briefly returns the liquid to a boil twice, then adds the powdered kansui. It records 1 shēng warm and stopping once there has been free evacuation.", "Five-Ingredient Poria Powder", "Polyporus 18 zhū (outer layer removed), white atractylodes 18 zhū, alisma 1 liǎng and 6 zhū, poria 18 zhū, and cinnamon twig ½ liǎng (outer layer removed).", "The text makes a powder from the five ingredients and pounds it in a mortar. It records a square-inch spoonful in plain rice drink three times a day and plenty of warm water, and treats sweating as a sign of recovery. With yellow discoloration, it lists an addition of yinchen wormwood 10 fēn.", "On the sixth or seventh day of cold damage, chest binding with heat and excess, a deep, tight, forceful pulse, and pain below the breastbone with hardness like stone under pressure is assigned Major Chest-Draining Decoction, given above.", "After more than ten days of cold damage, when heat binds in the interior and chills and fever alternate again, the text assigns Major Bupleurum Decoction. Chest binding without strong fever is attributed to water binding in the chest and ribs. If slight sweating is confined to the head, the text assigns Major Chest-Draining Decoction, given above.", "Major Bupleurum Decoction"], "notes": [["少腹鞕", "the lower abdomen is hard", "少腹鞕", "「少腹」指下腹，「鞕」同硬。这段还用能按到像核一样的东西来补充触感。", "少腹 is the lower abdomen and 鞕 means hard. The comparison to a lump adds detail to what is felt under pressure."], ["甘瀾水", "ganlan water", "甘澜水", "这是古方写的一种经搅动处理的水名。这里把它作为煎剂所用的水。", "Ganlan is the name of agitated water used in some classical preparations. This formula names it as the cooking liquid."], ["苔滑", "a slippery tongue coating", "苔滑", "「苔」指舌面可见的覆盖层。本段用滑苔和白滑苔说明作者辨别的两个相关情形。", "The coating is the visible layer on the tongue. The text describes a slippery coating and later specifies one that is white."], ["誤下", "mistaken purging", "误下", "「下」指攻下。本问答强调在作者判断不合的阶段使用这种办法，会改变原有病证。", "Purging is the treatment meant by 下. The passage describes how using it at the wrong stage changes the pattern."], ["柔痙", "soft spasm", "柔痉", "古书中的痉病名称。本句用「如」作比，着眼于头项强的相似表现。", "This is a classical spasm category. The text explicitly compares the head and neck stiffness to it."], ["方寸匙", "square-inch spoonful", "方寸匙", "这是古方取散剂的匙量，按古代的寸理解。英译保留方寸的形象。", "This is a historical spoon measure for powdered ingredients, named for a spoon about one cùn square."], ["心中懊憹", "vexation in the chest", "懊憹", "古文用以形容难以安宁的烦闷。本段把它与短气、躁烦并列。", "The expression describes deep, unsettled discomfort. The text mentions it alongside shortness of breath and agitation."], ["劑頸而還", "stops at the neck", "剂颈而还", "本句用来限定哪里有汗。上文说只头上汗出，其他地方没有汗，颈是其界。", "The expression sets the boundary of the sweat. It is confined to the head and does not extend beyond the neck."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-17", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part III", "page": 3, "english": ["Bupleurum root ½ jīn, 4 roasted immature bitter oranges, sliced fresh ginger 5 liǎng, scutellaria 3 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), 12 split jujubes, and rhubarb 2 liǎng.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu and 2 shēng of water for the eight ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then simmers the liquid again. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "If sweating is induced repeatedly in Greater Yang illness and purging follows, the text describes five or six days without a bowel movement, a dry tongue and thirst, and slight tidal fever toward late afternoon. With painful hardness and fullness extending from below the breastbone to the lower abdomen and making approach unbearable, the text assigns Major Chest-Draining Decoction, given above.", "Minor chest binding is centered just below the breastbone, hurts under pressure, and has a floating, slippery pulse. The text assigns Minor Chest-Draining Decoction.", "Minor Chest-Draining Decoction", "Coptis 1 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and 1 large trichosanthes fruit.", "The text boils the trichosanthes first in 6 shēng of water until 3 shēng remain, adds the other two ingredients, then reduces to 2 shēng and strains. It divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "On the second or third day of Greater Yang illness, the person cannot lie down and only wants to get up. The text expects binding below the breastbone and reads a faint, weak pulse as evidence of preexisting cold. If the person is purged anyway and diarrhea stops, it predicts chest binding. If diarrhea continues, it calls this diarrhea accompanied by heat.", "After purging in Greater Yang illness, the text takes a skipping pulse without chest binding as a sign of approaching resolution. It associates a floating pulse with chest binding, a tight pulse with sore throat, a wiry pulse with tightness on both sides beneath the ribs, a fine rapid pulse with ongoing headache, a deep tight pulse with an urge to vomit, a deep slippery pulse with diarrhea accompanied by heat, and a floating slippery pulse with bleeding from below.", "For illness in yang, the text expects resolution through sweating. If someone is sprayed or doused with cold water instead, it says the heat is trapped and agitation worsens, with gooseflesh and a desire to drink despite absence of thirst. It assigns Clam Shell Powder, or Five-Ingredient Poria Powder if the illness persists. For cold-excess chest binding without heat signs, it names Three-Ingredient Minor Chest-Draining Decoction or White Powder. The Five-Ingredient Poria Powder and minor decoction formulas appear above.", "Clam Shell Powder", "Clam shell 5 liǎng, ephedra 3 liǎng, licorice 3 liǎng, fresh ginger 3 liǎng, gypsum 5 liǎng, 50 apricot kernels (skins and tips removed), and 12 split jujubes.", "The text powders the seven ingredients and records mixing a square-inch spoonful with 5 hé of boiling water. It lists peony root 3 liǎng if abdominal pain appears after sweating.", "White Powder", "Platycodon root 3 fēn, croton seed 1 fēn, and fritillaria 3 fēn.", "The text powders the three ingredients, pounds them again in a mortar, and mixes the powder with plain rice drink. Its historical measure is half a qián spoonful for a robust person, with less for a frail person.", "The text predicts vomiting when illness is above the diaphragm and evacuation when it is below. It describes giving a cup of hot gruel if evacuation does not occur and a cup of cold gruel if evacuation does not stop.", "When Greater Yang and Lesser Yang are both involved, the text describes stiffness and pain in the head and neck, possible dizziness and clouding, an appearance at times resembling chest binding, and hard focal fullness below the breastbone. It records needling at the first interspace of Dazhui, Feishu, and Ganshu, and cautions against inducing sweat, which it associates with delirious speech and a wiry, large pulse. If the speech continues for five days, it names Qimen.", "In a woman with wind strike, fever and aversion to wind arise as menstruation begins. By the seventh or eighth day the fever has gone and the pulse is slow and the body cool. With fullness below the chest and ribs resembling chest binding and with delirious speech, the text calls the pattern heat entering the blood chamber. It names needling Qimen and draining according to the degree of excess.", "In a woman with wind strike, if chills and fever recur in bouts on the seventh or eighth day just as menstruation stops, the text calls it heat entering the blood chamber. It attributes the resemblance to intermittent fever to blood binding, and assigns Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Minor Bupleurum Decoction", "Bupleurum root ½ jīn, scutellaria 3 liǎng, ginseng 3 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), roasted licorice 3 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu and 2 shēng of water for the seven ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then reduces the liquid again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day."], "notes": [["日晡所", "toward late afternoon", "日晡所", "传统时段名，指下午较晚时。这里说明轻微潮热出现的时间。", "This names the later part of the afternoon and locates the slight tidal fever in time."], ["潮熱", "tidal fever", "潮热", "「潮」像潮水按时起伏。古文借它形容发热按一定时段出现。", "The tide image describes fever that comes on at recurring times."], ["小結胸", "Minor chest binding", "小结胸", "「小」在这里是分类用词。此条强调心下局部受压痛，以及浮滑的脉象。", "This name distinguishes a pattern centered below the breastbone, painful under pressure, with a floating slippery pulse."], ["協熱利", "diarrhea accompanied by heat", "协热利", "「利」这里指下利，即腹泻。「协热」说明古书把热与持续下利合起来判断。", "利 refers to diarrhea. The phrase describes the text’s grouping of persistent diarrhea with heat."], ["肉上粟起", "gooseflesh", "肉上粟起", "「粟」是小米粒，古文以一粒粒的样子形容皮肤起小疙瘩。", "The Chinese likens the little raised bumps on the skin to millet grains."], ["譫語", "delirious speech", "谵语", "古文指神志异常时说话错乱。本页数条以不同的前因和伴随表现来辨。", "This describes disordered speech during delirium. Several lines on this page distinguish it by accompanying signs and preceding events."], ["熱入血室", "heat entering the blood chamber", "热入血室", "古书中的证候名称。本页两段都把它和经水恰逢开始或停止连起来。", "This is a classical pattern name. Both passages connect it to the timing of menstrual bleeding."], ["如瘧狀", "resemblance to intermittent fever", "如疟状", "「如」表示相似，这里说寒热有时发作。这个词帮助读者注意发作的节律。", "The comparison concerns the rhythm of recurrent bouts of chills and fever."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-17", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part III", "page": 4, "english": ["When a woman has cold damage with fever just as menstruation begins, she may be clearheaded by day and talk deliriously at dusk as though seeing spirits. The text calls this heat entering the blood chamber. It predicts spontaneous recovery if stomach qi and the upper two burners are left undisturbed.", "On the sixth or seventh day of cold damage, when there is fever, slight aversion to cold, distressing joint pain, slight vomiting, and a sense of binding below the breastbone with exterior signs still present, the text assigns Bupleurum and Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Bupleurum and Cinnamon Twig Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 1½ liǎng, scutellaria 1½ liǎng, ginseng 1½ liǎng, roasted licorice 1 liǎng, pinellia 2½ hé (washed), peony root 1½ liǎng, 6 jujubes, sliced fresh ginger 1½ liǎng, and bupleurum root 4 liǎng.", "The text reduces 7 shēng of water with the nine ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "On the fifth or sixth day of cold damage, after induced sweating followed by purging, fullness and slight binding in the chest and ribs, difficult urination, thirst without vomiting, sweating only on the head, alternating chills and fever, and agitation indicate, in the text, that the illness remains unresolved. The text assigns Bupleurum, Cinnamon Twig, and Dried Ginger Decoction.", "Bupleurum, Cinnamon Twig, and Dried Ginger Decoction", "Bupleurum root ½ jīn, cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, dried ginger 2 liǎng, trichosanthes root 4 liǎng, scutellaria 3 liǎng, dry-roasted oyster shell 2 liǎng, and roasted licorice 2 liǎng.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu and 2 shēng of water for the seven ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then reduces again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day. It describes slight agitation after the first serving and takes sweating after another serving as a sign of recovery.", "On the fifth or sixth day of cold damage, sweating on the head, slight aversion to cold, cold hands and feet, fullness below the breastbone, lack of appetite, hard stool, and a fine pulse are called slight yang binding. The text places the illness both in the exterior and in the interior. A deep pulse points to the interior, while sweating points to faint yang. Pure yin binding, it says, would be entirely inside, with no exterior signs. Here the pattern is half inside and half outside. Although the pulse is deep and fine, the text reasons that sweating on the head distinguishes it from Lesser Yin illness. It allows Minor Bupleurum Decoction, given above, and associates eventual resolution of lingering symptoms with passage of stool.", "On the fifth or sixth day of cold damage, vomiting and fever make up the Bupleurum Decoction pattern. If another medicine has been used to purge but the Bupleurum pattern remains, the text still assigns the decoction. It says the previous purging does not preclude this approach and describes steaming heat and shaking, then fever and sweating, followed by resolution.", "If the area below the breastbone is full, hard, and painful, the text calls it chest binding and assigns Major Chest-Draining Decoction, given above. Fullness without pain is called focal fullness. The text rules out Bupleurum Decoction in that case and assigns Pinellia Heart-Draining Decoction.", "Pinellia Heart-Draining Decoction", "Pinellia ½ shēng (washed), scutellaria 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, ginseng 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, coptis 1 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu of water for the seven ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then reduces again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "The text says erroneous purging when Greater Yang and Lesser Yang are both involved can cause chest binding with hardness below the breastbone. If diarrhea continues and liquids cannot go down, it expects agitation. With a floating, tight pulse, further purging is said to send the tightness inward, causing focal fullness that feels soft under pressure. The text calls this fullness from qi and assigns Minor Blue Dragon Decoction.", "Minor Blue Dragon Decoction", "Ephedra 3 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, asarum 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and schisandra fruit ½ shēng.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu of water for the eight ingredients, boils the ephedra first until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng before straining. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day. For thirst the text omits pinellia and adds trichosanthes root 3 liǎng. For slight diarrhea or the sign written 嘻 in this transcription, it omits ephedra and adds 1 fire-processed aconite root. For difficult urination with lower abdominal fullness it omits ephedra and adds poria 4 liǎng. For panting it adds ½ shēng of apricot kernels with the skins and tips removed."], "notes": [["晝日明瞭", "clearheaded by day", "昼日明了", "本句以白天神志清楚和入暮后谵语相互对照，交代表现随时段而变。", "The line contrasts clear awareness in daylight with delirious speech at dusk."], ["上二焦", "the upper two burners", "上二焦", "三焦是古书分述身体区域的一套概念。「上二焦」在这里指上焦和中焦。", "The burners are a classical way to describe body regions. The upper two are the upper and middle burners."], ["外證未去", "exterior signs still present", "外证未去", "作者说明仍能看到归在外层的表现。句中的轻微恶寒和肢节疼有助于理解所说外证。", "The text says signs assigned to the exterior persist. Slight aversion to cold and joint pain help explain the description."], ["往來寒熱", "alternating chills and fever", "往来寒热", "「往来」写寒和热交替出现。本句把它和只头部汗出等表现并列。", "The phrase describes chills and fever alternating rather than occurring as a single fixed state."], ["半在裡半在外", "half inside and half outside", "半里半外", "这里是作者对表与里都有线索的概括。前面用脉沉、头汗出等表现说明判断。", "The phrase describes simultaneous clues at the exterior and interior. The discussion considers the deep pulse and head sweating in that distinction."], ["但滿而不痛", "Fullness without pain", "满而不痛", "原文用压痛和硬满来辨结胸，单有满而不痛则写为痞。一个疼痛差异改变了分类。", "This line classifies painful hardness and fullness as chest binding, and fullness without pain as focal fullness."], ["按之自濡", "feels soft under pressure", "按之自濡", "「濡」这里描述按下去的触感偏软。与本段前面心下硬的写法相照。", "Here 濡 describes yielding under pressure. The earlier sentence describes hardness in the same region."], ["若嘻者", "the sign written 嘻", "嘻", "本底本此处写「嘻」，在方后加减条的具体所指不明，英译保留原字。", "The transcription reads 嘻 in this modification clause. The translation preserves the character because its intended sign here is unclear."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-17", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part III", "page": 5, "english": ["In Greater Yang wind strike with diarrhea and retching, the text allows aggressive treatment only once the exterior has resolved. When sweat keeps seeping out in bouts, with headache, focal fullness below the breastbone, pain pulling beneath the ribs, dry retching, and shortness of breath, sweating without aversion to cold indicates that the exterior has resolved but the interior remains disharmonious. The text assigns Ten Jujube Decoction.", "Ten Jujube Decoction", "Daphne flower (dry-roasted), kansui, and euphorbia root.", "The text records pounding equal portions of the three ingredients separately. It boils 10 plump jujubes in 1½ shēng of water until 8 hé remain, strains the liquid, and adds the powder. It lists 1 qián spoonful for a robust person and half a qián for a frail person, taken warm at dawn. If evacuation is slight and illness persists, it lists another serving the next day increased by half a qián. After free evacuation it describes recuperation with soft gruel.", "In Greater Yang illness, a physician induces sweat, then purges when fever and aversion to cold develop. The text describes focal fullness below the breastbone, exterior and interior both deficient, and both yin and yang qi depleted, leaving yin alone when yang is absent. It says that further fire needling can bring distress in the chest, a blue-green and yellow complexion, and twitching of the skin, which it regards as difficult to treat. Slight yellowing with warm hands and feet is taken as more favorable.", "For focal fullness below the breastbone that is soft under pressure, with a floating, large bar pulse, the text assigns Rhubarb, Coptis, and Scutellaria Heart-Draining Decoction.", "Rhubarb, Coptis, and Scutellaria Heart-Draining Decoction", "Rhubarb 2 liǎng, coptis 1 liǎng, and scutellaria 1 liǎng.", "The text steeps the three ingredients in 2 shēng of vigorously boiling water, briefly infuses them, squeezes out the liquid, and divides it into two warm servings.", "For focal fullness below the breastbone with aversion to cold, the text assigns Aconite Heart-Draining Decoction.", "Aconite Heart-Draining Decoction", "Rhubarb 2 liǎng, coptis 1 liǎng, scutellaria 1 liǎng, and 1 aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, broken into eight pieces, and decocted separately for its liquid).", "The text chops the three ingredients other than aconite and steeps them in 2 shēng of vigorously boiling water. After a brief infusion it squeezes out the liquid, adds the aconite decoction, and divides the mixture into two warm servings.", "When purging causes focal fullness below the breastbone, the text describes giving Heart-Draining Decoction. If the fullness persists with thirst, an uncomfortably dry mouth, and difficulty urinating, it assigns Five-Ingredient Poria Powder, given above.", "After sweating has resolved cold damage, there may be disharmony of the stomach, hard focal fullness below the breastbone, dry belching with the smell of food, water qi below the ribs, loud abdominal rumbling, and diarrhea. The text assigns Fresh Ginger Heart-Draining Decoction.", "Fresh Ginger Heart-Draining Decoction", "Fresh ginger 4 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, ginseng 3 liǎng, dried ginger 1 liǎng, scutellaria 3 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), coptis 1 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu of water for the eight ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then reduces again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In cold damage or wind strike, a physician purges in error. The text describes diarrhea dozens of times a day with undigested food, loud abdominal rumbling, hard focal fullness below the breastbone, dry retching, and unrest. The physician takes the fullness for remaining illness and purges again, making it worse. The text attributes the hardness to stomach deficiency and invading qi moving upward, and rules out bound heat as its cause. It assigns Licorice Heart-Draining Decoction.", "Licorice Heart-Draining Decoction", "Roasted licorice 4 liǎng, scutellaria 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, ginseng 3 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), coptis 1 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu of water for the seven ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then reduces again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In cold damage with persistent diarrhea and hard focal fullness below the breastbone after a purging decoction, Heart-Draining Decoction fails and a further purge worsens the diarrhea. The physician gives Regulate the Middle without success. The text explains that the formula addresses the middle burner and places this diarrhea in the lower burner. It assigns Red Halloysite and Yuyuliang Decoction. If the diarrhea continues, it calls for promoting urination.", "Red Halloysite and Yuyuliang Decoction", "Red halloysite 1 jīn (crushed) and taiyi yuyuliang 1 jīn (crushed).", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the two ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and divides it into three warm servings."], "notes": [["縶縶汗出", "sweat keeps seeping out", "縶縶汗出", "这里把一阵阵发作时汗出的样子写在一起。后面还用汗出而不恶寒判断表已解。", "The opening describes ongoing sweat in bouts. Later in the line, sweat without aversion to cold helps the text judge that exterior signs have cleared."], ["平旦", "at dawn", "平旦", "古代时称，指天刚亮的时候。这里标记方后所记的一日用药时段。", "A traditional time expression for daybreak, used here to mark the time recorded in the formula."], ["膚瞤", "twitching of the skin", "肤瞤", "「瞤」写不由自主的小幅颤动。原文把它和面色变化一同列为难治的迹象。", "The word describes small involuntary movements. The text groups these with changes in complexion."], ["按之濡", "soft under pressure", "按之濡", "「濡」在此是按下去偏软的触感。下文另有「痞鞕」，写满而兼硬。", "Here 濡 means yielding to the touch. Other lines on this page describe focal fullness accompanied by hardness."], ["麻沸湯", "vigorously boiling water", "麻沸汤", "方中这里的「汤」是热水，接着用「渍」说明浸泡。", "Here 湯 is hot water. The verb that follows describes steeping the ingredients in it."], ["乾噫食臭", "dry belching with the smell of food", "干噫食臭", "「噫」指嗳气，「食臭」说明嗳气带食物的气味。", "噫 means belching. The rest of the phrase describes a smell of food with it."], ["腹中雷鳴", "loud abdominal rumbling", "腹中雷鸣", "用雷声比喻腹内鸣响。此页在两种伴下利的条文里都这样写。", "Thunder is an image for loud rumbling in the belly. The page uses it in two passages that also mention diarrhea."], ["理中焦", "addresses the middle burner", "理中焦", "中焦、下焦是古书区分病位的用语，本句用这一区别解释为何前方未止利。", "The middle and lower burners are classical location categories. The text uses the distinction to explain why the preceding formula did not stop diarrhea."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-17", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part III", "page": 6, "english": ["After cold damage has been treated with vomiting and purging and then with induced sweating, the text describes deficiency agitation and a very faint pulse. By the eighth or ninth day there is hard focal fullness below the breastbone, pain beneath the ribs, qi rushing up to the throat, dizziness and clouding, and twitching along the channels. If this persists, the text says it develops into a flaccidity disorder.", "After cold damage has resolved following sweating, vomiting, or purging, if hard focal fullness below the breastbone and belching persist, the text assigns Inula Flower and Hematite Decoction.", "Inula Flower and Hematite Decoction", "Inula flower 3 liǎng, ginseng 2 liǎng, fresh ginger 5 liǎng, hematite 1 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and 12 split jujubes.", "The text starts with 1 dǒu of water for the seven ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then reduces again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "In Greater Yang illness, repeated purging while exterior signs persist brings diarrhea accompanied by heat. If diarrhea continues, with hard focal fullness below the breastbone and exterior and interior both unresolved, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig and Ginseng Decoction.", "Cinnamon Twig and Ginseng Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 4 liǎng, roasted licorice 4 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, ginseng 3 liǎng, and dried ginger 3 liǎng.", "The text first boils the four ingredients other than cinnamon twig in 9 shēng of water until 5 shēng remain. It adds the cinnamon twig, reduces to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm twice by day and once by night.", "After strong purging followed by induced sweating in cold damage, focal fullness below the breastbone with aversion to cold indicates that the exterior is unresolved. The text calls for addressing the exterior first with Cinnamon Twig Decoction and then the fullness with Rhubarb, Coptis, and Scutellaria Heart-Draining Decoction. Both formulas appear above.", "In cold damage with fever and sweating that do not bring resolution, hard focal fullness below the breastbone, and vomiting without diarrhea, the text assigns Major Bupleurum Decoction, given above.", "An illness resembles the Cinnamon Twig pattern, yet the head does not hurt, the neck is not stiff, and the inch pulse is slightly floating. There is hard fullness in the chest and qi rises to the throat, making breathing difficult. The text calls this cold in the chest and assigns Melon Stalk Powder to induce vomiting.", "Melon Stalk Powder", "Melon stalk 1 fēn (dry-roasted) and adzuki bean 1 fēn.", "The text pounds and sifts the two ingredients separately, then combines the powders. It records a qián spoonful mixed with the strained liquid made by cooking 1 hé of fermented soybeans in 7 hé of hot water into a thin gruel. It records taking the mixture warm at once, adding a little if vomiting does not occur, and stopping once vomiting occurs freely. It rules this out for people weakened by blood loss.", "When longstanding focal fullness beneath the ribs extends beside the navel and pain pulls into the lower abdomen and the genital sinews, the text calls it organ binding and gives a fatal prognosis.", "If cold damage persists seven or eight days after vomiting or purging, the text describes heat binding inside and heat at both the exterior and interior. With occasional aversion to wind, great thirst, a dry tongue, agitation, and a desire for several shēng of water, it assigns White Tiger Decoction with Ginseng.", "White Tiger Decoction with Ginseng", "Anemarrhena rhizome 6 liǎng, gypsum 1 jīn (crushed), roasted licorice 2 liǎng, non-glutinous rice 6 hé, and ginseng 2 liǎng.", "The text boils the five ingredients in 1 dǒu of water until the rice is cooked, then strains the finished liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "For cold damage without high fever but with dry mouth, thirst, agitation, and slight aversion to cold at the back, the text assigns White Tiger Decoction with Ginseng, given above.", "With a floating pulse, fever, and no sweat in cold damage, the text says the exterior remains unresolved. It calls for inducing sweat and rules out White Tiger Decoction. For thirst with desire to drink, when exterior signs are absent, it assigns White Tiger Decoction with Ginseng, given above.", "When Greater Yang and Lesser Yang are both involved, with hardness below the breastbone, neck stiffness, and dizziness, the text names Dazhui, Feishu, and Ganshu for needling. It cautions against purging, which it says brings spasm.", "In combined Greater Yang and Lesser Yang illness with spontaneous diarrhea, the text assigns Scutellaria Decoction. With vomiting it assigns Scutellaria Decoction with Pinellia and Fresh Ginger.", "Scutellaria Decoction", "Scutellaria 3 liǎng, peony root 2 liǎng, licorice 2 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text reduces 1 dǒu of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm twice by day and once by night.", "Scutellaria Decoction with Pinellia and Fresh Ginger", "Scutellaria 3 liǎng, peony root 2 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), fresh ginger 1½ liǎng, and 12 split jujubes."], "notes": [["虛煩", "deficiency agitation", "虚烦", "本段把烦与很微的脉同写，属于作者在吐下、发汗后判断的虚象。", "The text pairs agitation with a very faint pulse after vomiting, purging, and sweating."], ["眩冒", "dizziness and clouding", "眩冒", "「眩」写头眼昏转，「冒」有被遮盖般昏蒙的意思。", "The first character evokes dizziness, and the second a veiled or clouded sensation."], ["噫氣不除", "belching persist", "噫气不除", "「噫气」是嗳气。本条说原病已解，这一表现与心下硬满却仍在。", "Belching and hard fullness linger after the initial illness has resolved."], ["先解表", "addressing the exterior first", "先解表", "先、后写出治法次序。此条用恶寒指出表证仍在。", "The line gives a sequence, taking aversion to cold as a sign that the exterior has yet to resolve."], ["亡血虛家", "people weakened by blood loss", "亡血虚家", "「家」指具有某种情况的人。这里说因失血而虚的人，并列作这一古法的禁例。", "家 refers to people characterized by the stated condition. The text rules the historical method out for those weakened by blood loss."], ["舌上乾燥", "a dry tongue", "舌上干燥", "条文把舌干、大渴及欲饮水并述，帮助读者看出作者如何表达渴的程度。", "Dry tongue, great thirst, and the desire to drink substantial amounts appear together to convey the severity."], ["並病", "both involved", "并病与合病", "本页两条连续提到太阳、少阳。前条写「并病」及头项表现，后条写「合病」及吐利表现。", "Two adjacent entries involve Greater and Lesser Yang. One discusses neck and head signs, the other diarrhea and vomiting."], ["自下利", "spontaneous diarrhea", "自下利", "「自」说明这里的下利自然出现。与前面有些条文写误攻下后发生的下利分别读。", "自 marks diarrhea arising as part of the illness itself. Earlier passages show it developing after purging."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-17", "title": "Greater Yang Diseases, Part III", "page": 7, "english": ["The text reduces 1 dǒu of water with the six ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm twice by day and once by night.", "In cold damage with heat in the chest, a pathogenic influence in the stomach, abdominal pain, and an urge to vomit, the text assigns Coptis Decoction.", "Coptis Decoction", "Coptis 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, ginseng 2 liǎng, pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and 12 split jujubes.", "The text reduces 1 dǒu of water with the seven ingredients to 6 shēng and strains. It lists 1 shēng warm three times by day and three times by night.", "The text interprets a floating, slippery pulse in cold damage as heat in the interior and no cold at the exterior. It assigns White Tiger Decoction.", "White Tiger Decoction", "Anemarrhena rhizome 6 liǎng, gypsum 1 jīn (crushed), roasted licorice 2 liǎng, and non-glutinous rice 6 hé.", "The text boils the four ingredients in 1 dǒu of water until the rice is cooked, then strains the finished liquid. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "For cold damage with a bound or skipping pulse and palpitations, the text assigns Roasted Licorice Decoction.", "Roasted Licorice Decoction", "Roasted licorice 4 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, ginseng 2 liǎng, rehmannia root ½ jīn, cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, ophiopogon root ½ shēng, donkey-hide gelatin 2 liǎng, hemp seed ½ shēng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text first boils the eight ingredients other than gelatin in 7 shēng of clear wine until 3 shēng remain. It strains the liquid, dissolves the gelatin completely in it, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day."], "notes": [["邪氣", "pathogenic influence", "邪气", "古书用来称干扰身体的致病因素。本条将它归在胃中，并把腹痛、欲呕并列。", "The classical term names an influence implicated in illness. This line locates it in the stomach alongside pain and nausea."], ["日三服，夜三服", "three times by day and three times by night", "日三服夜三服", "底本此方确作白天三次、夜里三次。与紧邻前条的「日再服、夜一服」分别读。", "This formula explicitly records three servings by day and three by night. The preceding formula gives a different schedule."], ["浮滑", "floating, slippery pulse", "浮滑", "浮说浅处可取，滑说来势流利。原文用这两个脉象解释所归的里热。", "Floating describes depth and slippery describes a smooth feel. The text uses this combination in its account of interior heat."], ["粳米", "non-glutinous rice", "粳米", "白虎汤把粳米列为一味，下条以米熟记煎成。", "The text includes non-glutinous rice and uses its being cooked as the marker that the decoction is ready."], ["脈結促", "a bound or skipping pulse", "结、促", "两者在本书平脉篇都写脉有停顿。结底速偏缓，促底速偏快。", "Both qualities are described earlier as having pauses. Their underlying pace is slower for bound and faster for skipping."], ["心動悸", "palpitations", "心动悸", "写感觉心跳明显、不能安定。本段把这种主观感觉与结或促脉同列。", "The person is aware of unsettled heartbeats. The line places this sensation alongside the named pulse qualities."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 1, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["A question asks what Taiyang-Yangming, primary Yangming, and Shaoyang-Yangming mean. The reply calls Taiyang-Yangming spleen constraint and defines primary Yangming as excess in the stomach system. It describes Shaoyang-Yangming as drying of the stomach after induced sweating and promotion of urination, with restlessness, repletion, and difficulty passing stool.", "Yangming illness is characterized by excess in the stomach system.", "A question asks how Yangming illness arises. The reply says that in Taiyang illness, inducing sweat, purging, or promoting urination can deplete body fluids and leave the stomach dry. The illness then shifts to Yangming. Absence of bowel movements, interior excess, and difficulty passing stool are the signs given for this shift.", "A question asks what outward signs Yangming illness presents. The reply describes bodily heat and spontaneous sweating, with aversion to heat instead of aversion to cold.", "A question asks why a person may have aversion to cold without fever on the first day. The reply says that even on day one the aversion to cold will soon cease on its own. Spontaneous sweating and aversion to heat then follow. Another question asks why the aversion to cold ceases by itself. The reply locates Yangming at the center and associates it with Earth, to which all things return, with nowhere further for the illness to pass. An initial aversion to cold therefore stops on its own on day two. The text calls this Yangming illness.", "When sweating is induced at the very beginning of a Taiyang illness but the initial sweating is incomplete, the illness shifts to Yangming.", "In cold damage, there is fever without sweating, vomiting, and inability to eat. If sweat unexpectedly beads on the skin, the text takes this as a shift to Yangming.", "If the Yangming pulse is large on the third day of cold damage, the text takes it as a sign that the illness will not pass onward.", "In cold damage, a floating, relaxed pulse with naturally warm hands and feet is taken as a sign that the illness is linked to Taiyin. In Taiyin the text expects the body to turn yellow, though this does not happen if urination is free. If the stool is hard on day seven or eight, the text calls this Yangming illness.", "When cold damage shifts to Yangming, the person breaks into a light sweat.", "In Yangming wind strike, there is a bitter taste, dry throat, abdominal fullness, slightly labored breathing, fever, aversion to wind, and a floating, relaxed pulse. The text says purging would lead to abdominal fullness and difficulty urinating.", "In Yangming illness, the text calls the pattern wind strike if the person can eat and cold strike if the person cannot.", "In Yangming cold strike, the person cannot eat, has difficulty urinating, and develops beaded sweat on the hands and feet. The text regards this as impending fixed accumulation, with stool that is initially hard and then loose. It explains the sequence by cold in the stomach, where fluids and food fail to separate.", "In Yangming illness, the person first wants to eat, has difficulty urinating but has regular stools, and has aching joints and a faint impression of heat. They suddenly become agitated and then break into a sweat, after which the illness resolves. The text explains this as water failing to overcome grain qi and leaving together with the sweat. It expects recovery once the pulse becomes small.", "The text places the likely time of resolution for Yangming illness between the Shen and Xu hours.", "In Yangming illness where the person cannot eat, the text predicts hiccups if the heat is attacked. It explains this by the person's preexisting deficiency and cold in the stomach.", "In Yangming illness with a slow pulse, it is hard to eat to fullness. Eating a full meal brings slight restlessness, dizziness, and difficulty urinating. The text regards this as impending grain jaundice and says abdominal fullness will persist even if purging is used. It bases this judgment on the slow pulse.", "Sweating is usually abundant in Yangming illness. If sweat is absent and the body feels as if insects are crawling under the skin, the text attributes this to longstanding deficiency.", "In Yangming illness, when there is unexpectedly no sweat and urination is free, then vomiting, coughing, and cold hands and feet after two or three days are taken as signs that headache will follow. If there is no coughing, vomiting, or coldness in the hands and feet, the text does not expect headache. With dizziness alone and no aversion to cold, the person can eat. In that case the text links a cough with sore throat and its absence with no sore throat.", "In Yangming illness, the text expects yellowing of the body when there is no sweat, urination is difficult, and there is gnawing distress in the chest.", "In Yangming illness treated with fire, the text expects yellowing if the forehead sweats slightly and urination is difficult.", "In Yangming illness, a floating and large pulse is taken to indicate tidal fever that flares at regular times. A pulse that is merely floating is linked with spontaneous sweating.", "In Yangming illness, the text expects a nosebleed when the mouth is dry and the person wants only to rinse with water without swallowing it."], "notes": [["脾約", "spleen constraint", "脾约", "这里是太陽轉入陽明一类的古名称。「约」有收束的意思，后文还有专门写脾约的条文。", "This is the classical name for the Taiyang-Yangming presentation. Yue conveys a sense of constraint, and a later passage returns to the term."], ["陽明之為病，胃家實是也", "Yangming illness is characterized by excess in the stomach system", "胃家实", "「胃家」在古书里指以胃和肠道为中心的一组功能。「实」是本书用来描述病势充盛的词。", "Stomach system refers to a group of functions centered on the stomach and intestines. Excess is the book's term for a state of repletion."], ["不更衣", "Absence of bowel movements", "不更衣", "字面是没有换衣。这里沿用古人委婉的写法，意思是没有大便。", "Literally, the phrase means not changing clothes. In this passage it is a classical euphemism for the absence of a bowel movement."], ["汗出濈濈然", "sweat unexpectedly beads on the skin", "濈濈然", "这个叠词描写汗在皮肤上持续渗出来的样子。句中的「反」写原先无汗，后来却有汗。", "The repeated word suggests moisture continuing to bead at the skin. The sentence marks its appearance as unexpected after an earlier absence of sweat."], ["大便初鞕後溏", "stool that is initially hard and then loose", "先硬后溏", "「鞕」就是硬，「溏」表示便稀软。原句着重写两种状态出现的先后。", "The first character means hard, and tang means loose or soft. The sentence emphasizes the order of the two consistencies."], ["從申至戌上", "between the Shen and Xu hours", "申至戌", "申、酉、戌是传统时辰，以十二地支标记。大致相当于今天下午三点至晚上九点的时段。", "Shen, You, and Xu are consecutive time periods in the traditional twelve-branch clock, corresponding roughly to the hours from 3 p.m. to 9 p.m."], ["心中懊憹", "gnawing distress in the chest", "懊憹", "「懊憹」是古书写令人坐立难安的内在烦闷的词。这里把它和无汗、小便不利放在一起。", "This classical expression depicts a deeply uncomfortable, restless feeling inside. The sentence lists it with absent sweating and difficult urination."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 2, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["In Yangming illness, the person was already sweating spontaneously when the physician induced heavy sweating. The illness has improved, but slight, lingering restlessness remains. The text attributes this to hard stool. Loss of fluids leaves the stomach dry and hardens the stool. It calls for asking how often the person urinates during the day. If this falls from three or four times a day to two today, the text expects stool to pass soon. It reasons that with less frequent urination, the fluids will return to the stomach.", "In cold damage with frequent vomiting, the text forbids attacking treatment even when Yangming signs are present.", "When Yangming signs occur with hardness and fullness below the heart, the text forbids attacking treatment. If the treatment leads to unrelenting diarrhea, the text predicts death. If the diarrhea stops, it predicts recovery.", "When Yangming signs are accompanied by closed eyes and a red complexion, the text forbids attacking treatment, which it says will bring on fever. A yellow complexion is associated here with difficulty urinating.", "In Yangming illness without vomiting or purging, when the person is restless, the text allows Stomach-Regulating Chengqi Decoction.", "Formula for Stomach-Regulating Chengqi Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Mangxiao (mirabilite), half a jin. Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang, rinsed in wine.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe boils two in 3 sheng of water until 1 sheng remains, strains out the dregs, adds the mirabilite, and brings it to a boil once or twice over low heat. It records the warm liquid as a single dose.", "In Yangming illness with a full pulse, the person sweats but has no aversion to heat. The text expects bodily heaviness, shortness of breath, abdominal fullness, and labored breathing. Tidal fever means the exterior is about to resolve and the interior can be attacked. Beaded sweat on the hands and feet means the stool has already hardened, for which the text assigns Major Chengqi Decoction. With heavy sweating, slight fever, and aversion to cold, the exterior is still involved. The text withholds Chengqi Decoction if the fever is not tidal. For marked abdominal fullness and obstruction, it allows Minor Chengqi Decoction to gently harmonize stomach qi and cautions against provoking strong purging.", "Formula for Major Chengqi Decoction.", "Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang, rinsed in wine. Houpo (magnolia bark), half a jin, roasted and stripped of the outer layer. Zhishi (immature bitter orange), 5 fruits, roasted. Mangxiao (mirabilite), 3 he.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe boils the first two in 1 dou of water down to 5 sheng and strains out the dregs. It adds the rhubarb, boils down to 2 sheng, strains again, adds the mirabilite, and brings it to a boil once or twice over low heat. It records two warm doses and directs that the remainder be set aside if the bowels move.", "Formula for Minor Chengqi Decoction.", "Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang, rinsed in wine. Houpo (magnolia bark), 2 liang, roasted and stripped of the outer layer. Zhishi (immature bitter orange), 3 fruits, roasted.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe boils 4 sheng of water down to 1 sheng and 2 he, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses. If the first dose produces a bowel movement, the later dose is withheld. Otherwise the remainder is taken.", "For Yangming illness with tidal fever and slightly hard stool, the text allows Major Chengqi Decoction and withholds it if the stool is soft. If there has been no bowel movement for six or seven days, it considers the possibility of dry stool and describes testing with a small amount of Minor Chengqi Decoction. If the person passes gas after the decoction reaches the abdomen, the text infers dry stool and allows attacking treatment. If there is no gas, it says only the first portion of stool is hard and the remainder is bound to be loose. It warns that attacking treatment would cause abdominal distension and fullness and inability to eat. If the person wants water, giving it is said to bring on hiccups. If fever develops later, stool will harden again and be scant, a pattern for which the text describes using Minor Chengqi Decoction to harmonize. It repeats the caution against attacking treatment in the absence of passing gas. The formulas are given above.", "In Yangming illness, the text associates excess with delirious speech and deficiency with Zheng speech, which it explains as repeated words. It predicts death when a fixed stare and delirious speech occur with labored breathing and fullness, and also gives a fatal prognosis for diarrhea in this passage.", "In Yangming illness, heavy induced sweating or repeated induction of sweat depletes yang in the text's account. With delirious speech and a short pulse, it predicts death. It expects survival if the pulse comes into harmony on its own.", "In cold damage unresolved after induced vomiting or purging, the person passes no stool for five or six days, sometimes for more than ten. Tidal fever appears toward late afternoon without aversion to cold, and the person talks to themselves as if seeing ghosts. In severe attacks they fail to recognize people, pick at clothing and feel around the bed, are apprehensive and restless, breathe with slight difficulty, and stare straight ahead. The text predicts survival with a wiry pulse and death with a rough pulse. For milder cases with only fever and delirious speech, it assigns Major Chengqi Decoction, given above.", "In Yangming illness with heavy sweating, the text says that fluids escape outward and leave the stomach dry, so the stool hardens. It links hard stool with delirious speech and assigns Minor Chengqi Decoction, given above."], "notes": [["重發汗，病已差", "induced heavy sweating. The illness has improved", "重发汗与病差", "此处的「重」强调在自汗之后又重用汗法。「差」表示病势已经好转。", "Zhong emphasizes strong induced sweating added to sweating already present. Cha indicates that the illness has improved."], ["傷寒嘔多", "cold damage with frequent vomiting", "呕多", "本句只多加「呕多」一项，就改变了对于攻法的判断。后面两条也各用一个表现来立禁例。", "Frequent vomiting changes the text's judgment about attacking treatment. The following two lines also use particular signs to frame their cautions."], ["其熱不潮者", "if the fever is not tidal", "热不潮", "「潮」借潮水有时而至来写发热周期。这句用是否按时发热来区分处理。", "The tide image refers to fever returning at its regular time. Here the text distinguishes what to do based on whether that timing is present."], ["得下餘勿服", "the remainder be set aside if the bowels move", "得下余勿服", "这里的「下」指服药后的排便。排便已发生，就是古方规定停止余药的条件。", "Here xia means a bowel movement after the dose. The historical recipe makes that the condition for setting aside the remainder."], ["轉失氣者", "the person passes gas", "转失气", "在这条语境中，指肠中气体由下排出。作者把用药后有没有这个表现作为推断燥屎的线索。", "Here the phrase refers to gas passing from the bowel. The passage uses its presence or absence after the decoction as a clue to dry stool."], ["鄭聲者，重語也", "Zheng speech, which it explains as repeated words", "郑声与重语", "作者紧接着用「重语」说明郑声，指话反复说。前半句另外用「谵语」作为对照。", "The text explains Zheng speech by saying that words are repeated. The preceding clause distinguishes it from delirious speech."], ["循衣摸床", "pick at clothing and feel around the bed", "循衣摸床", "两组动词写手在衣服、床面上反复摸索，是本段列出的重症表现之一。", "The two verbs depict a person picking at clothing and feeling around the bed. This is one of the severe signs in the passage."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 3, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["In Yangming illness with delirious speech, tidal fever, and a slippery pulse that moves quickly, the text assigns Minor Chengqi Decoction, given above.", "In Yangming illness, if the person does not pass gas after taking Chengqi Decoction and still has no bowel movement the next day, while the pulse has unexpectedly become faint and rough, the text identifies interior deficiency. It judges this difficult to treat and forbids further Chengqi Decoction.", "In Yangming illness with delirious speech and tidal fever, unexpected inability to eat leads the text to infer five or six pieces of dry stool in the stomach. If the person can eat, the stool is described as merely hard. The text assigns Major Chengqi Decoction for purging, given above.", "In Yangming illness with bleeding below and delirious speech, the text calls the pattern heat entering the blood chamber. When sweating is confined to the head, it describes needling Qimen and draining according to the extent of excess. It expects recovery once sweat beads on the body.", "In Yangming illness, sweating with delirious speech is attributed to dry stool in the stomach and classified as excess. The text says that purging must wait until the illness has passed through its channel stage. Purging too early is said to bring confused speech because the exterior is deficient while the interior is excessive. The text assigns Major Chengqi Decoction for the purge, given above.", "On the fourth or fifth day of cold damage, a deep pulse occurs with labored breathing and fullness. A deep pulse places the illness in the interior, yet the physician induces sweat. Fluids escape outward and stool becomes difficult to pass. The exterior becomes deficient while the interior is excessive. Over time, the text expects delirious speech.", "When all three yang channels are ill together, the text describes abdominal fullness, bodily heaviness, difficulty turning onto one's side, numbness around the mouth, and a grimy-looking face. It predicts delirious speech and involuntary urination after induced sweating, and reversal cold in the hands and feet with forehead sweating after purging. When there is spontaneous sweating, it assigns White Tiger Decoction. For spontaneous diarrhea, it assigns Gegen, Huanglian, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction.", "Zhimu (anemarrhena rhizome), 6 liang. Shigao (gypsum), 1 jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Jingmi (nonglutinous rice), 6 he.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water and boils until the rice is cooked. The dregs are strained out and the recipe records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "Formula for Gegen, Huanglian, Huangqin, and Gancao Decoction.", "Gegen (kudzu root), half a jin. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Huanglian (coptis rhizome), 3 liang. Huangqin (skullcap root), 3 liang.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 8 sheng of water. The kudzu is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 2 sheng. The dregs are strained out and the liquid is divided into two warm doses.", "In overlapping illness of the two yang channels, once the Taiyang signs cease, tidal fever alone remains, with clammy sweat on the hands and feet, difficulty passing stool, and delirious speech. The text expects purging to resolve this and assigns Major Chengqi Decoction, given above.", "In Yangming illness, the pulse is floating and large. The throat is dry, the mouth bitter, the abdomen full, and breathing labored. There is fever with sweating, aversion to heat rather than cold, and bodily heaviness. The text predicts that inducing sweat will bring agitation, mental confusion, and delirious speech. Adding warmed needling is said to produce fright, agitation, and inability to sleep. Purging leaves the stomach empty and deficient, with intrusive qi disturbing the diaphragm and causing gnawing distress in the chest. When there is a coating on the tongue, it assigns Zhizi and Chi Decoction.", "Formula for Zhizi and Chi Decoction.", "Zhizi (gardenia fruit), 14, split. Xiangchi (fermented soybeans), 4 he, wrapped in cotton.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe starts with 4 sheng of water and boils the gardenia first down to 2 and a half sheng. The dregs are strained out and the fermented soybeans are added. The liquid is boiled again down to 1 and a half sheng, strained, and divided into two doses. It records taking the first dose warm and setting the later dose aside if there is free vomiting.", "In Yangming illness, for thirst with a desire for water and a dry mouth and tongue, the text assigns White Tiger Decoction with Renshen.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction with Renshen.", "Zhimu (anemarrhena rhizome), 6 liang. Shigao (gypsum), 1 jin, crushed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Jingmi (nonglutinous rice), 6 he. Renshen (ginseng), 3 liang.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water and boils until the rice is cooked. The dregs are strained out and the recipe records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "In Yangming illness with a floating pulse, fever, thirst and desire for water, and difficulty urinating, the text assigns Zhuling Decoction.", "Formula for Zhuling Decoction.", "Zhuling (polyporus), 1 liang, outer layer removed. Fuling (poria), 1 liang. Zexie (alisma rhizome), 1 liang. Ejiao (donkey-hide gelatin), 1 liang. Huashi (talc), 1 liang, crushed.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe first boils four in 4 sheng of water down to 2 sheng. The dregs are strained out and the ejiao is added and dissolved. It records a warm dose of 7 he three times daily.", "For Yangming illness with profuse sweating and thirst, the text forbids Zhuling Decoction. It explains that the sweating has already dried the stomach and the decoction promotes further urination.", "In Yangming illness with a floating, slow pulse, exterior heat and interior cold, and diarrhea containing undigested grains, the text assigns Sini Decoction.", "Formula for Sini Decoction."], "notes": [["脈滑而疾", "a slippery pulse that moves quickly", "滑而疾", "「滑」说手下流利的感觉，「疾」说跳动快。本条用两个词分别写脉的触感和速度。", "Slippery describes a smooth sensation under the fingers. Fast describes the pace. The two words refer to different qualities of the same pulse."], ["脈反微澀者，裡虛也", "the pulse has unexpectedly become faint and rough, the text identifies interior deficiency", "微涩与里虚", "「反」说结果出乎预期。作者把排便情况和脉象合起来，判断为里虚。", "Fan marks an unexpected result. The author reads failure to pass stool together with the changed pulse and calls the pattern interior deficiency."], ["熱入血室", "heat entering the blood chamber", "热入血室", "「血室」是古书的理论用语。本条首先写下血与谵语，再用「热入血室」归类。", "Blood chamber is a term in the classical model. The passage names the pattern after describing bleeding below and delirious speech."], ["須過經乃可下之", "purging must wait until the illness has passed through its channel stage", "过经乃可下", "「须……乃……」写必须先到某一阶段，才考虑下一步。后文以表虚里实解释古书为何强调顺序。", "The sentence sets out a prerequisite before purging. The next clause explains the author's timing in terms of exterior deficiency and interior excess."], ["三陽合病", "all three yang channels are ill together", "三阳合病", "三阳指太阳、阳明、少阳。「合病」在本句写三经并见的局面。", "The three yang channels are Taiyang, Yangming, and Shaoyang. This phrase places all three in the same presentation."], ["客氣動膈", "intrusive qi disturbing the diaphragm", "客气动膈", "「客」借来客的比喻，写古书认为有一股气侵扰。「膈」在这一段与心中烦闷连说。", "Guest is the image behind intrusive qi. The phrase connects disturbance around the diaphragm with the distress felt in the chest."], ["下利清穀", "diarrhea containing undigested grains", "下利清谷", "「下利」指泄泻，「清谷」说便中见到不化的食物。作者把它和表热里寒放在同一条。", "Xiali means diarrhea. Qinggu specifies food that has passed undigested. The author presents it alongside exterior heat and interior cold."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 4, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 1 and a half liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng and 2 he, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "In Yangming illness, when the stomach is deficient and cold and the person cannot eat, the text cautions against giving water and says drinking it will bring on hiccups.", "In Yangming illness with a floating pulse, fever, a dry mouth and nose, and the ability to eat, the text expects a nosebleed.", "After purging in Yangming illness, the exterior is hot and the hands and feet are warm, with no chest bind. The person feels gnawing distress in the chest, is hungry but cannot eat, and sweats only from the head. The text assigns Zhizi and Chi Decoction, given above.", "For Yangming illness with tidal fever, loose stool, normal urination, and persistent fullness of the chest and flanks, the text gives Minor Chaihu Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Chaihu Decoction.", "Chaihu (bupleurum), half a jin. Huangqin (skullcap root), 3 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 3 liang. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the recipe boils 1 dou and 2 sheng of water down to 6 sheng. The dregs are strained out and the liquid is boiled again down to 3 sheng. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "For Yangming illness with hardness and fullness below the ribs, absent bowel movements, vomiting, and a white coating on the tongue, the text allows Minor Chaihu Decoction, given above. It says the upper burner opens, fluids descend, and stomach qi comes into harmony, with beaded sweating and resolution.", "In Yangming wind strike, the pulse is wiry, floating, and large. The person is short of breath with fullness throughout the abdomen and pain below the ribs and around the heart. Even with prolonged pressure, the qi fails to pass freely. The nose is dry and produces no discharge. The person wants to lie down, the whole body and eyes are yellow, and urination is difficult. There is tidal fever, occasional hiccups, and swelling in front of and behind the ears. Needling brings a little improvement, while the exterior remains unresolved. If the pulse remains floating after more than ten days of illness, the text gives Minor Chaihu Decoction, above. When the pulse is only floating and there are no other signs, it gives Mahuang Decoction. If urination ceases and abdominal fullness is joined by hiccups, it judges the illness untreatable.", "Formula for Mahuang Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang, outer layer removed. Gancao (licorice), 1 liang, roasted. Xingren (apricot kernels), 70, skins and tips removed.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe starts with 9 sheng of water and boils the ephedra until the volume falls by 2 sheng. The foam is skimmed off and the remaining ingredients are added. The liquid is boiled down to 2 and a half sheng and the dregs are strained out. It records a warm dose of 8 he and covering the patient until sweat is barely perceptible. Rice gruel is omitted. The remaining aftercare follows the method for Guizhi Decoction.", "When moving brings on headache and there is shortness of breath with tidal fever, the text assigns the illness to Yangming and names White Honey Decoction.", "Formula for White Honey Decoction.", "Renshen (ginseng), 1 liang. Dihuang (rehmannia root), 6 liang. Maren (hemp kernels), 1 sheng. White honey, 8 he.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe first boils three in 1 dou of water down to 5 sheng. The dregs are strained out, honey is added, and the liquid is brought to a boil once or twice. It records a dose of 1 sheng three times in the day and twice at night.", "In Yangming illness with spontaneous or induced sweating and free urination, the text regards the fluids inside as exhausted. Even if the stool is hard, it forbids attacking treatment. It waits for a natural urge to pass stool and describes using a honey suppository to assist passage. The text also names wanggua root and the bile from a large pig's gallbladder as historical alternatives.", "Honey suppository recipe.", "Edible honey, 7 he.", "The historical recipe puts the honey in a copper vessel and heats it gently until it thickens like malt syrup, stirring so it does not scorch or stick. Once moldable, the honey is rolled by hand into a pointed rod as thick as a finger and about 2 cun long. The text says to shape it while hot because it hardens when cool. It records insertion into the anus and holding it in place until the urge to move the bowels, when it is removed.", "Pig bile recipe.", "One large pig's gallbladder.", "The historical recipe drains the bile and mixes it with a little vinegar, then records administering it through the anus. It expects a bowel movement with abundant retained food after about the time it takes to eat a meal.", "In Yangming illness with a slow pulse, profuse sweating, and slight aversion to cold, the text judges that the exterior is still unresolved. It describes inducing sweat and assigns Guizhi Decoction.", "Formula for Guizhi Decoction.", "Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang, outer layer removed. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe boils 7 sheng of water down to 3 sheng, strains out the dregs, and records a warm dose of 1 sheng. Shortly afterward, it describes sipping 1 sheng of hot rice gruel to support the medicine's effect and covering the patient until sweat is barely perceptible."], "notes": [["脅下鞕滿", "hardness and fullness below the ribs", "胁下硬满", "「胁下」是肋侧之下。「硬」「满」分别写紧硬的触感与胀满的感觉。", "The location is beneath the sides of the ribs. Hardness and fullness describe the firm and distended qualities there."], ["上焦得通，津液得下", "the upper burner opens, fluids descend", "上焦得通", "「上焦」是三焦的上部。在本句的古代解释中，上焦通畅之后，津液便能下行。", "The upper burner is the upper region of the three burners. In the passage's explanation, its opening lets fluids move downward."], ["耳前後腫", "swelling in front of and behind the ears", "耳前后肿", "「前后」都以耳为参照。这是这一长条列出的多个症状之一，后面才说刺后略有好转。", "Both directions are relative to the ears. The phrase is one sign in a long list, followed by slight improvement after needling."], ["津液內竭", "the fluids inside as exhausted", "津液内竭", "「竭」表示耗尽。本段把出汗、排尿都写在前面，再由这两项解释古书所谓津液内竭。", "Jie means exhaustion. The text first describes sweating and urination and uses them to explain why it calls the fluids exhausted."], ["蜜煎導方", "Honey suppository recipe", "蜜煎导", "「导」在这里是从下部帮助排便的古法。接下来的条文记录蜂蜜成形和使用方式。", "Here dao refers to a historical local method for assisting passage of stool. The following lines record how the honey was shaped and used."], ["如一食頃", "after about the time it takes to eat a meal", "一食顷", "「顷」表示一段时间。古书以吃一顿饭所需的时间作粗略参照。", "Qing marks an interval. The author gives a rough measure by comparing it to the time it takes to eat a meal."], ["啜熱粥一升", "sipping 1 sheng of hot rice gruel", "啜热粥", "「啜」是小口饮用。「升」是古容量单位。本句把喝热粥列在桂枝汤服后的护理中。", "Chuo means to sip, and sheng is a historical measure of volume. The passage lists hot rice gruel as part of the aftercare for Guizhi Decoction."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 5, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["In Yangming disease with a floating pulse, no sweating, and panting, the text expects resolution after induced sweating and indicates Ephedra Decoction, given earlier.", "In Yangming disease with fever and sweating, heat vents outward and yellowing does not develop. If only the head sweats, stopping at the neck, with no sweating on the body, difficult urination, and thirst that prompts drinking, the text identifies stagnant heat in the interior and expects the body to turn yellow. It indicates Yinchenhao Decoction.", "Formula for Yinchenhao Decoction.", "Yinchenhao, six liang. Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Dahuang, two liang, with the outer layer removed.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water. Yinchen is boiled first until the volume has dropped by six sheng. The other two ingredients are added, boiled to yield three sheng, strained, and divided into three warm portions. It expects urine to flow freely, reddish and like soap-pod liquid. After one night the disease is expected to diminish, with the yellowing leaving through urination.", "In Yangming disease, the text takes frequent forgetfulness as a sign of accumulated blood. It attributes this to longstanding blood stasis. Although stool is hard, it passes readily and is black. The text indicates purging with Didang Decoction.", "Formula for Didang Decoction.", "Thirty Shuizhi. Thirty Mengchong, with wings and legs removed. Dahuang, three liang, washed with wine. Twenty peach kernels, with skins and tips removed.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm and a further portion if there is no purgative effect.", "In Yangming disease with distress and vexation after purging, the text allows an attacking method if there is dry stool in the stomach. With slight abdominal fullness and stool that starts hard and ends loose, it rules out attacking. With dry stool it indicates Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "With five or six days without a bowel movement, pain around the navel, and restlessness that flares at intervals, the text attributes the absence of stool to dry stool lodged within.", "The text assigns vexing heat that eases with sweating, then recurs in bouts resembling alternating chills and fever, to Yangming when fever occurs at the time the text calls Chou-bu. A replete pulse points to purging with Major Qi-Order Decoction. A floating and large pulse points to inducing sweat with Cinnamon Twig Decoction. Both are given earlier.", "If after strong purging there is no bowel movement for six or seven days, vexation persists, and the abdomen is full and painful, the text identifies dry stool. It attributes this to previously retained food and indicates Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "With disturbed urination, stools now difficult and now easy to pass, occasional slight fever, and panting that prevents lying down, the text identifies dry stool and indicates Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "A desire to vomit after eating grain is assigned to Yangming and the text indicates Wuzhuyu Decoction. Worsening after the decoction is assigned to the upper burner and the text indicates Minor Banxia Decoction.", "Formula for Wuzhuyu Decoction.", "Wuzhuyu, one sheng. Renshen, three liang. Fresh ginger, six liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained. It records seven ge taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for Minor Banxia Decoction.", "Banxia, one sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, half a jin.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to one and a half sheng and strained, then divided into two warm portions.", "In Taiyang disease with a relaxed pulse at the inch, floating at the bar, and weak at the cubit, there is fever, sweating, and renewed aversion to cold. If there is only obstructed fullness below the heart and no vomiting, the text attributes this to the physician's purging. If no purging has occurred, and there is thirst without aversion to cold, it assigns the changed condition to Yangming.", "Frequent urination is followed by hard stool in the text's account. Even ten days without a bowel movement may bring no distress. If the person is thirsty and wants water, the text describes giving a little at a time and responding according to the pattern. If much water is drunk but urination remains difficult, it indicates Five Ling Powder.", "Formula for Five Ling Powder.", "Zhuling, eighteen zhu. Baizhu, eighteen zhu. Fuling, eighteen zhu. Zexie, one liang and six zhu. Guizhi, half a liang, with the bark removed.", "The text records making a powder of the five ingredients and taking a square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice liquid three times daily. For yellowing it adds ten fen of Yinchenhao.", "A faint yang pulse with little sweat indicates spontaneous harmonization. Heavy sweating is excessive.", "If the yang pulse is replete and sweating is induced, profuse sweating is likewise excessive. The text says excess here exhausts yang within, depletes fluids, and consequently hardens the stool."], "notes": [["熱越", "heat vents outward", "热越", "越写热往外透达。此句用发热而全身汗出说明热越，下句接写只头部出汗。", "越 conveys outward venting. The next clause changes the picture to sweating limited to the head."], ["劑頸而還", "stopping at the neck", "汗至颈止", "先点出「但头汗出」，这里再给出汗的边界。", "The preceding words locate sweat on the head. This phrase sets its boundary at the neck."], ["善忘", "frequent forgetfulness", "善忘", "善放在忘之前，表示常常如此。作者接着用久瘀血解释这个表现。", "善 before an action marks a tendency. The text then explains frequent forgetting through longstanding blood stasis."], ["大便初鞕後溏", "stool that starts hard and ends loose", "先硬后溏", "鞕是硬，溏是稀软。初、后在这里给出一次排便中前后两部分的性状。", "鞕 means hard and 溏 means loose. 初 and 後 trace the change from the beginning to the end of a bowel movement."], ["醜晡所發熱", "fever occurs at the time the text calls Chou-bu", "醜晡", "这里底本写「醜晡」，英译保留这一字样。句子用发热的时点来辅助辨别。", "The source reads 醜晡 here. The translation preserves the wording as Chou-bu, the timing marker used to classify the fever."], ["寸緩，關浮，尺弱", "relaxed pulse at the inch, floating at the bar, and weak at the cubit", "寸关尺", "寸、关、尺是腕部三个取脉位置。这里按位置各配一种脉象。", "Inch, bar, and cubit are three wrist positions. The sentence assigns a different pulse quality to each."], ["不更衣十日", "ten days without a bowel movement", "更衣", "更衣字面为换衣，本句借它指排便。可联系前面同段的「大便」。", "更衣 literally means changing clothes. Here it is a euphemism for a bowel movement, as the preceding clause helps show."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 6, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["When the pulse is floating and hollow, the text assigns floating to yang and hollow to yin. When these two qualities contend, stomach qi gives rise to heat and its yang is exhausted.", "When the Fuyang pulse is floating and rough, the floating quality means strong stomach qi and the rough quality means frequent urination. When floating and frequency combine, stool hardens and the spleen becomes constrained. The text indicates Maziren Pills.", "Formula for Maziren Pills.", "Maziren, two sheng. Shaoyao, half a jin. Roasted Zhishi, half a jin. Dahuang, one jin, with the outer layer removed. Roasted Houpo, one jin. Apricot kernels, one sheng, with skins and tips removed.", "The text records shaping the six ingredients into honey pills the size of wutong seeds. Ten pills are taken with liquid three times daily, increasing gradually until an effect is felt.", "If induced sweating on the second day of Taiyang disease fails to resolve it and heat steams outward, the text assigns the condition to Yangming and indicates Stomach-Regulating Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "For abdominal distention and fullness after induced vomiting in cold damage, the text indicates Stomach-Regulating Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "If mild vexation, frequent urination, and consequent hard stool follow induced vomiting, purging, or sweating in Taiyang disease, the text describes resolution by harmonizing with Minor Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "On the second or third day, with a weak pulse, no Taiyang or Bupleurum pattern, restlessness, and hardness below the heart, the person may still be able to eat by the fourth or fifth day. The text describes giving Minor Qi-Order Decoction in small amounts to harmonize gently and bring some relief, then giving one sheng on the sixth day. If no bowel movement occurs for six or seven days and urine is scant, it expects stool that begins hard but ends loose. The stool is not yet fully hardened, and purging will bring loose stool. Once urine flows freely and the stool has hardened throughout, the text permits attacking with Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "On the sixth or seventh day of cold damage, if vision is unclear, the eyes appear unsettled, there are no clear exterior or interior signs, stool is difficult to pass, and the body is slightly hot, the text calls this excess and indicates urgent purging with Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "In Yangming disease with fever and profuse sweating, the text indicates urgent purging with Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "If induced sweating brings no resolution and the abdomen is full and painful, the text indicates urgent purging with Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "If abdominal fullness does not diminish, or improves only slightly, the text indicates purging with Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "In combined Yangming and Shaoyang disease, diarrhea is expected. The text regards a pulse without mutual overpowering as favorable and overpowering as unfavorable. It calls mutual restraint and pressure overpowering. A slippery and rapid pulse indicates retained food and the text indicates purging with Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "With no clear exterior or interior signs and fever for seven or eight days, the text allows purging even when the pulse is floating and rapid. If it stays rapid after purging, accompanying heat is expected to promote digestion and frequent hunger. If there is no bowel movement by the sixth or seventh day, the text identifies stagnant blood and indicates Didang Decoction. If the pulse stays rapid with persistent diarrhea, it expects heat-associated passage of pus and blood, as discussed earlier.", "If the body and eyes turn yellow after sweating has been induced in cold damage, the text explains it through unresolved cold-dampness in the interior. It rules out further induced sweating and directs attention to the cold and dampness.", "If on the seventh or eighth day of cold damage the body turns orange-yellow, with difficult urination and slight abdominal fullness, the text indicates Yinchenhao Decoction, given earlier.", "In cold damage with yellowing of the body and fever, the text indicates Gardenia and Huangbai Decoction.", "Formula for Gardenia and Huangbai Decoction.", "Fifteen gardenia fruits, split. Roasted Gancao, one liang. Huangbai, two liang.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water, boiled down to one and a half sheng and strained, then divided into two warm portions.", "If heat stagnates in the interior in cold damage, the text expects the body to turn yellow and indicates Mahuang, Lianyao, and Red Adzuki Bean Decoction.", "Formula for Mahuang, Lianyao, and Red Adzuki Bean Decoction.", "Mahuang, two liang. Lianyao, two liang. Forty apricot kernels, with skins and tips removed. Red adzuki beans, one sheng. Twelve jujubes. Fresh catalpa inner bark, one jin, sliced. Fresh ginger, two liang, sliced. Roasted Gancao, two liang.", "For these eight ingredients, the text gives one dou of rainwater. Mahuang is boiled briefly first and the surface foam removed. The other ingredients are added, boiled to yield three sheng, and strained. The text records three warm portions, all taken within half a day."], "notes": [["浮而芤", "floating and hollow", "浮与芤", "浮写脉在浅处即可感到。芤写按下时有中空感。", "Floating describes a pulse felt at a shallow level. Hollow describes the impression felt under pressure."], ["趺陽脈", "Fuyang pulse", "趺阳", "趺阳是足背取脉处。该段以这里的浮涩来说明脾约。", "Fuyang is a pulse site on the top of the foot. The passage uses its floating and rough qualities in the account of spleen constraint."], ["以知為度", "until an effect is felt", "以知为度", "度是限度。知在这里说察觉到服后的变化，和前面的「渐加」连读。", "度 gives the limit. 知 here means noticing an effect, following the reference to gradual increase."], ["脈不負", "a pulse without mutual overpowering", "负", "下一句解释「负」为互相克责。此处把这个说法用于阳明、少阳合病的脉。", "The following clause explains 負 through mutual restraint and pressure. Here the term is applied to the pulse in combined Yangming and Shaoyang disease."], ["消穀善饑", "promote digestion and frequent hunger", "消谷善饥", "谷说谷食，善饥说常觉饥饿。这是本文把数脉与热相连后写出的表现。", "穀 refers to grain or food. 善饑 describes a tendency to feel hungry, which the sentence links to heat and a persistently rapid pulse."], ["身黃如橘子色", "the body turns orange-yellow", "橘子色", "这句用橘子的颜色直接描写黄的色调。", "The text uses the color of an orange to describe the shade of yellow."], ["潦水", "rainwater", "潦水", "潦水读作 lǎo shuǐ，指积聚的雨水。本方特别写了煎药的水。", "潦水 is pronounced lǎo shuǐ and refers to collected rainwater, the water specified in this formula."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 7, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["In Yangming disease, the body is hot and the person cannot eat. Eating brings dizziness and discomfort in the chest and heart region. Yellowing eventually develops. This is called grain jaundice, and the text indicates Yinchenhao Decoction, given earlier.", "In Yangming disease with bodily heat, yellowing, and distress or burning pain in the heart region attributed to alcohol and food, the text calls this alcohol jaundice and indicates Gardenia and Dahuang Decoction.", "Formula for Gardenia and Dahuang Decoction.", "Fourteen gardenia fruits. Dahuang, one liang. Five Zhishi fruits. Douchi, one sheng.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In Yangming disease with yellowing, depleted fluids and dryness, and a dark, dull complexion, the text attributes this to heat entering the blood aspect and indicates Lard and Hair Decoction.", "Formula for Lard and Hair Decoction.", "Lard, half a jin. Three egg-sized balls of loose hair.", "The text records cooking the two ingredients together in the fat until the hair dissolves, then dividing the preparation into two portions. The illness is said to leave through urination.", "In jaundice with abdominal fullness, difficult reddish urination, and spontaneous sweating, the text considers the exterior harmonized and the interior replete. It describes purging and indicates Dahuang and Xiaoshi Decoction.", "Formula for Dahuang and Xiaoshi Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang. Huangbai, four liang. Mangxiao, four liang. Fifteen gardenia fruits.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water. Three ingredients are boiled first to yield two sheng and the dregs are removed. The xiao is added and the liquid boiled down further to one sheng, taken at once.", "For the various yellowing disorders with abdominal pain and vomiting, the text indicates Major Bupleurum Decoction.", "Formula for Major Bupleurum Decoction.", "Chaihu, half a jin. Huangqin, three liang. Shaoyao, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, five liang, sliced. Four roasted Zhishi fruits. Twelve jujubes, split. Dahuang, two liang.", "For these eight ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, boiled down to six sheng. The dregs are removed and the liquid is boiled again. It records two sheng taken warm three times daily.", "If urine flows freely and its color remains unchanged in a yellowing illness, with abdominal fullness and panting, the text warns against clearing heat, saying it will bring hiccups. For hiccups it indicates Minor Banxia Decoction, given earlier.", "For people with yellowing disorders, the text directs attention to promoting urination and indicates Five Ling Powder with Yinchenhao. If the pulse is floating and relief is expected through sweating, it indicates Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Huangqi. The text refers back to Five Ling Powder and describes grinding ten fen of Yinchenhao together with it.", "Formula for Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Huangqi.", "Guizhi, three liang. Shaoyao, three liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Fifteen jujubes. Huangqi, two liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "For yellowing disorders with free urination, the text follows the approach used for deficiency taxation and indicates Minor Center-Fortifying Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Center-Fortifying Decoction.", "Guizhi, three liang. Shaoyao, six liang. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes. Malt syrup, one sheng.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water. Five ingredients are boiled first to yield three sheng. The dregs are removed and the malt syrup added and dissolved over low heat. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In Yangming disease with abdominal fullness, difficult urination, a withered, yellow, dry tongue, and inability to sleep, the text assigns the case to the yellowing disorders.", "For jaundice the text gives a period of eighteen days. It expects improvement after ten or more days of treatment and considers worsening a sign of difficulty.", "When the pulse is deep, the person thirsts for water, and urination is difficult, the text expects later yellowing.", "With a faint and wiry Fuyang pulse, the text expects abdominal fullness. If fullness is absent, it expects difficult stool and pain on either side of the chest. It identifies deficiency cold, describes warming, and indicates Wuzhuyu Decoction, given earlier.", "For pain around the navel, the text identifies Yangming wind-cold and impeded movement of grain qi. Purging instead is said to bring a surge of qi. If no surge occurs, fullness and obstruction develop below the heart. The text describes warming and indicates Regulate the Middle Decoction.", "Formula for Regulate the Middle Decoction.", "Renshen, three liang. Baizhu, three liang. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Ganjiang, three liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In Yangming disease with fever for more than ten days, a floating and rapid pulse, abdominal fullness, and unchanged eating and drinking, the text indicates Seven-Ingredient Houpo Decoction.", "Formula for Seven-Ingredient Houpo Decoction."], "notes": [["穀疸", "grain jaundice", "谷疸", "这一个名称承接不能食、食后头眩。下一段另列与酒食相关的酒疸。", "The name follows inability to eat and dizziness after eating. The next passage gives another jaundice name linked with alcohol and food."], ["酒疸", "alcohol jaundice", "酒疸", "前面「因于酒食」交代命名所据。这里把发黄与心中不适等一并写出。", "The preceding phrase links the name to alcohol and food. The description includes yellowing as well as distress in the heart region."], ["血分", "blood aspect", "血分", "分说层面。句子先给津液枯燥、颜色晦暗，再用热入血分来归纳。", "分 denotes an aspect or level. The passage first describes dry, depleted fluids and a dull complexion, then interprets them through heat entering the blood aspect."], ["表和裡實", "the exterior harmonized and the interior replete", "表和里实", "这里给表和里用了两种形容。汗自行出与腹满、小便赤涩合在一起，是前文提供的表现。", "The exterior and interior receive different descriptions. The text groups spontaneous sweating with abdominal fullness and difficult reddish urination."], ["大黃硝石湯方", "Formula for Dahuang and Xiaoshi Decoction", "硝石与芒硝", "这一底本方用硝石为名，列药时写芒硝，译文保留两行各自的写法。", "The heading says Xiaoshi and the ingredient list says Mangxiao. The translation preserves the wording of both lines."], ["虛勞法", "the approach used for deficiency taxation", "虚劳", "虚劳是古书的一类虚损说法。这段以小便自利作为改用这种读法的条件。", "Deficiency taxation names a classical category of depletion. Here free urination is the condition for approaching the case in that way."], ["飲食如故", "unchanged eating and drinking", "如故", "如故就是像平常一样。原文把饮食照常与发热、腹满并列。", "如故 means as before. Eating and drinking as usual is listed alongside fever and abdominal fullness."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-18", "page": 8, "title": "Yang Brightness Diseases", "english": ["Houpo, half a jin. Gancao, three liang. Dahuang, three liang. Five Zhishi fruits. Guizhi, two liang. Fresh ginger, five liang. Ten jujubes.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, boiled down to four sheng and strained. It records eight ge taken warm three times daily.", "In Yangming disease with cutting abdominal pain, thunderous rumbling, rising fullness, and vomiting, the text identifies deficiency cold and indicates Fuzi and Rice Decoction.", "Formula for Fuzi and Rice Decoction.", "One Fuzi, processed by heating, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Gancao, one liang. Ten jujubes. Non-glutinous rice, half a sheng.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, with boiling continued until the rice is cooked. The dregs are removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In Yangming disease with cold abdominal pain, vomiting, inability to eat, and a sudden protrusion as if showing a head and feet, with pain too severe for anyone to approach, the text indicates Major Center-Fortifying Decoction.", "Formula for Major Center-Fortifying Decoction.", "Shujiao, two ge, prepared by removing the seeds and heating to release moisture. Ganjiang, four liang. Renshen, one liang. Malt syrup, one sheng.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Three ingredients are boiled first to yield two sheng. The dregs are removed, malt syrup is added, and the liquid is boiled gently to yield one and a half sheng, divided into two warm portions. After the time needed to cook a meal, the text allows two sheng of gruel and records taking the next portion afterward. It describes eating gruel for a day and keeping warmly covered.", "In Yangming disease with abdominal fullness, pain to one side below the ribs, slight fever, and a wiry and tight pulse, the text describes purging with warming ingredients and indicates Dahuang, Fuzi, and Xixin Decoction.", "Formula for Dahuang, Fuzi, and Xixin Decoction.", "Dahuang, three liang. Fuzi, three liang, processed by heating and peeled. Xixin, two liang.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained, then divided into three warm portions. After the first, it gives the time needed to walk four or five li before the next portion.", "A question: How can retained food in Yangming be distinguished? The teacher replied: The pulse at the inch opening is floating and large but feels rough on pressure. At the cubit it is also faint and rough. This is how retained food is recognized. The text indicates Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "A rapid and slippery pulse at the inch opening is described as indicating retained food.", "Diarrhea without a desire to eat is described as indicating retained food.", "A tight pulse like a twisting rope is described as indicating retained food.", "A tight pulse with abdominal pain and aversion to wind and cold is described as indicating retained food.", "When retained food lies in the upper stomach region, the text describes inducing vomiting and indicates Guadi Powder.", "Formula for Guadi Powder.", "Guadi, one fen. Red adzuki beans, one fen.", "The text records pounding the two ingredients into powder and boiling seven ge of Xiangchi to obtain a liquid. It describes taking one qian spoonful of powder mixed with the warm liquid. If vomiting does not occur, it records a gradual increase, with no further portion after vomiting."], "notes": [["厚朴半斤", "Houpo, half a jin", "接前页药方", "前一页最后一行是厚朴七物汤方名。这里从厚朴开始列出七味。", "The previous page ends with the heading for Seven-Ingredient Houpo Decoction. This line starts its seven-ingredient list with Houpo."], ["雷鳴", "thunderous rumbling", "腹中雷鸣", "雷鸣是形容声音大的比喻，和本句的腹痛、胀满、呕吐合读。", "Thunder describes the loudness of the abdominal sounds. The passage lists them alongside pain, fullness, and vomiting."], ["有物突起，如見頭足", "a sudden protrusion as if showing a head and feet", "如见头足", "「如」引出所见形状的比喻，头足写突起看起来有轮廓。", "如 introduces an image for the shape of the protrusion, likened to a head and feet."], ["如一炊頃", "the time needed to cook a meal", "一炊顷", "顷说短时间。古文用煮一餐饭所需的时间来交代两段之间的间隔。", "頃 indicates an interval. The text measures it by the time it takes to cook a meal."], ["如人行四五裡", "the time needed to walk four or five li", "行路计时", "这里把走四五里所需的时间作为两次之间的间隔。里保留为古代距离单位。", "The interval is expressed through how long it takes to walk four or five li, with li retained as the historical distance unit."], ["脈緊如轉索", "A tight pulse like a twisting rope", "转索", "索是绳。本句借绳索扭转的样子帮助读者想象紧脉。", "索 means rope. The image of a rope twisting conveys the felt quality of a tight pulse."], ["宿食在上脘", "retained food lies in the upper stomach region", "宿食与上脘", "宿食指停留未化的饮食。上脘在这里给这种停留标出上部位置。", "Retained food describes food that remains undigested. The upper stomach region specifies where the text places it."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-24", "page": 1, "title": "Spasm, Yin–Yang Exchange, and Illness after Recovery", "english": ["In Greater Yang disease, fever without sweating, aversion to cold, and a deep, slow pulse are termed rigid spasm.", "In Greater Yang disease, fever with sweating, no aversion to cold, and a floating, rapid pulse are termed yielding spasm.", "Greater Yang disease with fever and a deep, fine pulse is termed spasm and described as difficult to treat.", "The text says that excessive induced sweating in Greater Yang disease can bring on spasm.", "The text associates purging a wind illness with spasm and subsequent induced sweating with severe tightening.", "For people with sores, the passage cautions against inducing sweat, which it associates with spasm.", "Spasm illness is described as body heat with cold feet, stiffness of the neck and nape, aversion to cold, intermittent head heat, red face and eyes, shaking of the head alone, a suddenly clenched mouth, and back arching. The passage describes induced sweat as worsening aversion to cold when cold and dampness combine and the exterior grows weaker. A snakelike pulse suddenly becoming long and large after sweating is taken to herald resolution. An unchanged or hidden, wiry pulse is taken to indicate continuing illness.", "The spasm pulse, when pressed, feels tight and taut as a string and runs straight up and down.", "Spasm accompanied by sores from moxibustion is described as difficult to treat.", "When the signs of Greater Yang disease are present but the body is markedly rigid and the pulse unexpectedly deep and slow, the passage classifies it as spasm and names Guālóu and Guìzhī Decoction.", "Formula record: Guālóu and Guìzhī Decoction.", "The record lists guālóu root, three liǎng; peeled guìzhī, three liǎng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; sháoyào, three liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, two liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method uses a low fire to reduce seven shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and records one comfortably warm shēng per serving, three times a day.", "In Greater Yang disease with no sweating, unexpectedly scant urination, qi surging into the chest, and a clenched mouth that prevents speech, the passage anticipates rigid spasm and names Gěgēn Decoction.", "Formula record: Gěgēn Decoction.", "The record lists gěgēn, four liǎng; máhuáng with joints removed, three liǎng; guìzhī, processed gāncǎo, and sháoyào, two liǎng each; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method begins with one dǒu of water and reduces it by two shēng while simmering máhuáng and gěgēn. It removes the foam, adds the other ingredients, reduces the liquid to three shēng, and removes the dregs. The passage records a warm one-shēng serving and covering for a slight sweat, with a second serving only if sweat does not appear.", "In spasm illness with cold hands and feet, intermittent fever, blue-green lips, sunken eyes, and a deep, wiry pulse, the passage describes wind entering Reverting Yin and names Guìzhī Decoction with Fùzǐ, Dāngguī, Xìxīn, Rénshēn, and Dried Ginger Added.", "Formula record: Guìzhī Decoction with Fùzǐ, Dāngguī, Xìxīn, Rénshēn, and Dried Ginger Added.", "The record lists guìzhī and sháoyào, three liǎng each; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; dāngguī, four liǎng; xìxīn, one liǎng; one processed, peeled fùzǐ split into eight pieces; rénshēn, two liǎng; dried ginger, one and a half liǎng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method reduces one dǒu and two shēng of water to four shēng and removes the dregs. It records warm servings of one shēng, three by day and one by night.", "Spasm begins under Greater Yang in this account. Fever, sweating, and a wiry, full pulse are taken as a shift to Yang Brightness, for which the passage names the Order the Qi class of formulas.", "Chest fullness, a clenched mouth, inability to lie flat on the mat, spasms of the legs, and clenched teeth are described in this pattern. The passage names Major Order the Qi Decoction.", "Formula record: Major Order the Qi Decoction.", "The record lists wine-washed dàhuáng, four liǎng; peeled hòupò, half a jīn; five toasted zhǐshí; and mángxiāo, three gě.", "The historical method starts with one dǒu of water and simmers zhǐshí and hòupò to five shēng, then removes the dregs. It adds dàhuáng and reduces the liquid to two shēng before straining again. It records adding mángxiāo, heating gently to one or two boils, and dividing into two warm servings, with the second omitted after a purgative effect.", "Cold-damage yin–yang exchange is described as bodily heaviness, shortness of breath, lower-abdominal tension that may draw into genital spasms, heat surging into the chest, a head too heavy to raise, spots before the eyes, and tension in the knees and shins. The text names Burned Garment Powder.", "Formula record: Burned Garment Powder.", "The historical text records burning cloth taken from the crotch area of an opposite-sex undergarment and administering the ashes mixed with water. It records three servings in a day and interprets free urination and slight swelling of the glans as signs of improvement in a male patient."], "notes": [["剛痙", "rigid spasm", "刚痉", "首两条都说发热，区别在有无汗、恶寒以及脉的深浅快慢。", "Compare the paired openings: both have fever, while sweat, aversion to cold, and pulse qualities distinguish the two labels."], ["柔痙", "yielding spasm", "柔痉", "这里「柔」用于与刚痉相对。正文给的是汗出、不恶寒、脉浮数的一组表现。", "Yielding is the counterpart to rigid in this pair. The line associates it with sweating, lack of aversion to cold, and a floating, rapid pulse."], ["口噤", "a suddenly clenched mouth", "口噤", "「噤」写口闭紧，后文又有「不得语」。", "The mouth is clenched. A later line makes the practical consequence explicit: inability to speak."], ["背反張", "back arching", "背反张", "「反」写向后，「张」写伸张。它和颈项紧急一同描述身体的姿态。", "This is a description of the back arching backward, alongside stiffness of the neck and nape."], ["伏弦", "hidden, wiry pulse", "伏弦", "两个词合写脉。「伏」指深隐难取，「弦」形容如弦的紧直感。", "Hidden describes how deeply or faintly the pulse can be found, while wiry describes its taut quality."], ["轉屬陽明", "a shift to Yang Brightness", "转属", "「转属」是原文重新给这组表现分类。它接在发热、汗出和脉象变化之后。", "The phrase reclassifies the pattern. Notice the changed signs immediately before the new assignment."], ["陰陽易", "yin–yang exchange", "阴阳易", "这是古籍里的病名。这一条先列身体感觉与拘急部位，再用古代的解释来统摄。", "This is a historical illness label. The passage groups the listed bodily sensations and sites of tightening under it."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-24", "page": 2, "title": "Spasm, Yin–Yang Exchange, and Illness after Recovery", "english": ["For relapse from exertion after a serious illness has improved, the text names Zhǐshí, Zhīzǐ, and Fermented Soybean Decoction. If retained food is present, it records adding five or six gaming-piece-sized portions of dàhuáng.", "Formula record: Zhǐshí, Zhīzǐ, and Fermented Soybean Decoction.", "The record lists three toasted zhǐshí, fourteen split zhīzǐ, and one shēng of fragrant fermented soybeans wrapped in cloth.", "The historical method reduces seven shēng of clear fermented grain water to four before adding the first two ingredients. It reduces the liquid to two shēng, adds the fermented soybeans for five or six more boils, removes the solids, and describes two warm servings with covering for slight sweating.", "For recurring fever after recovery from cold damage, the text names Minor Cháihú Decoction. It also distinguishes a floating pulse, for which it describes release by sweating, from a deep, full pulse, for which it describes release by purging.", "Formula record: Minor Cháihú Decoction.", "The record lists cháihú, eight liǎng; huángqín and rénshēn, three liǎng each; processed gāncǎo, three liǎng; rinsed bànxià, half a shēng; sliced fresh ginger, three liǎng; and twelve split jujubes.", "The historical method reduces one dǒu and two shēng of water to six shēng, removes the dregs, reduces it again to three, and records three warm one-shēng servings over a day.", "For water qi from the waist down after a serious illness has improved, the text names Mǔlì and Zéxiè Powder.", "Formula record: Mǔlì and Zéxiè Powder.", "The record lists mǔlì, zéxiè, guālóu root, washed shǔqī, toasted tínglì, heated shānglù root, and washed hǎizǎo.", "The record takes equal portions, pounds them separately, sifts them, and works the powder further in a mortar. It describes three servings a day mixed with a plain drink, stopping once urination is free.", "Long-lasting frequent spitting after recovery from a serious illness is attributed to cold above the chest. The text names warming with Rectify the Middle Pills and refers to the Sudden Turmoil chapter for the formula.", "For thinness, weakness, shortness of breath, upward counterflow, and nausea after cold damage resolves, the text names Bamboo Leaf and Gypsum Decoction.", "Formula record: Bamboo Leaf and Gypsum Decoction.", "The record lists two handfuls of bamboo leaves; shígāo, one jīn; rinsed bànxià, half a shēng; rénshēn, three liǎng; màiméndōng, one shēng; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; and non-glutinous rice, half a shēng.", "The historical method starts with one dǒu of water, reduces it with the first six ingredients to six shēng, removes the dregs, adds the rice and cooks it, then removes the rice. The record specifies three warm one-shēng servings a day.", "For slight evening vexation after a serious illness has resolved, the passage explains that the patient was pressed to eat while the spleen and stomach were still weak and unable to digest grain. It associates reducing food with improvement."], "notes": [["差後", "has improved", "差后", "这里的「差」读 chài，指病势好转。后面几条都从恢复阶段接着讲。", "Here 差 means improvement. Several following cases are set during recovery."], ["勞複", "relapse from exertion", "劳复", "「劳」指劳倦，「复」指病情又起。这条把两件事连起来讲。", "The phrase combines exertion and recurrence, so the text is speaking of symptoms returning after exertion."], ["香豉", "fragrant fermented soybeans", "香豉", "豉是发酵豆制品。原方把它比枳实、栀子更晚下锅，可从方文的前后次序看出。", "Chǐ is fermented soybean. In this formula record it is added later than the other two ingredients."], ["脈沉實", "deep, full pulse", "沉实", "沉说按得深才明显，实形容脉动较有力。这里与前一分句的浮脉相对。", "Deep describes how much pressure is needed to feel the pulse. Full describes the impression of force or substance."], ["水氣", "water qi", "水气", "这是本书的一种病因解释。这里先用「从腰以下」限定它被观察到的范围。", "Water qi is a category in the text. The line specifies that it is observed from the waist downward."], ["日暮微煩", "slight evening vexation", "日暮微烦", "「微」表示轻微，「日暮」交代时间。下一句才给出古书对进食与烦躁关系的解释。", "The phrase states degree and time: slight, toward evening. The following clause supplies the text’s explanation."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-23", "title": "Sudden Turmoil, Vomiting, and Diarrhea", "page": 1, "english": ["A question: What sort of illness is sudden turmoil? The reply: Vomiting together with diarrhea is called sudden turmoil.", "The teacher said: The text places sudden turmoil under Greater Yin. It always involves vomiting and diarrhea, though vomiting and diarrhea have other causes as well. In sudden turmoil, cold and heat intermingle and cause disorder in the middle.", "The text explains vomiting as heat qi going upward against its course, and diarrhea as cold qi pouring downward. It also describes irregular eating and drinking leading to congestion in the middle. When the congestion takes an upward course, vomiting follows, and when it takes a downward course, diarrhea follows. The text classifies this as vomiting and diarrhea apart from sudden turmoil.", "A question: What illness gives fever, headache, body aches, aversion to cold, vomiting, and diarrhea? The reply distinguishes this from sudden turmoil, in which vomiting and diarrhea arise on their own. The aversion to cold, aches, and further fever are the clues it gives for this distinction.", "For sudden turmoil with retching, vomiting, and diarrhea, without chills or fever and with a yielding, weak pulse, the text assigns Regulate the Middle Decoction.", "Regulate the Middle Decoction", "Ginseng 3 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, and dried ginger 3 liǎng.", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "When vomiting comes before diarrhea, with abdominal fullness and pain, no chills or fever, and a yielding, weak, rough pulse, the text attributes the signs to retained food. It assigns White Atractylodes, Poria, Pinellia, and Immature Bitter Orange Decoction.", "White Atractylodes, Poria, Pinellia, and Immature Bitter Orange Decoction", "White atractylodes 3 liǎng, poria 4 liǎng, pinellia 1 shēng (washed), and immature bitter orange 1½ liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and divides it into three warm servings.", "With chest fullness, an urge to vomit without vomiting, diarrhea at wider intervals, no chills or fever, gripping abdominal pain, and a weak, bound wrist pulse, the text places retained food above and assigns Melon Stalk Powder.", "Melon Stalk Powder", "Melon stalk 1 fēn and adzuki bean 1 fēn.", "The text pounds the two ingredients into powder and uses liquid cooked from 7 hé of fermented soybeans to mix a qián spoonful. It records taking it warm, adding a little if vomiting does not occur, and stopping once vomiting occurs freely.", "For sudden turmoil with retching, vomiting, diarrhea with undigested food, cold hands and feet in reversal, and a deep, slow pulse, the text assigns Four Reversals Decoction.", "Four Reversals Decoction", "Roasted licorice 2 liǎng, dried ginger 1½ liǎng, 1 aconite root (used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), and ginseng 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "For vomiting and diarrhea with fever and a yielding, weak, large pulse, the text assigns White Atractylodes, Gypsum, Pinellia, and Dried Ginger Decoction.", "White Atractylodes, Gypsum, Pinellia, and Dried Ginger Decoction", "White atractylodes 3 liǎng, gypsum ½ jīn (wrapped in cotton), pinellia ½ shēng (washed), and dried ginger 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "If there is thirst, the text adds ginseng 2 liǎng and coptis 1 liǎng.", "The text describes vomiting severe enough for roundworms to come up, diarrhea now frequent and now infrequent, mild body heat, cold hands and feet in reversal, a blue-green complexion, and a deep, wiry, tight pulse. It assigns Four Reversals Decoction with Evodia and Coptis.", "Four Reversals Decoction with Evodia and Coptis", "1 aconite root (used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), dried ginger 1½ liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, ginseng 2 liǎng, evodia fruit ½ shēng, and coptis 1 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the six ingredients to 2 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm twice a day.", "For sudden turmoil with vomiting, diarrhea, thirst, sweating, shortness of breath, and a weak, yielding pulse, the text assigns Regulate the Middle Decoction with Extra Ginseng and Trichosanthes Root.", "Regulate the Middle Decoction with Extra Ginseng and Trichosanthes Root", "Ginseng 4 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, and trichosanthes root 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "When drinking water immediately brings vomiting and eating brings diarrhea, with a slow, weak pulse, the text assigns Regulate the Middle Decoction with Aconite.", "Regulate the Middle Decoction with Aconite", "Ginseng 3 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, and 1 aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces).", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day."], "notes": [["霍亂", "sudden turmoil", "霍乱", "本篇开头用呕吐、下利引出「霍乱」，接下来的问答进一步把这里的古代证候与其他吐利分开。", "The opening introduces the classical category through vomiting and diarrhea, then narrows its meaning in the exchange that follows."], ["屬太陰", "under Greater Yin", "属太阴", "太阴是全书六类病证之一。这里是作者归类霍乱的方式，后句补充寒热杂合于中的解释。", "Greater Yin is one of the six pattern categories. The passage classifies sudden turmoil there and then describes cold and heat mingling in the middle."], ["寒氣下注", "cold qi pouring downward", "寒气下注", "「下注」的意象是往下流。作者用这个方向解释下利，和前句向上的呕吐相对。", "The image is of a downward flow, used here to explain diarrhea alongside the upward movement associated with vomiting."], ["宿食", "retained food", "宿食", "「宿」有停留一段时间的意思。本段将这一古代判断和先吐后利、腹满痛等表现联系起来。", "The word evokes food that has lingered. The line relates the category to vomiting before diarrhea and painful abdominal fullness."], ["時疏", "at wider intervals", "时疏", "「疏」说间隔较开。本篇也有「时密时疏」，用疏密写下利发作的频率。", "疏 means spaced out in time. A later line describes the bouts alternating between frequent and infrequent."], ["清穀", "undigested food", "清谷", "这里指大便中可辨认出未消化的食物。「下利」说明同时有腹泻。", "The phrase describes recognizable, undigested food passed with diarrhea."], ["蚘出", "roundworms to come up", "蚘出", "「蚘」在这里是蛔虫的旧字。原文把它列在呕吐很重时的表现之后。", "蚘 is an older character for roundworm. The text names worms coming up in an episode of severe vomiting."], ["食穀則利", "eating brings diarrhea", "食谷则利", "「谷」在这里从日常食物来理解。这句同前面的饮水即吐并列。", "Here 穀 refers broadly to food. The line pairs diarrhea after eating with vomiting after drinking water."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-23", "title": "Sudden Turmoil, Vomiting, and Diarrhea", "page": 2, "english": ["For abdominal distention and pain that shift upward and downward, there is vomiting when the painful qi rises and diarrhea when it descends. With a yielding, rough pulse, the text assigns Regulate the Middle Decoction, given above.", "The text says sudden turmoil can take deficient or excess forms according to the person’s underlying condition. A yielding, weak pulse is assigned to deficiency and Regulate the Middle Decoction. A tense, skipping pulse is assigned to excess and Kudzu Root, Coptis, Scutellaria, and Licorice Decoction. The Regulate the Middle formula appears above.", "Kudzu Root, Coptis, Scutellaria, and Licorice Decoction", "Kudzu root ½ jīn, coptis 3 liǎng, scutellaria 3 liǎng, and roasted licorice 2 liǎng.", "The text starts with 8 shēng of water, boils the kudzu root first until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, then reduces to 2 shēng and strains. It divides the liquid into two warm servings.", "The text explains sinew spasms in sudden turmoil as the result of a cold influence already lodged among the sinews, where it injures nutritive qi and manifests in the course of the illness. The pulse is expected to be yielding and weak. If it is instead wiry and tense and there is reversal cold, the text assigns Regulate the Middle Decoction with Aconite, given above.", "For sudden turmoil with headache, fever, and body pains, the text assigns Five-Ingredient Poria Powder when heat predominates and the person wants water. When cold predominates and the person does not drink, it assigns Regulate the Middle Pill.", "Five-Ingredient Poria Powder", "Polyporus 18 zhū, white atractylodes 18 zhū, poria 18 zhū, cinnamon twig ½ liǎng, and alisma 1 liǎng and 6 zhū.", "The text pounds the five ingredients into powder and records a square-inch spoonful mixed in plain rice drink three times a day, with plenty of warm water. It takes sweating as a sign of recovery and refers to the customary aftercare.", "Regulate the Middle Pill", "Ginseng 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 3 liǎng, and white atractylodes 3 liǎng.", "The text pounds and sifts the four ingredients and binds them with honey into pills the size of an egg yolk. It records crushing a pill in several hé of boiling water and taking the mixture warm three times by day and twice by night. If warmth in the abdomen has yet to develop, it mentions increasing to three or four pills.", "In cold damage with a faint, rough pulse, the text describes an illness that began as sudden turmoil and has now become cold damage. Another four or five days bring it to the yin channels. Passage into yin is said to bring diarrhea. If the person feels as though about to pass stool, passes gas instead, and still has no diarrhea, the text assigns the pattern to Yang Brightness. It expects hard stool and recovery on the thirteenth day, when the channel course is complete.", "After diarrhea, the text expects the stool to harden and regards recovery of appetite at that point as favorable. If the person still cannot eat, the text traces improvement in appetite through the next channel and continued ability to eat through another. It expects recovery a day later and assigns continued illness outside Yang Brightness.", "If cold damage with a faint pulse brings diarrhea that then stops by itself, the text attributes the change to loss of blood and assigns Four Reversals Decoction with Extra Ginseng.", "Four Reversals Decoction with Extra Ginseng", "Roasted licorice 2 liǎng, 1 aconite root (used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), dried ginger 1½ liǎng, and ginseng 3 liǎng.", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 1 shēng and 2 hé, strains, and divides the liquid into two warm servings.", "If vomiting and diarrhea stop but body pain continues, the text calls for assessing the signs and gently resolving the exterior. It assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, fresh ginger 3 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes.", "The text reduces 7 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day. It follows Greater Yang instructions for aftercare and restrictions.", "For vomiting, diarrhea, sweating, fever, aversion to cold, tightness in the limbs, and cold hands and feet in reversal, the text assigns Four Reversals Decoction, given above.", "When vomiting and diarrhea are joined by free urination and profuse sweating, with undigested food in the diarrhea, cold within and heat without, and a faint pulse almost gone, the text assigns Four Reversals Decoction, given above.", "When vomiting and diarrhea have stopped but sweating with reversal cold, persistent tightness in the limbs, and a faint pulse almost gone remain, the text assigns Restore the Pulse Four Reversals Decoction with Pig Bile.", "Restore the Pulse Four Reversals Decoction with Pig Bile", "Roasted licorice 2 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, 1 large aconite root (used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), pig bile ½ hé, and ginseng 2 liǎng.", "The text first boils the four ingredients other than pig bile in 3 shēng of water until 1 shēng remains. It strains, stirs in the bile, and divides the liquid into two warm servings."], "notes": [["虛實", "deficient or excess forms", "虚实", "本句把所见证候的虚实和病人原来的情况联系起来。接着用两组脉象作区分。", "The line relates the two presentations to the person’s underlying condition and distinguishes them by pulse qualities."], ["轉筋", "sinew spasms", "转筋", "用来形容筋脉抽搐、拘挛的现象。下文按古书的寒邪与营气理论解释它。", "This expression describes cramping or spasms of the sinews. The text explains it through its account of cold and nutritive qi."], ["方寸匙", "square-inch spoonful", "方寸匙", "方书取散剂用的古量名，按古代的「寸」理解。", "A historical measure for powders, named for a spoon about one cùn square."], ["如雞子黃大", "the size of an egg yolk", "如鸡子黄大", "用蛋黄作大小参照。古书方后常见以容易想象的物品形容丸剂大小。", "The egg yolk gives a visual size comparison for the historical pill."], ["經盡", "the channel course is complete", "经尽", "这里用一经病程走完，解释作者所预计的转愈日数。", "Completion of the illness’s course through a channel is the text’s explanation for its expected day of recovery."], ["消息和解其外", "assessing the signs and gently resolving the exterior", "消息和解", "「消息」在这里指审察变化、酌量处置。本段说吐利已止，余下的身痛另行考虑。", "The expression calls for gauging the remaining signs and adjusting the approach. Vomiting and diarrhea have already stopped in this line."], ["脈微欲絕", "a faint pulse almost gone", "脉微欲绝", "「微」写脉来极弱，「欲绝」说几乎摸不到。它是在本页连续两条里都出现的严重脉象。", "The pulse is so faint it is almost imperceptible. The description recurs in these two passages on severe illness."], ["通脈", "Restore the Pulse", "通脉", "这是方名的一部分，紧接前文对微脉将绝的描写读，能知道方名在回应什么。", "This part of the formula name should be read alongside the preceding description of a pulse that is barely detectable."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-23", "title": "Sudden Turmoil, Vomiting, and Diarrhea", "page": 3, "english": ["After vomiting and diarrhea, if there is sweating, the pulse is even, and there is slight agitation, the text attributes it to recent depletion that leaves the person unable to handle qi from food."], "notes": [["新虛不勝穀氣", "recent depletion that leaves the person unable to handle qi from food", "新虚不胜谷气", "「新虚」说虚弱刚发生，「谷气」是古书形容来自饮食的气。这里是作者对吐利后微烦的解释。", "The text attributes slight agitation after vomiting and diarrhea to a recent depletion that makes it hard to handle what it calls qi from food."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-27", "page": 1, "title": "Blood Impediment and Deficiency Taxation", "english": ["A question: How does blood impediment arise? The teacher replied: In a person living in ease and honor, the bones may be weak while the flesh is ample. Fatigue, sweating, lying down and moving at an ill-timed moment, and exposure to a slight wind are linked here to the onset.", "The passage describes a faint, rough inch pulse and a slightly tight bar pulse. It records needling to draw yang qi and harmonize the pulse, with resolution expected when the tightness goes.", "In blood impediment, both yin and yang pulses may be faint, or the inch and bar faint with slight tightness at the cubit. The outward sign is impaired sensation resembling wind impediment. The text names Five-Ingredient Huángqí and Guìzhī Decoction.", "Formula record: Five-Ingredient Huángqí and Guìzhī Decoction.", "The record lists huángqí, guìzhī, and sháoyào, three liǎng each; fresh ginger, six liǎng; and twelve jujubes.", "The historical directions reduce six shēng of water to two and record three warm servings of seven gě in a day.", "In an apparently healthy man, a large pulse is read here as taxation; extreme vacuity is also read as taxation.", "The text associates a thin or pale complexion in a man with thirst and blood loss. Sudden panting and palpitations with a floating pulse are read as interior deficiency.", "In a man with a vacuous, deep, wiry pulse, no cold or heat signs, shortness of breath, internal urgency, difficult urination, a white complexion, periodic dimness of vision with nosebleeds, and fullness below the navel, the text attributes the signs to taxation.", "The text describes taxation illness with a floating, large pulse and vexation of the hands and feet. It worsens in spring and summer and improves in autumn and winter. The account includes genital cold, spontaneous emission, aching and wasting, and difficulty walking.", "In a man, a floating, weak, rough pulse is associated here with childlessness and cold, thin essence.", "The text associates loss of essence with a wiry, urgent Lesser Yin pulse, cold of the glans, dizziness, hair loss, and an extremely vacuous, hollow, slow pulse. It also lists undigested grain in the stool and loss of blood and essence.", "The passage associates hollow, stirring, faint, or tight pulse qualities with loss of essence in men and dreams of intercourse in women. It names Guìzhī, Lónggǔ, and Mǔlì Decoction and also Tiānxióng Powder.", "Formula record: Guìzhī, Lónggǔ, and Mǔlì Decoction.", "The record lists guìzhī and sháoyào, three liǎng each; processed gāncǎo, two liǎng; fresh ginger, three liǎng; twelve jujubes; and lónggǔ and mǔlì, three liǎng each.", "The historical directions reduce seven shēng of water to three, remove the dregs, and divide the liquid into three warm servings.", "Formula record: Tiānxióng Powder.", "The record lists processed tiānxióng, three liǎng; báizhú, eight liǎng; guìzhī, six liǎng; and lónggǔ, three liǎng.", "The historical directions describe pounding the four ingredients to a powder, with half a qián-spoon in wine three times a day and gradual adjustment according to the response.", "In a man who appears healthy, a vacuous, weak, fine, and faint pulse is associated here with a tendency to night sweating.", "In people in their fifties or sixties, the text associates a large pulse with impediment extending alongside the back. It also attributes intestinal rumbling and elongated or goiter-like swellings to taxation. A small, deep, slow pulse is associated with loss of qi, panting and thirst on walking fast, and cold extremities.", "For deficiency taxation with internal urgency, palpitations and nosebleeds, abdominal pain, loss of essence in sleep, aching limbs, heat and vexation in the hands and feet, and dryness of the throat and mouth, the text names Minor Fortify the Middle Decoction.", "Formula record: Minor Fortify the Middle Decoction.", "The record lists guìzhī, three liǎng; sháoyào, six liǎng; processed gāncǎo, three liǎng; fresh ginger, three liǎng; twelve jujubes; and maltose syrup, one shēng.", "The historical directions reduce seven shēng of water to three, remove the dregs, add thick maltose syrup and dissolve it over low heat, and record three warm servings of one shēng in a day.", "For deficiency taxation with internal urgency and various deficiencies, the text names Huángqí Fortify the Middle Decoction.", "Formula record: Huángqí Fortify the Middle Decoction.", "The record adds huángqí, one and a half liǎng, to the preceding formula. It adds fresh ginger, one liǎng, for shortness of breath and chest fullness; removes jujubes and adds fúlíng, one and a half liǎng, for abdominal fullness; removes jujubes and adds zhǐshí, one and a half liǎng, for bound stool; and adds bànxià, three liǎng, for depletion of lung qi.", "For deficiency taxation with low back pain, tightening below the navel, and difficult urination, the text names Kidney Qi Pills.", "Formula record: Kidney Qi Pills.", "The record lists dìhuáng, eight liǎng; shǔyù and shānzhūyú, four liǎng each; zéxiè, mǔdānpí, and fúlíng, three liǎng each; guìzhī, one liǎng; and one processed fùzǐ.", "The historical directions describe pounding and sifting the eight ingredients, adding prepared honey and forming pills the size of a Chinese parasol-tree seed. They record fifteen pills in wine, increasing gradually to twenty-five, twice daily, with a plain drink as an alternative to wine.", "For deficiency taxation with vexation and inability to sleep, the text names Suānzǎorén Decoction.", "Formula record: Suānzǎorén Decoction."], "notes": [["血痹", "blood impediment", "血痹", "「痹」在古籍中可写闭阻不通的感觉。此篇用外证「身体不仁」帮助说明血痹。", "Bì conveys obstruction. The chapter illustrates the term with impaired sensation in the body."], ["身體不仁", "impaired sensation", "不仁", "这里说的是身体感觉不灵敏，不是品德上的「不仁」。", "Here bù rén describes dulled bodily sensation, rather than a moral quality."], ["平人", "apparently healthy man", "平人", "本条一开头说平人，下一句才描述脉上所见。作者刻意把外表平常和脉象并列。", "The person appears well at the outset. The passage then contrasts that appearance with what is felt at the pulse."], ["虛勞", "deficiency taxation", "虚劳", "「劳」在这一篇也用于虚损的病类，不限于刚刚劳累的感觉。", "Taxation is a historical category. In this chapter it includes depletion, beyond ordinary tiredness."], ["失精", "loss of essence", "失精", "此处既列在脉象上下文里，后文又写「梦失精」。读这一篇要按具体句意判断「精」的范围。", "The term refers to loss of essence, discussed here alongside the pulse and, later, emission during sleep."], ["盜汗", "night sweating", "盗汗", "这里「盗」形容睡中不自知地出汗。", "The image of stealth refers to sweating during sleep."], ["膠飴", "thick maltose syrup", "胶饴", "按方文的顺序，它是去滓后才加入，以微火使之化开。", "The thick syrup is added after straining and dissolved over low heat in this formula record."], ["虛煩不得眠", "vexation and inability to sleep", "虚烦不得眠", "这一条用虚劳作背景，随后并写烦与睡不着两项。", "The line places vexation and sleeplessness in the context of deficiency taxation."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-27", "page": 2, "title": "Blood Impediment and Deficiency Taxation", "english": ["The record lists suānzǎorén, two shēng; gāncǎo, one liǎng; zhīmǔ and fúlíng, two liǎng each; and xiōngqióng, one liǎng.", "The historical directions simmer suānzǎorén in eight shēng of water until six remain, add the other ingredients, reduce to three, remove the dregs, and record three warm one-shēng servings in a day.", "In extreme deficiency from the five taxations, the text describes emaciation with abdominal fullness and inability to eat or drink. It lists injury from food, grief, drink, sexual activity, hunger, and overwork, as well as damage to the channels and nutritive and defensive qi. Dried blood is said to be present inside, with scaly skin and darkening around both eyes. The text describes easing the middle and supplementing deficiency and names Dàhuáng and Zhèchóng Pills.", "Formula record: Dàhuáng and Zhèchóng Pills.", "The record lists dàhuáng, ten liǎng; huángqín, two liǎng; gāncǎo, three liǎng; táorén and xìngrén, one shēng each; sháoyào, four liǎng; dìhuáng, ten liǎng; dried lacquer, one liǎng; gadflies, one shēng; a hundred leeches; beetle larvae, one shēng; and zhèchóng, half a shēng.", "The historical directions powder the twelve ingredients, combine them with prepared honey, and form pills the size of a small bean. The record gives five pills in wine, three times a day.", "For the pattern termed nǚláo, the text describes tension at the bladder, lower-abdominal fullness, general yellowing, darkening on the forehead, heat under the feet, and swelling of the abdomen as though from water. Loose, black stools and chest fullness are considered signs of difficulty. The text names Xiāoshí and Fánshí Powder.", "Formula record: Xiāoshí and Fánshí Powder.", "The record lists equal portions of xiāoshí heated to yellow and calcined fánshí.", "The historical directions make a powder and record a square-inch spoonful mixed with barley porridge liquid, three times a day. Black stools and yellow urine are described as the expected signs."], "notes": [["五勞虛極", "extreme deficiency from the five taxations", "五劳", "「五劳」是古书对劳伤的分类名。本句重点在「虚极」，下面列出形体、饮食和皮肤所见。", "The five taxations are a classical grouping. Here the key is extreme depletion, elaborated by body, appetite, and skin signs."], ["乾血", "Dried blood", "干血", "本条用「干血」解释内在病机，接着写外面可见的皮肤与眼周变化。", "Dried blood is the text's description of an internal condition, followed by outward changes in the skin and eyes."], ["肌膚甲錯", "scaly skin", "肌肤甲错", "「甲」以鳞甲作比，说皮肤干涩，呈片片交错的样子。", "The image of overlapping scales describes the appearance of the skin."], ["女勞", "nǚláo", "女劳", "此处沿用古书病名。理解这条时先看它自己列出的身黄、额黑和腹胀等表现。", "The historical label is retained. Read it through the cluster the text actually lists, including yellowing, a dark forehead, and abdominal swelling."], ["方寸匙", "square-inch spoonful", "方寸匙", "这是古方记散剂分量的量具名称，词面是方寸大小。这里照录古法，不能直接换成现代厨房匙。", "This is an older measure for a powdered preparation, named for a square cùn. The translation preserves the historical form."], ["其候", "expected signs", "其候", "「候」指书中所说应观察到的迹象。本句用它收束本方记述。", "Here 候 means the observable signs expected in the text's account."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-21", "page": 1, "title": "Lesser Yin Diseases", "english": ["Shaoyin illness is characterized by a faint, fine pulse and a constant desire to sleep.", "When a person with Shaoyin illness feels like vomiting without bringing anything up, is restless, and wants only to sleep, spontaneous diarrhea and thirst on day five or six are taken as signs that the illness belongs to Shaoyin. The text says the person drinks water to compensate for deficiency. Pale urine completes the picture of Shaoyin illness in this account. The text attributes the pale color to deficiency and cold in the lower burner, which can no longer regulate water.", "When both the yin and yang aspects of the pulse are tight but the patient unexpectedly sweats, the text calls it loss of yang. It classifies the illness as Shaoyin and expects a sore throat followed by vomiting and diarrhea. In Shaoyin illness, a cough with diarrhea and delirious speech is attributed to forced heat treatment. The text expects difficulty urinating after an attempt to force out Shaoyin sweat.", "In Shaoyin illness, a fine, deep, rapid pulse places the illness in the interior. The text forbids inducing sweat.", "In Shaoyin illness with a faint pulse, the text forbids inducing sweat because of loss of yang. When yang is already deficient and the cubit pulse is weak and rough, it also forbids purging.", "In Shaoyin illness with a tight pulse, spontaneous diarrhea may occur on day seven or eight and the pulse may abruptly become faint. If the hands and feet become warm and the tight quality disappears, the text takes the illness to be nearing resolution. Despite restlessness and diarrhea, it expects spontaneous recovery.", "When diarrhea in Shaoyin illness stops on its own and the person has aversion to cold and lies curled up, but the hands and feet are warm, the text considers the case treatable.", "In Shaoyin illness with aversion to cold and a curled posture, the text considers the case treatable if the person sometimes grows restless and wants to remove clothing and covers.", "In Shaoyin wind strike, a faint yang aspect and floating yin aspect of the pulse are taken as signs of impending recovery. The text places the likely time of resolution for Shaoyin illness between the Zi and Yin hours.", "In Shaoyin illness with vomiting and diarrhea, if the hands and feet do not turn cold and fever appears instead, the text expects survival. If the pulse fails to arrive at the site, the text describes applying seven cones of moxa at Shaoyin.", "When the whole body and all four extremities are hot on day eight or nine of Shaoyin illness, the text locates the heat in the bladder and expects blood to be passed.", "In Shaoyin illness with reversal cold alone and no sweat, the text warns that forcing sweat will disturb the blood. Where it will emerge is uncertain. It may come out through the mouth, nose, or ears. The text names this reversal below and exhaustion above and considers it difficult to treat.", "In Shaoyin illness with aversion to cold, a curled body, diarrhea, and reversal cold of the hands and feet, the text gives a grave prognosis.", "In Shaoyin illness with vomiting, diarrhea, agitation, and reversal cold in all four extremities, the text predicts death.", "In Shaoyin illness, when diarrhea stops but dizziness and intermittent spells of clouded awareness follow, the text predicts death.", "In Shaoyin illness with reversal cold, aversion to cold, a curled body, an absent pulse, and restlessness with agitation, the text predicts death.", "In Shaoyin illness, high, labored breathing on day six or seven is taken as a fatal sign. The text also predicts death when a faint, fine, deep pulse, desire only to lie down, sweating without restlessness, and an urge to vomit are followed on day five or six by spontaneous diarrhea, renewed agitation, and inability to lie down and sleep.", "At the onset of Shaoyin illness, when fever appears unexpectedly and the pulse is deep, the text assigns Mahuang, Fuzi, and Xixin Decoction.", "Formula for Mahuang, Fuzi, and Xixin Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 2 liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Xixin (asarum), 2 liang.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe starts with 1 dou of water. It boils the ephedra first until the volume falls by 2 sheng and skims off the foam. The other ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng, and the dregs are strained out. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "On day two or three of Shaoyin illness, the text describes using Mahuang, Fuzi, and Gancao Decoction to induce slight sweating. It allows this because no interior signs have appeared by that time.", "Formula for Mahuang, Fuzi, and Gancao Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 2 liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe uses 7 sheng of water. The ephedra is brought to a boil once or twice first, and the foam is skimmed off. The other ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng, and the dregs are strained out. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "On day two or three or later of Shaoyin illness, if the person feels restless inside and cannot lie down, the text assigns Huanglian and Ejiao Decoction.", "Formula for Huanglian and Ejiao Decoction.", "Huanglian (coptis rhizome), 4 liang. Huangqin (skullcap root), 2 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 2 liang. Ejiao (donkey-hide gelatin), 3 liang. Egg yolks, 2.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe first boils three in 6 sheng of water down to 2 sheng and strains out the dregs. It adds the gelatin and dissolves it completely. After the liquid has cooled a little, it adds the egg yolks and blends the mixture. It records a warm dose of 7 he three times daily.", "On the first or second day of Shaoyin illness, if there is no discomfort in the mouth but the back feels averse to cold, the text describes moxibustion and assigns Fuzi Decoction.", "Formula for Fuzi Decoction."], "notes": [["脈微細，但欲寐", "a faint, fine pulse and a constant desire to sleep", "微细与欲寐", "「微」说脉势弱，「细」说脉体窄。「但欲寐」说人一直想睡，是本篇开头给出的另一线索。", "Faint describes weak pulsation and fine a narrow beat. The wish only to sleep is the other opening sign."], ["欲吐不吐", "feels like vomiting without bringing anything up", "欲吐不吐", "前一个「吐」在说想吐的感觉，后一个在说实际有没有吐出来。", "The first verb describes the urge to vomit. The second says that nothing actually comes up."], ["被火劫", "forced heat treatment", "火劫", "「劫」字突出了强制。在这条语境中，指被用加热的办法强行取汗。", "The character jie emphasizes force. Here it refers to the use of heat to try to force sweating."], ["惡寒而蜷臥", "aversion to cold and lies curled up", "蜷卧", "「蜷」写身体缩着、屈着。本句还看手脚是否温暖，据此判断这一组表现。", "Quan describes the body curling up. The text also checks whether the hands and feet are warm when reading these signs together."], ["從子至寅上", "between the Zi and Yin hours", "子至寅", "子、丑、寅是三个相连的传统时辰，大致相当于晚上十一点到清晨五点。这里用来写病势可能缓解的时段。", "Zi, Chou, and Yin are consecutive traditional time periods, roughly eleven at night to five in the morning. They mark the expected time of relief in this account."], ["灸少陰七壯", "applying seven cones of moxa at Shaoyin", "灸七壮", "「壮」是古书计艾灸次数的单位。这句记载古法用量。", "Zhuang is the historical unit used here to count applications of moxa. The line records the traditional procedure."], ["下厥上竭", "reversal below and exhaustion above", "下厥上竭", "「下厥」承接前面说的厥，「上竭」承接上面孔窍出血的情形。合起来是作者对此证的命名。", "Below refers back to reversal cold, while above points to bleeding from the upper openings. The author combines them as a name for the pattern."], ["納膠烊盡", "adds the gelatin and dissolves it completely", "烊尽", "「烊」在方书里指使胶溶开。「尽」说完全溶开，下句才加入鸡蛋黄。", "Yang means to melt or dissolve the gelatin. Jin means completely. The egg yolks enter in the next step."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-21", "page": 2, "title": "Lesser Yin Diseases", "english": ["Fuzi (aconite lateral root), two pieces, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Fuling (poria), 3 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 4 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe boils 8 sheng of water down to 3 sheng, strains out the dregs, and records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "For Shaoyin illness with body aches, cold hands and feet, aching joints, and a deep pulse, the text assigns Fuzi Decoction, given above.", "For Shaoyin illness with a faint, weak pulse and pulling pain throughout the body, the text blames disharmony of nutritive and defensive qi and assigns Danggui Sini Decoction.", "Formula for Danggui Sini Decoction.", "Danggui (Chinese angelica root), 3 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang. Xixin (asarum), 3 liang. Mutong, 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Dazao (jujubes), 25, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the recipe uses 8 sheng of water, boils down to 3 sheng, and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "For Shaoyin illness with diarrhea and passage of pus and blood in the stool, the text assigns Peach Blossom Decoction.", "Formula for Peach Blossom Decoction.", "Chishizhi (red halloysite), 1 jin, half used whole and half sifted into powder. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 1 liang. Jingmi (nonglutinous rice), 1 sheng.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe cooks the rice in 7 sheng of water and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 7 he with one square-inch spoonful of red halloysite powder added, taken three times daily. If the illness resolves after one dose, the rest is set aside.", "From day two or three through day four or five of Shaoyin illness, when there is abdominal pain, difficulty urinating, persistent diarrhea, and passage of pus and blood in the stool, the text assigns Peach Blossom Decoction, given above.", "For Shaoyin illness with diarrhea and pus and blood in the stool, the text allows needling the Foot Yangming channel.", "For Shaoyin illness with vomiting, diarrhea, reversal cold of the hands and feet, and agitation so severe the person feels about to die, the text assigns Wuzhuyu Decoction.", "Formula for Wuzhuyu Decoction.", "Wuzhuyu (evodia fruit), 1 sheng. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 6 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe boils 7 sheng of water down to 2 sheng and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 7 he three times daily.", "For Shaoyin illness with diarrhea, sore throat, chest fullness, and restlessness, the text assigns Pigskin Decoction.", "Formula for Pigskin Decoction.", "Pigskin, 1 jin.", "The recipe boils the pigskin in 1 dou of water down to 5 sheng and strains out the solids. It adds 1 sheng of white honey and 5 he of white powder, cooks until fragrant, blends the mixture, and divides it into six warm doses. The text identifies the white powder as rice flour.", "For a sore throat on day two or three of Shaoyin illness, the text allows Gancao Decoction. If the condition does not improve, it gives Jiegeng Decoction.", "Formula for Gancao Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 2 liang.", "The recipe boils the ingredient in 3 sheng of water down to 1 and a half sheng and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 7 he twice daily.", "Formula for Jiegeng Decoction.", "Jiegeng (balloon flower root), 1 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "For Shaoyin illness with injury and sores in the throat, pain extending to the sides of the throat, and loss of voice that prevents speech, the text assigns Vinegar Decoction.", "Formula for Vinegar Decoction.", "Banxia (pinellia), 14 pieces, washed and broken into pieces the size of jujube pits. One egg, yolk removed and vinegar added to the shell.", "For these two ingredients, the historical recipe places the pinellia in the vinegar. The eggshell is set in a knife ring over the fire and brought to a boil three times. The solids are removed and the liquid is held in the mouth and swallowed a little at a time. If there is no improvement, the recipe describes making another three batches.", "For Shaoyin illness with sore throat and an unexpectedly floating pulse, the text assigns Banxia Powder or its decoction.", "Formula for Banxia Powder.", "Banxia (pinellia), washed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig). Gancao (licorice), roasted.", "The three ingredients are taken in equal parts, pounded and sifted separately, then combined. The recipe records one square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice liquid, three times daily. If the powder cannot be taken that way, it describes bringing 1 sheng of water to a boil seven times, adding two square-inch spoonfuls of powder, and bringing it to a boil three more times. The decoction is removed from the heat, cooled a little, and swallowed in small amounts.", "For Shaoyin illness with diarrhea, the text assigns Baitong Decoction.", "Formula for Baitong Decoction.", "Congbai (white part of scallion), four stalks. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 1 liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "For Shaoyin illness with diarrhea and a faint pulse, the text gives Baitong Decoction. If the diarrhea persists with reversal cold, an absent pulse, dry retching, and restlessness, it assigns Baitong Decoction with Pig Bile. After the decoction, it predicts death if the pulse returns abruptly and survival if it returns gently and continues."], "notes": [["身痛如掣", "pulling pain throughout the body", "痛如掣", "「掣」有牵扯的意思。「如掣」用这个动作形容身体疼起来的感觉。", "Che means to pull or tug. The phrase uses that movement to describe what the pain feels like."], ["下利便膿血者，桃花湯主之", "diarrhea and passage of pus and blood in the stool, the text assigns Peach Blossom Decoction", "便脓血", "「便」在这里是排出的动作；「脓血」说明大便里夹着什么。紧接着列出原方名。", "Bian is a verb here, meaning to pass stool. Pus and blood describe what is passed. The sentence then names the historical formula."], ["一半全用，一半篩末", "half used whole and half sifted into powder", "全用与筛末", "这里把一味赤石脂分成两半处理。下一条煎服法另外说明粉末何时加入。", "The red halloysite is divided in two and prepared in two forms. The next line specifies when the powder is added."], ["白粉即米粉", "the white powder as rice flour", "白粉即米粉", "这是底本自己的括注，解释豬膚湯煎法里的白粉所指。", "This is the edition's parenthetical gloss identifying the white powder in the recipe for Pigskin Decoction."], ["不差，與桔梗湯", "If the condition does not improve, it gives Jiegeng Decoction", "不差", "「差」在这里指病好转。全句先列甘草汤，再换一种方来写病势未见好转的情况。", "Cha means recovery or improvement. The text mentions Gancao Decoction first and names Jiegeng Decoction for failure to improve."], ["以雞子殼，置刀環中", "The eggshell is set in a knife ring", "鸡子壳与刀环", "「鸡子」是鸡蛋。这段用蛋壳盛液，以刀环托住蛋壳，是古方记载的器具用法。", "Jizi is an egg. The eggshell holds the liquid, with a knife ring supporting it in the historical recipe."], ["白飲和服方寸匙", "one square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice liquid", "白饮与方寸匙", "「白饮」一般理解为淡米饮。「方寸匙」是方书量散剂的古代用语。", "Baiyin is generally understood as plain rice liquid. The square-inch spoonful is a historical measure for powders."], ["脈暴出者死；微續者生", "death if the pulse returns abruptly and survival if it returns gently and continues", "暴出与微续", "「暴」写突然出现，「微续」写恢复微弱却接续不断。古书用两个脉象对举，给出不同预后。", "Bao marks a sudden return. Wei xu describes a gentle return that continues. The text pairs the pulse descriptions with different outcomes."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-21", "page": 3, "title": "Lesser Yin Diseases", "english": ["Formula for Baitong Decoction with Pig Bile.", "Congbai (white part of scallion), four stalks. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 1 liang. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Human urine, 5 he. Pig bile, 1 he.", "For these five ingredients, the historical recipe boils three in 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng. The dregs are strained out, the urine and bile added and blended, and the liquid divided into two warm doses. The text allows the preparation without bile if none is available.", "When Shaoyin illness persists from day two or three through day four or five, with abdominal pain, difficulty urinating, heavy and aching limbs, and spontaneous diarrhea, the text identifies retained fluid. The person may cough, have difficulty urinating, have diarrhea, or vomit. The text assigns True Warrior Decoction.", "Formula for True Warrior Decoction.", "Fuling (poria), 3 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 2 liang. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one piece, fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe boils 8 sheng of water down to 3 sheng and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 7 he three times daily.", "For cough, the recipe adds half a sheng of wuweizi and 1 liang each of xixin and dried ginger. For difficulty urinating, it adds 1 liang of poria. For diarrhea, it omits peony root and adds 2 liang of dried ginger. For vomiting, it omits fuzi and adds enough fresh ginger to bring the total to half a jin.", "In Shaoyin illness, the text describes diarrhea containing undigested food, interior cold with exterior heat, reversal cold of the hands and feet, a pulse faint to the point of disappearing, and an unexpected absence of aversion to cold. The face is red. There may be abdominal pain, dry retching, sore throat, or cessation of diarrhea with no detectable pulse. The text assigns Pulse-Restoring Sini Decoction.", "Formula for Pulse-Restoring Sini Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one large piece, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 3 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng and 2 he, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses. The text takes a prompt return of the pulse as a sign of recovery.", "For a red face, the recipe adds nine stalks of scallion. For abdominal pain, it removes the scallion and adds 2 liang of peony root. For vomiting, it adds 2 liang of fresh ginger. For sore throat, it removes the peony root and adds 1 liang of balloon flower root. If diarrhea stops but the pulse remains absent, it removes the balloon flower root and adds 2 liang of ginseng.", "For Shaoyin illness with reversal cold in all four extremities, the person may have coughing, palpitations, difficulty urinating, abdominal pain, or diarrhea with rectal urgency. The text assigns Sini Powder.", "Formula for Sini Powder.", "Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one large piece, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces, with an editorial note saying it was supplied from the preceding text. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 1 and a half liang. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang.", "The recipe pounds and sifts the four ingredients and records one square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice liquid.", "For cough, the recipe removes ginseng and adds 5 fen each of wuweizi and dried ginger. It also assigns this modification to diarrhea. For palpitations, it adds 5 fen of cinnamon twig. For difficult urination, it adds 5 fen of poria. For diarrhea with rectal urgency, it first boils 3 liang of xiebai in 5 sheng of water down to 3 sheng and strains out the dregs. It adds three square-inch spoonfuls of powder and boils down to 1 and a half sheng, divided into two warm doses.", "For Shaoyin illness with diarrhea lasting six or seven days, coughing and vomiting, thirst, restlessness, and inability to sleep, the text assigns Zhuling Decoction.", "Formula for Zhuling Decoction.", "Zhuling (polyporus), 1 liang, outer layer removed. Fuling (poria), 1 liang, outer layer removed. Ejiao (donkey-hide gelatin), 1 liang. Zexie (alisma rhizome), 1 liang. Huashi (talc), 1 liang.", "For these five ingredients, the recipe boils four in 4 sheng of water down to 2 sheng and strains out the dregs. It adds the gelatin and dissolves it fully. It records a warm dose of 7 he three times daily.", "On day two or three of Shaoyin illness, when the mouth and throat are dry, the text calls for urgent purging with Major Chengqi Decoction.", "Formula for Major Chengqi Decoction.", "Zhishi (immature bitter orange), 5 fruits, roasted. Houpo (magnolia bark), half a jin, outer layer removed and roasted. Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang, rinsed. Mangxiao (mirabilite), 3 he.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe boils the first two in 1 dou of water down to 5 sheng and strains out the dregs. It adds the rhubarb, boils down to 2 sheng, and strains again. It adds the mirabilite and returns the liquid to the heat to bring it to a boil once or twice. The liquid is divided into two warm doses. If the first dose brings on a bowel movement, the later dose is withheld.", "In Shaoyin illness with spontaneous watery diarrhea that is entirely blue-green, pain below the heart, and a dry mouth, the text allows purging with Major Chengqi Decoction, given above."], "notes": [["若無膽汁亦可用", "allows the preparation without bile if none is available", "若无胆汁", "这条在古方服法之后补充材料缺少时的一种情况。「若」领条件，「亦」写仍可。", "Ruo introduces a condition and yi means still. The sentence records how the historical recipe handles unavailable bile."], ["此為有水氣", "the text identifies retained fluid", "有水气", "作者先列腹痛、小便不利、四肢沉重等，再用「水气」归纳。在这里它是古书对体液停留的理解。", "The text gives abdominal pain, difficult urination, and heavy limbs before grouping them under a classical idea of retained fluid."], ["加生薑足前成半斤", "adds enough fresh ginger to bring the total to half a jin", "足前成半斤", "「前」指前面原有的生姜。「足」说加到合计半斤。", "Qian refers to the ginger already in the base recipe. Zu means to bring the combined amount up to half a jin."], ["脈微欲絕", "a pulse faint to the point of disappearing", "微欲绝", "「微」说脉很弱。「欲绝」把这个程度再推进一步，写脉几乎接续不上。", "Faint describes a weak pulse. The following words carry that description further, to the point where the pulse is nearly absent."], ["其脈即出者愈", "a prompt return of the pulse as a sign of recovery", "脉即出", "「即」写很快，「出」说原本摸不到的脉重新可以感觉到。古书以此为转归线索。", "Ji means promptly. The pulse that could not be felt becomes perceptible again, which the text takes as a sign of recovery."], ["按前補入", "an editorial note saying it was supplied from the preceding text", "按前补入", "这是底本的编辑括注，说明这一处文字是参照前文补入的。", "This is an editorial note in the source stating that text was supplied with reference to the preceding passage."], ["泄利下重者", "For diarrhea with rectal urgency", "泄利下重", "「泄利」说排便稀泻。「下重」在这里写便意急迫、下部沉坠的感觉。", "The first part means diarrhea. Xiazhong describes urgency with a heavy or bearing-down sensation below."], ["一服得利，止後服", "If the first dose brings on a bowel movement, the later dose is withheld", "得利止后服", "本句的「得利」说服后已排便。古方把这作为后续剂次停服的条件。", "Deli means that the bowels have moved after the dose. That is the recipe's condition for withholding the later dose."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-21", "page": 4, "title": "Lesser Yin Diseases", "english": ["On day six or seven of Shaoyin illness, with abdominal distension and no bowel movement, the text calls for urgent purging with Major Chengqi Decoction, given above.", "In Shaoyin illness with a deep pulse, the text calls for urgent warming with Sini Decoction.", "Formula for Sini Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Fuzi (aconite lateral root), one large piece, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 2 and a half liang. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng and 2 he, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "In Shaoyin illness, food or drink is vomited as soon as it enters the mouth, or the person feels queasy inside and wants to vomit but cannot. When this occurs at onset with cold hands and feet and a wiry, slow pulse, the text identifies excess in the chest. It forbids purging and describes inducing vomiting. If instead there is cold retained fluid above the diaphragm with dry retching, it forbids inducing vomiting and describes warming with Sini Decoction, given above.", "In Shaoyin illness with diarrhea, a faint, rough pulse, vomiting, and sweating, the text expects frequent trips to move the bowels. If little comes out each time, it describes warming the upper region using moxibustion."], "notes": [["脈沉者，急溫之", "a deep pulse, the text calls for urgent warming", "脉沉与急温", "「沉」说脉在较深处才感觉得到。「急」表示这条古文给温法的紧迫程度。", "Deep describes where the pulse is felt. Ji expresses the urgency with which this historical text calls for warming."], ["膈上有寒飲", "cold retained fluid above the diaphragm", "膈上寒饮", "「饮」在这里指古书所谓停留的体液。作者以膈上寒饮和干呕来区别前半条写的胸中实。", "Yin here is a classical term for retained fluid. The passage uses the fluid above the diaphragm and dry retching to distinguish this case from excess in the chest."], ["數更衣，反少者", "frequent trips to move the bowels. If little comes out each time", "数更衣", "「数」读 shuò，指多次。「更衣」是如厕排便的委婉说法。「反少」接着说每次所排的量却少。", "Shuo means repeatedly. Gengyi is a euphemism for going to move the bowels. The next phrase says little is passed each time."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-30", "page": 1, "title": "Chest Impediment", "english": ["The teacher said: In examining the pulse, attend to excess and insufficiency. A faint yang pulse with a wiry yin pulse is associated here with painful chest impediment. The text looks for extreme deficiency: deficient yang places the pattern in the upper burner, while the wiry quality is used to account for the pain.", "A person who otherwise seems well, without chills or fever, but with chest impediment and breath too short to sustain normal respiration is classified here as having a full pattern.", "In chest impediment with labored breathing, coughing with sputum, chest and back pain, a deep slow inch pulse, and a slightly tight rapid bar pulse, the text names Guālóu, Xièbái, and White Wine Decoction.", "Formula record: Guālóu, Xièbái, and White Wine Decoction.", "The record lists one pounded guālóu fruit, half a jīn of xièbái, and seven shēng of white wine.", "The historical method boils the three together until two shēng remain and divides the liquid into two warm servings.", "When chest impediment prevents lying down and pain in the heart region penetrates through to the back, the text names Guālóu, Xièbái, and Bànxià Decoction.", "Formula record: Guālóu, Xièbái, and Bànxià Decoction.", "The record lists one pounded guālóu fruit, three liǎng of xièbái, half a shēng of rinsed bànxià, and one dǒu of white wine.", "The historical method boils the four together to four shēng, removes the dregs, and records three warm one-shēng servings in a day.", "For chest impediment with obstruction in the heart region, retained qi binding in the chest, chest fullness, and a counterflow from below the ribs that rushes toward the heart, the text names Zhǐshí, Xièbái, Guìzhī, Hòupò, and Guālóu Decoction. It also names Guìzhī and Rénshēn Decoction.", "Formula record: Zhǐshí, Xièbái, Guìzhī, Hòupò, and Guālóu Decoction.", "The record lists four heated zhǐshí, half a jīn of xièbái, one liǎng of guìzhī, four liǎng of hòupò, and one pounded guālóu fruit.", "The historical method starts with five shēng of water and simmers zhǐshí and hòupò to two shēng. After removing the dregs, the remaining ingredients are added for several boils and the liquid divided into three warm servings.", "Formula record: Guìzhī and Rénshēn Decoction.", "The record lists guìzhī, four liǎng; rénshēn, gāncǎo, dried ginger, and báizhú, three liǎng each.", "The historical method reduces one dǒu of water with the first four ingredients to five shēng, adds guìzhī, reduces the liquid to three, and removes the dregs. It records three warm one-shēng servings a day.", "For chest impediment with qi obstructed in the chest or shortness of breath, the text attributes the pattern to water qi in the chest. It names Fúlíng, Xìngrén, and Gāncǎo Decoction, and also Citrus Peel, Zhǐshí, and Fresh Ginger Decoction.", "Formula record: Fúlíng, Xìngrén, and Gāncǎo Decoction.", "The record lists two liǎng of fúlíng, fifty xìngrén, and one liǎng of processed gāncǎo.", "The historical method reduces one dǒu of water to five shēng, removes the dregs, and records three warm one-shēng servings in a day, with further servings if there is no improvement.", "Formula record: Citrus Peel, Zhǐshí, and Fresh Ginger Decoction.", "The record lists citrus peel, one jīn; heated zhǐshí, three liǎng; and fresh ginger, half a jīn.", "The historical method reduces five shēng of water to two, removes the dregs, and divides it into two warm servings.", "For chest impediment that eases at times and tightens at others, the text names Yìyǐ and Fùzǐ Powder.", "Formula record: Yìyǐ and Fùzǐ Powder.", "The record lists fifteen liǎng of yìyǐ and ten large processed, peeled fùzǐ.", "The historical directions pound the two ingredients into powder and record a square-cùn spoonful in a plain drink three times a day.", "For chest impediment with a hanging pain in the heart region, the text names Guìzhī, Fresh Ginger, and Zhǐshí Decoction.", "Formula record: Guìzhī, Fresh Ginger, and Zhǐshí Decoction.", "The record lists guìzhī and fresh ginger, three liǎng each, and five heated zhǐshí.", "The historical method reduces six shēng of water to three, removes the dregs, and divides it into three warm servings.", "For chest pain penetrating to the back and back pain penetrating to the chest, the text names Wūtóu and Red Halloysite Pills.", "Formula record: Wūtóu and Red Halloysite Pills.", "The record lists one liǎng each of processed wūtóu, shǔjiāo, dried ginger, and red halloysite; and half a liǎng of processed, peeled fùzǐ.", "The historical directions powder the five ingredients and form honey pills the size of Chinese parasol-tree seeds. They record one pill before food, three times a day, with gradual adjustment according to the response.", "For chest impediment when the person regularly wants pressure applied to the chest and, before the discomfort began, wanted only hot drinks, the text names Xuánfùhuā Decoction.", "Formula record: Xuánfùhuā Decoction.", "The record lists three liǎng of xuánfùhuā, fourteen scallion stalks, and a small amount of new crimson silk.", "The historical method reduces three shēng of water to one and records taking it as a single serving.", "For chest impediment with throbbing below the heart, the text looks to phlegm and names Bànxià and Máhuáng Pills.", "Formula record: Bànxià and Máhuáng Pills.", "The record lists equal portions of rinsed bànxià and máhuáng with its joints removed.", "The historical directions powder the ingredients, form honey pills the size of small beans, and record three pills in a drink, three times a day."], "notes": [["胸痹", "chest impediment", "胸痹", "「痹」带有阻滞的意思。这个篇名统摄原书所列的胸痛、胸满、短气等一组表现。", "Impediment conveys obstruction. Here it groups several presentations, including chest pain, fullness, and shortness of breath."], ["太過不及", "excess and insufficiency", "太过与不及", "开头两词给出了作者观察脉象的比较尺度。后句才具体说阳微、阴弦。", "The two terms establish the comparison. The next phrase gives the particular faint and wiry pulse qualities."], ["平人", "person who otherwise seems well", "平人", "这个说法表示从整体看来平常。后面还是接着说胸痹和呼吸表现。", "The expression describes the person's general appearance before the text singles out the chest and breathing signs."], ["心痛徹背", "pain in the heart region penetrates through to the back", "痛彻背", "「彻」指贯通。这里把疼痛从胸前到背部的感觉写出来。", "The verb conveys pain felt as extending through to the back."], ["心中痞", "obstruction in the heart region", "心中痞", "「痞」用来描写堵塞不舒的感觉。后面另有胸满，两个词并在一起读。", "Pǐ describes an obstructed or blocked sensation. Chest fullness is listed separately in the same passage."], ["水氣", "water qi", "水气", "这是古书给胸中气塞或短气提出的一种解释。读时要把这个术语与它前面的观察并起来看。", "Water qi is the text's explanation for the obstruction or shortness of breath described immediately before."], ["時緩時急", "eases at times and tightens at others", "时缓时急", "两个「时」交代交替出现：一阵缓，一阵紧。", "The repeated 時 marks alternation: easing at some times, tightening at others."], ["烏頭", "Wūtóu", "乌头与附子", "这里记录的是古方药名，乌头和附子均有毒性。这段应按古籍方文理解，不能据此自行配制服用。", "Wūtóu and fùzǐ are historical aconite drugs with serious toxicity. This passage records an old formula and is not a preparation or dosing guide."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-30", "page": 2, "title": "Chest Impediment", "english": ["For chest impediment with pain below the heart and, in some cases, congealed cold and disordered blood, the text names Nine Pains Pills.", "Formula record: Nine Pains Pills.", "The record lists processed, peeled fùzǐ, three liǎng; wild lángdú roasted until fragrant, four liǎng; bādòu processed after removing the skin and core and ground to an oily paste, one liǎng; and rénshēn, dried ginger, and wúzhūyú, one liǎng each.", "The historical record describes powdered ingredients formed with honey into pills the size of Chinese parasol-tree seeds and swallowed with wine. It records three pills initially for stronger people and two at a time for weaker people, three times a day. It assigns the formula to sudden collapse with abdominal distention, pain and inability to speak, long-standing cold accumulations, shifting disorders, heart and chest pain, upward-surging cold qi, falls from horses or carts, and blood disorders, with its usual dietary restrictions."], "notes": [["惡血積冷", "congealed cold and disordered blood", "恶血积冷", "这是原书在心下痛之外再补充的一种古代病机描述。", "The phrase adds a classical account of accumulated cold and abnormal blood to the description of pain."], ["巴豆", "bādòu", "巴豆", "巴豆是这则古方中的有毒药物。原文关于古代服法的记载，只用于辨认古籍内容。", "Bādòu is a toxic drug named in this historical recipe. Its presence and the old directions are matters of textual record."], ["卒中惡", "sudden collapse", "卒中恶", "「卒」意为突然。后面的腹胀痛、口不能言是原文随即列下的表现。", "The term emphasizes sudden onset. The text immediately follows it with abdominal distention, pain, and inability to speak."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-22", "page": 1, "title": "Reverting Yin Diseases", "english": ["Jueyin disease is characterized by wasting thirst, qi surging upward and striking the heart, pain and heat in the heart region, and hunger without a desire to eat. Eating brings vomiting of roundworms. Purging leads to unceasing diarrhea.", "In Jueyin wind strike, a slightly floating pulse indicates approaching recovery. If it does not float, the illness has yet to resolve.", "The text gives the period from Chou to Mao as the time when Jueyin disease tends toward resolution.", "In Jueyin disease with thirst and a desire to drink, the text describes giving water a little at a time and expects recovery.", "The text warns against purging in all cases of reversal cold of the four extremities and in people with deficiency.", "If reversal cold comes first in cold damage and diarrhea accompanies later fever, the diarrhea will stop on its own. If reversal cold returns, the diarrhea returns.", "If cold damage begins with six days of fever, followed unexpectedly by nine days of reversal cold with diarrhea, the text normally expects inability to eat. If the person can eat instead, the text suspects loss of the center. If plain cake is eaten and fever does not develop, stomach qi is still present and recovery is expected. There is concern that heat may emerge suddenly and then disappear again. If fever persists when the pulse is taken the next day, recovery is predicted for midnight of the following day. The initial six days of fever plus the three days of returning fever make nine days, matching the nine days of reversal cold. That is the basis for the prediction. If three days later the pulse is rapid and the heat has not ceased, the text identifies surplus heat and expects an abscess with pus.", "If around the sixth or seventh day of cold damage the pulse is slow, yet Huangqin Decoction is given to clear heat, the text takes the slow pulse as a sign of cold. Clearing heat again should leave the abdomen cold. If the person can eat after all, the text calls this loss of the center and regards it as fatal.", "If reversal cold precedes fever in cold damage, diarrhea is expected to stop on its own. If sweating occurs instead and the throat hurts, the text identifies throat obstruction. With fever and no sweating, the diarrhea is also expected to stop. If it persists, pus and blood pass in the stool. When this happens, the throat does not become obstructed.", "If reversal cold appears between the first or second and the fourth or fifth day of cold damage, fever is expected. Earlier fever likewise predicts later reversal cold. Deep reversal corresponds to deep heat, and slight reversal to slight heat. If sweating is induced when the text calls for purging, it expects the mouth to become red, raw, and ulcerated.", "If cold damage brings five days of reversal cold and five days of fever, the sixth day would be expected to bring reversal cold again. If it does not, the text expects spontaneous recovery. With reversal cold lasting no more than five days and fever also lasting five days, it expects recovery on the same basis.", "Reversal occurs when yin and yang qi fail to meet in their proper sequence. The defining sign here is cold in the hands and feet.", "In cold damage with a faint pulse and reversal, if by the seventh or eighth day the skin is cold and the person is restless without a moment of ease, the text calls this visceral reversal. In roundworm reversal it expects vomiting of worms. Here the person is quiet but becomes vexed at intervals. The text attributes this to visceral cold and worms coming up into the diaphragm region. The vexation soon stops. If food brings vomiting and renewed vexation, the text says the smell of the food draws the worms out and expects the person to vomit them. It indicates Wumei Pills for roundworm reversal and also for persistent diarrhea.", "Formula for Wumei Pills.", "Three hundred Wumei fruits. Xixin, six liang. Ganjiang, ten liang. Huanglian, sixteen liang. Danggui, four liang. Fuzi, six liang, heat-processed and peeled. Shujiao, four liang, heated to release moisture. Guizhi, six liang, with the bark removed. Renshen, six liang. Huangbai, six liang.", "The text records pounding and sieving the ten ingredients separately and combining them. The Wumei are soaked overnight in vinegar, pitted, and steamed beneath five dou of rice. When the rice is cooked, the fruit is pounded to a paste and blended with the other ingredients. The mixture is put in a mortar with honey, pounded two thousand strokes, and shaped into pills the size of wutong seeds. The text records ten pills before food, taken with liquid three times daily, gradually increasing to twenty pills, with raw and cold foods, slippery foods, and strong-smelling foods excluded.", "In cold damage with little heat, slight reversal, cold fingertips, quiet withdrawal, lack of appetite, and restlessness, free passage of pale urine after several days indicates that the heat has cleared. Return of appetite indicates recovery. If there is reversal with vomiting and vexing fullness in the chest and flanks, the text expects later passage of blood in the stool.", "Cold hands and feet with reversal, absence of chest binding, and lower abdominal fullness that hurts when pressed indicate cold binding at the bladder and Guanyuan.", "If cold damage brings four days of fever, then three days of reversal cold, then another four days of fever, the text expects recovery because there have been fewer days of reversal cold than fever. If the heat persists from the fourth to the seventh day, it expects pus and blood in the stool."], "notes": [["消渴", "wasting thirst", "消渴", "这是篇首列出的古典病候词。这里先把它与气上冲、心中疼热连读，篇末还会另列几条消渴。", "This classical term opens the chapter's list of signs. Here it appears alongside upward-surging qi and pain and heat. Several further passages on it appear at the end of the chapter."], ["從丑至卯上", "the period from Chou to Mao", "丑至卯", "丑、寅、卯依次是地支时段。此处用首尾两个名字标出作者所说的趋解时间。", "Chou, Yin, and Mao are successive time divisions named by the Earthly Branches. The endpoints mark the text's expected period of resolution."], ["見厥，複利", "If reversal cold returns, the diarrhea returns", "厥与利", "这里的利指泄泻。复写泄泻曾停又起，可与同句前半的自止对读。", "利 here denotes diarrhea. 複 indicates that it recurs after stopping on its own earlier in the line."], ["除中", "loss of the center", "除中", "作者先说厥利时本当不能食，再把反常能食作为疑点。下文借是否再发热和胃气是否尚在来继续辨别。", "The text first expects inability to eat during reversal cold with diarrhea. Unexpected appetite raises suspicion, and the paragraph goes on to consider fever and remaining stomach qi."], ["厥深者，熱亦深", "Deep reversal corresponds to deep heat", "厥深热深", "深与微接连两次出现，用来比较程度。古文在这句话把厥和热的轻重联系起来。", "Deep and slight are used in parallel clauses to compare degree. The text links the severity of reversal with that of heat."], ["蚘厥", "roundworm reversal", "蚘厥", "蚘是蛔的异写。该段用烦作有时、食后呕并吐蛔来描述这一类厥。", "蚘 is a variant of 蛔, roundworm. The passage describes this kind of reversal through intermittent vexation and vomiting of worms."], ["嘿嘿不欲食", "quiet withdrawal, lack of appetite", "嘿嘿", "嘿嘿写默然少言。它与「不欲食」并列，分别是神态与进食意愿。", "嘿嘿 conveys quiet withdrawal. The following words add lack of desire to eat."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-22", "page": 2, "title": "Reverting Yin Diseases", "english": ["If cold damage brings four days of reversal cold, then three days of fever, then five more days of reversal cold, the text judges the disease to be progressing because cold outweighs heat and yang qi recedes. On the sixth or seventh day, if the pulse is faint, the hands and feet are cold with reversal, and there is restlessness, the text describes moxibustion at Jueyin. It takes failure to recover from reversal as fatal.", "Fever, diarrhea, reversal cold, and restlessness that prevents lying down in cold damage are regarded as fatal.", "In cold damage with fever and extremely severe diarrhea, unremitting reversal cold is regarded as fatal.", "If there is no diarrhea for six or seven days in cold damage and then fever and diarrhea develop, the text regards unceasing sweating as fatal. It explains this as yin without yang.", "On the fifth or sixth day of cold damage with no chest binding, a soft abdomen, a deficient pulse, and renewed reversal cold, the text warns against purging. It interprets the signs as blood loss and regards purging as fatal. If cold damage with fever and reversal cold brings diarrhea on the seventh day, it is considered difficult to treat.", "In cold damage with a skipping pulse and reversal cold of the hands and feet, the text warns against moxibustion.", "In cold damage with a slippery pulse and reversal cold, the text identifies interior heat and indicates White Tiger Decoction.", "Formula for White Tiger Decoction.", "Zhimu, six liang. Shigao, one jin, crushed and wrapped in cotton. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Non-glutinous rice, six ge.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, with boiling continued until the rice is cooked. The dregs are removed. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "In cold damage with reversal cold in the hands and feet and a fine pulse nearly disappearing, the text indicates Danggui Sini Decoction with Renshen and Fuzi. For longstanding interior cold it indicates Danggui Sini Decoction with Wuzhuyu, Fresh Ginger, and Fuzi.", "Formula for Danggui Sini Decoction with Renshen and Fuzi.", "Danggui, three liang. Guizhi, three liang, with the bark removed. Shaoyao, three liang. Xixin, three liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Mutong, two liang. Twenty-five jujubes, split. Renshen, three liang. One Fuzi, heat-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these nine ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for Danggui Sini Decoction with Wuzhuyu, Fresh Ginger, and Fuzi.", "Wuzhuyu, two sheng. Fresh ginger, half a jin. One Fuzi, heat-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Danggui, three liang. Guizhi, three liang, with the bark removed. Shaoyao, three liang. Xixin, three liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Mutong, two liang. Twenty-five jujubes, split.", "For these ten ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water and six sheng of clear wine, boiled together to yield three sheng. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "When fever persists after profuse sweating and there is internal cramping, pain in the limbs, further diarrhea, reversal cold, and aversion to cold, the text indicates Sini Decoction.", "Formula for Sini Decoction.", "Renshen, two liang. Gancao, two liang. Ganjiang, one and a half liang. One Fuzi, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water, boiled down to one sheng and two ge and strained, then divided into two warm portions. For a robust person it records one large Fuzi and two liang of Ganjiang.", "With reversal cold after profuse sweating or severe diarrhea, the text indicates Sini Decoction, given earlier.", "If the hands and feet are cold with reversal and the pulse becomes tight abruptly, the text locates the bound pathogenic influence in the chest. Fullness below the heart, vexation, and hunger with inability to eat likewise locate the condition in the chest. The text calls for induced vomiting with Guadi Powder.", "Formula for Guadi Powder.", "Guadi and red adzuki beans.", "The text records equal parts of the two ingredients, pounded and sieved separately, then combined and worked again in a mortar. Separately, one ge of Xiangchi is cooked with seven ge of hot water to make a thin gruel and strained. The text mixes a one-qian spoonful of powder with the liquid and records it taken warm in one portion. If vomiting does not occur, it records increasing by small amounts and stopping once free vomiting occurs. It excludes people with blood loss or deficiency.", "In cold damage with reversal cold and palpitations below the heart, the text addresses retained water first with Fuling and Gancao Decoction and deals with the reversal afterward. Otherwise it expects the water to seep into the stomach and cause diarrhea.", "Formula for Fuling and Gancao Decoction.", "Fuling, two liang. Roasted Gancao, one liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Guizhi, two liang, with the bark removed.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained, then divided into three warm portions."], "notes": [["寒多熱少", "cold outweighs heat", "寒多热少", "前面以四日、三日、五日计算厥和热持续的时间。这里的多与少承接的是日数的比较。", "The preceding numbers count the days of reversal cold and fever. Here the comparison concerns their duration."], ["腹濡", "a soft abdomen", "腹濡", "濡在此说腹部触感柔软，和前面的「不结胸」一并构成作者的判断线索。", "濡 describes softness when the abdomen is felt. The text pairs it with absence of chest binding."], ["脈促", "a skipping pulse", "促脉", "前篇把促解释为脉跳得快且时有停顿。这里在手足厥冷之前添这一脉象作为条件。", "The earlier pulse chapter describes skipping as a rapid pulse with occasional pauses. Here it qualifies the account of cold hands and feet."], ["脈細欲絕", "a fine pulse nearly disappearing", "欲绝", "细写脉感窄，欲绝进一步写将要摸不到。欲是将要的意思。", "Fine describes a narrow pulse sensation. 欲 means about to, so the next words describe a pulse nearly disappearing."], ["清酒六升", "six sheng of clear wine", "清酒", "这一方同时写水和清酒，各六升。英译沿用升作为原文的计量单位。", "The formula lists water and clear wine, six sheng of each. Sheng is retained as the source's unit."], ["脈乍緊", "the pulse becomes tight abruptly", "乍紧", "乍在这里写忽然出现。紧是脉的形容，和后面定位到胸中的说法相连。", "乍 marks a sudden change. Tight describes the pulse quality, which the text uses alongside the signs locating the disorder in the chest."], ["卻治其厥", "deals with the reversal afterward", "先水后厥", "前面已写「先治水」，却在此接到后一步，说明作者安排的先后。", "The text has already assigned priority to retained water. 卻 introduces the following step, addressing reversal."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-22", "page": 3, "title": "Reverting Yin Diseases", "english": ["On the sixth or seventh day of cold damage, after strong purging, the inch pulse is deep and slow, the hands and feet are cold with reversal, and no pulse reaches the lower position. The throat is obstructed, the person spits pus and blood, and diarrhea persists. The text considers this difficult to treat and indicates Renshen and Fuzi Decoction, followed by Renshen and Ganjiang Decoction if the illness persists.", "Formula for Renshen and Fuzi Decoction.", "Renshen, two liang. One Fuzi, heat-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Heat-processed Ganjiang, two liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Ejiao, two liang. Baiye, three liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained. Ejiao is added and melted. It records one sheng taken warm twice daily.", "Formula for Renshen and Ganjiang Decoction.", "Renshen, two liang. One Fuzi, heat-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Ganjiang, three liang. Guizhi, two liang, with the bark removed. Roasted Gancao, two liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives two sheng of water, boiled down to one sheng and strained. It records taking the full portion warm at once.", "On the fourth or fifth day of cold damage, abdominal pain with a turning movement of qi descending toward the lower abdomen indicates that diarrhea is about to start on its own.", "If a person with cold damage already has cold diarrhea and the physician induces vomiting and purging, the text describes obstruction by cold with further adverse vomiting and diarrhea. It indicates Mahuang and Shengma Decoction. If food is vomited as soon as it enters the mouth, it indicates Ganjiang, Huangqin, Huanglian, and Renshen Decoction.", "Formula for Mahuang and Shengma Decoction.", "Mahuang, two and a half liang, with the joints removed. Shengma, one liang. Zhimu, one liang. Huangqin, one and a half liang. Guizhi, two liang. Baizhu, one liang. Roasted Gancao, one liang.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. Mahuang is boiled first and the surface foam removed. The other ingredients are added, boiled to yield three sheng, and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for Ganjiang, Huangqin, Huanglian, and Renshen Decoction.", "Ganjiang, Huangqin, Huanglian, and Renshen, three liang each.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained, then divided into two warm portions.", "With diarrhea, slight fever, thirst, and a weak pulse, the text expects spontaneous recovery.", "With diarrhea, a rapid pulse, slight fever, and sweating, the text expects approaching recovery. A tight pulse indicates the illness has yet to resolve.", "With diarrhea, reversal cold of the hands and feet, and an absent pulse, the text describes moxibustion. If the extremities remain cold, the pulse does not return, and slight panting starts instead, it considers this fatal.", "The text regards the Shaoyin pulse yielding to the Fuyang pulse as favorable.", "If there is diarrhea and the inch pulse is unexpectedly floating and rapid while the cubit is rough, the text expects pus and blood in the stool and indicates Baiye and Ejiao Decoction.", "Formula for Baiye and Ejiao Decoction.", "Baiye, three liang. Ejiao, two liang. Heat-processed Ganjiang, two liang. Moutan, three liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water. Three ingredients are boiled first to yield two sheng. The dregs are removed and Ejiao is added and melted. It records one sheng taken warm twice daily.", "With diarrhea containing undigested food, the text warns against attacking the exterior. Sweating is expected to lead to distention and fullness.", "In diarrhea with a deep and wiry pulse, the text expects heaviness and urgency at the anus. A large pulse means the diarrhea has yet to stop. A faint, weak, and rapid pulse means it is about to stop on its own, with survival expected even if there is fever.", "With diarrhea, a deep and slow pulse, slight redness of the face, mild bodily heat, and undigested food in the stool, the text expects a clouded state, followed by relief once sweating occurs. It also expects slight reversal cold, explaining this as yang showing on the face with deficiency below.", "With diarrhea, a rapid pulse, and thirst, the text expects spontaneous recovery. If the illness persists, it expects pus and blood in the stool and explains this through heat.", "If the pulse disappears after diarrhea and the hands and feet are cold with reversal, return of the pulse shortly afterward together with warmth of the hands and feet indicates survival. Failure of the pulse to return is regarded as fatal.", "With more than ten episodes of diarrhea in a day of cold damage and an unexpectedly replete pulse, the text gives a fatal prognosis.", "In diarrhea containing undigested food, with interior cold, exterior heat, sweating, and reversal cold, the text indicates Tongmai Sini Decoction.", "Formula for Tongmai Sini Decoction.", "Roasted Gancao, two liang. One large Fuzi, used raw, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Ganjiang, three liang. Renshen, two liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water, boiled down to one sheng and two ge and strained, then divided into two warm portions. The return of a palpable pulse indicates recovery.", "For heat diarrhea with heaviness and urgency at the anus, the text indicates Baitouweng Decoction.", "Formula for Baitouweng Decoction.", "Baitouweng, two liang. Huanglian, Huangbai, and Qinpi, three liang each.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm and another sheng if the disorder persists."], "notes": [["下部脈不至", "no pulse reaches the lower position", "下部脉", "句首写寸脉仍能感到，接着写下部不至。这里在比较不同位置可否摸到脉。", "The inch pulse remains perceptible, while no pulse reaches the lower position. The text distinguishes what can be felt at the two positions."], ["納膠烊消", "Ejiao is added and melted", "烊消", "膠指药味表里的阿胶。先去滓后纳胶，烊消写的是后放入的胶受热化开。", "膠 refers to the Ejiao in the ingredients. The remaining liquid is strained before the Ejiao is added and melted."], ["轉氣下趨少腹", "a turning movement of qi descending toward the lower abdomen", "下趋少腹", "下趋写运动向下，少腹给出所向的位置。下一句把这种动势和将要自利连起来。", "The phrase gives a downward direction and places the movement in the lower abdomen. The next clause links it with impending diarrhea."], ["圊膿血", "pus and blood in the stool", "圊", "圊在本句作排便解，后面的膿血是所排出的内容。", "圊 here refers to passing stool. The following words specify that it contains pus and blood."], ["下利清穀", "diarrhea containing undigested food", "清谷", "这里的清按排便来读，谷指食下的谷物。合起来描述未化食物随泄泻排出。", "清 here concerns passing stool and 穀 refers to grain or food. Together they describe undigested food passed with diarrhea."], ["戴陽下虛", "yang showing on the face with deficiency below", "戴阳", "戴借在上承戴的形象。此句把面色少赤与下部虚、轻微厥冷并列来说明。", "戴 evokes something carried above. The text uses the expression to explain facial redness occurring with deficiency below and slight reversal cold."], ["其脈出者愈", "The return of a palpable pulse indicates recovery", "脉出", "出在这里指先前弱微的脉重新可感。原文以此作为该条趋愈的标志。", "出 here describes the pulse becoming palpable again. The passage takes its return as a sign of recovery."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-22", "page": 4, "title": "Reverting Yin Diseases", "english": ["For diarrhea in a person with extreme deficiency, the text indicates Baitouweng Decoction with Ejiao and Gancao.", "Formula for Baitouweng Decoction with Ejiao and Gancao.", "Baitouweng, two liang. Gancao, two liang. Ejiao, two liang. Huanglian, three liang. Huangbai, three liang. Qinpi, three liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to two and a half sheng and strained. Ejiao is added and melted, and the liquid is divided into three warm portions.", "When diarrhea occurs with abdominal distention and fullness and body pain, the text first warms the interior and only then attacks the exterior. It indicates Sini Decoction for warming the interior and Cinnamon Twig Decoction for the exterior. Sini Decoction is given earlier.", "Formula for Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Guizhi, three liang, with the bark removed. Shaoyao, three liang. Gancao, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm and, shortly afterward, sipping one sheng of hot gruel to support its effect. If the disorder persists, it records a further portion. The remaining instructions follow the usual rest and dietary restrictions.", "With diarrhea and a desire to drink water, the text identifies heat and indicates Baitouweng Decoction, given earlier.", "With diarrhea and delirious speech, the text identifies dry stool and indicates Minor Qi-Order Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Qi-Order Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang, washed with wine. Three roasted Zhishi fruits. Houpo, two liang, with the outer layer and tips removed.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Two ingredients are boiled first to yield one sheng and two ge and the dregs are removed. Dahuang is added and the liquid brought briefly to a boil once or twice, then strained and divided into two warm portions. If one portion stops the delirious speech and a bowel movement follows, the later portion is omitted. Otherwise it records taking the rest.", "When vexation recurs after diarrhea and the area below the heart is soft upon pressure, the text calls this deficiency vexation and indicates Gardenia and Douchi Decoction.", "Formula for Gardenia and Douchi Decoction.", "Fourteen gardenia fruits, split. Xiangchi, four ge, wrapped in cotton.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives four sheng of water. Gardenia is boiled first to yield two sheng. Douchi is added and the liquid boiled down further to one and a half sheng, then strained and divided into two warm portions. If vomiting follows the first, the later portion is omitted.", "For diarrhea with abdominal pain or chest pain, the text indicates Zishen Decoction.", "Formula for Zishen Decoction.", "Zishen, half a jin. Gancao, three liang.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water. Zishen is boiled first to yield two sheng. Gancao is added and the liquid boiled down to one and a half sheng, then strained and divided into two warm portions.", "For qi diarrhea, the text indicates Helile Powder.", "Formula for Helile Powder.", "Ten Helile fruits, roasted in embers.", "The text records making the single ingredient into a powder, mixing it with thin gruel, and taking the full amount at once.", "When a person prone to vomiting has an abscess with pus, the text advises against treating the vomiting itself. It expects recovery on its own when the pus is exhausted.", "For vomiting with chest fullness, the text indicates Wuzhuyu Decoction.", "Formula for Wuzhuyu Decoction.", "Wuzhuyu, one sheng. Renshen, three liang. Fresh ginger, six liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained. It records seven ge taken warm three times daily.", "For dry retching, spitting frothy saliva, and headache, the text indicates Wuzhuyu Decoction, given above.", "For vomiting with fever, the text indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Chaihu, eight liang. Huangqin, three liang. Renshen, three liang. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, boiled down to six sheng. The dregs are removed and the liquid boiled again to yield three sheng. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "Vomiting with a weak pulse, free urination, slight bodily heat, and reversal cold is considered difficult to treat. The text indicates Sini Decoction, given earlier.", "For dry retching, upward-surging vomiting, and spitting frothy saliva, the text indicates Banxia and Ganjiang Powder.", "Formula for Banxia and Ganjiang Powder.", "Washed Banxia and Ganjiang, in equal parts.", "The text records pounding the two ingredients to a powder. A square-inch spoonful is boiled in one and a half sheng of fermented grain water to yield seven ge, taken all at once.", "If strong induced vomiting and purging in cold damage lead to extreme deficiency, with further heavy sweating, the text describes the exterior qi as pent up. Water is then given to induce further sweating, and hiccups follow. The text attributes the hiccups to cold in the stomach.", "For cold damage with hiccups and abdominal fullness, the text says to examine the two outlets to tell which part needs to be freed. It expects relief once that passage is opened."], "notes": [["先溫其裡，乃攻其表", "first warms the interior and only then attacks the exterior", "先里后表", "先、乃把动作分出次序。下两句分别列出温里和攻表的方名。", "先 and 乃 give the order. The next clauses assign a formula to the interior and another to the exterior."], ["啜熱粥", "sipping one sheng of hot gruel", "啜粥", "啜写小口喝。这里是服汤后的另一步，原文还交代它在「须臾」后进行。", "啜 means to sip. The gruel follows the decoction after a short interval."], ["更衣", "a bowel movement", "更衣", "更衣字面是换衣，本句委婉指排便。它与谵语止一起成为停后服的条件。", "Literally changing clothes, 更衣 is a euphemism for a bowel movement here. The line links it with stopping the later portion."], ["心下濡", "the area below the heart is soft upon pressure", "心下与濡", "心下是古文对心口下方一带的称呼。前面的「按之」提示濡写按下去的触感。", "心下 names the region below the heart. 按之 signals that softness describes what is felt under pressure."], ["氣利", "qi diarrhea", "气利", "这一句用两个字来命名一类下利，随后直接列方。译文沿用原文的「气」。", "The two characters name a kind of diarrhea and lead directly to a formula. The translation retains qi in the name."], ["乾嘔", "dry retching", "干呕", "干呕是作呕的动作。这里接着写吐涎沫，说明原文同时描写动作和所吐出的东西。", "Dry retching describes the action. The next phrase separately tells what is spat up."], ["前後", "the two outlets", "前后", "在末句中，前后指前后二便。作者用这两个方向来判断哪一处需要通利。", "At the end of the page, front and back refer to the urinary and bowel outlets. The text uses them to locate the passage it describes freeing."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-22", "page": 5, "title": "Reverting Yin Diseases", "english": ["When the chest feels as though there is panting, vomiting, or hiccuping without any fully occurring, and the person feels deeply muddled and distressed in the heart region, the text indicates Fresh Ginger and Banxia Decoction.", "Formula for Fresh Ginger and Banxia Decoction.", "Fresh ginger, one jin. Banxia, half a sheng, washed.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water. Banxia is boiled first to yield two sheng. Fresh ginger juice is added and boiled down to one sheng. The dregs are removed and the liquid cooled slightly and divided into four portions, three by day and one at night. Once vomiting stops, the later portions are omitted.", "For dry retching, hiccups, or reversal cold of the hands and feet, the text indicates Jupi Decoction.", "Formula for Jupi Decoction.", "Jupi, four liang. Fresh ginger, half a jin.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm and expects relief as soon as it is swallowed.", "For hiccups with upward counterflow in a person with deficiency, the text indicates Jupi and Zhuru Decoction.", "Formula for Jupi and Zhuru Decoction.", "Jupi, two jin. Zhuru, two sheng. Renshen, one liang. Gancao, five liang. Fresh ginger, half a jin. Thirty jujubes.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "For vomiting in which grain cannot go down, the text indicates Minor Banxia Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Banxia Decoction.", "Banxia, one sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, half a jin.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to one and a half sheng and strained, then divided into two warm portions.", "Passing pus and blood in the stool in an illness that spreads from person to person is called epidemic diarrhea. In the text's account it begins in summer. In autumn heat and dryness contend, injure qi and blood in turn, and spread into the intestines. The disorder then intensifies, and a surging pulse becomes rapid. The text indicates Huanglian and Fuling Decoction.", "Formula for Huanglian and Fuling Decoction.", "Huanglian, two liang. Fuling, three liang. Ejiao, one and a half liang. Shaoyao, three liang. Huangqin, three liang. Banxia, one sheng, washed.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. Five ingredients are boiled first to yield three sheng. The dregs are removed and Ejiao is added and melted. The liquid is divided into three warm portions.", "For severe heat in the chest the text adds one liang of Huanglian, bringing the total to three liang. For abdominal fullness it adds two liang of Houpo. For deficiency it adds two liang of Gancao. For thirst it omits Banxia and adds two liang of Gualou root.", "If the person vomits or spits frothy saliva, has pain in the heart region or intermittent abdominal pain, and the pulse is unexpectedly surging and large, the text attributes this to worms and indicates Gancao, Rice Flour, and Honey Decoction.", "Formula for Gancao, Rice Flour, and Honey Decoction.", "Gancao, two liang. White flour, specified as rice flour, one liang. Honey, four liang.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water. Gancao is boiled first to yield two sheng and the dregs are removed. Flour and honey are stirred in and cooked to the consistency of thin gruel. It records one sheng taken warm, with the later portion omitted after improvement.", "In Jueyin disease with a wiry and tight pulse, wiriness means defensive qi does not circulate and tightness means no desire to eat. As pathogenic and correct qi contend, cold shan develops. Pain around the navel and reversal cold in the hands and feet are its signs. With a deep and tight pulse the text indicates Major Wutou Reduction.", "Formula for Major Wutou Reduction.", "Five large Wutou roots, heated and peeled.", "For this one ingredient, the text records three sheng of water boiled down to one sheng and strained, then two sheng of honey added and the liquid reduced to two sheng. It gives seven ge for a robust person and five ge for a weak person, with another portion the next day if the disorder persists.", "For cold shan with abdominal pain or flank pain and urgent internal cramping, the text indicates Danggui, Fresh Ginger, and Mutton Decoction.", "Formula for Danggui, Fresh Ginger, and Mutton Decoction.", "Danggui, three liang. Fresh ginger, five liang. Mutton, one jin.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng. It records seven ge taken warm three times daily.", "For predominance of cold the text increases fresh ginger to one jin. For more severe pain and vomiting it adds two liang of Jupi and one liang of Baizhu. When fresh ginger is increased, it also adds five sheng of water, boils to yield three sheng and two ge, and divides the liquid into three warm portions.", "For cold shan with abdominal pain, numbness of the hands and feet, reversal cold, or body pain unresolved by moxibustion, needling, or medicine, the text indicates Wutou and Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Formula for Wutou and Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Five Wutou roots.", "For this one ingredient, the text records boiling it in two sheng of honey until reduced by half, removing the dregs, and loosening it with five ge of Cinnamon Twig Decoction to yield one sheng. It gives two ge first, three ge if there is no discernible effect, then up to five ge if there is still no discernible effect. It describes the effect as resembling intoxication and interprets vomiting as reaching the disorder."], "notes": [["似喘不喘", "feels as though there is panting", "似与不", "开头连续三次用「似……不……」，写感觉似有某种表现，却没有完全发作。", "The opening repeats 'as though' across three sensations, each described as not fully occurring."], ["日三，夜一", "three by day and one at night", "日夜分服", "前面先分成四服，此处再分别记白天三份、夜间一份。", "The preceding words divide the liquid into four portions. This phrase allocates three to the day and one to the night."], ["噦逆", "hiccups with upward counterflow", "哕逆", "哕在本页的数处与呕、喘分别列出。此处借「逆」说明向上的势头。", "噦 is listed separately from vomiting and panting on this page. 逆 adds a sense of upward movement."], ["相傳為病", "an illness that spreads from person to person", "疫利", "这句用能相传来解释为何称疫，同时用便脓血交代所见。", "The text explains its epidemic label through spread between people and describes pus and blood in the stool."], ["米粉", "rice flour", "白粉", "括号把白粉解释为米粉。下文用薄粥来形容粉、蜜和入以后的稀稠。", "The parenthesis identifies the white flour as rice flour. Thin gruel describes the consistency after the flour and honey are added."], ["寒疝", "cold shan", "寒疝", "疝是本段采用的古典病类名称。后面以绕脐痛和手足冷来描写本条的寒疝。", "Shan is the classical category named here. This passage describes cold shan through pain around the navel and cold hands and feet."], ["知者如醉狀", "the effect as resembling intoxication", "知与如醉", "知在这里说察觉服后的反应。原文把它比作醉态。乌头含有毒性成分，这里记录的是历史文本中的反应。", "知 here means noticing a response, which the source compares to intoxication. Wutou contains toxic compounds, and this wording records a response described in the historical text."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-22", "page": 6, "title": "Reverting Yin Diseases", "english": ["If one testicle differs in size and at times moves up or down, the text calls this fox shan. It describes needling the Jueyin transport point first and then giving Spider Powder.", "Formula for Spider Powder.", "Fourteen spiders, heated. Guizhi, one liang.", "The text records making the two ingredients into powder and taking a square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice liquid twice daily. Honey pills are also mentioned.", "When the inch-opening pulse is floating and slow, floating indicates deficiency and slow indicates taxation. Deficiency means defensive qi is insufficient, and taxation means nutritive qi is exhausted.", "When the Fuyang pulse is floating and rapid, floating indicates qi and rapid indicates rapid digestion and hard stool. Abundant qi leads to frequent urination, which in turn hardens the stool. Hard stool and frequent urination combine in what the text calls wasting thirst.", "For wasting thirst in which a person drinks one dou and also urinates one dou, the text indicates Kidney Qi Pills.", "Formula for Kidney Qi Pills.", "Dihuang, eight liang. Shuyu, four liang. Shanzhuyu, four liang. Zexie, three liang. Moutan bark, three liang. Fuling, three liang. Guizhi, one liang. One Fuzi, heat-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces.", "The text records powdering the eight ingredients, mixing them with refined honey, and making pills the size of wutong seeds. It gives fifteen pills with wine, gradually increasing to twenty-five, twice daily. Plain rice liquid is also given as an option.", "For wasting thirst with a floating pulse, slight fever, and difficult urination, the text indicates Five Ling Powder.", "Formula for Five Ling Powder.", "Zhuling, eighteen zhu, with the outer layer removed. Zexie, one liang and six zhu. Baizhu, eighteen zhu. Fuling, eighteen zhu. Guizhi, half a liang.", "The text records powdering the five ingredients and taking a square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice liquid three times daily. It mentions drinking plenty of warm water and expects relief once sweating occurs.", "For wasting thirst with desire for water that the stomach brings back up, the text indicates Fuling and Zexie Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling and Zexie Decoction.", "Fuling, half a jin. Zexie, four liang. Gancao, two liang. Guizhi, two liang. Baizhu, three liang. Fresh ginger, four liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "For wasting thirst with desire for water and incessant eating and drinking, the text indicates Wenge Decoction.", "Formula for Wenge Decoction.", "Wenge, five liang. Mahuang, three liang. Gancao, three liang. Fresh ginger, three liang. Shigao, five liang. Fifty apricot kernels. Twelve jujubes.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, boiled down to two sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm, expecting recovery once sweating occurs, and a further portion if sweat does not appear.", "When urination is painful and the discharge resembles millet, with a taut, cramped lower abdomen and pain drawing into the navel, the text calls it lin. It identifies heat binding in the lower burner and indicates Minor Bupleurum Decoction with Fuling.", "Formula for Minor Bupleurum Decoction with Fuling.", "Chaihu, half a jin. Huangqin, three liang. Renshen, two liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Gancao, three liang. Fresh ginger, two liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split. Fuling, four liang.", "For these eight ingredients, the text gives one dou and two sheng of water, boiled down to six sheng. The dregs are removed and the liquid boiled again to yield three sheng. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily."], "notes": [["狐疝", "fox shan", "狐疝", "本条用一侧大小不同、时而上下移动来描写它。疝在上一页也见于寒疝一词。", "The line describes differing size on one side and movement up and down. The preceding page also uses shan in its term cold shan."], ["白飲", "plain rice liquid", "白饮", "白饮是用来把粉末调和送服的液体。后文肾气丸旁也列为送服用液。", "The text uses plain rice liquid to carry the powder. It also lists it later as a liquid for taking Kidney Qi Pills."], ["衛氣不足", "defensive qi is insufficient", "卫气与营气", "前面先用浮和迟分出虚和劳，再分别落到卫气不足、营气竭。", "The text pairs floating with deficiency and slow with taxation, then links those two to insufficient defensive qi and exhausted nutritive qi."], ["飲一鬥，小便亦一鬥", "drinks one dou and also urinates one dou", "一斗进出", "亦是也。作者把饮入和排出写成同一容量，斗是底本所用的古代容量单位。", "亦 means also. The source gives intake and output as the same volume, using the historical unit dou."], ["胃反而吐", "the stomach brings back up", "胃反而吐", "前面写想饮水，这几个字写喝下去的水又从胃反上而吐。", "The preceding words describe wanting water. This phrase gives the next movement, with water coming back up from the stomach."], ["下如粟狀", "the discharge resembles millet", "如粟", "粟是小米一类谷物。这里用它的样子比喻尿中所见。", "粟 is millet. Its appearance supplies the comparison for what is seen in the urine."], ["小柴胡加茯苓湯方", "Formula for Minor Bupleurum Decoction with Fuling", "加茯苓", "方名用「加」提示在小柴胡汤的名称上添入茯苓。药味表末尾列出茯苓四两。", "The title names Fuling as an addition to Minor Bupleurum Decoction. The ingredient list ends with four liang of Fuling."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-25", "title": "Hundred-Union Disease, Fox-Creeper Disease, and Yin and Yang Toxins", "page": 1, "english": ["In Hundred-Union disease, the text says the many vessels share one source and are all involved. The person wants to eat but cannot and usually keeps silent. They want to lie down but cannot settle, want to walk but cannot, and sometimes enjoy food and drink but at other times cannot bear the smell of food. They feel as though cold without being cold or hot without being hot. The mouth is bitter and the urine reddish. The text says the usual medicines fail and may bring severe vomiting and diarrhea. It likens the puzzling presentation to the work of a spirit. The body appears at ease, while the pulse is faint and rapid.", "If headache occurs every time the person urinates, the text predicts recovery after sixty days. If urinating brings no headache but does bring shivers, it gives forty days. If urination feels easy and the head is merely dizzy, it gives twenty days.", "The signs may appear before the illness becomes evident, on the fourth or fifth day, around the twentieth day, or after a month. The text calls for judging each case by the signs that appear.", "When Hundred-Union disease appears after induced sweating, the text assigns Lily Bulb and Anemarrhena Decoction.", "Lily Bulb and Anemarrhena Decoction", "7 lily bulbs and anemarrhena rhizome 3 liǎng.", "The text first washes the lily bulbs and soaks them overnight, then discards the water when white foam has formed. It boils the bulbs in 2 shēng of fresh spring water until 1 shēng remains and strains it. It separately reduces another 2 shēng of spring water with the anemarrhena to 1 shēng and strains it. The two liquids are combined and reduced to 1 shēng and 5 hé, then divided into two warm servings.", "When Hundred-Union disease appears after purging, the text assigns Lily Bulb, Talc, and Hematite Decoction.", "Lily Bulb, Talc, and Hematite Decoction", "7 lily bulbs, talc 3 liǎng, and a small pellet-sized piece of hematite (crushed and wrapped in cotton).", "The text washes and cooks the lily bulbs as above. It separately boils the other two ingredients in 2 shēng of spring water until 1 shēng remains, strains it, combines the liquids, and reduces them to 1 shēng and 5 hé for two warm servings.", "When Hundred-Union disease appears after induced vomiting, the text assigns Lily Bulb and Egg Yolk Decoction.", "Lily Bulb and Egg Yolk Decoction", "7 lily bulbs and 1 egg yolk.", "The text washes and boils the lily bulbs as above and strains the liquid. It adds the egg yolk, stirs it evenly, and records taking it in one serving.", "For Hundred-Union disease that still looks as it did at onset and has received no sweating, emetic, or purging treatment, the text assigns Lily Bulb and Rehmannia Decoction.", "Lily Bulb and Rehmannia Decoction", "7 lily bulbs and rehmannia juice 1 shēng.", "The text washes and boils the lily bulbs as above, strains, adds the rehmannia juice, and reduces to 1 shēng and 5 hé for two warm servings. It says to stop when the desired effect appears and predicts stool like dark lacquer.", "If Hundred-Union disease persists for a month and develops into thirst, the text assigns a Lily Bulb Wash. If the illness persists, it assigns Trichosanthes and Oyster Shell Powder.", "Lily Bulb Wash", "Lily bulbs 1 shēng.", "The text soaks the lily bulbs overnight in 1 dǒu of water for bathing the body. After the wash it mentions boiled wheat cakes without salted fermented soybeans.", "Trichosanthes and Oyster Shell Powder", "Equal amounts of trichosanthes root and dry-roasted oyster shell.", "The text pounds the two ingredients into powder and lists a square-inch spoonful mixed with plain rice drink three times a day.", "When Hundred-Union disease changes and fever develops, the text assigns Lily Bulb and Talc Powder.", "Lily Bulb and Talc Powder", "Roasted lily bulb 1 liǎng and talc 2 liǎng.", "The text makes a powder and records a square-inch spoonful taken in a drink three times a day. It expects slight looseness of the bowels and says to stop when fever clears.", "For Hundred-Union disease presenting in yin, the text calls for a yang method, and for a presentation in yang it calls for a yin method. The text calls it an error to see yang signs, attack yin, and then induce sweat again. It also calls it an error to see yin signs, attack yang, and then purge again.", "Fox-Creeper disease resembles cold damage. The person is quiet and wants to sleep, but the eyes will not close, and they find no rest lying down or getting up.", "Erosion of the throat is termed huo, or Creeper, and erosion of the genital region is termed hu, or Fox.", "The person has no desire to eat or drink and cannot bear the smell of food. The face and eyes may look red, black, or white by turns. The text associates erosion above with hoarseness and assigns Licorice Heart-Draining Decoction. It associates erosion below with a dry throat and describes a Sophora Root Decoction wash. For erosion at the anus it records realgar fumigation.", "Licorice Heart-Draining Decoction", "Roasted licorice 4 liǎng, scutellaria 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, pinellia 1½ shēng (washed), coptis 1 liǎng, and 12 split jujubes."], "notes": [["百脈一宗", "the many vessels share one source", "百脉一宗", "「百脉」泛指众脉，「一宗」说归于共同根源。这是篇首对这一古代病类的概括。", "The phrase gathers the many vessels under one source. It gives the text’s general account of this classical disease category."], ["如寒無寒，如熱無熱", "as though cold without being cold or hot without being hot", "如寒无寒，如热无热", "两个「如」都写似有某种感觉。整句以相似和不确定来形容感受。", "Both uses of 如 introduce an appearance or sensation. The paired phrases show how elusive the perceived cold and heat are."], ["溺時", "every time the person urinates", "溺时", "这里的「溺」读 niào，表示排尿。本段按排尿时的不同感受给出不同转愈日数。", "Here 溺 means to urinate. The passage makes its different prognoses depend on what the person feels at that time."], ["漬一宿", "soaks them overnight", "渍一宿", "「渍」是浸泡，「一宿」是一整夜。这条也描述泡出白沫后如何处理。", "漬 means to soak and 一宿 means overnight. The formula also describes what is done when white foam appears."], ["中病勿更服", "stop when the desired effect appears", "中病勿更服", "「中病」说方法已达到本条所预期的作用，「勿更服」指随即止后服。", "The phrase makes the appearance of the intended effect the point at which further servings stop."], ["大便當如漆", "stool like dark lacquer", "如漆", "用深色漆来描写大便外观，是本条药后所记的一种表现。", "Dark lacquer is the visual comparison the text uses for stool after the formula."], ["蝕於喉", "Erosion of the throat", "蚀于喉", "「蚀」描写局部受损。本段按发生位置分别写作「惑」或「狐」。", "Erosion describes local tissue damage in this historical account. Its location distinguishes the two named forms."], ["聲嗄", "hoarseness", "声嗄", "「嗄」指声音嘶哑。本段把它与上部受蚀联系起来。", "嗄 means a hoarse voice. The text associates it here with erosion in the upper region."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-25", "title": "Hundred-Union Disease, Fox-Creeper Disease, and Yin and Yang Toxins", "page": 2, "english": ["The text starts with 1 dǒu of water for the six ingredients, reduces to 6 shēng and strains, then reduces again to 3 shēng. It lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "Sophora Root Decoction", "Sophora root 1 jīn.", "The text reduces 1 dǒu of water with this single ingredient to 7 shēng and records a steam wash three times a day.", "Realgar Powder", "Realgar 1 liǎng.", "The historical text describes containing the powdered realgar between two curved roof tiles and heating it to fumigate the anal area.", "With a rapid pulse, no fever, slight agitation, quietness, a desire to lie down, and sweating, the text describes eyes red as dove eyes on the third or fourth day and darkening at the corners of the eyes by the seventh or eighth. If the person can eat at this stage, it says pus has formed and assigns Adzuki Bean and Danggui Powder.", "Adzuki Bean and Danggui Powder", "Adzuki beans 3 shēng (soaked until fine sprouts appear, then dried in the sun) and danggui 10 liǎng.", "The text pounds the two ingredients into powder and lists a square-inch spoonful with sour fermented grain water three times a day.", "For yang toxin, the text describes a red face with brocade-like mottling, sore throat, and spitting pus and blood. It regards the illness as treatable at five days and untreatable at seven. It assigns Cimicifuga and Softshell Turtle Shell Decoction.", "Cimicifuga and Softshell Turtle Shell Decoction", "Cimicifuga 2 liǎng, Sichuan pepper 1 liǎng (dry-roasted until it releases moisture), realgar ½ liǎng (ground), danggui 1 liǎng, licorice 2 liǎng, and a palm-sized piece of softshell turtle shell (roasted).", "The text reduces 4 shēng of water with the six ingredients to 1 shēng. It records taking it at once and repeating if the illness persists, seeking sweating.", "For yin toxin, the text describes a blue-green face and eyes, body pain as though beaten with a staff, and sore throat. It regards the illness as treatable at five days and untreatable at seven, and assigns Cimicifuga and Softshell Turtle Shell Decoction without Realgar or Sichuan Pepper.", "Cimicifuga and Softshell Turtle Shell Decoction without Realgar or Sichuan Pepper", "Cimicifuga 2 liǎng, danggui 1 liǎng, licorice 2 liǎng, and a palm-sized piece of softshell turtle shell.", "The text reduces 2 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 1 shēng, strains, and records taking it at once and repeating if the illness persists."], "notes": [["薰洗", "a steam wash", "薰洗", "「薰」涉及蒸气，「洗」涉及洗拭。这条是古方记载的外用处理。", "The two characters refer to exposure to vapor and washing. This formula describes an external application in the historical text."], ["筒瓦", "curved roof tiles", "筒瓦", "筒瓦是弧形屋瓦。本条用两片合起写古代器具，便于理解文中的器形。", "These are curved roof tiles. The text describes two put together to make a container."], ["目赤如鳩眼", "eyes red as dove eyes", "目赤如鸠眼", "以鸟眼的颜色来比喻眼红。后句另写目眦变黑，标出作者所说的先后。", "The color of a dove’s eyes is the comparison for redness. The next clause places darkening at the eye corners later."], ["漿水", "sour fermented grain water", "浆水", "这里指古方用来调服散剂的酸浆类饮液。下文的「方寸匙」另说散药用量。", "The formula mixes the powder with a sour grain-based liquid. The spoonful refers to the amount of powder."], ["斑斑如錦紋", "brocade-like mottling", "斑斑如锦纹", "借织锦上的纹样描写红斑。这个比喻强调颜色在脸上呈斑纹分布。", "Patterned brocade is the image for the arrangement of colored blotches across the face."], ["身痛如被杖", "body pain as though beaten with a staff", "身痛如被杖", "用受杖打来比喻一身疼痛。这里是一种疼痛描写。", "Being struck with a staff is the comparison for widespread bodily pain."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-29", "title": "Blood Stasis, Vomiting Blood, Nosebleeds, Blood in the Stool, and Sores and Abscesses", "page": 1, "english": ["The text describes chest fullness, withered lips, a blue-green tongue, and a dry mouth. The person only wants to rinse the mouth with water, without swallowing it. There are no chills or fever, and the pulse is slightly large and comes slowly. The abdomen does not look full, yet the person says it feels full. The text takes this as blood stasis.", "When someone looks feverish, feels agitated and full, and has dry mouth and thirst but the pulse lacks signs of heat, the text calls it concealed yin and identifies it with blood stasis. It assigns Purge Blood Stasis Decoction.", "Purge Blood Stasis Decoction", "Rhubarb 3 liǎng, 20 peach kernels, and 20 ground cockroaches (legs removed).", "The text makes a powder of the three ingredients, forms pills with refined honey, and cooks them in 1 shēng of wine and 1 shēng of water until 8 hé remain. It records taking the liquid at once and regards the passage of blood likened to pig liver as a sign of recovery.", "When stagnant blood lodges near the diaphragm and the blood vomited is black, the text assigns Platycodon Decoction.", "Platycodon Decoction", "Platycodon root 1 liǎng and licorice 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the two ingredients to 1 shēng, strains it, and divides it into two warm servings.", "The following passage is cited from the Second Chapter on the Pulse. A relaxed pulse that stops from time to time and then returns is called bound. A rapid pulse that stops from time to time and then returns is called skipping. The text associates abundant yang with skipping and abundant yin with binding and classes both as pulses seen in illness.", "When a stirring pulse stops midway, resumes slightly rapid, and returns to its stirring movement within the sequence, the text calls it yin binding. When a stirring pulse stops midway, cannot return on its own, and subsequently stirs again, it calls it yin intermittence. It gives a difficult prognosis for this pulse.", "When both yin and yang pulse aspects skip, the text associates this with a blood disorder and excess. When both bind, it associates them with blood loss and deficiency. Skipping at the upper inch opening is associated with vomiting blood or nosebleeding, and skipping at the lower cubit with blood passing below. Alternation of skipping and binding is described as difficult to treat. The quotation from the Second Chapter on the Pulse ends here.", "For vomiting blood that does not stop, the text assigns Arborvitae Leaf Decoction or Yellow Earth Decoction.", "Arborvitae Leaf Decoction", "Arborvitae leaves 3 liǎng, dried ginger 3 liǎng, and mugwort leaves 3 handfuls.", "For these three ingredients the historical text records combining 5 shēng of water with 1 shēng of liquid from horse dung, reducing the mixture to 1 shēng, straining, and dividing it into two warm servings.", "Yellow Earth Decoction. The transcription writes 主 in place of 土 in this heading.", "Yellow earth from the stove ½ jīn, licorice 3 liǎng, rehmannia root 3 liǎng, white atractylodes 3 liǎng, fire-processed aconite root 3 liǎng, donkey-hide gelatin 3 liǎng, and scutellaria 3 liǎng.", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the seven ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "With insufficient heart qi and either vomiting blood or a nosebleed, the text assigns Heart-Draining Decoction.", "Heart-Draining Decoction", "Rhubarb 2 liǎng and coptis 1 liǎng.", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the two ingredients to 1 shēng, strains, and records taking the liquid at once.", "If blood comes after the stool, the text calls it distant bleeding and assigns Yellow Earth Decoction, given above.", "If blood comes before the stool, the text calls it near bleeding and assigns Adzuki Bean and Danggui Powder.", "Adzuki Bean and Danggui Powder", "Adzuki beans 3 shēng (soaked until fine sprouts appear, then dried in the sun) and danggui 10 liǎng.", "The text pounds the two ingredients into powder and lists a square-inch spoonful with sour fermented grain water three times a day.", "The teacher said: With a pale complexion, no chills or fever, and a deep, wiry pulse, the text expects a nosebleed. With a floating, weak pulse that disappears under pressure, it expects bleeding below. With agitation and cough, it expects vomiting blood. It assigns nosebleeds from spring to summer to Greater Yang and those from autumn to winter to Yang Brightness. A floating cubit pulse and yellow haze in the eyes are taken as signs the nosebleed continues. When the yellow color clears and the eyes look bright, the text takes this as a sign it has stopped.", "A question: A faintly floating, rough wrist pulse is expected with loss of blood or sweating. How is it understood if there is no sweating? The teacher attributes it to blood lost through a wound inflicted by a knife or axe, calling it a blade wound. Without pus the text assigns Vaccaria Seed Powder. When pus is present it assigns Discharge Pus Powder or Discharge Pus Decoction.", "Vaccaria Seed Powder", "Vaccaria seed 10 fēn (burned), fine dwarf elder leaves 10 fēn (burned), white mulberry root bark 10 fēn (burned), licorice 18 fēn, scutellaria 2 fēn, Sichuan pepper 3 fēn (seeds removed), magnolia bark 2 fēn, dried ginger 2 fēn, and peony root 2 fēn.", "The text makes a powder and lists a square-inch spoonful taken in a drink. It records dusting it on a small wound, taking it internally for a larger wound, and using it after childbirth as well.", "Discharge Pus Powder"], "notes": [["嗽水", "rinse the mouth with water", "嗽水", "这里借水漱口。后接「不欲咽」，说明水到了口中却不想吞下。", "Here 嗽 describes rinsing the mouth. The next phrase makes clear that the person does not want to swallow."], ["陰伏", "concealed yin", "阴伏", "「伏」有隐藏之意。本句以外表像热而脉无热象，说明这个古代判断如何得出。", "伏 conveys hidden or lying low. The text arrives at this description when the outward signs resemble heat but the pulse is read differently."], ["〈平脈法第二〉", "Second Chapter on the Pulse", "平脉法第二", "底本注明前三段括号内文字的出处。前文以此重述结、促和两种脉的判断。", "The transcription credits the three parenthetical passages to the Second Chapter on the Pulse. They restate the descriptions of bound and skipping pulses."], ["馬通汁", "liquid from horse dung", "马通汁", "「马通」是马粪的古称。本条把这种液体列在古方所记的煮法中。", "馬通 is an old term for horse dung. The formula lists its liquid among the historical preparation materials."], ["黃主湯方", "The transcription writes 主 in place of 土", "黄主汤方", "方题在底本作「黄主」，本页两条正文均称「黄土汤」，方中也明列黄土。英译按正文方名。", "The heading reads 黃主 in the transcription. The surrounding entries and the ingredient list support the reading Yellow Earth."], ["遠血", "distant bleeding", "远血与近血", "本条先见大便、后见血叫远血。下一条以血先出叫近血。两名在这里是凭出现先后分的。", "The text calls blood after stool distant, and blood before stool near. The labels are distinguished here by the order of appearance."], ["衄血", "a nosebleed", "衄血", "和本段的吐血并列。此处「衄」指鼻出血。", "Here 衄 means bleeding from the nose. The text lists it alongside vomiting blood."], ["金瘡", "a blade wound", "金疮", "这里的「金」借指刀斧等金属刃器。问答以这类伤口解释没有汗出时的亡血。", "金 refers to metal blades such as the knife or axe mentioned in the line. The passage uses the wound to explain blood loss."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-29", "title": "Blood Stasis, Vomiting Blood, Nosebleeds, Blood in the Stool, and Sores and Abscesses", "page": 2, "english": ["16 immature bitter oranges, peony root 6 fēn, and platycodon root 2 fēn.", "The text pounds the three ingredients into a powder. It describes kneading a portion equal in amount to one egg yolk into the yolk, blending well, and taking it in a drink once a day.", "Discharge Pus Decoction", "Licorice 2 liǎng, platycodon root 3 liǎng, fresh ginger 1 liǎng, and 10 jujubes.", "The text reduces 3 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 1 shēng, strains, and lists 5 hé warm twice a day.", "For spreading sores, the text regards spread from the mouth toward the limbs as treatable and spread from the limbs toward the mouth as untreatable.", "For spreading sores, the text assigns Coptis Powder.", "Coptis Powder", "Coptis 10 fēn and licorice 10 fēn.", "The text pounds the two ingredients into a fine powder and records a square-inch spoonful taken in a drink and powder dusted on the sore.", "With floating, rapid pulses, the text expects fever. If the person instead shivers with aversion to cold and there is a painful spot, it expects an abscess to develop there.", "The teacher asks how to tell whether an abscess-like swelling contains pus. The text says to place a hand over the swelling and takes warmth as a sign of pus and absence of warmth as a sign that pus is absent.", "For intestinal abscess, the text describes rough, overlapping patches of skin, taut abdominal skin that yields under pressure as if swollen, no accumulated mass in the abdomen, no bodily fever, and a rapid pulse. It takes these as signs of an abscess within the intestines and assigns Job’s Tears, Aconite, and Patrinia Powder.", "Job’s Tears, Aconite, and Patrinia Powder", "Job’s tears 10 fēn, aconite root 2 fēn, and patrinia 5 fēn.", "The text pounds the three ingredients into powder, boils a square-inch spoonful in 2 shēng of water until the volume is halved, strains, and records taking it at once. It expects blood to pass in the urine. The transcription reads 頓眼, understood here as 頓服 from the formula context.", "When there is swollen focal fullness in the lower abdomen, pressure brings pain like difficult urination, though urination itself is normal. With recurring fever, spontaneous sweating, and renewed aversion to cold, the text identifies an intestinal abscess. It interprets a deep, tight pulse as meaning pus has yet to form and expects blood on purging. With a surging, rapid pulse, it says pus has formed, allows purging, and assigns Rhubarb and Moutan Decoction.", "Rhubarb and Moutan Decoction", "Rhubarb 4 liǎng, moutan bark 1 liǎng, 50 peach kernels, winter melon seeds ½ shēng, and mirabilite 3 hé.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 1 shēng, strains, and records taking it at once. It expects pus to pass if present and blood otherwise."], "notes": [["浸淫瘡", "spreading sores", "浸淫疮", "「浸淫」写病处逐渐蔓延。此句按蔓延的方向作古书所记的判断。", "The name evokes lesions spreading gradually. The text assigns different prognoses according to the direction of spread."], ["灑浙惡寒", "shivers with aversion to cold", "洒浙恶寒", "此处用这一连语写发冷战栗。原文先说从脉预计有热，再写实际所见。", "This expression describes shivering and feeling averse to cold. The text contrasts the sign with the fever it expected from the pulse."], ["以手掩腫上", "place a hand over the swelling", "以手掩肿上", "「掩」在这里是覆手于肿处。后句写作者如何据局部冷暖作判断。", "掩 here means covering the swelling with the hand. The text then gives its interpretation of local warmth."], ["身甲錯", "rough, overlapping patches of skin", "身甲错", "用甲片交错的形象写皮肤粗涩的外观。下文另写腹皮紧和按之偏软。", "The image is of rough, overlapping scales. The next clause gives separate features of the abdominal skin."], ["頓眼", "reads 頓眼", "顿眼", "底本作「頓眼」。方后同类说法常见「頓服」，这里据上下文译为一次服下。", "The transcription has 頓眼. The formula context supports reading it as 頓服, taking the liquid in one serving."], ["脈洪數", "a surging, rapid pulse", "洪数", "洪形容来势大而盛，数说频率快。条文将这组脉象与前面的沉紧相对。", "Surging describes the amplitude and rapid describes the rate. The passage compares this combination with the earlier deep, tight pulse."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-28", "title": "Cough, Retained Fluids, Yellow Sweat, and Joint-Running", "page": 5, "english": ["The text describes kidney water with an enlarged abdomen, a swollen navel, low back pain, and inability to urinate. The genital area is damp like the sweat on an ox’s nose, the feet are cold in reversal, and the face looks thin.", "For water disorders, the text assigns swelling below the waist to methods that promote urination, and swelling above the waist to methods that induce sweating.", "A deep, slow wrist pulse is read as water and cold respectively. When cold and water interact, the text associates decline of spleen qi with loose, watery stools and decline of stomach qi with bodily swelling. It calls this the water aspect.", "With a low Lesser Yang pulse and a fine Lesser Yin pulse, the text expects difficulty urinating in men and difficulty with menstrual flow in women. It calls this the blood aspect.", "If menstruation stops before a water disorder arises, the text calls it the blood aspect and regards it as difficult to treat. If the water disorder comes first and menstruation stops afterward, it calls it the water aspect and considers it easier to treat. It expects menstruation to return once the water has cleared.", "With a deep, rapid wrist pulse, rapidity is read as outward movement and depth as inward movement. The text interprets movement out as yang excess and movement in as yin binding. At the foot yang pulse, faintness signifies loss of stomach qi and wiriness signifies difficulty breathing. At the Lesser Yin pulse, depth indicates the interior and slipperiness indicates excess. Together they are said to indicate blood binding at the gateway of the womb, with an accumulation that does not discharge and obstructed channels and networks. The text calls this the blood aspect.", "A question: A person afflicted by water has swelling of the face, eyes, body, and limbs, and difficulty urinating. On reading the pulse you speak instead of chest pain, qi rushing to the throat with a sensation like a piece of grilled meat, and an expected cough and panting. What sort of pulse leads to this account? The teacher replied: The wrist pulse is deep and tight, read as water and cold. Together these bind at the Gate of Origin. At first this is slight and may go unnoticed in one’s vigorous years. When yang declines, nutritive and defensive qi interfere with each other, yang is diminished, yin is abundant, and bound cold begins to stir. Kidney qi rushes upward, with choking at the throat and tight pain below the ribs. Taking it for retained fluid, the physician purges strongly. The deep, tight pulse and the illness persist. Strong induced vomiting follows, leaving the stomach system deficient and agitated, the throat dry and wanting water, urination difficult, and food and fluids untransformed. The face, eyes, hands, and feet become puffy. The physician then uses tingli seed to drive water down. There seems to be slight relief, but after excessive food and drink the swelling returns. Chest and rib pain resembles running piglet. When the water surges, coughing and panting rise against their course. The text says to first stop the upward rush, then address the cough. It expects the panting to improve after the cough stops. The new illness comes first, and the water disorder afterward.", "In water disorders, a deep, small pulse is assigned to Lesser Yin and called stone water, while a deep, slow pulse is assigned to Lesser Yin and called proper water. A floating pulse with aversion to wind is called wind-water, and a floating pulse without aversion to wind is called skin water. Both are assigned to Greater Yang. Soft swelling is assigned to the qi aspect, which the text says resolves with sweating. When the pulse is deep it assigns Ephedra, Aconite, and Licorice Decoction. When floating, it assigns Ephedra Decoction with Atractylodes.", "Ephedra, Aconite, and Licorice Decoction", "Ephedra 2 liǎng, 1 aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), and roasted licorice 2 liǎng.", "The text starts with 7 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first and skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng. It strains and divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "Ephedra Decoction with Atractylodes", "Ephedra 3 liǎng, cinnamon twig 2 liǎng, 70 apricot kernels, roasted licorice 1 liǎng, and white atractylodes 4 liǎng.", "The text starts with 9 shēng of water, boils the ephedra until 2 shēng have boiled away, skims the foam, adds the remaining ingredients, and reduces to 2½ shēng. It strains and records 8 hé warm, covering to obtain slight sweating. It records repeating if there is no sweat and stopping once sweating occurs.", "For wind-water with a floating pulse, bodily heaviness, sweating, and aversion to wind, the text assigns Fangji and Astragalus Decoction.", "Fangji and Astragalus Decoction", "Fangji 1 liǎng, roasted licorice 5 qián, white atractylodes 7½ qián, and astragalus 1 liǎng.", "The text chops the four ingredients to roughly the size of hemp seeds or beans. Per five qián spoonfuls it lists four slices of fresh ginger, one jujube, and 1½ shēng of water. The liquid is reduced to 8 hé, strained, and taken warm."], "notes": [["腎水", "kidney water", "肾水", "这是古书给水病分的一个名称。本段从腹部、腰、足和排尿几方面一同描写。", "This is one of the classical water categories. The text describes the abdomen, lower back, feet, and urination together."], ["騖溏", "loose, watery stools", "骛溏", "「溏」写大便稀软。本条把这种表现和作者所说脾气衰联系起来。", "The term refers to loose stools. The line links them to a decline in spleen qi in the text’s account."], ["前斷後病水", "menstruation stops before a water disorder arises", "经断在先", "本条以经断和病水出现的先后来区分血分和水分。前半先说经断，后半次序相反。", "The order of events distinguishes the blood and water aspects here. This first case begins with menstruation stopping."], ["胞門", "gateway of the womb", "胞门", "「胞门」是古书所用的部位说法。此句说血结于此，接着写结聚未泻、经络不通。", "This is a classical anatomical term. The text puts blood binding there and follows it with an accumulation and obstruction."], ["奔豚", "running piglet", "奔豚", "以小猪奔突的形象比喻气上冲。本问答把它和胸胁痛、咳喘连起来写。", "The striking name evokes a small pig running upward, an image for the rushing sensation described with chest and rib pain."], ["石水", "stone water", "石水、正水", "本条用沉而小和沉而迟两组脉分别配石水与正水，都列在少阴。", "The text distinguishes stone water from proper water by pulse quality and assigns both to Lesser Yin."], ["風水", "wind-water", "风水与皮水", "这里是两种古代水病名称。本条以浮脉同见时是否恶风区分二者。", "These are two classical water categories. Both have a floating pulse in this line, and aversion to wind distinguishes them."], ["剉如麻豆大", "the size of hemp seeds or beans", "剉如麻豆大", "「剉」表示切碎。「麻豆」用小颗粒给出粗细的参照。", "The verb calls for chopping. Hemp seeds and beans are the size comparison for the resulting pieces."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-28", "title": "Cough, Retained Fluids, Yellow Sweat, and Joint-Running", "page": 6, "english": ["For panting the text adds ephedra 5 qián, for disharmony in the stomach peony root 3 fēn, for qi rushing upward cinnamon twig 3 fēn, and for longstanding cold below asarum 3 fēn. After taking the preparation it describes a crawling sensation under the skin and icy cold below the waist, then sitting on a quilt and wrapping another around the lower body. It takes a light sweat from the warmth as a sign of improvement.", "For wind-water with aversion to wind, swelling all over, a floating pulse, no thirst, continued spontaneous sweating, and no high fever, the text assigns Yuebi Decoction.", "Yuebi Decoction", "Ephedra 6 liǎng, gypsum ½ jīn, licorice 2 liǎng, fresh ginger 3 liǎng, and 12 jujubes.", "The text starts with 6 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first and skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng. It strains and divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "In skin water the limbs are swollen and water qi is in the skin. For slight twitching of the limbs the text assigns Fangji and Poria Decoction.", "Fangji and Poria Decoction", "Fangji 3 liǎng, astragalus 3 liǎng, cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, poria 6 liǎng, and roasted licorice 2 liǎng. The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the five ingredients to 3 shēng and divides it into three warm servings.", "For interior water with yellow discoloration and swelling of the body, face, and eyes, a deep pulse, and difficult urination, the text assigns Licorice and Ephedra Decoction or Yuebi Decoction with Atractylodes.", "Licorice and Ephedra Decoction", "Licorice 2 liǎng and ephedra 4 liǎng.", "The text starts with 5 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first and skims the foam, adds the licorice, and reduces to 3 shēng before straining. It records 1 shēng warm to bring on sweating, and repeating if sweat does not appear.", "Yuebi Decoction with Atractylodes", "Ephedra 6 liǎng, gypsum ½ jīn, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, fresh ginger 3 liǎng, 15 jujubes, and white atractylodes 4 liǎng.", "The text starts with 6 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first and skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng. It divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "A question: What causes yellow sweat, with swelling, heavy sweating, fever and thirst resembling wind-water, sweat that stains clothing bright yellow like cypress juice, and a deep pulse? The teacher attributed it to entering water to bathe while sweating, allowing water to enter through the sweat pores. The text assigns Astragalus, Peony Root, and Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Astragalus, Peony Root, and Cinnamon Twig Decoction", "Astragalus 5 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, and cinnamon twig 3 liǎng.", "The text combines 1 shēng of sour wine with 7 shēng of water, reduces with the three ingredients to 3 shēng and strains. It records 1 shēng warm, expects agitation, and places resolution at six or seven days. If agitation continues, it attributes it to the sour wine and calls for substituting a better wine vinegar.", "With yellow sweat, the shins are usually cold. If there is fever, the text assigns the case to joint-running. Sweating after meals and recurrent night sweating toward evening are attributed to heat in nutritive qi. If fever comes after sweating, the text predicts that in time the skin will grow rough and scaly. Persistent fever is said to lead to severe sores. If bodily heaviness repeatedly eases after sweating, the text says twitching will eventually occur and bring chest pain. It also describes sweating above the waist but none below, loose aching around the waist and hips, and a sensation of something under the skin. Severe cases bring inability to eat, bodily heaviness and pain, restlessness, and difficult urination. The text identifies this as yellow sweat and assigns Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Astragalus.", "Cinnamon Twig Decoction with Astragalus", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 3 liǎng, 15 jujubes, and astragalus 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 8 shēng of water with the six ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day.", "With a deep, weak wrist pulse, the text associates depth with the bones and kidney system, and weakness with the sinews and liver system. It then describes entering water while sweating and the water affecting the heart. It calls the ensuing pain through the joints and yellow sweat joint-running.", "The text says sour flavor injures the sinews, leaving them slack, a condition called leakage. Salty flavor injures the bones, leaving weakness, a condition called withering. Together these are called severance and leakage. Nutritive qi is obstructed and defensive qi cannot move on its own. Both become faint, the three burners lose their protection, and their connections with the four limbs break down. The text calls emaciation with pronounced swelling confined to the feet, yellow sweat, and heat in the shins joint-running."], "notes": [["陳寒", "longstanding cold below", "陈寒", "「陈」说积留已久。本段以下部久寒说明古方所记的一种加味。", "陳 conveys something longstanding. Here longstanding cold below is one of the patterns used to modify the formula."], ["聶聶動", "slight twitching of the limbs", "聂聂动", "重叠的「聂聂」用来写轻微颤动。原文把它与四肢肿并列。", "The repeated characters convey slight movement or twitching, described alongside swollen limbs."], ["裡水", "interior water", "里水", "「里」提示作者所归的病位在内。本条又列面身黄肿、脉沉和小便不利。", "The name places the water disorder in the interior. This line lists discoloration and swelling, a deep pulse, and urinary difficulty."], ["色正黃如柏汁", "bright yellow like cypress juice", "黄如柏汁", "以柏汁的颜色比喻汗渍。问句用染衣的颜色作为辨认黄汗的一项线索。", "The comparison describes the yellow color of the sweat stain on clothing."], ["苦酒", "sour wine", "苦酒", "古方用语，常指醋或酸酒。此处沿称酸酒，后文以「美酒醢」写所换的液体。", "The historical term can denote vinegar or sour wine. The following clause gives a substitute in the same family of sour liquids."], ["甲錯", "rough and scaly", "甲错", "「甲」借甲片形容皮肤外观。本条把这一变化写作久而后见。", "The term evokes scales or overlapping plates. The text describes this as a later development."], ["歷節痛", "pain through the joints", "历节痛", "「历节」用来说明疼痛涉及多个关节，也是本篇所列病候的名称。", "The term evokes pain passing through multiple joints and gives its name to a classical disease pattern."], ["榮氣不通", "Nutritive qi is obstructed", "营气不通", "本底本用「荣」写营。句中说营气不通，卫也不能独行，借这对概念解释四肢失养。", "This transcription uses 榮 for nutritive qi. The text links its obstruction to defensive qi being unable to move independently."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-28", "title": "Cough, Retained Fluids, Yellow Sweat, and Joint-Running", "page": 7, "english": ["A floating, weak Lesser Yin pulse is read as wind and insufficient blood. The text says their interaction brings pain like pulling. In a person of heavy build, a small, rough pulse, shortness of breath, spontaneous sweating, and joint-running pain that prevents bending or straightening are attributed to drinking alcohol and exposure to wind while sweating.", "For pain in the joints, emaciation, swollen feet with a sensation as though they might give way, dizziness, shortness of breath, and persistent nausea, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig, Peony Root, Anemarrhena, and Licorice Decoction.", "Cinnamon Twig, Peony Root, Anemarrhena, and Licorice Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, anemarrhena rhizome 2 liǎng, and licorice 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 6 shēng of water with the four ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and lists 1 shēng warm three times a day. The transcription reads 眼 in place of 服.", "For joint-running with pain preventing bending or straightening and a deep, weak pulse, the text assigns Wutou Aconite, Ephedra, Astragalus, Peony Root, and Licorice Decoction.", "Wutou Aconite, Ephedra, Astragalus, Peony Root, and Licorice Decoction", "5 sliced wutou aconite roots, ephedra 3 liǎng, astragalus 3 liǎng, peony root 3 liǎng, and licorice 3 liǎng.", "The text first cooks the wutou aconite in 2 shēng of honey until 1 shēng remains and strains it. It separately boils the other four ingredients in 3 shēng of water until 1 shēng remains, strains, adds the honey liquid, and briefly returns it to a boil once or twice. It records 7 hé, followed by the remainder if no effect is felt.", "For joint-running with pain, swollen feet, and difficulty passing both stool and urine, the text assigns Licorice and Ephedra Decoction when the pulse is deep and tight, and Yuebi Decoction with White Atractylodes when the pulse is deep, fine, and rapid. Both formulas appear above.", "The teacher said: A slow, rough wrist pulse is read as cold and insufficient blood. A faint, slow foot yang pulse is read in terms of qi and cold. When stomach qi is insufficient, the hands and feet are cold in reversal. When nutritive and defensive qi are impeded, abdominal fullness and intestinal rumbling follow one another. Qi turns toward the bladder, and both nutritive and defensive qi are taxed.", "When yang qi cannot pass, the body becomes cold. When yin qi cannot pass, the bones ache. If yang opens first there is aversion to cold, and if yin opens first there is impediment with numbness. When yin and yang come together, qi circulates. When the great qi makes its turn, cold qi disperses. In excess there is passing of gas, and in deficiency there is involuntary urination. The text calls this the qi aspect.", "For the qi aspect with hardness below the breastbone, as large as a platter and edged like a turned cup, the text assigns Cinnamon Twig, Licorice, Ephedra, Fresh Ginger, Jujube, Asarum, and Aconite Decoction.", "Cinnamon Twig, Licorice, Ephedra, Fresh Ginger, Jujube, Asarum, and Aconite Decoction", "Cinnamon twig 3 liǎng, roasted licorice 2 liǎng, ephedra 2 liǎng, sliced fresh ginger 2 liǎng, 12 jujubes, asarum 3 liǎng, and 1 aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces).", "The text starts with 7 shēng of water, boils the ephedra first and skims the foam, adds the other ingredients, and reduces to 3 shēng. It divides the liquid into three warm servings and regards sweating as a sign of recovery.", "For retained water and fluid with hardness below the breastbone, as large as a platter and edged like a turned cup, the text assigns Immature Bitter Orange and White Atractylodes Decoction.", "Immature Bitter Orange and White Atractylodes Decoction", "7 immature bitter oranges and white atractylodes 2 liǎng.", "The text reduces 5 shēng of water with the two ingredients to 3 shēng, strains, and divides the liquid into three warm servings.", "With difficult urination and water qi, if the person is thirsty, the text assigns Trichosanthes, Dianthus, and Chinese Yam Pill.", "Trichosanthes, Dianthus, and Chinese Yam Pill", "Trichosanthes root 2 liǎng, dianthus 1 liǎng, Chinese yam 2 liǎng, 1 aconite root (fire-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces), and poria 3 liǎng.", "The text powders the five ingredients and binds them with refined honey into pills the size of Chinese parasol tree seeds. It records two pills in a drink three times a day, increasing to seven or eight if no effect is felt. It takes easier urination and abdominal warmth as signs of effect.", "With difficult urination and water qi in the blood aspect, the text assigns Talc, Loose Hair, and Baiyu Powder or Poria, White Atractylodes, and Rock Salt Decoction.", "Talc, Loose Hair, and Baiyu Powder", "Talc 1 jīn, loose hair 1 jīn (burned), and baiyu 1 jīn.", "The text pounds the three ingredients into powder and records a square-inch spoonful taken in a drink three times a day.", "Poria, White Atractylodes, and Rock Salt Decoction", "Poria ½ jīn, white atractylodes 2 liǎng, and 2 pieces of rock salt, each the size of a small pellet.", "The text first boils the two ingredients other than salt in 1 dǒu of water until 3 shēng remain. It strains, adds the rock salt, and briefly simmers once or twice over low heat to dissolve it. It divides the liquid into three warm servings."], "notes": [["疼痛如掣", "pain like pulling", "疼痛如掣", "「掣」有牵拉之意。这里先写疼痛的感觉，再举另一组关节症状。", "掣 conveys a pulling sensation. The passage gives the quality of the pain before describing another set of joint signs."], ["日三眼", "reads 眼 in place of 服", "日三眼", "底本此处作「眼」，据全书同类煎服格式读为「服」，指一天三次。", "The transcription reads 眼. The usual formula wording supports reading 服, yielding three servings a day."], ["趺陽脈", "foot yang pulse", "趺阳脉", "趺阳在古书是足部取脉处，和句开头腕处的寸口分别说明。", "This is a classical pulse site on the foot. The passage discusses it separately from the wrist pulse."], ["遺溺", "involuntary urination", "遗溺", "「溺」指尿，「遗」说不由自主排出。这里是作者解释气分时所列的虚象。", "溺 refers to urine. The phrase describes loss of control and is listed here under deficiency."], ["大如盤", "as large as a platter", "大如盘", "用盘子形容心下硬处的范围。相邻两条使用同一比喻，却分别归作气分和水饮。", "The platter gives a size comparison. Two adjacent entries use the same image for different classical categories."], ["如梧桐子大", "the size of Chinese parasol tree seeds", "如梧桐子大", "梧桐子是古方常用的丸粒大小参照。这里用作形象说明。", "The seed supplies a familiar visual size comparison for the pills in historical formulas."], ["白魚", "baiyu", "白鱼", "底本列作药味名。译文保留拼音，因为单凭本方所写的名字不容易确认其所指。", "The ingredient name is retained in transliteration because its precise identification is uncertain from this entry alone."], ["戎鹽", "Rock Salt", "戎盐", "古方的盐类药名，后文说把它后下煮化。这是方中独立的一味。", "This is a historical name for a mineral salt. The preparation line dissolves it after the other ingredients have been boiled."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-28", "page": 1, "title": "Cough, Retained Fluids, Yellow Sweat, and Joint-Running", "english": ["The teacher said that coughing arises in the lung but may result from illness elsewhere. A pathogenic influence affecting any of the five zang or six fu organs can give rise to cough. In the text's explanation, the influence rises against its normal direction and impinges on the lung. The movement of qi there produces the sound of a cough. To trace the origin, the reader must examine the pulse and breathing. The teacher sets these points out for the student to pass on to later readers.", "The pulse of lung cough is short and rough. If it is floating and rough, the text infers wind. A tight, short, rough pulse indicates cold, a rapid, short, rough pulse heat, an urgent, short, rough pulse dryness, and a soft, short, rough pulse dampness. These are the causes it gives for lung cough. Breathing is labored and audible. Severe cases involve spitting blood.", "The pulse of heart cough is large and scattered. If it is also floating, the text infers wind, if tight, cold, if rapid, heat, if urgent, dryness, and if soft, dampness. These are its proposed causes of heart cough. The signs are pain around the heart and a persistent sensation of something lodged in the throat. Severe cases involve throat swelling and throat obstruction.", "The pulse of liver cough is wiry and rough. If it is also floating, the text infers wind, if tight, cold, if rapid, heat, if urgent, dryness, and if soft, dampness. These are its proposed causes of liver cough. There is pain below both sides of the ribs. In severe cases the person cannot turn, because turning brings on fullness below both flanks.", "The pulse of spleen cough is soft and rough. If it is also floating, the text infers wind, if deep, cold, if rapid, heat, if urgent, dryness, and if slow, dampness. These are its proposed causes of spleen cough. Pain under the right ribs extends as a dull ache to the back. In severe cases the person cannot move without worsening the cough.", "The pulse of kidney cough is deep and soft. If it is also wiry, the text infers wind, if tight, cold, if rapid, heat, if urgent, dryness, and if stagnant, dampness. These are its proposed causes of kidney cough. Pain pulls between the shoulders and back. In severe cases the person coughs up saliva.", "If lung cough persists, the text says it spreads to the large intestine. The pulse remains that of lung cough, and coughing brings on involuntary passage of stool.", "If heart cough persists, the text says it spreads to the small intestine. The pulse remains that of heart cough, and gas passes at the very moment the person coughs.", "If liver cough persists, the text says it spreads to the gallbladder. The pulse remains that of liver cough, and the person vomits bitter fluid.", "If spleen cough persists, the text says it spreads to the stomach. The pulse remains that of spleen cough, and there is vomiting. With severe vomiting, roundworms come up.", "If kidney cough persists, the text says it spreads to the bladder. The pulse remains that of kidney cough, and coughing brings on involuntary urination.", "If a long-standing cough persists, the text says it shifts to the three burners. The pulse changes with the presentation. Coughing occurs with abdominal fullness and loss of desire for food or drink.", "When cough is accompanied by retained fluid, the person cannot lie down because breathing becomes urgent when lying down. The text calls this excess cough. When the person cannot speak because speaking makes them short of breath, it calls this deficiency cough. Illness has many forms and their treatments differ. The teacher urges careful adherence to the appropriate method.", "In a person with cough, the text takes a wiry pulse as a sign of retained water and assigns Ten Jujube Decoction.", "Formula for Ten Jujube Decoction.", "Yuanhua (daphne flower), dry-heated. Gansui (kansui root) and daji (euphorbia root). All three are used in equal parts.", "The three ingredients are pounded and sifted. The historical recipe boils ten plump jujubes in 1 sheng and 5 he of water down to 8 he, removes the dregs, and adds the medicinal powder. It records a warm dose at daybreak of one qian-spoonful for a robust person or half a qian-spoonful for a frail person. If there is no bowel movement, it adds half a qian the following day. After a full evacuation, the text describes recovery on thin rice gruel.", "For coughing with upward surging of qi and a sound in the throat like a water bird, the text assigns Shegan and Mahuang Decoction.", "Formula for Shegan and Mahuang Decoction.", "Shegan (blackberry lily rhizome), 3 liang. Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed. Wuweizi (schisandra fruit), half a sheng. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 4 liang. Xixin (asarum), 3 liang. Dazao (jujubes), 7.", "For these seven ingredients, the recipe starts with 1 dou and 2 sheng of water. Ephedra is boiled first and the foam skimmed off. The remaining ingredients are added, boiled down to 3 sheng, and divided into three warm doses."], "notes": [["五臟，六腑，感受客邪", "A pathogenic influence affecting any of the five zang or six fu organs", "五脏六腑与客邪", "「客」把外来的致病因素比作来客。五脏六腑是古书的功能分组，下一段开始逐一用脏名给咳嗽分类。", "Guest is the image behind an influence coming from outside. The zang and fu are the classical functional groupings the text uses to categorize cough."], ["脈短而澀", "short and rough", "短而涩", "「短」说脉搏在手下显现的范围，「涩」说触感不流利。下面又加浮、紧等字细分。", "Short describes how far the beat can be felt, and rough describes its uneven feel. The next clauses add other qualities such as floating and tight."], ["介介如梗", "a persistent sensation of something lodged in the throat", "介介如梗", "「梗」借东西卡住的形象写咽喉的不适。「如」提示这是对感觉的描写。", "The image is of something stuck in the throat. The comparison describes how the throat feels."], ["咳而遺矢", "coughing brings on involuntary passage of stool", "遗矢", "「矢」在这里借作大便，「遗」说无法自主控制。它写的是咳嗽时一并出现的表现。", "Here shi refers to stool and yi means losing control over passage. The sentence links it to the moment of coughing."], ["咳而失氣", "gas passes at the very moment the person coughs", "咳而失气", "「失气」在此指排出肠气。下一小句用「俱」强调它与咳同时发生。", "Here gas means gas from the bowel. The next clause emphasizes that it passes at the same time as the cough."], ["此為實咳", "The text calls this excess cough", "实咳与虚咳", "本段将一躺下就气急、无法卧，与一说话便气短、无法言并列，分别归入实咳和虚咳。", "The text contrasts urgent breathing on lying down with breathlessness on speaking, grouping them under excess cough and deficiency cough respectively."], ["喉中作水雞聲", "a sound in the throat like a water bird", "水鸡声", "这是声音的比喻。作者以类似水鸡鸣声的声音来写咳嗽时喉中所闻。", "The expression is a sound comparison. A call like that of a water bird illustrates what is heard in the throat."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-28", "page": 2, "title": "Cough, Retained Fluids, Yellow Sweat, and Joint-Running", "english": ["For cough with qi surging upward, intermittent spitting of turbid phlegm, and the need to remain seated because sleep is impossible, the text assigns Zaojia Pills.", "Formula for Zaojia Pills.", "Zaojia (soap pod), 8 liang, outer layer scraped off and roasted with clarified butter.", "The historical recipe powders the ingredient and forms honey pills the size of Chinese parasol tree seeds. It mixes jujube paste with hot water and records three pills per dose, three doses by day and one at night.", "For cough with a floating pulse, the text assigns Houpo and Mahuang Decoction.", "Formula for Houpo and Mahuang Decoction.", "Houpo (magnolia bark), 5 liang. Mahuang (ephedra), 4 liang. Shigao (gypsum), a piece the size of a hen's egg. Xingren (apricot kernels), half a sheng. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed. Wuweizi (schisandra fruit), half a sheng.", "For these six ingredients, the recipe uses 1 dou of water. Ephedra is boiled first and the foam skimmed off. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng. The liquid is strained and divided into three warm doses.", "For cough with a deep pulse, the text assigns Zeqi Decoction.", "Formula for Zeqi Decoction.", "Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed. Zishen, 5 liang. Zeqi, 3 jin. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 5 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 3 liang, roasted.", "For these six ingredients, the historical recipe uses 5 dou of east-flowing water. The zeqi is boiled first down to 1 dou and 5 sheng. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 5 sheng. The text records warm doses of 5 he through the day and night until the liquid is finished.", "For cough with qi rising, discomfort in the throat, and a rapid pulse, the text assigns Maimendong Decoction.", "Formula for Maimendong Decoction.", "Maimendong (ophiopogon tuber), 7 sheng. Banxia (pinellia), 1 sheng, washed. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Jingmi (nonglutinous rice), 3 he. Dazao (jujubes), 12.", "For these six ingredients, the recipe boils 1 dou and 2 sheng of water down to 6 sheng and strains out the dregs. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times by day and three at night.", "For cough with upward surging, needing to prop oneself up to breathe, inability to lie down, and a floating, wiry pulse, the text assigns Minor Green Dragon Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Green Dragon Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 3 liang, roasted. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang. Shaoyao (peony root), 3 liang. Wuweizi (schisandra fruit), half a sheng. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 3 liang. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed. Xixin (asarum), 3 liang.", "For these eight ingredients, the recipe starts with 1 dou of water. Ephedra is boiled first until the volume falls by 2 sheng and the foam is skimmed off. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng. The dregs are removed and the liquid divided into three warm doses.", "For cough with chest fullness, shaking chills, a rapid pulse, dry throat without thirst, and intermittent spitting of turbid, foul-smelling saliva, the text says that pus like rice gruel eventually comes up. It calls the condition lung abscess and assigns Jiegeng Decoction.", "Formula for Jiegeng Decoction.", "Jiegeng (balloon flower root), 1 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 2 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "For cough with labored breathing, eyes that look as though they might protrude from their sockets, and a floating, large pulse, the text names lung distension. It assigns Yuebi Decoction with Banxia and also names Minor Green Dragon Decoction with Shigao.", "Formula for Yuebi Decoction with Banxia.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 6 liang. Shigao (gypsum), half a jin. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang. Dazao (jujubes), 15. Banxia (pinellia), half a sheng, washed.", "For these six ingredients, the recipe uses 6 sheng of water. Ephedra is boiled first and the foam skimmed off. The remaining ingredients are added and boiled down to 3 sheng. The liquid is strained and divided into three warm doses.", "Formula for Minor Green Dragon Decoction with Shigao.", "The preceding Minor Green Dragon Decoction with 2 liang of gypsum added.", "For cough with upward surging qi, wheezing, oppressive chest fullness and distension, swelling of the body and face, clear nasal discharge, and loss of smell, the text names lung distension and assigns Tinglizi and Dazao Lung-Draining Decoction.", "Formula for Tinglizi and Dazao Lung-Draining Decoction.", "Tinglizi (pepperweed seed), dry-heated until yellow, pounded, and made into a pellet the size of a shot pellet. Dazao (jujubes), 12.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils the jujubes first in 3 sheng of water down to 2 sheng, then removes them. It adds the tinglizi, boils down to 1 sheng, strains out the dregs, and records the entire amount as one dose.", "When the person seems to cough but does not really cough, spits abundant frothy saliva, and has no thirst, the text calls this lung cold and assigns Gancao and Ganjiang Decoction.", "Formula for Gancao and Ganjiang Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 4 liang, roasted. Ganjiang (dried ginger), 2 liang, fire-processed.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 3 sheng of water down to 1 sheng and 5 he, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "For cough with persistent spitting of frothy saliva, dry throat, thirst, and a floating, fine, rapid pulse, the text calls the pattern lung wilting and assigns Roasted Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Roasted Gancao Decoction.", "Gancao (licorice), 4 liang, roasted. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang. Maimendong (ophiopogon tuber), half a sheng. Maren (hemp kernels), half a sheng. Dihuang (rehmannia root), 1 jin. Ejiao (donkey-hide gelatin), 2 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 2 liang. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang. Dazao (jujubes), 30."], "notes": [["但坐不得眠", "the need to remain seated because sleep is impossible", "但坐不得眠", "「但」写只能这样。「坐」与后句不得眠连用，突出咳喘使人无法安睡。", "Dan limits the person to remaining seated. The wording emphasizes how coughing and difficult breathing interfere with sleep."], ["東流水五鬥", "5 dou of east-flowing water", "东流水", "这是原方指定的水源，按水流方向来称呼。「斗」是古容量单位。", "The original recipe specifies water by the direction it flows. Dou is a historical unit of volume."], ["咳逆倚息", "needing to prop oneself up to breathe", "倚息", "「倚」有凭靠的意思，「息」是呼吸。下文以不得卧进一步说明姿势与呼吸的关系。", "Yi means to lean or support oneself. Xi means to breathe. The following words add that the person cannot lie down."], ["如米粥者", "pus like rice gruel", "如米粥", "这里用米粥的外观来描写吐出的脓，接着才给出古书的病名肺痈。", "The passage compares the appearance of the pus to rice gruel and then gives the classical name lung abscess."], ["目如脫狀", "eyes that look as though they might protrude from their sockets", "目如脱状", "「如……状」说明这是外观比喻，形容眼睛像要突出。", "The wording signals a comparison describing the appearance of eyes that seem about to protrude."], ["不聞香臭", "loss of smell", "不闻香臭", "「闻」在这里是闻气味。用「香」「臭」并列，表示各种气味都闻不到。", "Wen means to smell here. Fragrant and foul odors stand for the range of smells the person cannot detect."], ["似咳非咳", "seems to cough but does not really cough", "似咳非咳", "「似……非……」写像咳却又不像通常的咳。下一小句把注意力转向涎沫很多。", "The phrase compares the action to a cough before qualifying that impression. The next clause draws attention to abundant frothy saliva."], ["此為肺痿", "the text calls the pattern lung wilting", "肺痿", "「痿」带有衰弱、失去力量的意思。这里是作者给这组咳、渴、唾涎沫等表现起的古病名。", "Wei carries the sense of weakening. Lung wilting is the text's historical name for the group of signs including cough, thirst, and frothy saliva."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-28", "page": 3, "title": "Cough, Retained Fluids, Yellow Sweat, and Joint-Running", "english": ["For these nine ingredients, the recipe uses 7 sheng of wine and 8 sheng of water. It boils eight ingredients down to 3 sheng, strains out the dregs, and dissolves the gelatin completely in the liquid. It records a warm dose of 1 sheng three times daily.", "A question asks about illnesses due to retained fluid. The teacher names four types: phlegm-fluid, suspended fluid, overflowing fluid, and propping fluid.", "When someone formerly robust becomes thin and fluid moves through the intestines with a trickling sound, the text calls the pattern phlegm-fluid.", "When fluid flows below the flanks and coughing or spitting draws on the pain, the text calls it suspended fluid.", "When fluid moves into the limbs, sweat fails to appear when expected, and the body feels painful and heavy, the text calls it overflowing fluid.", "When fluid collects below the diaphragm, coughing forces the person to prop themselves up to breathe, and shortness of breath prevents lying down. The person looks swollen. The text calls this propping fluid.", "When water is in the heart, the text describes a firm, pounding sensation below the heart, shortness of breath, and an aversion to drinking water.", "When water is in the lung, the text expects frothy saliva to be brought up and a desire to drink water.", "When water is in the spleen, the text describes weak breathing and bodily heaviness.", "When water is in the liver, the text describes a propping fullness beneath the flanks, with flank pain on sneezing.", "When water is in the kidney, the text describes palpitations below the heart.", "With lingering fluid below the heart, the text expects a patch of cold on the back about the size of a palm. Coughing brings pain below the ribs that draws upward to the hollows above the collarbones.", "With lingering fluid in the chest, the text expects shortness of breath with thirst and pain running through the joints of the limbs.", "When an otherwise well person eats little and drinks a great deal, the text says water collects below the heart and eventually causes illness. A severe case brings palpitations, a mild case shortness of breath. Wiriness at both pulse positions indicates cold, and wiriness on one side indicates retained fluid.", "For shortness of breath with a small amount of retained fluid, the text describes expelling it through urination.", "The person's pulse is hidden, and they feel an urge for their bowels to move on their own. The movement brings relief, but firmness and fullness below the heart continue. The text calls this lingering fluid and assigns Gansui and Banxia Decoction.", "Formula for Gansui and Banxia Decoction.", "Gansui (kansui root), 3 large pieces. Banxia (pinellia), 12 pieces, washed. Shaoyao (peony root), 5 pieces. Gancao (licorice), one finger-sized piece, roasted.", "For these four ingredients, the historical recipe boils 2 sheng of water down to half a sheng and strains out the dregs. It mixes the liquid with half a sheng of honey, boils down to 8 he, and records the whole amount as one dose.", "For phlegm-fluid below the heart with propping fullness of the chest and flanks, dizziness, and a deep, wiry pulse, the text assigns Fuling, Guizhi, Baizhu, and Gancao Decoction.", "Formula for Fuling, Guizhi, Baizhu, and Gancao Decoction.", "Fuling (poria), 4 liang. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 3 liang. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 3 liang. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted.", "For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 6 sheng of water, boils down to 3 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into three warm doses. The text expects recovery once urination becomes free.", "For suspended fluid with internal pain and a deep, wiry pulse, the text assigns Ten Jujube Decoction, given above.", "For overflowing fluid illness, the text calls for inducing sweat. It assigns Major Green Dragon Decoction and also names Minor Green Dragon Decoction, given earlier.", "Formula for Major Green Dragon Decoction.", "Mahuang (ephedra), 6 liang, joints removed. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang, outer layer removed. Xingren (apricot kernels), 40, skins and tips removed. Gancao (licorice), 2 liang, roasted. Shigao (gypsum), a piece the size of a hen's egg, crushed. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), 3 liang, sliced. Dazao (jujubes), 12, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the recipe uses 9 sheng of water. Ephedra is boiled first until the volume drops by 2 sheng and the foam is skimmed. The rest is added and boiled down to 3 sheng, and the dregs are strained out. The recipe records a warm dose of 1 sheng and covering the patient until sweating is barely perceptible. It describes repeating the dose if there is no sweat.", "With propping fluid around the diaphragm, the person has labored breathing and fullness, hard focal fullness below the heart, a dark complexion, and a deep, tight pulse. If the illness has lasted several tens of days and fails to resolve after the physician induces vomiting and purging, the text assigns Mufangji Decoction. If the illness still does not improve, it gives Mufangji Decoction without Shigao and with Fuling and Mangxiao.", "Formula for Mufangji Decoction.", "Mufangji, 3 liang. Shigao (gypsum), 12 pieces the size of hen's eggs. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 4 liang. For these four ingredients, the recipe uses 6 sheng of water, boils down to 2 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "Formula for Mufangji Decoction without Shigao and with Fuling and Mangxiao.", "Mufangji, 2 liang. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), 2 liang. Fuling (poria), 4 liang. Renshen (ginseng), 4 liang. Mangxiao (mirabilite), 3 he.", "The method refers to four ingredients and boils them in 6 sheng of water down to 2 sheng. After straining, the mirabilite is added and simmered briefly. The liquid is divided into two warm doses. The text expects recovery after a slight bowel movement.", "For propping fluid below the heart with spells of clouded vision and dizziness, the text assigns Zexie Decoction.", "Formula for Zexie Decoction.", "Zexie (alisma rhizome), 5 liang. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 2 liang.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 2 sheng of water down to 1 sheng and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "For propping fluid with chest fullness, the text assigns Houpo and Dahuang Decoction.", "Formula for Houpo and Dahuang Decoction."], "notes": [["曰痰飲，曰懸飲，曰溢飲，曰支飲", "phlegm-fluid, suspended fluid, overflowing fluid, and propping fluid", "四饮", "「饮」在这里是古书给停留体液的总称。接下来四条分别按体液的位置、流向和伴随表现解释四个名字。", "Retained fluid is the broad classical category. The next four lines distinguish the names by location, direction, and associated signs."], ["瀝瀝有聲", "a trickling sound", "沥沥有声", "「沥沥」是拟声词，模拟液体细细流动的声音。这是本条用来辨痰饮的线索之一。", "The doubled word imitates a quiet trickling sound. It is one of the signs given for phlegm-fluid."], ["咳唾引痛", "coughing or spitting draws on the pain", "咳唾引痛", "「引」强调咳嗽或吐唾时牵动疼痛。前句用胁下来交代作者所说的位置。", "Yin conveys pain being pulled on or brought out by coughing or spitting. The preceding phrase places the fluid below the flanks."], ["當汗不汗", "sweat fails to appear when expected", "当汗不汗", "「当」说在本段的解释里原本应当出汗。「不汗」写实际没有出现。", "Dang marks what the text expects. The following phrase says that sweating fails to occur."], ["肋下痛引缺盆", "pain below the ribs that draws upward to the hollows above the collarbones", "痛引缺盆", "「缺盆」在这里指锁骨上方的凹处。「引」写疼痛牵到另一个位置。", "Quepen here names the hollows above the collarbones. The verb describes pain drawing from one location to another."], ["脈雙弦者寒也，脈偏弦者飲也", "Wiriness at both pulse positions indicates cold, and wiriness on one side indicates retained fluid", "双弦与偏弦", "本条用「双」「偏」对举，区别两处都弦与只在一侧见弦在本书中的解释。", "The two terms compare wiriness at both positions with wiriness on only one side. The text gives each a separate reading."], ["面色黧黑", "a dark complexion", "黧黑", "「黧」读 lí，指颜色暗黑。本条把面色与喘满、心下痞坚、沉紧脉并列。", "Pronounced lí, this character describes a dark color. Complexion appears alongside breathing difficulty, focal fullness, and a deep, tight pulse."], ["苦冒眩", "spells of clouded vision and dizziness", "冒眩", "「冒」有蒙住的感觉，「眩」写晕眩。两个字放在一起描写头目不清的感受。", "Mao evokes a sense of being clouded over, and xuan means dizziness. Together they describe a disturbance centered on the head and vision."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-28", "page": 4, "title": "Cough, Retained Fluids, Yellow Sweat, and Joint-Running", "english": ["Houpo (magnolia bark), 8 liang. Dahuang (rhubarb), 4 liang.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 5 sheng of water down to 2 sheng and records a warm first dose of 1 sheng, with a second dose if the condition has not improved.", "For propping fluid with inability to catch one's breath, the text assigns Tinglizi and Dazao Lung-Draining Decoction, given above.", "For propping fluid without thirst, when the person retches or vomits water, the text assigns Minor Banxia Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Banxia Decoction.", "Banxia (pinellia), 1 sheng, washed. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), half a jin.", "For these two ingredients, the recipe boils 7 sheng of water down to 1 and a half sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "For abdominal fullness, dryness of the mouth and tongue, and retained fluid in the intestines, the text assigns Fangji, Jiaomu, Tinglizi, and Dahuang Pills.", "Formula for Fangji, Jiaomu, Tinglizi, and Dahuang Pills.", "Fangji, jiaomu (Sichuan pepper seeds), tinglizi (pepperweed seed), and dahuang (rhubarb), 1 liang each.", "The four ingredients are pounded and sifted. The recipe makes pills with refined honey, each the size of a Chinese parasol tree seed. It records one pill with a drink before meals three times daily, with gradual increases when no effect is felt.", "For retained fluid around the diaphragm with retching, vomiting, dizziness, and palpitations, the text assigns Minor Banxia Decoction with Fuling.", "Formula for Minor Banxia Decoction with Fuling.", "Banxia (pinellia), 1 sheng, washed. Shengjiang (fresh ginger), half a jin. Fuling (poria), 4 liang.", "For these three ingredients, the recipe boils 7 sheng of water down to 2 sheng, strains out the dregs, and divides the liquid into two warm doses.", "When there is throbbing below the navel with spitting of frothy saliva and dizziness, the text infers retained water and assigns Wuling Powder.", "Formula for Wuling Powder.", "Zhuling (polyporus), 18 zhu, outer layer removed. Zexie (alisma rhizome), 1 liang and 6 zhu. Baizhu (white atractylodes), 18 zhu. Fuling (poria), 18 zhu. Guizhi (cinnamon twig), half a liang.", "The five ingredients are pounded to a powder. The recipe describes mixing a square-inch spoonful with plain rice liquid and taking it three times daily. It calls for plenty of warm water, expects relief when sweating occurs, and refers to the usual aftercare.", "The teacher said that these illnesses are classified as wind-water, skin-water, proper water, stone-water, or yellow sweat.", "In wind-water, the pulse is naturally floating. The signs are aching joints and aversion to wind.", "In skin-water, the pulse is also floating. The swelling pits beneath the finger when pressed. There is no aversion to wind, the abdomen is like a drum, and there is no thirst. The text describes inducing sweat.", "In proper water, the pulse is deep and slow, and the sign is labored breathing.", "In stone-water, the pulse is naturally deep. There is abdominal fullness without labored breathing. The text describes promoting urination.", "In yellow sweat, the pulse is deep and slow. There is fever and chest fullness, and the limbs, head, and face swell. The text expects persistent cases to lead to abscesses and pus.", "When the pulse is floating and surging, the text associates floating with wind and surging with qi. Where wind and qi interact, dominant wind brings an itchy rash. The text calls the resulting itching discharging wind and says it eventually produces crusted sores. Dominant qi brings a water pattern with extensive swelling and difficulty bending forward or looking up. The text expects relief after sweating. If there is aversion to wind, it infers deficiency and calls the pattern wind-water. If there is no aversion to wind, urination is free, cold is present in the upper burner, and saliva is abundant, it calls the pattern yellow sweat.", "The text takes a deep, slippery wrist pulse as a sign of internal retained fluid. It calls swelling and heat around the face and eyes wind-water. There is slight puffiness above the eye sockets, compared to the appearance of a silkworm just rising from rest. The pulse in the neck is perceptible, the person coughs from time to time, and pressure on the hands or feet leaves an indentation that does not promptly rise. The text also classifies this as wind-water.", "In Taiyang illness, a floating, tight pulse normally goes with aching joints. If the joints do not hurt but the body feels heavy and sore and there is no thirst, the text calls it wind-water and expects recovery through sweating. If there is aversion to cold, it diagnoses extreme deficiency due to induced sweating. It calls thirst without aversion to cold skin-water. For cold swelling resembling generalized impediment, with blockage in the chest, inability to eat, pain that gathers, agitation, and inability to sleep, it uses the name yellow sweat.", "When pain lies in the joints and there is cough with labored breathing but no thirst, the text calls it proper water. The person looks swollen, and the text expects recovery after induced sweating. For these illnesses, however, when thirst occurs with diarrhea and frequent urination, it forbids inducing sweat and describes promoting urination instead.", "In heart-water illness, the body feels heavy, breathing is weak, the person cannot lie down and is agitated, and there is swelling in the genital region.", "In liver-water illness, the abdomen is enlarged, the person cannot turn unaided, and there is pain beneath the flanks. The text describes scant production of fluids with urination becoming free again.", "In lung-water illness, the body swells, urination is difficult, and there are occasional loose, duck-like stools.", "In spleen-water illness, the abdomen is enlarged, the limbs feel distressingly heavy, fluids fail to form, and the person has weak breathing and difficulty urinating."], "notes": [["如梧桐子大", "the size of a Chinese parasol tree seed", "梧桐子大", "这里以梧桐种子作大小参照。这类写法在古方描述丸剂时常见。", "The seed serves as a size comparison, a common way historical formulas describe pills."], ["臍下悸", "throbbing below the navel", "脐下悸", "「悸」指跳动感。「脐下」把感到跳动的位置放在肚脐下面。", "Ji refers to a throbbing sensation. The first two characters place it below the navel."], ["日三眼", "taking it three times daily", "日三眼", "底本此处作「眼」。按同类方书的惯用句和全句语意，英译作每日三服。", "This edition reads 眼 where the surrounding recipe and standard wording indicate 服. The translation reads it as three doses a day."], ["有風水，有皮水，有正水，有石水，有黃汗", "wind-water, skin-water, proper water, stone-water, or yellow sweat", "水病五类", "老师先列五个古病名，下面逐条配以脉象和可辨认的表现。", "The teacher lists five classical names before pairing them with their pulse qualities and distinguishing signs."], ["按之沒指", "The swelling pits beneath the finger when pressed", "按之没指", "「没」指手指按进去，说明肿处受按时出现凹陷。下文还另有「陷而不起」的说法。", "The wording describes the finger sinking into swelling when pressure is applied. A later paragraph describes the indentation persisting."], ["如蠶新臥起狀", "compared to the appearance of a silkworm just rising from rest", "蚕新卧起", "这里是外观的比喻，接在眼眶上微肿之后，用来让读者想象那一点浮起的形态。", "This is an image for the slight swelling above the eye sockets, meant to help the reader picture its raised appearance."], ["體重而酸", "the body feels heavy and sore", "体重而酸", "「重」说身体感觉沉重，「酸」说酸楚。它们和本来预期的关节痛放在一起对照。", "Heavy describes the sensation of weight, and sore describes aching. The text considers these signs in light of the joint pain usually expected."], ["時時鴨溏", "occasional loose, duck-like stools", "鸭溏", "「溏」指便稀软。「鸭」借鸭粪的外观作比喻，「时时」指间或出现。", "Tang means loose stool, with duck droppings used as a comparison. Shishi indicates that it happens from time to time."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-31", "page": 1, "title": "Conditions in Women", "english": ["The teacher said: If a woman's pulse is generally regular but the yin pulse is small and weak, and she vomits, cannot eat, and has neither chills nor fever, the text identifies pregnancy and names Cinnamon Twig Decoction. It expects these signs at sixty days. It says inappropriate treatment sets the pregnancy back by a month and that adding induced vomiting or purging ends it.", "Formula for Cinnamon Twig Decoction.", "Guizhi, three liang, with the bark removed. Shaoyao, three liang. Roasted Gancao, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained, then divided into three warm portions.", "If a woman has a longstanding fixed mass, her periods have been absent for under three months, bleeding continues, and movement is felt above the navel, the text attributes it to interference from the fixed mass. Movement at six months dates the pregnancy to when the menses still flowed three months earlier. It calls the bleeding an aberrant discharge during the three months after menstruation ceased and says the persistent mass accounts for the continuing bleeding. It records expelling the mass with Guizhi and Fuling Pills.", "Formula for Guizhi and Fuling Pills.", "Guizhi, Fuling, Moutan, peach kernels, and Shaoyao, in equal parts.", "The text records grinding the five ingredients and forming pills with refined honey, the size of rabbit droppings. It gives one daily before food, gradually increasing to three if no effect is felt.", "In the sixth or seventh month of pregnancy, the text describes a wiry pulse, fever, increased swelling of the pregnant abdomen, abdominal pain, aversion to cold, and a lower abdomen that feels fanned by cold air. It attributes this to the womb opening and names Fuzi Decoction to warm it.", "Formula for Fuzi Decoction.", "Two Fuzi, heat-processed, peeled, and broken into eight pieces. Fuling, three liang. Renshen, two liang. Baizhu, four liang. Shaoyao, three liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "The teacher said: Some women have persistent uterine bleeding, and some continue to bleed without a break after miscarriage. If there is abdominal pain during pregnancy, the text calls this obstruction of the womb and names Ejiao and Aiye Decoction.", "Formula for Ejiao and Aiye Decoction.", "Dihuang, six liang. Xiongqiong, two liang. Ejiao, two liang. Aiye, three liang. Danggui, three liang. Shaoyao, four liang. Gancao, two liang.", "For the seven ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water and three sheng of clear wine. Six ingredients are boiled to yield three sheng and strained. Ejiao is added and melted. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "For cramping abdominal pain during pregnancy, the text names Danggui and Shaoyao Powder.", "Formula for Danggui and Shaoyao Powder.", "Danggui, three liang. Shaoyao, one jin. Fuling, four liang. Baizhu, four liang. Zexie, half a jin. Xiongqiong, three liang.", "The text records pounding all six ingredients into powder and mixing a square-inch spoonful with warm wine, three times daily.", "For persistent vomiting during pregnancy, the text names Ganjiang, Renshen, and Banxia Pills.", "Formula for Ganjiang, Renshen, and Banxia Pills.", "Ganjiang, one liang. Renshen, one liang. Banxia, two liang, washed.", "The text records grinding the three ingredients and making a paste with fresh ginger juice, then forming pills the size of wutong seeds. It gives five pills with liquid three times daily.", "For difficult urination during pregnancy with eating and drinking as usual, the text names Danggui, Beimu, and Kushen Pills.", "Formula for Danggui, Beimu, and Kushen Pills.", "Danggui, Beimu, and Kushen, four liang each.", "The text records grinding the three ingredients and forming pills the size of small beans with refined honey. It gives three pills taken with liquid three times daily.", "For pregnancy with water qi, difficult urination, shivering aversion to cold, and dizziness upon standing, the text names Kuizi and Fuling Powder.", "Formula heading for Kuizi and Ling Powder.", "Kuizi, one jin. Fuling, three liang.", "The text records pounding the two ingredients into powder and taking a square-inch spoonful with liquid three times daily. Recovery is expected once urination flows freely.", "For pregnancy without another illness, the text records regular use of Danggui Powder in the belief that it eases childbirth and helps prevent illness afterward.", "Formula for Danggui Powder.", "Danggui, Huangqin, Shaoyao, and Xiongqiong, one jin each. Baizhu, half a jin.", "The text records pounding the five ingredients into powder and taking a square-inch spoonful with wine twice daily.", "For pregnancy with cold-dampness, sometimes with abdominal pain, vexation, pain in the heart region, inability to eat or drink, and pronounced fetal movement, the text speaks of nourishing the pregnancy and names Baizhu Powder.", "Formula for Baizhu Powder.", "Baizhu, Xiongqiong, Shujiao with the seeds removed and heated to release moisture, and Muli, in equal parts."], "notes": [["平脈", "pulse is generally regular", "平脉", "这一句先说脉大体平常，再说阴脉小弱，接着才列呕与不能食。", "The line gives the overall impression of the pulse first, then specifies a small and weak yin pulse before listing the other signs."], ["經斷", "her periods have been absent", "经断", "经指月经。下文以经断前后数了几个月，把出血和所感到的动势放在时间线上。", "經 refers to menstruation. The passage counts months on either side of its cessation to situate the bleeding and perceived movement."], ["宿有症病", "longstanding fixed mass", "宿与症", "宿写原来已有。症在本段指留滞的一种积块，下文以其症仍在解释出血。", "宿 means already present. 症 here refers to a persistent mass, which the passage uses to explain continuing bleeding."], ["少腹如扇", "a lower abdomen that feels fanned by cold air", "如扇", "少腹指下腹。原文借扇动时吹来的风形容下腹所感到的寒凉。", "少腹 means the lower abdomen. The image of a fan conveys a sensation of cooling air there."], ["半產", "miscarriage", "半产", "这是古文对妊娠中途终止的称呼，本句说的是随后持续出血。", "This is a classical term for a pregnancy ending before term. Here it is followed by a description of persistent bleeding."], ["飲食如故", "eating and drinking as usual", "如故", "如故就是和平常一样。这一句单列小便困难，同时交代饮食状态未变。", "如故 means as before. The line records difficult urination alongside unchanged eating and drinking."], ["葵子苓散方", "Formula heading for Kuizi and Ling Powder", "方题里的苓", "上句写葵子茯苓散，本行方题省写为葵子苓散。下行药味明确列茯苓。", "The preceding line names Kuizi and Fuling Powder. The heading shortens Fuling to Ling, and the ingredient list gives Fuling in full."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-31", "page": 2, "title": "Conditions in Women", "english": ["The text records pounding the four ingredients into powder and taking a one-qian spoonful with wine three times by day and once at night.", "At seven months of pregnancy, the text describes abdominal fullness, inability to urinate, and a waterlogged appearance from the waist down. It interprets this as failure to nourish Taiyin when nourishment is due, together with repletion of heart qi. It records reducing treatment at Laogong and Guanyuan and expects recovery once urine passes freely.", "A question: The text names three disorders after childbirth: spasms, clouded awareness, and difficult bowel movements. What does this mean? The teacher replied: After childbirth blood is deficient and there is much sweating, making wind strike more likely. This accounts for spasms. Blood loss, further sweating, and abundant cold account for clouded awareness. Loss of fluids dries the stomach, accounting for difficult bowel movements.", "In clouded awareness after childbirth, the pulse is faint and weak. The woman vomits and cannot eat, yet the stool is hard and only the head sweats. The text says blood deficiency brings reversal, which brings clouded awareness. Profuse sweating marks approaching relief. It explains sweating confined to the head by deficiency of blood and reversal below, with isolated yang emerging above. After childbirth, loss of yin and blood leaves yang qi predominant on its own. The text therefore expects sweating before yin and yang are restored. For hard stool, vomiting, and inability to eat it names Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Formula for Minor Bupleurum Decoction.", "Chaihu, half a jin. Huangqin, three liang. Renshen, three liang. Gancao, three liang. Banxia, half a sheng, washed. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split.", "For the seven ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, boiled down to six sheng and strained, then boiled again to yield three sheng. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "If clouded awareness resolves and the woman can eat but fever recurs seven or eight days later, the text identifies stomach repletion and names Major Qi-Order Decoction.", "Formula for Major Qi-Order Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang, washed with wine. Houpo, half a jin, roasted and with the outer layer removed. Five roasted Zhishi fruits. Mangxiao, three ge.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives one dou of water. Two ingredients are boiled first to yield five sheng and strained. Dahuang is added and boiled down to two sheng, then strained. Mangxiao is added and the liquid brought briefly to a boil once or twice over low heat, then divided into two warm portions. A bowel movement is the condition for omitting the later portion.", "For cramping abdominal pain after childbirth attributed to deficiency and cold, the text names Danggui, Fresh Ginger, and Mutton Decoction.", "Formula for Danggui, Fresh Ginger, and Mutton Decoction.", "Danggui, three liang. Fresh ginger, five liang. Mutton, one jin.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "For abdominal pain after childbirth with vexation and fullness that prevents lying down, the text rules out purging and names Zhishi and Shaoyao Powder to harmonize the condition.", "Formula for Zhishi and Shaoyao Powder.", "Zhishi and Shaoyao, in equal parts.", "The text records pounding the two ingredients into powder and taking a square-inch spoonful with wheat gruel three times daily.", "The teacher said: For pain after childbirth the text first names Zhishi and Shaoyao Powder. If the pain persists, it attributes it to stagnant blood lodged below the navel and names Decoction to Expel Stagnant Blood.", "Formula for Decoction to Expel Stagnant Blood.", "Dahuang, three liang. Twenty peach kernels, with skins and tips removed. Twenty Zhechong, with legs removed.", "The text records grinding the three ingredients, making pills with refined honey, and boiling them in one sheng of wine to yield eight ge, taken all at once. It expects blood to pass resembling pig liver.", "Seven or eight days after childbirth, a hard, painful lower abdomen without Taiyang signs is attributed to postpartum discharge that has not fully cleared. If there is no bowel movement, with restlessness, fever, and a slightly replete pulse, the text describes harmonizing. If restlessness comes in the late afternoon and eating brings delirious speech that resolves by night, it names Major Qi-Order Decoction, given earlier.", "For postpartum wind strike that persists for several tens of days with headache, aversion to cold, fever, fullness below the heart, dry retching, and continuing slight spontaneous sweating, the text names Minor Bupleurum Decoction, given earlier.", "For postpartum wind strike with fever, red face, headache, sweating with panting, and a wiry and rapid pulse, the text names Bamboo Leaf Decoction.", "Formula for Bamboo Leaf Decoction.", "One handful of bamboo leaves. Gegen, three liang. Jiegeng, one liang. Renshen, one liang. Gancao, one liang. Fresh ginger, five liang. Fifteen jujubes, split.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives eight sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "For postpartum distress and disquiet with retching and no exterior signs, the text describes deficiency in the nursing period and names Large Bamboo Bark Pills.", "Formula for Large Bamboo Bark Pills."], "notes": [["右四味", "the four ingredients", "承接白术散", "上一页末列白术散的四味，本页从研粉和用酒送下接着写。", "The previous page ends with the four ingredients of Baizhu Powder. The opening line continues with their preparation and the wine used with the powder."], ["懷身七月", "At seven months of pregnancy", "怀身", "怀身表示怀孕。七月说孕期到了第七个月。", "懷身 refers to pregnancy. 七月 places the description in the seventh month."], ["郁冒", "clouded awareness", "郁冒", "冒有蒙覆之意。原文以此为产后三种情况之一，并另起一段描述脉、汗和饮食。", "冒 evokes a covering or clouding. The text lists it among three postpartum disorders, then describes the pulse, sweating, and ability to eat."], ["但頭汗出", "only the head sweats", "但头汗出", "但表示只有。此处用来限制出汗的部位，和下文所说大汗出相呼应。", "但 means only. It limits the location of sweating, which the paragraph later discusses alongside profuse sweating."], ["瘀血著臍下", "stagnant blood lodged below the navel", "著脐下", "著是停着、留着。此句是在产后腹痛一直未愈的条件下作的解释。", "著 means to lodge or become fixed. The text gives this explanation when postpartum abdominal pain persists."], ["惡露不盡", "postpartum discharge that has not fully cleared", "恶露", "恶露指产后排出的分泌物和血液。「不尽」在本句被用来说明下腹硬痛。", "惡露 refers to postpartum discharge. The text attributes hardness and pain in the lower abdomen to its incomplete clearance."], ["日晡所煩躁", "restlessness comes in the late afternoon", "日晡", "日晡指下午偏晚的时候。这段接着写吃东西后谵语，到夜间止。", "日晡 is late afternoon. The sentence goes on to describe delirious speech after eating that resolves at night."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-31", "page": 3, "title": "Conditions in Women", "english": ["Zhuru and Shigao, two fen each. Guizhi, one fen. Gancao, seven fen. Baiwei, one fen.", "The text records grinding the five ingredients and forming pills the size of pellets with jujube flesh. It gives one pill with liquid at a time, three by day and two at night, and doubles Baiwei when heat is present.", "For postpartum diarrhea with an extremely deficient pulse, the text names Baitouweng Decoction with Gancao and Ejiao.", "Formula for Baitouweng Decoction with Gancao and Ejiao.", "Baitouweng, two liang. Huanglian, three liang. Baipi, three liang. Qinpi, three liang. Gancao, two liang. Ejiao, two liang.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water. Five ingredients are boiled first to yield three sheng and strained. Ejiao is added and melted. The liquid is divided into three warm portions.", "For a woman with a feeling as if a morsel of roasted meat were lodged in the throat, the text names Banxia, Houpo, Fuling, and Fresh Ginger Decoction.", "Formula for Banxia, Houpo, Fuling, and Fresh Ginger Decoction.", "Banxia, one sheng, washed. Houpo, three liang. Fuling, four liang. Fresh ginger, five liang. Suye, two liang.", "For these five ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, boiled down to four sheng and strained, then divided into four warm portions, three by day and one at night. If pain is distressing, the text omits Suye and adds two liang of Jiegeng.", "When a woman has visceral dryness, with sadness and a desire to cry, repeated yawning and stretching, and an appearance as though spirits were causing these signs, the text names Gancao, Wheat, and Jujube Decoction.", "Formula for Gancao, Wheat, and Jujube Decoction.", "Gancao, three liang. Wheat, one sheng. Ten jujubes, split.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives six sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained, then divided into three warm portions.", "If a woman spits frothy saliva and the physician purges her instead, obstructed fullness develops below the heart. The text first addresses the frothy saliva with Jiegeng, Gancao, Fuling, and Zexie Decoction, then addresses the obstructed fullness with Xiexin Decoction.", "Formula for Jiegeng, Gancao, Fuling, and Zexie Decoction.", "Jiegeng, three liang. Gancao, two liang. Fuling, three liang. Zexie, two liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "Formula for Xiexin Decoction.", "Dahuang, two liang. Huanglian, one liang.", "The text records steeping the two ingredients in two sheng of vigorously boiling water, squeezing out the liquid after a short interval, and dividing it into two warm portions.", "In conditions affecting women, the text describes deficiency, accumulated cold, and binding that stops menstrual flow and leaves blood bound at the opening of the womb. Pain may circle the navel like cold shan, or lie at Guanyuan with skin like fish scales. There may be pulling pain in the genital region and aversion to cold in the lower abdomen. Pain may draw into the lower back and spine or descend to Qijie. The text groups these varied conditions under daixia. It calls for assessing cold and heat, deficiency and excess, and tight and wiry pulses, and identifying each source when choosing needling or medicine. The teacher urges the student to distinguish and remember these points.", "A question: Why might a woman around fifty have bleeding for several tens of days, fever in the evening, internal tightness in the lower abdomen, abdominal fullness, vexing heat in the palms, and dry lips and mouth? The teacher replied: The text assigns this to daixia. How is it known? She previously had a miscarriage and stagnant blood has remained in the lower abdomen. The text attributes the dry lips and mouth to this and names Warm the Channels Decoction.", "Formula for Warm the Channels Decoction.", "Wuzhuyu, three liang. Danggui, Xiongqiong, Shaoyao, Renshen, Guizhi, Ejiao, Moutan bark, Gancao, and fresh ginger, two liang each.", "For these ten ingredients, the text gives one dou of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "For impaired menstrual flow with fullness and pain in the lower abdomen, or two periods in a single month, the text names Wanggua Root Powder. It also lists genital swelling.", "Formula for Wanggua Root Powder.", "Wanggua root, Shaoyao, and Guizhi, three fen each. Three Zhechong.", "The text records pounding the four ingredients into a powder and taking a square-inch spoonful with wine three times daily.", "For miscarriage or persistent uterine bleeding, the text names Xuanfuhua Decoction. For a deficient and weak pulse, it names Huangqi and Danggui Decoction.", "Formula for Xuanfuhua Decoction.", "Xuanfuhua, three liang. Fourteen scallion stalks. A little Xinjiang, newly dyed crimson silk.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water, boiled down to one sheng and strained, taken all at once.", "Formula for Huangqi and Danggui Decoction.", "Huangqi, three liang. Danggui, half a liang.", "For these two ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily."], "notes": [["如彈子大", "the size of pellets", "弹子大", "弹子指可弹射的小丸。这里用弹子的大小帮助读者想象所制药丸。", "彈子 describes a small pellet. The source uses it as a size comparison for the pills."], ["如有炙臠", "as if a morsel of roasted meat were lodged", "炙脔", "炙是烤，脔是切成的小块。原文用烤肉块比喻咽中有东西的感觉。", "炙 means roasted and 臠 is a cut morsel. The image describes how the throat feels."], ["數欠伸", "repeated yawning and stretching", "欠伸", "欠是打呵欠，伸是伸展身体。数在这里读 shuò，表示动作屡次出现。", "欠 is yawning and 伸 is stretching. 數 is read shuò and tells how often these actions occur."], ["右四味", "For these four ingredients", "药味计数的原文差异", "本条煎法实际写「右四味」，上行只列甘草、小麦、大枣三味。译文保留煎法所记四味。", "The preparation gives a count of four, while the preceding ingredient line lists three: Gancao, wheat, and jujube. The count in the directions is preserved."], ["心下即痞", "obstructed fullness develops below the heart", "心下痞", "心下指心口下方一带，痞写痞塞、发满的感觉。下文将其和吐涎沫分两步叙述。", "心下 refers to the area below the heart. 痞 conveys blockage and fullness. The line then discusses it separately from frothy saliva."], ["此皆帶下", "these varied conditions under daixia", "带下", "这里用带下概括前面多种妇人病候。下一段也把长期下血的例子列在带下之下。", "Here daixia covers several conditions affecting women. The next paragraph also uses the term for a case of prolonged bleeding."], ["年五十所", "around fifty", "五十所", "数词后的所表示约数。它与后面说的出血持续时间分别交代年龄和病程。", "所 after a number makes it approximate. This phrase gives the person's age before the text gives the duration of bleeding."]]}, {"chapter": "chapter-31", "page": 4, "title": "Conditions in Women", "english": ["For sunken menses with persistent uterine bleeding that is dark and clot-like, the text names Ejiao and Ginger Decoction.", "Formula for Ejiao and Ginger Decoction.", "Ejiao, three liang. Dihuang, six liang. Xiongqiong, two liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Danggui, three liang. Shaoyao, three liang.", "Roasted Gancao, two liang.", "For these seven ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water and three sheng of clear wine. Six ingredients are boiled first to yield three sheng and strained. Ejiao is added and melted. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "If a woman's lower abdomen is full like a rounded dun vessel, urination is slightly difficult, and there is no thirst, sometimes after menstruation or childbirth, the text says water and blood have bound together in the blood chamber. It names Dahuang, Gansui, and Ejiao Decoction.", "Formula for Dahuang, Gansui, and Ejiao Decoction.", "Dahuang, four liang. Gansui, two liang. Ejiao, two liang.", "For these three ingredients, the text gives three sheng of water. Two ingredients are boiled to yield one sheng and strained. Ejiao is added and melted. It records the full portion taken warm at once.", "For intermittent abdominal pain when the menstrual flow alternately starts, stops, and starts again, the text names Didang Decoction.", "Formula for Didang Decoction.", "Thirty Shuizhi, heated. Thirty Mengchong, heated after removing wings and legs. Thirty peach kernels. Dahuang, three liang.", "For these four ingredients, the text gives five sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. It records one sheng taken warm and a further portion if there is no purgative effect.", "If menstrual flow ceases, the pelvic region has a hard fixed mass, and white discharge persists, the text attributes this to dried blood and names Alum Pills.", "Formula for Alum Pills.", "Heat-treated alum, three fen. Apricot kernel, one fen.", "The text records grinding the two ingredients, forming pills the size of jujube pits with refined honey, and placing one in the genital passage. For severe cases it records another application.", "For the sixty-two types of wind signs in women, when pain in the abdominal qi and blood feels like stabbing, the text names Honglanhua Wine.", "Formula for Honglanhua Wine.", "Honglanhua, one liang.", "The text records boiling this ingredient in one dou of wine until reduced by half, straining, and dividing the liquid into two warm portions.", "For various abdominal pains in women, the text names Danggui and Shaoyao Powder and also Minor Center-Fortifying Decoction.", "Danggui and Shaoyao Powder is given earlier.", "Formula for Minor Center-Fortifying Decoction.", "Guizhi, three liang. Shaoyao, six liang. Roasted Gancao, three liang. Fresh ginger, three liang, sliced. Twelve jujubes, split. Malt syrup, one sheng.", "For these six ingredients, the text gives seven sheng of water, boiled down to three sheng and strained. Malt syrup is added and dissolved over low heat. It records one sheng taken warm three times daily.", "A question: Why would a woman eat and drink as usual, yet have vexing heat that prevents lying down and need to lean on something to breathe? The teacher replied: The text calls this twisting of the bladder, with inability to urinate. It attributes the condition to twisting of the bladder's tether, expects recovery when urine flows freely, and names Kidney Qi Pills.", "Formula for Kidney Qi Pills.", "Dihuang, eight liang. Shuyu, four liang. Shanzhuyu, four liang. Zexie, three liang. Moutan bark, three liang. Fuling, three liang. Guizhi, one liang. One Fuzi, heat-processed and peeled.", "The text records grinding the eight ingredients and forming pills the size of wutong seeds with refined honey. It gives fifteen pills with warm wine twice daily, gradually increasing to twenty-five if no effect is felt.", "For cold in the genital region, the text names Shechuangzi Powder.", "Formula for Shechuangzi Powder.", "Shechuangzi, one liang.", "The text records grinding the ingredient and blending it with a little white powder, forming a piece the size of a jujube, wrapping it in cotton, and placing it in the genital passage, where it warms on its own.", "A slippery and rapid Shaoyin pulse is interpreted as sores in the genital region. Where the tissue is eroded and ulcerated, the text names Langya Decoction.", "Formula for Langya Decoction.", "Langya, three liang.", "The text records boiling the one ingredient in four sheng of water to yield half a sheng and straining. It describes wrapping cotton around a chopstick to the size of a cocoon, dipping it into the decoction, and letting it drip into the genital passage as a wash four times daily."], "notes": [["漏下色黑如塊", "persistent uterine bleeding that is dark and clot-like", "暗色血块", "漏下写出血淋漓不止。「色黑如块」再补颜色和结块的样子。", "漏下 conveys persistent bleeding. The following words add the dark color and clot-like appearance."], ["如敦狀", "like a rounded dun vessel", "敦的形状", "敦是一种古代器物。这里以它的形状比喻下腹胀满。", "A dun is an ancient vessel. The passage uses its rounded form to picture lower abdominal fullness."], ["水與血俱結在血室", "water and blood have bound together in the blood chamber", "血室", "血室是原文用来定位水血同结的古典名称。俱表示水和血两者都在此结聚。", "Blood chamber is the classical location named for the binding. 俱 means both, so the passage describes water and blood binding together."], ["時行時止", "alternately starts, stops", "时行时止", "两个时连用表示时而这样、时而那样。后面的复行补写停后又来。", "The repeated 時 means now one way, now another. The words that follow add a return after stopping."], ["不得臥，而反倚息", "prevents lying down and need to lean on something to breathe", "倚息", "倚是依靠，息是呼吸。这里与不得卧合读，写从躺不下转到倚靠而息的姿态。", "倚 means to lean and 息 means to breathe. The preceding words explain the posture through difficulty lying down."], ["不知漸增", "gradually increasing to twenty-five if no effect is felt", "不知", "不知在方后指尚未感觉到作用。原文连着用「渐增」和二十五丸写它的记录。", "不知 means that an effect has yet to be noticed. The directions link it with gradual increase and give twenty-five pills as the endpoint."], ["脈滑而數", "slippery and rapid Shaoyin pulse", "滑数", "滑写脉来流利的感觉，数写脉率较快。原句把两者放在同一脉上。", "Slippery describes the smooth movement of the pulse and rapid describes its rate. Both are given for the same pulse."]]}];
